{
  "regions": {
    "Trogo": {
      "name": "Trogo",
      "color": "#c0392b",
      "lore": "# Trogo\n\nTrogo is the name given to the southeastern face of the Dinova Peninsula — the territory that begins where the Meroshe's canyon country ends and descends toward the southern ocean through what is, by any geographic expectation, the wrong kind of landscape. The Meroshe is desert. The southern ocean is warm and wet. Between them, on the slopes and river valleys of the peninsula's southeastern reach, the desert's last water meets the coast's first moisture and produces a consequence that neither the desert peoples to the northwest nor the Maroshi kingdom to the north has entirely figured out what to do with: tropical rainforest.\n\nThe name does not come from any language family associated with the main Azhoran traditions. The Moreshi of the canyon south call it something that renders approximately as \"the wet below.\" The Maroshi court in Dinelv calls it Trogo in their administrative correspondence and notes this as a term used by the people there; where the term originates, the correspondence does not say. It does not appear to be Mittoli. It does not appear to be Moreshi in the triconsonantal root structure. It may be the local peoples' own word for their own place, which would be the most sensible explanation and the one that the administrative records are slowest to offer.\n\n## The Transition\n\nStand at the southern edge of the Meroshe's canyon country and face southeast. Below you, the canyon walls drop and the stone changes color — the red-orange of the high desert giving way to a darker, wetter rock where the canyon rivers are running. Follow the rivers down, and within a day's walk the vegetation changes faster than seems reasonable. Thorn scrub gives way to broadleaf growth. The broadleaf growth gives way to something taller. The air changes — humid first in the morning, then constantly, then oppressively to anyone who arrived from the desert. The stone is still there, still dramatic, still carved canyon-country geology, but the canyons are now full of canopy rather than heat shimmer.\n\nThe transition is not gradual everywhere. The topography creates edges: a ridgeline that catches the southern moisture and drops a fog wall on its windward face while the leeward side stays desert. A canyon that has been sheltered enough to hold humidity for long enough that the vegetation inside it is ten kilometers ahead of the vegetation on the surrounding plateau. And then the reverse — gaps in the ridge where the desert air pushes through in the dry months, creating corridors of sparse growth cutting into the forest. The transition zone is not a line. It is a territory with its own character, belonging to neither system fully.\n\nThe fog is significant. Where desert air meets ocean-loaded humidity along the southeastern ridge, fog forms and stays, sometimes for days. This is not the cold sea-fog of Bouén's coast. It is warm and thick and close, and it waters the middle elevation forest through the dry months when the rainfall alone would not be enough. The fog-forest of the upper transition zone is its own ecosystem, distinct from the canyon desert above and the full tropical canopy below — an intermediate world of mosses and cloud-dependent plants that exists only because two atmospheric systems are pressing against each other at this latitude.\n\n## Geography\n\nThe southeastern drainage of the Dinova peninsula runs through Trogo to the coast. Three named river systems collect the meltwater — such as it is, in a desert — from the canyon country and gain strength as they descend into the wetter south. By the time they reach the coast, they are full rivers carrying substantial volume year-round. The largest, which Maroshi records call the Trogoreth (\"the Trogo river,\" a construction that acknowledges they have no better name for it), has a wide delta mouth that has silted into a shallow estuary system. Fishing communities live on the estuary. The river is navigable for small vessels some distance upstream before the canyon narrowing stops boat traffic.\n\nThe land itself is steep. Trogo is not a lowland rainforest in the manner of the river basins of the southern archipelago; it is a slope forest, on the descending face of the peninsula's interior plateau, and the terrain reflects this — rivers run fast, elevation changes quickly, and the forest grows on ground that would challenge cultivation even if anyone were trying to cultivate it in the conventional sense. The coastal strip, where the rivers slow and the land flattens near the southern sea, is the most hospitable section. The upper slopes are forest on grades that make travel difficult.\n\nThe southeastern coast of Trogo faces the southern ocean and the Azhor Stones, which are visible from the higher coastal headlands on clear days. The coast is not extensively sheltered — no deep natural harbors on the scale of Hama — but several river mouths have been developed into small anchorages adequate for the coastal and regional trade that Trogo's communities conduct.\n\n## The Ecology\n\nTrogo's ecology is layered by altitude. The upper edge, where the canyon meets the fog zone, is adapted to intermittent moisture: plants that can survive dry months and wet ones, that take the fog when it comes and store it. The canyon fig species that grow along the upper river courses root in the rock faces and extend canopy far beyond what the soil would support. This forest is old by Azhoran standards and dense in its species composition at every layer.\n\nThe mid-slope forest is full tropical rainforest by the measures that matter — closed canopy, high humidity year-round, the layer structure of tall emergents over a canopy over understory over ground. The species composition overlaps partially with the forests of the southern archipelago's larger islands, suggesting either a shared ancient forest cover or long-distance seed dispersal along ocean and river routes. Whether the same species arrived in Trogo from the south or whether the southern islands' forests descend from stock that once covered this coast is a question that requires a botanist willing to spend years here, and the Academy has not yet sent one.\n\nAt the coast and along the lower river systems, the mangrove and estuary ecology takes over — brackish-adapted vegetation, root systems that make the river mouths look solid but are not, and the bird and fish populations that the estuary communities depend on.\n\nThe transition zone supports animals from both the desert and the forest at its edges. Meroshe species that follow the canyon rivers down encounter the forest edge and some go no further; some have adapted to the wet over generations and are now found in both zones, distinguishable from their canyon relatives by behavioral and slight physical differences that the local peoples identify and name. Tropical species from the forest proper come to the desert edge for specific resources — particular insects, particular plants, water — and return. The carnivores that hunt both zones are the most studied by the communities here, because they present the most immediate practical interest.\n\n## The Peoples\n\nTrogo is inhabited by communities that do not fit cleanly into any major Azhoran category. The canyon peoples of the southern Meroshe follow their river systems down into the upper reaches of Trogo — the transition zone is inhabited by families who know both desert and forest and work seasonally between them, moving up with the dry season and down with the rains. These are Moreshi in language and ancestry, and their connections are northward, into the canyon country.\n\nThe forest itself — the mid-slope and coastal sections — has its own inhabitants, whose ancestry does not trace to the Moreshi tradition and whose language belongs to neither the Moreshi family nor any Azhoran mainland family that has been classified. Whether this forest people's language relates to the southern archipelago languages, as some vocabulary patterns suggest, or whether it is an independent development, is not established. The southern peoples of the archipelago, who have made contact with the Trogo coast through the Azhor Stones route, treat the coast here with a recognition that suggests familiarity — they have words for the local plant species that are neither Azhoran approximations nor locally borrowed, which would require explanation.\n\nThe transition zone itself is inhabited by a third population that is neither clearly Moreshi nor clearly forest people — communities that have been in the ecotone long enough to have drawn from both and now constitute something of their own. They speak a contact language with vocabulary from multiple directions, operate as intermediaries between the canyon and forest communities above and below them, and have a practical knowledge of the transition zone's resources that neither the canyon Moreshi nor the forest peoples fully replicate.\n\nThe relationships between these groups are functional but not frictionless. The canyon Moreshi follow river rights they consider traditional; the forest peoples maintain territorial arrangements the canyon communities do not always acknowledge; the transition population navigates between both and does not always feel recognized by either. There is no political structure that encompasses all three, and Marosh's administrative claim to Trogo does not resolve this — a kingdom that governs by desert road has limited practical authority over forest slope populations that have no roads and no reason to use them.\n\n## Commerce\n\nTrogo produces things the desert cannot and the Mediterranean coast does not. Tropical timber of species not found further north — dense-grained, rot-resistant woods suited for ship construction that Hama has been quietly sourcing for a century. Resins from the upper forest that the Moreshi trade network carries north into the caravan routes; their uses in preservation, medicine, and scent have established a market as far as the Caeras cities, where the origin is imprecisely known. Dye plants from the lower river systems that produce colors in the blue-black and deep red range not achievable from Azhoran mainland sources. Medicinal material from the transition zone, where the overlap of desert pharmacopeia and tropical pharmacopeia creates compounds that neither tradition alone has access to.\n\nHama's Merchant Houses source much of this through intermediaries who do not explain their supply chains. The resins appear in Maroshi court records as tariffed exports, which means Marosh has noticed them and has found a way to claim administrative revenue without fully understanding what it is taxing. The timber appears in Hama's shipbuilding records with a geographic attribution that is vague in ways that suggest the vagueness is intentional.\n\nThe transition zone communities are the primary commercial interface. They move goods from the forest into the canyon river system and from the canyon system to the coast, extracting intermediary value from both directions and maintaining the relationships that make both flows possible. They do not discuss the internal workings of these arrangements with Maroshi administrators, whom they regard with the specific patience of people who have learned to be patient with claims that do not match the practical reality.\n\n## Marosh's Relationship with Trogo\n\nMarosh claims Trogo as the southeastern territory of the Dinova Peninsula. This claim is recorded in the kingdom's administrative documents and appears on Maroshi maps with the same boundary conventions as the Meroshe interior. What the claim means in practice depends on which part of Trogo you are discussing.\n\nThe canyon river systems in the upper transition zone, where Moreshi-speaking communities operate, are administered — loosely, at a distance — through the southern canyon governance structures that Marosh has established for the deep Meroshe. Tax collection is intermittent and conducted by intermediaries who retain a portion of what they collect; this is considered a reasonable administrative cost for reaching a population that is not easily reached otherwise.\n\nThe forest itself and the coast are governed, in practice, by no Maroshi institution. The kingdom's administrative reach stops at the lower canyon country. The forest communities have no obligation to Marosh that is currently enforced, no road that connects them to the Maroshi administrative structure, and no particular incentive to develop one. Marosh does not have the institutional capacity of a forest-governance kingdom — it is a desert-road power — and a program of forest administration would require building that capacity from nothing.\n\nThe current equilibrium is that Marosh collects what revenue it can from the upper transition zone, allows the Hama trade network to function in the coastal region without formal acknowledgment, and classifies Trogo as administered territory on maps that the forest communities do not see and would not find accurate if they did.\n\n## Language in the Contact Zone\n\nThe languages of Trogo form a spectrum rather than a set of discrete varieties. At the canyon edge, recognizable canyon Moreshi. At the coast, the forest language and its apparent southern-ocean relatives. In the transition zone, a contact register that draws from both and has been developing its own character long enough to have grammatical features that are neither Moreshi nor forest in origin.\n\nOne feature of the transition language that has drawn attention: it has a grammatical category that neither Moreshi nor any Azhoran mainland language possesses — a verb aspect marking actions that are conditional on the current state of the fog. Whether the fog is present or not changes the form of certain verbs describing movement, visibility, and resource access. The category is semantically precise and grammatically regular, which means it was not invented recently. It names a distinction that is genuinely important to people whose decisions depend on whether the fog wall is up. The canyon Moreshi find it unnecessary. The coastal forest people find it imprecise. The transition zone communities find the criticism from both sides unsurprising.\n\nThe forest language of the mid-slope communities has not been systematically studied. The vocabulary that has been collected shows consistent phonological patterns — heavy use of nasal consonants, long vowels, and a series of tone distinctions that Azhoran languages do not have — and the vocabulary clusters that have been compared against southern archipelago languages show enough correspondence to require explanation. Whether this represents deep common ancestry, ancient contact, or something else is open. The question is not currently being investigated.\n\n## The Canyon South and Trogo\n\nThe southern Meroshe's canyon country — the oldest-inhabited section of the desert, where the canyon communities do not excavate by choice and the ruins of something pre-Moreshi are present in the rock — drains directly into Trogo through the river systems. The water that makes Trogo possible comes through those canyons.\n\nWhether anything else comes through is a question the canyon communities address by changing the subject. They follow the rivers seasonally and return from the Trogo direction with forest goods they trade through the caravan network. What they observe in the lower canyon reaches, and how far they follow the rivers into the forest, and what they encounter there — they are informative about the goods and quiet about the rest.\n\nThe forest peoples of Trogo proper, asked about the canyon country above them, describe it as the place where the water comes from and where certain things live that they do not follow upriver. They do not use the word \"ruins\" because the word in their language for what is in the upper canyon carries different connotations — something closer to \"what was placed there\" than \"what was built and abandoned.\" The distinction may be a translation artifact. It may not be.",
      "loreRef": "trogo",
      "climate": "Af"
    },
    "Babon": {
      "name": "Babon",
      "color": "#e67e22",
      "lore": "# Babon\n\nSoutheast of Trogo across the Trogo Channel lies Babon — a large island of dense tropical jungle, steep interior terrain, and a coast well enough known to Hama's maritime network to appear on commercial charts, and poorly enough understood beyond the coast that those same charts simply leave the interior blank. The coastal city of Tathilium is the island's point of contact with the outside world. Everything inland of Tathilium's sight-line is, to outside knowledge, a report rather than a map.\n\nThe name appears first in Hama commercial records approximately two hundred years ago, in the context of a resin trade. It does not derive from any Mittoli root or from any Moreshi family that has been studied. Whether it is the coastal people's own word, the inland peoples' word, or a Hama trader's approximation of something else entirely has not been established. The island's own coastal people use it without explaining where it comes from, which may mean it is theirs or may mean they have used it long enough that the origin no longer matters.\n\n## The Trogo Channel\n\nThe channel separating Babon from Trogo's southeastern coast is navigable — narrow enough that the island is visible from the Trogo shore on clear days and wide enough that the crossing requires a proper vessel and some knowledge of the currents. The channel runs fast in its middle sections, where the gap between landmasses compresses the tidal flow. Vessels that know the timing cross without difficulty. Vessels that do not know the timing cross eventually, having been pushed several miles off course and acquired an appreciation for why local pilots exist.\n\nThe channel has been in regular use between Trogo's coastal communities and Tathilium for long enough that the relationship between the two coasts is one of familiar commerce rather than exploration. Trogo's transition-zone products — forest resins, particular timber, medicinal material — move outward through Tathilium as often as through Hama directly. Tathilium knows the Trogo coast and the Trogo coast knows Tathilium; what neither side discusses in depth is what lies further into the other's interior.\n\n## Tathilium\n\nTathilium sits at the northeastern end of Babon's most hospitable coastal stretch — a natural harbor backed by a broad flat coastal plain, which is unusual on an island whose interior rises steeply into densely forested hills within a few miles of the water. The city is the largest settlement on the island by a considerable margin, and its size is a function of its position: it is where the channel opens, where the coastal plain allows construction, and where the island's only reliable anchorage for deep-draft vessels is located. Everything about Tathilium's existence follows from these facts.\n\nThe city is not primarily Azhoran in character, though it has absorbed enough Hama commercial presence over two centuries that certain districts — the warehousing quarter, the pilot's hall, the factor houses along the main harbor road — are recognizably shaped by that contact. The majority population is the island's coastal people, a distinct group neither closely related to the southern archipelago peoples nor to any Azhoran mainland tradition, with their own language, their own architectural forms, and their own calendar that does not correspond to either the Mittoli astronomical system or the Mithala flood calendar. The Hama factors who live in Tathilium have learned to work with this and find it less inconvenient than expected, because the Tathilium people are commercially minded in a way that translates.\n\nThe city's commerce is built around the island's coastal products — timber from the lower forest slopes, dried fish from the channel and surrounding sea, the resin trade that first brought Hama's attention here — and around its position as the channel waypoint for traffic between Trogo's coast and whatever lies further southeast. Vessels bound for the Azhor Stones or the southern archipelago that approach from the west pass through the channel and typically stop in Tathilium. This gives the city a transit economy on top of its export economy, and the combination makes it prosperous enough to maintain a population that the coastal plain's direct productivity alone could not support.\n\nThe Tathilium people do not venture into the interior of the island. This is not law or prohibition — it is simply not what they do, in the same way that the people of the Lizeem delta do not attempt to farm the deep Meroshe. The jungle begins at the inland edge of the coastal plain and the Tathilium people recognize that edge clearly. Visitors who ask about the interior are given accurate practical information — what is known about where the edge is, what the first few miles of forest are like, what is found there — and then a silence that is not unfriendly but is complete.\n\n## The Coast\n\nBabon's coast is not uniform. The northeastern stretch, where Tathilium stands, is the exception — the only section where the interior terrain does not push directly to the shore. Moving around the island in either direction, the coastal plain narrows and then disappears: jungle-covered hills run directly to the sea, producing dramatic cliff coastlines interrupted by the occasional small beach or river mouth. The outer coast, facing the open southern sea, is exposed enough that the few coastal communities beyond Tathilium are small, sheltered in specific geographic pockets, and not easily visited from the water.\n\nThese outer-coast communities are related to the Tathilium people but distinct — isolated by the terrain into their own development, with dialect differences and local traditions that Tathilium cannot always translate. They fish the reef and the deeper sea beyond it. They know the coast of their section of island and not much of any other section. Communication between them and Tathilium is by sea rather than overland; going through the interior is not done.\n\nThe reef around Babon's coast is an extension of the same system that surrounds the Azhor Stones, though richer and more varied for being anchored to a larger landmass with river outflow. Southern archipelago fishing vessels are seen in the outer reef sections seasonally. Their arrangement with the Babon coastal communities appears to predate any Azhoran involvement, and the specific terms of it have not been shared with Hama's commercial representatives despite polite inquiry.\n\n## The Interior\n\nThe interior of Babon rises from the coastal plain into steep forested hills whose topography is not clearly mapped. The few accounts that exist — from traders who attempted to cross the island overland rather than sail around it, from Tathilium guides who accompanied early Hama expeditions to the forest edge, from one Academy-sponsored survey that reached the first major ridge and produced a report — describe a jungle of unusual density and age. Not unusual for a tropical island in the abstract; unusual in the specific quality of the forest, which has the character of a place that has been continuously covered for longer than the coastal people's memory reaches or the survey party's instruments could measure.\n\nThe canopy is high and closed. Below it, the layers of the forest interior receive almost no direct sunlight for most of the day. The understory is dense with things adapted to this condition — palms, broad-leaf shade plants, the epiphytes that cover every available surface — and the ground layer is correspondingly deep in accumulated organic material. The Academy survey noted that sounds from the coastal plain do not penetrate more than a short distance into the forest, and that sounds within the forest itself behave in ways the survey's naturalist described as \"not characteristic of open-canopy acoustics\" without specifying further.\n\nThe river systems that drain the interior emerge at the coast as the brown, silt-carrying currents that feed the reef's nutrients. They are navigable by small boat a short distance upstream before the gradient increases and the passage becomes obstructed. What they drain from — what the interior terrain actually looks like at elevation, whether there are valleys, high clearings, water bodies — is not established from outside sources.\n\n## The Inland Peoples\n\nThe interior of Babon is inhabited. The Tathilium people know this, mention it matter-of-factly, and do not have detailed knowledge of it. Figures are occasionally seen at the forest edge, particularly near the river mouths. Objects of clear crafted origin — specific types of worked fiber, particular resins in forms that indicate processing rather than raw harvest — sometimes appear in Tathilium's market through channels that involve several intermediary exchanges and no direct contact with whoever produced them. This is the extent of the commercial relationship between the coast and the interior.\n\nAzhoran and Hama sources describe the interior people as primitive and their territory as ungoverned. The description reflects the limits of outside knowledge rather than what is actually the case. A people who have occupied a dense tropical interior long enough to develop their own trade goods, their own routes to the coastal market, and their own terms for contact with the outside — conducted entirely on their own initiative, through intermediaries they have selected — are not ungoverned. They have simply established a governance that the outside world is not party to and cannot observe.\n\nWhat the Tathilium people say about the interior people, when asked carefully and at length: they are old here. They know the forest entirely. They do not come to the coast because they do not need to. The objects that reach the coastal market are not trade goods offered under economic pressure; they are what the interior people choose to release. Whether contact could be made directly and what the interior people would make of it, the Tathilium people decline to speculate.\n\nThe one consistent observation — from Tathilium people, from the outer coastal communities, and from the single Academy survey — is that the interior people have a relationship with the island's larger fauna that is not the relationship of a population living in fear of dangerous animals. How this works in practice has not been observed.\n\n## The Creatures\n\nBabon's interior fauna is the source of the island's reputation among Azhoran sailors, who use \"the monsters of Babon\" as a reference in the way that people use things they have heard about but not seen. The reputation is not entirely wrong, though \"monster\" is an outsider's word applied to things that are animals and that the insider's perspective might categorize differently.\n\nThe island's isolation and the age of its forest have produced fauna at scales not found on the mainland or on the younger volcanic islands to the south. The largest of the island's reptilian species — seen at the forest edge, observed at river mouths, identified in the coastal communities' extremely detailed knowledge of what does not come to the beach — reach lengths that the Academy survey declined to publish without corroboration, noting that the tracks and waterline markings observed were consistent with the reported dimensions and inconsistent with any species in the existing Azhoran natural catalogue. These are not mythological; they are animals, with the behaviors of animals, occupying the ecological role that large apex predators occupy. They are simply larger than the category accommodates comfortably.\n\nA second category of creature is described by the Tathilium people with a vocabulary distinct from the vocabulary they use for the large reptiles. This second category is not characterized by size. The descriptions involve behavior: things seen at the forest edge at specific times of day or year, in circumstances that do not match foraging, territorial, or mating behavior as the Tathilium people understand these things. The naturalist on the Academy survey wrote that the behavioral accounts, taken together, \"suggest an animal with a social relationship to its territory that exceeds what we associate with individual species and implies something more systematic, though I cannot specify further from the available evidence.\" The survey did not enter the interior. The naturalist recommended further study without volunteering to conduct it.\n\nThe inland people's apparent ease with both categories — the large predators and the second category — is observed from outside without being understood. The Tathilium people do not have a tradition of entering the interior; they do not have a tradition of being harmed by what comes out of it either. The edge is observed. Things from both sides observe the edge. Contact is not made.\n\nWhether the interior peoples have a relationship with the second category of creature, or whether the second category of creature has a relationship with them, is a distinction the available evidence does not allow. The Tathilium people treat the question as the kind of question that people who do not live here ask.\n\n## What Is Known\n\nTathilium is known. The channel is known. The outer coast, approximately, is known. The reef system and the commercial arrangements around it are partially known. The products that emerge from the interior through intermediary channels are known as objects without a clear source.\n\nThe interior terrain is not mapped. The inland peoples' language, population, political structure, and material culture are not documented. The full species composition of the interior fauna is not catalogued. The age of the island's forest cover has not been measured. Whether the island has structural relationship to the southern archipelago — geological, ecological, or in terms of deep human history — has not been studied.\n\nThe Tathilium people have lived next to all of this for long enough that they find outside interest in it somewhat academic. They have what they need from the coast. The interior has what it needs from the interior. The edge between them holds.\n\n## The Name of the City\n\n*Tathilium* — the name appears in its current form in Hama records from initial contact and has not changed since. The *-ilium* ending pattern is familiar from the Mittoli tradition (as in *Te Nillium*), which has led some Academy cartographers to assume a Mittoli founding or naming. The Tathilium people do not confirm this. The first element, *Tath-*, does not trace to any Mittoli root in the existing vocabulary, and the local language — which has not been formally studied — appears to use *tath* as a word with its own meaning, though the meaning has not been recorded in any document accessible to the Academy.\n\nThe commercial shorthand in Hama records is simply \"Tath.\" The pilots who work the channel use this.",
      "loreRef": "babon",
      "climate": "Csa"
    },
