or tradition but a family of them, varying in detail between communities. In West Pyros it is associated with agricultural ritual — seasonal ceremonies that ask permission of the land before planting, that leave offerings at the fumarole sites, that treat the volcanic soil as a gift from something that could have chosen not to give it. In East Pyros it has been absorbed into the trade culture and become more metaphorical — \"the fire beneath the deal,\" meaning the long-term consequences that an agreement will have when it eventually surfaces.\n\nThe fumaroles and hot springs scattered across both regions are considered liminal places — places where the interior of the world is close to the surface, and where the distance between what is and what was is thinner than elsewhere. People make requests at them, leave objects, and in some communities, speak words that are not meant for other people to hear.",
      "loreRef": "pyros",
      "climate": "BSh"
