    "Legemum": {
      "name": "Legemum",
      "color": "#c0392b",
      "lore": "# The Legemum Peninsula\n\nJust west of the Ascarth Peninsula, where the coastline turns away from the Iberos Sea and faces the open western ocean, the land narrows again into Legemum — a shorter, wider peninsula than its neighbor, lower along the spine, greener, and wet in a way that the Iberos coast never is. Where Ascarth is rocky and sun-bleached, Legemum is rain-grey and productive. Where Ascarth faces a sheltered sea full of islands and trade routes, Legemum faces the open ocean, which has no islands worth speaking of and which the Legemi have learned to fish with equipment and techniques entirely unlike anything used on the other coast.\n\nThere are no major cities in Legemum. This is the first thing the region is known for among its neighbors — defined, in part, by what it lacks. The second thing it is known for is tin.\n\n---\n\n## Geography\n\nThe peninsula runs roughly northwest from its base, where a low range of hills separates it from the neck of the Ascarth Peninsula. These hills are the most significant physical feature of Legemum's interior — not dramatic, not particularly high, but mineral-rich in ways that have shaped the peninsula's character for as long as people have lived here. The streams that drain off the hills run dark with mineral content; the soil in the valley floors is heavy clay, difficult to work initially and extremely productive once broken. The upland areas, where the clay gives way to thin soils over rock, are the grazing grounds — sheep and goats in the lower reaches, cattle only where the ground is level enough to sustain them.\n\nThe coastline is the other defining feature. The western shore of Legemum takes the full force of the ocean without the shelter of islands or headlands to break it. The cliffs are high, the surf is constant, and good harbors are rare. There are natural anchorages at three points along the coast — the Legemi call them the Three Mouths — where river estuaries provide enough shelter for working boats to launch and return. None of the three is large enough for deep-draft ocean vessels to enter safely in winter weather, which is six months of the year. This has, over the centuries, conclusively settled the question of whether Legemum would develop into a maritime trading power.\n\nThe eastern shore of the peninsula, which faces the neck of Ascarth and the narrow bay between them, is calmer. Most of the Legemi population concentrates here, with easier access to the mainland and to Ascarth's markets.\n\n---\n\n## The Legemi\n\nThe people of Legemum call themselves the Legemi and their language is a dialect of western Mittoli sufficiently distinct that speakers from the Mittoli heartland, when they first arrive, take several days to adjust. The differences are mostly in the agricultural and craft vocabulary — the Legemi have words for soil conditions, rock formations, tidal patterns, and fishing techniques that standard Mittoli simply does not have, and which the Legemi have stopped apologizing for when their cousins from inland cannot follow.\n\nThe Legemi are not a single people in any historical sense that would satisfy a genealogist. The peninsula has absorbed several waves of settlement from the western interior, and the current population is a composite — visible in the variation in physical type from village to village, audible in the dialect differences between the mining settlements in the hills and the fishing communities on the coast, legible in the inconsistency of inheritance customs between the eastern and western halves of the peninsula. What makes them a distinct culture is not common origin but common circumstance: the same hills, the same rain, the same difficult coast, and several centuries of shared practical necessity.\n\nThey are, in the assessment of most outside observers, a practical people. This is meant as a compliment by some and as a mild criticism by others — the mild criticism being leveled particularly by the Avites of Ascarth, who find the Legemi's indifference to glory approximately as alien as Legemi visitors find Avite bronze-cladding on public buildings. The Legemi do not cultivate heroic reputation. They cultivate fields, mine seams, and fish carefully in water that does not forgive mistakes. When asked about this, they tend to point out that their granaries are full, which they regard as a more durable achievement than most.\n\n---\n\n## Economy\n\n### Agriculture\n\nThe clay soils of Legemum's river valleys produce grain in quantity — emmer, barley, and in the more sheltered southern valleys a variety of wheat that the Legemi breed with care and sell at prices that reflect this. The upland grazing supports a significant wool and leather trade. Root vegetables — turnips, parsnips, and several varieties with no Mittoli names that Legemi farmers have been cultivating since before the period of written records — fill the dietary gaps that grain leaves and survive the wet winters that would rot other stores.\n\nThe agricultural calendar is organized around the guilds. The Legemum agricultural guilds are not, like their mining counterparts, organized around a single craft — they are regional bodies, covering a specific river valley or upland district, maintaining the communal infrastructure (drainage, seed stores, milling rights) that no individual farm can sustain alone. Guild membership is inherited, but guild leadership is elected annually, in a vote of all member-heads-of-household. The elected leader, called the *hannar* in the local dialect, arbitrates disputes over water rights and field boundaries, sets the collective sale price when the guild sells in bulk to outside buyers, and is personally responsible for maintaining the guild's seed stores in viable condition. The *hannar* of a guild whose seed stores fail in a bad winter carries this with them for the rest of their life.\n\n### Mining\n\nThe hills produce tin, copper in smaller quantities, and good building stone. The tin is the significant one. Every king of Ascarth who has spent time thinking about where their bronze comes from eventually arrives at the same answer: tin comes, in large part, from Legemum. The copper is local to the Ascarth Peninsula. The tin has to be bought, and the best and closest tin is across the narrow bay in a peninsula that has no great love for Ascarth's political ambitions but a clear-eyed appreciation of its own commercial leverage.\n\nThe mining settlements of the interior hills are the peninsula's oldest permanent communities. Some of the upper workings have been in continuous operation long enough that the miners have traditions about passages opened by their grandparents' grandparents, and the ore veins those early workings followed have not been exhausted — they have deepened. Legemum's tin extraction is not industrial in scale; it is methodical, careful, and sustained, producing a reliable annual output rather than a dramatic surge.\n\nThe miners' guilds are the most formally organized institutions on the peninsula. They maintain written records of seam locations, extraction rates, and ore quality going back further than any other institution in Legemum. They have their own system of underground markers, their own protocol for when a passage becomes unsafe and must be sealed, and their own set of obligations to the dead — miners who die in the workings are interred near the entrance of the passage where they fell, and their names are added to the guild's record in a specific ink made from mineral residue of the seam they were working. The guild record is not a document most outside visitors are shown.\n\n### Fishing\n\nThe western-coast fishing communities live differently from the farming and mining settlements — different rhythms, different equipment, different relationship to weather. The open-ocean fish that run off Legemum's coast are larger and less predictable than the sheltered-sea species of the Iberos, and the boats required to catch them in Atlantic swells are beamier, heavier, and built for endurance rather than speed. A Legemi fishing boat is not beautiful, by Iberos standards. It is very difficult to sink.\n\nThe fishing communities are the most culturally distinct part of Legemum, with the strongest claim to a continuous tradition that predates the various settlement waves that produced the current population. The coastal villages have their own names for the winds, their own lunar calendar for predicting the fish-runs, and their own set of taboos around what is said before a boat launches. They trade their catch inland for grain and metal goods, sell the surplus — smoked, salted, and dried — to the eastern Legemum markets that connect with Ascarth, and otherwise maintain a self-sufficiency that the farming communities respect and the mining settlements find excessive.\n\n---\n\n## Political Structure\n\nLegemum has no king and has not had one in any period of its documented history. This is not a deliberate political philosophy; it is a consequence of geography. The peninsula's population is dispersed across river valleys, upland settlements, and coastal villages that have no natural center — no port large enough to anchor a maritime empire, no agricultural heartland fertile enough to sustain a capital, no single settlement dominant enough to compel the others. The closest thing to a Legemi political center is the **Council of Legemum**, which is neither a permanent body nor a governing one — it is a seasonal assembly, convened twice a year at the largest of the peninsula's neutral market sites, where guild representatives, village heads, and anyone with a legitimate grievance or proposal can speak.\n\nThe Council does not legislate. It arbitrates disputes that have escalated beyond local resolution, sets collective positions on external trade negotiations (most significantly with Ascarth), and maintains the shared infrastructure — road maintenance, bridge repair, the upkeep of the Three Mouths anchorages — that the individual communities cannot sustain without coordination. Decisions require something approaching consensus; bare majorities are considered insufficient because a bare majority leaves too many people with no obligation to comply. This makes the Council slow. The Legemi are aware of this and regard it as a reasonable price for decisions that hold.\n\nThe assembly site itself — called the **Haur** in local dialect, approximately \"the flat place\" — is a wide, naturally level meadow in the hills at the peninsula's center, equidistant from the three major population areas. It is used for nothing else. A market grows up around it during Council weeks, and the market is, in practice, as important as the Council — the period when all three parts of the peninsula's economy are in the same place at the same time and can trade with each other directly.\n\n---\n\n## Relationship with Ascarth\n\nThe narrow bay between Legemum's eastern shore and the Ascarth Peninsula is not wide enough to function as a serious barrier. Legemi and Avite fishing boats cross it; Legemi tin merchants cross it in larger vessels; Avite bronze traders cross it in the other direction. The two peninsulas have been in continuous commercial contact for as long as their respective histories extend.\n\nThey are not friends, in the way that neighboring peoples who have something the other needs are rarely friends. The Avites find the Legemi useful and, occasionally, aggravating in the specific way that a necessary supplier who will not be pushed around is aggravating. The Legemi find the Avites impressive and, consistently, alarming — the Ascarth kings have, on four recorded occasions, extended their ambitions to include the idea that controlling Legemum's tin supply directly would be preferable to purchasing it, and on four recorded occasions have discovered that Legemum, while it does not have much of a warrior tradition, has terrain that rewards defenders, sufficient agricultural stores to outlast a siege, and a Council prepared to destroy its own mines rather than yield them intact. This last point was demonstrated once, partially, when a section of the primary tin workings was flooded in advance of an Avite campaign that subsequently did not happen. The flooding was not repaired for six years. The Avites noticed.\n\nThe current arrangement is commercial: Legemum sells tin at a price set by Council negotiation, in quantities set annually, in exchange for bronze goods and for the implicit protection that comes from being the supplier of something Ascarth cannot produce itself. This is not entirely comfortable for either party. It works.\n\nLegemum also benefits, obliquely, from Ascarth's military reputation. No external power that might otherwise covet the peninsula's mineral resources wants to deal with the peninsula *and* its neighbor. The Avites have never offered this protection formally. The Legemi have never asked for it formally. It exists anyway, in the way that useful arrangements often do.\n\n---\n\n## Among the Neighboring Regions\n\nTo the north and west, Legemum's land border connects it to the Mittoli interior — the same agricultural country that stretches across western Azhora, inhabited by people who speak a language close enough to Legemi dialect that commerce is straightforward. The Legemi sell surplus grain and wool into the Mittoli market towns; they buy metalwork, cloth, and the manufactured goods that the peninsula's dispersed economy does not produce efficiently. This relationship is quieter and more durable than the Ascarth one, and is less remarked upon because nothing in it is precarious.\n\nThe Selemi have a presence in Legemum's eastern coastal market, though not a formal outpost — the market town that handles most of the Ascarth tin trade has a resident Selemi merchant family that has been there for three generations and is, at this point, partly Legemi in culture and entirely Legemi by marriage. The Selemis count this as intelligence; the Legemi count the family as neighbors. Both assessments are accurate.\n\nThe Nyloni know Legemum primarily through the tin — any commercial intelligence about Ascarth's bronze production includes Legemum as an input variable, and the harbor records in Nylon track Legemi tin prices as a proxy for Ascarth's military readiness. A Legemum tin price spike suggests Ascarth is stockpiling. The Nyloni have found this a reliable early indicator and do not advertise that they track it.",
      "loreRef": "telemonia",
      "climate": "Csa"
    },
    "Gala": {
      "name": "Gala",
      "color": "#e67e22",
      "lore": "# Gala\n\nGala is the land on the western bank of the Lizeem near its mouth — the coastal plain that lies between the rocky base of the Ascarth Peninsula to the north and the river's broad delta to the east, opening south onto the Iberos Sea. By latitude and soil it is among the more comfortable places on Azhora's eastern coast: warm, well-watered by the Lizeem's distributaries and seasonal rains off the sea, sheltered from the worst of the interior weather by the Ascarth hills behind it. It grows food without great effort and has a harbor that, while never as deep or as busy as Nylon's across the river, handles enough traffic to make it a stop on any coastal itinerary. The region is named for its principal city. Both the city and its hinterland have spent most of their history in the uncomfortable position of being worth wanting by someone larger.\n\nThe King of Aevis controls Gala more often than not. This control is not always the same thing — sometimes it is garrison and direct administration, sometimes tribute and nominal acknowledgment, sometimes an arrangement so loose that Gala is in practice independent while technically not saying so. The distinction matters to the Galans in practical ways and less than expected in cultural ones. Galan culture has not become Avite. The decades under Aevis's suzerainty have left marks on the vocabulary, on the bronze fittings of the city's doors, and on the political reflexes of its merchant families, but the Galans remain recognizably and specifically Galan in a way that several generations of Avite kings have found either charming or irrelevant depending on their priorities.\n\n---\n\n## Geography\n\nThe region of Gala runs roughly thirty miles along the coast from the southern base of the Ascarth Peninsula to where the Lizeem's delta distributaries fan out into the sea, and twenty to twenty-five miles inland before the terrain rises into the foothills that mark the edge of the Ascarth Peninsula's highland interior. The coastal plain is flat, fertile, drained by a network of small rivers fed from those hills and supplemented by irrigation channels in the drier southern stretches.\n\nThe northern edge of Gala, where the plain meets the foothills, has a different character from the coast: more pastoral, with the upland farming communities that supply the coastal markets, distinct enough from the port towns to be considered almost a different region by the Galans themselves. The people of the foothills have a relationship with Aevis that is more direct — they are closer to the peninsula's territory, their young men have more often been called into Avite campaigns, their accent has drifted further toward the Avite dialect.\n\nThe coastal strip and the city of Gala itself are where Galan culture lives in its most concentrated form.\n\n---\n\n## The City of Gala\n\nThe city sits at the junction of the coastal road from Aevis and the Lizeem's western channel — close enough to the river mouth to receive goods coming down from the Mittoli interior, close enough to Nylon's harbor to be in commercial competition with it on nearly every category of trade, and close enough to the Ascarth Peninsula to be in the field of attention of every Avite king who has considered the implications of a market city on his doorstep that he does not control.\n\nGala is not as large as Nylon. It does not have Nylon's walls, Nylon's intellectual reputation, or Nylon's position at the river's mouth rather than the river's side. What it has is the western approach: every trader coming overland from the Ascarth Peninsula, from the Mittoli country south of the Oremindi, or from the Pyrosi lands moving toward the Iberos Sea arrives at Gala before they arrive at Nylon. The distinction matters commercially. Gala has shaped the terms of trade before the goods reach the larger harbor, and the Galans are aware of this leverage and have built their commercial institutions around it.\n\nThe city's harbor is functional rather than magnificent — a workmanlike port with stone quays, solid warehousing, and the infrastructure of a city that takes trade seriously without aestheticizing it. The upper town has more architectural ambition: the council hall is the oldest public building, built in a Mittoli civic style with later modifications — several of the facing panels are Avite bronze work from a period of particularly stable Ascarth control, when the king of the time invested in Gala's prestige as a way of investing in his own. The bronze panels remain. Three kings since then have considered replacing them with something more locally representative and have not, for the same reason they were installed: the bronze is excellent and its removal would be noticed.\n\nThe market district is the city's real center. It runs from the harbor north to the road junction and is organized by goods: the grain quarter, the oil and wine quarter, the metal-goods stalls (Avite bronze prominently featured, at prices that reflect both quality and origin), the textile section, the spice and preserved-food row that serves the maritime trade. Dispute arbitration in the market is handled by the Guild of Assessors, a Galan institution older than the current council structure, whose members are trained in commercial law and whose decisions are respected by traders from every polity that uses the market because the alternative is not using the market, which is worse.\n\n---\n\n## Galan Culture\n\nThe Galans are not warriors by aspiration. The Avite heroic tradition — the training from childhood, the glory in battle, the *veth* who remember your deeds for generations — has not taken hold in Gala despite centuries of Avite proximity and intermittent Avite rule. The Galans who end up in Avite campaigns do so because tribute sometimes takes that form, not because they consider it the highest aspiration available to them. They fight adequately when required, return home as soon as permitted, and generally regard the distinction between an adequate soldier who comes back and a celebrated warrior who does not as not difficult to resolve.\n\nWhat Galan culture produces instead is competence in exchange: an extensive tradition of commercial law, a hospitality practice that functions simultaneously as ethics and as trade infrastructure, and a public culture organized around arbitration and deal-making rather than around competition for glory. The Galan guest-right tradition — which governs the treatment of any trader who arrives in the city, establishes what protections they are owed, and specifies the penalties for violation — is old enough to predate the oldest records of Avite contact and is taken seriously enough that the Guild of Assessors has jurisdiction over violations by Galan hosts as well as by visiting traders. A merchant who is robbed in Nylon has a legal problem. A merchant who is robbed in Gala has created a civic crisis.\n\nThis tradition of protected exchange is not idealism. It is the rational infrastructure of a city whose prosperity depends on traders from incompatible polities choosing to conduct business in the same place. The Galans have made it easy and safe to trade in Gala, and they have made it expensive to violate the conditions that make trading there possible. The reputation thus built is old enough to be self-sustaining.\n\nThe food culture of Gala is the region's most visible cultural expression. The coastal plain produces grain, olives, several varieties of fig and stone fruit, and a local wine that Svaleen merchants consider rough but that the Galans defend with a specificity of argument that suggests they are not entirely wrong and are certainly not uncertain. The cooking tradition is the one that most clearly distinguishes Galan households from their Avite and Nyloni neighbors: elaborate, ingredient-focused, with a tradition of communal meal preparation at festivals that visitors from Nylon find either excessive or admirable depending on how hungry they arrived. The Nyloni have a food culture of their own and are not inclined to cede superiority in it; the Galans have never claimed superiority, only sufficiency, which the Nyloni find insufficiently competitive.\n\nThe Galan calendar is organized around the agricultural year and the trading seasons rather than the flood calendar of the Mittoli interior or the navigational seasons of the Bouéni. The major festivals correspond to harvest, the opening and closing of the sailing season, and a winter feast that is specifically about the obligation of households to provide for those who have nothing to provide for themselves — an old tradition whose origin the Galan histories assign to a period of occupation when the occupying forces' requisitions left parts of the population short, and the community developed the habit of pooling what remained. Whether this history is accurate or illustrative, the Galans maintain the festival.\n\n---\n\n## The Avite Relationship\n\nGala's value to Aevis is commercial. Its merchant families control trading relationships with Nylon across the river, with the Mittoli cities upstream, with the island cities of the Iberos Sea, and with maritime traders from the Svaleen and beyond. These relationships are the product of generations of cultivation. They cannot be transferred to an Avite governor who does not have them. A king of Aevis who takes Gala and then governs it as he governs the peninsula's tributary cities — demanding warriors, imposing the Avite prestige economy, disrupting the commercial arrangements — will find, within a generation, that the thing worth having has become worth less.\n\nThe Avite kings who have handled Gala well have understood this. They take tribute in provisions and access — Gala's grain and oil supplement the peninsula's food supply, and Gala's market gives Avite bronze goods a commercial outlet beyond the peninsula's direct trade. They maintain garrison enough to keep Gala nominally in hand without disrupting the city's function. They leave the Guild of Assessors alone. In exchange, Gala's political institutions do not press the question of independence, the roads are maintained, and the trade flows through channels that Aevis is positioned to tax.\n\nThe Avite kings who have handled Gala badly have demanded that it behave as a conquered city — warriors, conscription, tribute in forms that suit the Avite prestige economy rather than the Galan commercial one. This produces a version of Gala that is less useful and resentful, and eventually produces a period in which the Kingdom of Ascarth is in one of its dissolution phases and Gala reasserts its council governance and begins the slow work of rebuilding the commercial relationships that were disrupted.\n\nThe Galans observe this cycle with the equanimity of people who have outlasted several iterations. They know how Avite kings tend to end. They know how long the Kingdom of Ascarth has historically persisted before it comes apart. Their council records, which go back further than their city's independence does, contain assessments of every period of Avite control — what was taken, what was left, what needed rebuilding afterward. These records are not vengeful documents. They are maintenance logs. The city that built them knows it will be in the same position again and wants to know what to expect.\n\n---\n\n## Gala and Nylon\n\nThe river separates them; commerce connects them. Nylon sits at the Lizeem's mouth on the eastern bank, drawing the river's goods and the Iberos Sea trade into its harbor and its salons. Gala sits on the western bank a few miles upstream, drawing the overland trade and the coastal road traffic before it reaches the river.\n\nThe two cities are in commercial competition and know it, and they manage the competition through a combination of formal treaty arrangements that are renegotiated every twenty years or so and a culture of measured respect between their merchant and diplomatic classes. Nylon's questori and Gala's Guild of Assessors have a professional relationship — the questori find the Assessors rigorous in ways they respect, if not in ways they entirely agree with, and the Assessors find the questori sophisticated in ways that are occasionally useful and more often than not beside the practical point.\n\nThe Nyloni consider themselves the grander city, which is accurate in most of the ways grandness is measured. The Galans do not contest this. They note, privately, that grandness and commercial position are not the same thing, and that the road west from Aevis reaches their city before it reaches Nylon. The Nyloni, when this is put to them, have several responses prepared, all of them technically correct and none of them quite the point either.\n\n---\n\n## Language\n\nGala speaks a Mittoli-derived mainland dialect — related to the speech of the Lizeem valley communities upstream, distinct from Nylon's coastal variant, and leavened with accumulated vocabulary from every political and commercial contact the region has sustained. Avite borrowings appear in the legal and administrative registers: terms for tribute, garrison, the protocols of royal acknowledgment, and several words for categories of military obligation that Galan has no independent term for because Galan culture did not develop the need. Iberos Sea vocabulary appears in the maritime and commercial registers. A layer of pre-Mittoli terms persists in the names of geographical features — the small rivers, the coastal inlets, the specific soils of the agricultural plain — in the way that the names of things that were there before the current speakers arrived tend to persist.\n\nThe name *Gala* itself is from this older layer. What it meant to whoever named the place before the current population arrived is not established.",
      "loreRef": "gala",
      "climate": "BSh"
    },
    "Northern Ascarth": {
      "name": "Northern Ascarth",
      "color": "#d4ac0d",
      "lore": "# The Ascarth Peninsula\n\nSouthwest of Nylon, the Iberos coast bends and narrows into the Ascarth Peninsula — a rugged finger of land that juts into the southern Iberos Sea, rocky and forested in its interior, cliff-faced along much of its coast, with good anchorage only in the sheltered bays on its eastern and northern shores. It is not, geographically, a fertile or easy place. The Avites who have lived here for as long as record extends have made this irrelevant by being people to whom difficulty is, at worst, a neutral condition and, at best, the point.\n\nThe peninsula contains many small cities and towns, each with its own traditions, its own warrior lineages, its own pride. The largest and most powerful is Aevis, which sits on the northern coast where the peninsula connects to the main body of Azhora — positioned to receive trade from Nylon and the Lizeem River mouth while controlling the overland access to the rest of the peninsula behind it. When Aevis is strong enough, and its kings ambitious enough, the other cities of the peninsula swear allegiance and the entity that results calls itself the Kingdom of Ascarth. When it is not, they do not, and the peninsula returns to a state of competitive coexistence that the Avites consider normal and that outside observers tend to describe as chronic low-level warfare.\n\n---\n\n## The Avites\n\nThe people of Aevis, and by extension the dominant culture of the peninsula, are called the Avites. The name is theirs; it predates the city it comes from, or is claimed to — a point the Avites would make and the historians would dispute and the Avites would regard the dispute as evidence of the historians' unsuitability for the things that actually matter.\n\nWhat matters to the Avites is glory. Not glory in the abstract, not glory as metaphor for civic achievement or commercial success, but glory in the specific and personal sense: the kind that is won in contest, witnessed by others, and sung about afterward. The Avite heroic tradition is old enough to have accumulated a substantial body of epic poetry — cycles of verse that name individual warriors and their deeds going back to a period that may be partially legendary and is certainly at least partially historical, depending on which scholars you ask and whether the Avites are in earshot when you ask. The epics are performed publicly at festivals, recited by specialists called the *veth* — the rememberers — who train for years in the oral tradition and who occupy a social position roughly equivalent to a very well-regarded general: honored, listened to, expected to perform, not expected to fight.\n\nThe epics' function is not merely entertainment. They are the cultural record of what an Avite life well-lived looks like. They document which families have produced heroes, what those heroes did, how they died. An Avite who knows their lineage is expected to know which of their ancestors appear in the cycles, and in what context, and whether the context reflects well on the family. The *veth* are consulted on genealogical disputes as readily as on poetic questions. This is not considered unusual.\n\nDeath in battle is the optimal outcome of a heroic life in Avite tradition, which sounds worse from the outside than it feels from the inside. The tradition does not celebrate dying specifically; it celebrates the kind of living that makes dying in battle possible rather than accidental — the quality of attention, the physical cultivation, the willingness to be present at the moment the moment arrives. An Avite who lives to old age is not disgraced; they are simply a person whose moment did not come in the form of battle. This happens. It is not the point.\n\nWomen in Avite culture do not fight in the formal sense, but they occupy the epic tradition as principals rather than ornaments — queens who direct strategy, mothers who shape the character of heroes, figures whose judgment determines outcomes. Whether this reflects ancient political reality or is a later idealization is not established.\n\n---\n\n## Bronze\n\nThe Avites work bronze. Every culture in the region is aware of this; fewer are aware of what it means in practice until they encounter the product.\n\nAvite bronze is not technologically primitive. It is technologically exceptional. The Ascarth Peninsula has two things that most of the Iberos coast does not: a reliable source of the right copper ore in the interior hills, and several centuries of accumulated craft knowledge about what to do with it. The alloying ratios the Avite smiths use are precise enough and refined enough that their bronze outperforms what their neighbors produce with iron — harder at the edge, less brittle at the core, with a working quality under the hammer that experienced smiths describe with something close to reverence. The knowledge of exactly how this is achieved is not widely shared. It is organized within a guild system that is also a religious institution, and the information moves from master to apprentice in conditions of considerable formality.\n\nThe Avite commitment to bronze over iron is therefore not sentiment and not tradition in the pejorative sense. It is the rational preference of people who are better at one thing than anyone else, applied to the case where they are better. Iron, in Avite use, is for tools and fasteners — practical material for practical purposes.\n\nThe bronze economy has one persistent vulnerability: the peninsula produces copper but not tin, and without tin there is no bronze. Tin comes primarily from Legemum, the peninsula immediately to the west, whose hill mining settlements have supplied the Ascarth smiths for as long as the guild records on either side extend. The Legemum tin price is not a number any serious Avite strategist ignores. Every king of Ascarth who has considered expanding his territory has also considered, at some length, what it would mean to control that supply directly rather than purchase it. None have managed it permanently. See the Legemum entry for the reasons. Bronze is for the things that matter: weapons, armor, the great gates of Aevis that are the city's most famous physical feature, the grave goods that accompany warriors to the burial mounds on the peninsula's highland interior, the enormous bull-headed figures that stand at the entrances to the city's major public spaces, verdigris-green now after generations of sea air, staring toward the water.\n\nBronze architecture — specifically bronze-clad architecture — is the visual signature of Avite achievement. The great buildings of Aevis are stone at their foundations and structure, but their facing, their doors, their decorative panels, and in the most important structures their roofing elements are clad or cast in bronze. The effect, when the buildings were new, would have been extraordinary — an entire city gleaming at the harbor's edge. The patina changes this over time: the newer bronze-work of more recent construction shines golden-orange, while the older districts have gone deep green, so that the city reads, from the sea, as two colors depending on how old the neighborhood is.\n\nThe Avites export bronze goods throughout the Iberos Sea trade. Avite-made weapons command premium prices from buyers who can afford them. The merchant cities of Nylon and the Svaleen buy Avite bronze; the Moroshé trade routes carry it east. This commercial dimension is not lost on anyone, including the Avites, who understand perfectly well that their military culture and their craft economy reinforce each other. The reputation of Avite weapons sells the weapons. The weapons maintain the reputation.\n\n---\n\n## Aevis\n\nAevis is built on and around a rocky promontory at the northern end of the peninsula — a site chosen, if the epics are believed, because it was the highest defensible point available and the founding king wanted to be able to see anyone coming from any direction. The city has grown down from that original citadel height over centuries, spreading into the lower town around the harbor, but the citadel remains the political and symbolic center: the palace-complex of the King of Ascarth, when a king exists, or of the high lord of Aevis when it does not.\n\nThe citadel is the most completely bronze-clad structure on the peninsula. Its gates are the famous ones — two full stories of cast bronze, depicting in relief the campaigns of the kings of the first Ascarth period. The detail is extraordinary and was clearly produced over multiple generations of craftsmen. The gates are not purely symbolic; they are functional, and they are heavy enough that opening them requires a mechanism and several men. Visitors are made aware of this.\n\nThe harbor below the city is active with trade — bronze goods going out, tin and other necessary metals coming in (the peninsula has copper but must import tin, and the management of tin supply is a persistent strategic concern for any Ascarth king), timber, foodstuffs, and the miscellaneous requirements of a substantial urban population. The harbor district has a different character from the citadel: busier, more mixed, with resident merchants from Nylon and the Svaleen quarter-districts who have found that being present in Aevis is useful for knowing what is happening before it happens to them.\n\nThe *veth* maintain their archives in a building adjacent to the citadel — technically part of the palace complex but architecturally distinct, lower and wider, with a courtyard used for public recitation during festivals. The *veth* archives are the closest thing to a formal history that Ascarth culture produces. Scholars from Nylon have petitioned to study them and have been politely informed that the archives are for the *veth* and the *veth* are for the Avites, and that this arrangement has served both well.\n\n---\n\n## The Kingdom of Ascarth\n\nThe Kingdom of Ascarth is not a permanent political entity. It is a condition that the peninsula enters periodically, when Aevis is powerful enough and its king is capable enough to compel or convince the smaller cities to acknowledge suzerainty. The smaller cities acknowledge suzerainty by sending warriors when called, paying tribute in bronze goods and provisions, and allowing Aevis's markets to set the prices for the peninsula's external trade. In exchange they receive protection, participation in the glory of the King's campaigns, and the economic advantages of being part of a larger and more feared polity.\n\nThe Kingdom dissolves when the king dies without a strong successor, when the tributary cities accumulate grievances beyond the manageable level, or when an external situation — a failed campaign, a trade collapse, a plague — damages Aevis's prestige enough that the smaller cities conclude the calculation has changed. It has dissolved and reformed several times within recorded history. The current period is one of partial unity: Aevis controls most of the northern and central peninsula under terms that the southern cities are actively contesting.\n\nThe Avite heroic tradition complicates royal succession in ways that are culturally interesting and politically unstable. The king must be worthy — this is not a formal criterion but it is a genuine one. A king who cannot lead warriors, who cannot be praised by the *veth* in terms that the warriors accept, who cannot maintain the reputation of the house, will find the tributary cities reviewing their arrangements. Several kingdoms have ended this way, without formal conflict — the king simply found, one day, that the allegiances he thought he had were no longer operative.\n\n---\n\n## The Peninsula's Other Cities\n\nThe smaller cities and towns of the Ascarth Peninsula are not merely Aevis in miniature. Each has its own tradition, its own lineage claims, its own contribution to the epic cycle. Some are coastal fishing communities that supplement their income with bronze trade and have their own small warrior aristocracies. Some are inland hill towns that produce the bulk of the peninsula's agricultural output and negotiate carefully for their independence. A few are old enough to have pre-Avite foundations — evidence of earlier inhabitants whose language and culture have been largely absorbed, preserved only in place names and certain ritual practices that the *veth* have incorporated without fully explaining.\n\nThe southern tip of the peninsula is the part most consistently outside Aevis's control. The cities there are small, remote, and have survived by being not worth the effort of conquering — their harbors are poor, their agricultural land thin, their bronze production limited. They maintain their own warrior traditions, speak a dialect of Avite that the northern cities consider archaic, and regard Aevis with a mixture of cultural pride and political wariness that summarizes the peninsula's general relationship to its dominant city.\n\n---\n\n## Among the Coastal Cities\n\nSouth of the peninsula's base, on the western bank of the Lizeem, lies Gala — a market city and agricultural region that the kings of Aevis control more often than not, through tribute and access arrangements rather than direct garrison. Gala is Ascarth's provisioning ground and its commercial outlet on the mainland side; its grain and oil supplement what the rocky peninsula cannot grow, and its market gives Avite bronze goods a trading floor that reaches the Lizeem's river network. The Galans are not Avites and do not become Avites, which the Avites find either irrelevant or mildly grating depending on the king. What matters strategically is that the arrangements hold. See the Gala entry for detail.\n\nWhen the Kingdom of Ascarth is strong, the cities of the Iberos coast — Nylon, the Svaleen confederation, Nyross — pay attention. A united Ascarth controls a stretch of coastline that any ship moving north or south must pass, and a king who decides that taxing that passage is a good idea has the military capacity to enforce the decision. This has happened before. It is not remembered fondly in the maritime trading records of the coastal cities.\n\nThe management strategy has been consistent across several generations: keep Aevis supplied with what it needs from outside the peninsula — tin, in particular, which the city requires in volume and which the coastal trade controls — at terms favorable enough that disruption is not in Aevis's interest, while maintaining diplomatic relationships with the tributary cities that give coastal merchants early warning when the Kingdom's internal politics are shifting. The Nyloni are, characteristically, systematic about this; their harbor records contain what amounts to a continuous assessment of Ascarth's internal coherence, updated whenever a trading ship returns from Aevis with news.\n\nThe coastal cities admire the Avites in the way that people admire something that is genuinely excellent at what it does, and fear it for the same reason. Nylon's salons do not produce many defenses of the Avite heroic tradition — it is too alien to the city's values — but they also do not produce many comfortable dismissals of it. The Avites are inconveniently difficult to dismiss.",
      "loreRef": "gala",
      "climate": "Csa"
    },
    "Southern Ascarth": {
      "name": "Southern Ascarth",
      "color": "#27ae60",
      "lore": "# The Ascarth Peninsula\n\nSouthwest of Nylon, the Iberos coast bends and narrows into the Ascarth Peninsula — a rugged finger of land that juts into the southern Iberos Sea, rocky and forested in its interior, cliff-faced along much of its coast, with good anchorage only in the sheltered bays on its eastern and northern shores. It is not, geographically, a fertile or easy place. The Avites who have lived here for as long as record extends have made this irrelevant by being people to whom difficulty is, at worst, a neutral condition and, at best, the point.\n\nThe peninsula contains many small cities and towns, each with its own traditions, its own warrior lineages, its own pride. The largest and most powerful is Aevis, which sits on the northern coast where the peninsula connects to the main body of Azhora — positioned to receive trade from Nylon and the Lizeem River mouth while controlling the overland access to the rest of the peninsula behind it. When Aevis is strong enough, and its kings ambitious enough, the other cities of the peninsula swear allegiance and the entity that results calls itself the Kingdom of Ascarth. When it is not, they do not, and the peninsula returns to a state of competitive coexistence that the Avites consider normal and that outside observers tend to describe as chronic low-level warfare.\n\n---\n\n## The Avites\n\nThe people of Aevis, and by extension the dominant culture of the peninsula, are called the Avites. The name is theirs; it predates the city it comes from, or is claimed to — a point the Avites would make and the historians would dispute and the Avites would regard the dispute as evidence of the historians' unsuitability for the things that actually matter.\n\nWhat matters to the Avites is glory. Not glory in the abstract, not glory as metaphor for civic achievement or commercial success, but glory in the specific and personal sense: the kind that is won in contest, witnessed by others, and sung about afterward. The Avite heroic tradition is old enough to have accumulated a substantial body of epic poetry — cycles of verse that name individual warriors and their deeds going back to a period that may be partially legendary and is certainly at least partially historical, depending on which scholars you ask and whether the Avites are in earshot when you ask. The epics are performed publicly at festivals, recited by specialists called the *veth* — the rememberers — who train for years in the oral tradition and who occupy a social position roughly equivalent to a very well-regarded general: honored, listened to, expected to perform, not expected to fight.\n\nThe epics' function is not merely entertainment. They are the cultural record of what an Avite life well-lived looks like. They document which families have produced heroes, what those heroes did, how they died. An Avite who knows their lineage is expected to know which of their ancestors appear in the cycles, and in what context, and whether the context reflects well on the family. The *veth* are consulted on genealogical disputes as readily as on poetic questions. This is not considered unusual.\n\nDeath in battle is the optimal outcome of a heroic life in Avite tradition, which sounds worse from the outside than it feels from the inside. The tradition does not celebrate dying specifically; it celebrates the kind of living that makes dying in battle possible rather than accidental — the quality of attention, the physical cultivation, the willingness to be present at the moment the moment arrives. An Avite who lives to old age is not disgraced; they are simply a person whose moment did not come in the form of battle. This happens. It is not the point.\n\nWomen in Avite culture do not fight in the formal sense, but they occupy the epic tradition as principals rather than ornaments — queens who direct strategy, mothers who shape the character of heroes, figures whose judgment determines outcomes. Whether this reflects ancient political reality or is a later idealization is not established.\n\n---\n\n## Bronze\n\nThe Avites work bronze. Every culture in the region is aware of this; fewer are aware of what it means in practice until they encounter the product.\n\nAvite bronze is not technologically primitive. It is technologically exceptional. The Ascarth Peninsula has two things that most of the Iberos coast does not: a reliable source of the right copper ore in the interior hills, and several centuries of accumulated craft knowledge about what to do with it. The alloying ratios the Avite smiths use are precise enough and refined enough that their bronze outperforms what their neighbors produce with iron — harder at the edge, less brittle at the core, with a working quality under the hammer that experienced smiths describe with something close to reverence. The knowledge of exactly how this is achieved is not widely shared. It is organized within a guild system that is also a religious institution, and the information moves from master to apprentice in conditions of considerable formality.\n\nThe Avite commitment to bronze over iron is therefore not sentiment and not tradition in the pejorative sense. It is the rational preference of people who are better at one thing than anyone else, applied to the case where they are better. Iron, in Avite use, is for tools and fasteners — practical material for practical purposes.\n\nThe bronze economy has one persistent vulnerability: the peninsula produces copper but not tin, and without tin there is no bronze. Tin comes primarily from Legemum, the peninsula immediately to the west, whose hill mining settlements have supplied the Ascarth smiths for as long as the guild records on either side extend. The Legemum tin price is not a number any serious Avite strategist ignores. Every king of Ascarth who has considered expanding his territory has also considered, at some length, what it would mean to control that supply directly rather than purchase it. None have managed it permanently. See the Legemum entry for the reasons. Bronze is for the things that matter: weapons, armor, the great gates of Aevis that are the city's most famous physical feature, the grave goods that accompany warriors to the burial mounds on the peninsula's highland interior, the enormous bull-headed figures that stand at the entrances to the city's major public spaces, verdigris-green now after generations of sea air, staring toward the water.\n\nBronze architecture — specifically bronze-clad architecture — is the visual signature of Avite achievement. The great buildings of Aevis are stone at their foundations and structure, but their facing, their doors, their decorative panels, and in the most important structures their roofing elements are clad or cast in bronze. The effect, when the buildings were new, would have been extraordinary — an entire city gleaming at the harbor's edge. The patina changes this over time: the newer bronze-work of more recent construction shines golden-orange, while the older districts have gone deep green, so that the city reads, from the sea, as two colors depending on how old the neighborhood is.\n\nThe Avites export bronze goods throughout the Iberos Sea trade. Avite-made weapons command premium prices from buyers who can afford them. The merchant cities of Nylon and the Svaleen buy Avite bronze; the Moroshé trade routes carry it east. This commercial dimension is not lost on anyone, including the Avites, who understand perfectly well that their military culture and their craft economy reinforce each other. The reputation of Avite weapons sells the weapons. The weapons maintain the reputation.\n\n---\n\n## Aevis\n\nAevis is built on and around a rocky promontory at the northern end of the peninsula — a site chosen, if the epics are believed, because it was the highest defensible point available and the founding king wanted to be able to see anyone coming from any direction. The city has grown down from that original citadel height over centuries, spreading into the lower town around the harbor, but the citadel remains the political and symbolic center: the palace-complex of the King of Ascarth, when a king exists, or of the high lord of Aevis when it does not.\n\nThe citadel is the most completely bronze-clad structure on the peninsula. Its gates are the famous ones — two full stories of cast bronze, depicting in relief the campaigns of the kings of the first Ascarth period. The detail is extraordinary and was clearly produced over multiple generations of craftsmen. The gates are not purely symbolic; they are functional, and they are heavy enough that opening them requires a mechanism and several men. Visitors are made aware of this.\n\nThe harbor below the city is active with trade — bronze goods going out, tin and other necessary metals coming in (the peninsula has copper but must import tin, and the management of tin supply is a persistent strategic concern for any Ascarth king), timber, foodstuffs, and the miscellaneous requirements of a substantial urban population. The harbor district has a different character from the citadel: busier, more mixed, with resident merchants from Nylon and the Svaleen quarter-districts who have found that being present in Aevis is useful for knowing what is happening before it happens to them.\n\nThe *veth* maintain their archives in a building adjacent to the citadel — technically part of the palace complex but architecturally distinct, lower and wider, with a courtyard used for public recitation during festivals. The *veth* archives are the closest thing to a formal history that Ascarth culture produces. Scholars from Nylon have petitioned to study them and have been politely informed that the archives are for the *veth* and the *veth* are for the Avites, and that this arrangement has served both well.\n\n---\n\n## The Kingdom of Ascarth\n\nThe Kingdom of Ascarth is not a permanent political entity. It is a condition that the peninsula enters periodically, when Aevis is powerful enough and its king is capable enough to compel or convince the smaller cities to acknowledge suzerainty. The smaller cities acknowledge suzerainty by sending warriors when called, paying tribute in bronze goods and provisions, and allowing Aevis's markets to set the prices for the peninsula's external trade. In exchange they receive protection, participation in the glory of the King's campaigns, and the economic advantages of being part of a larger and more feared polity.\n\nThe Kingdom dissolves when the king dies without a strong successor, when the tributary cities accumulate grievances beyond the manageable level, or when an external situation — a failed campaign, a trade collapse, a plague — damages Aevis's prestige enough that the smaller cities conclude the calculation has changed. It has dissolved and reformed several times within recorded history. The current period is one of partial unity: Aevis controls most of the northern and central peninsula under terms that the southern cities are actively contesting.\n\nThe Avite heroic tradition complicates royal succession in ways that are culturally interesting and politically unstable. The king must be worthy — this is not a formal criterion but it is a genuine one. A king who cannot lead warriors, who cannot be praised by the *veth* in terms that the warriors accept, who cannot maintain the reputation of the house, will find the tributary cities reviewing their arrangements. Several kingdoms have ended this way, without formal conflict — the king simply found, one day, that the allegiances he thought he had were no longer operative.\n\n---\n\n## The Peninsula's Other Cities\n\nThe smaller cities and towns of the Ascarth Peninsula are not merely Aevis in miniature. Each has its own tradition, its own lineage claims, its own contribution to the epic cycle. Some are coastal fishing communities that supplement their income with bronze trade and have their own small warrior aristocracies. Some are inland hill towns that produce the bulk of the peninsula's agricultural output and negotiate carefully for their independence. A few are old enough to have pre-Avite foundations — evidence of earlier inhabitants whose language and culture have been largely absorbed, preserved only in place names and certain ritual practices that the *veth* have incorporated without fully explaining.\n\nThe southern tip of the peninsula is the part most consistently outside Aevis's control. The cities there are small, remote, and have survived by being not worth the effort of conquering — their harbors are poor, their agricultural land thin, their bronze production limited. They maintain their own warrior traditions, speak a dialect of Avite that the northern cities consider archaic, and regard Aevis with a mixture of cultural pride and political wariness that summarizes the peninsula's general relationship to its dominant city.\n\n---\n\n## Among the Coastal Cities\n\nSouth of the peninsula's base, on the western bank of the Lizeem, lies Gala — a market city and agricultural region that the kings of Aevis control more often than not, through tribute and access arrangements rather than direct garrison. Gala is Ascarth's provisioning ground and its commercial outlet on the mainland side; its grain and oil supplement what the rocky peninsula cannot grow, and its market gives Avite bronze goods a trading floor that reaches the Lizeem's river network. The Galans are not Avites and do not become Avites, which the Avites find either irrelevant or mildly grating depending on the king. What matters strategically is that the arrangements hold. See the Gala entry for detail.\n\nWhen the Kingdom of Ascarth is strong, the cities of the Iberos coast — Nylon, the Svaleen confederation, Nyross — pay attention. A united Ascarth controls a stretch of coastline that any ship moving north or south must pass, and a king who decides that taxing that passage is a good idea has the military capacity to enforce the decision. This has happened before. It is not remembered fondly in the maritime trading records of the coastal cities.\n\nThe management strategy has been consistent across several generations: keep Aevis supplied with what it needs from outside the peninsula — tin, in particular, which the city requires in volume and which the coastal trade controls — at terms favorable enough that disruption is not in Aevis's interest, while maintaining diplomatic relationships with the tributary cities that give coastal merchants early warning when the Kingdom's internal politics are shifting. The Nyloni are, characteristically, systematic about this; their harbor records contain what amounts to a continuous assessment of Ascarth's internal coherence, updated whenever a trading ship returns from Aevis with news.\n\nThe coastal cities admire the Avites in the way that people admire something that is genuinely excellent at what it does, and fear it for the same reason. Nylon's salons do not produce many defenses of the Avite heroic tradition — it is too alien to the city's values — but they also do not produce many comfortable dismissals of it. The Avites are inconveniently difficult to dismiss.",
