    "Selemi": {
      "name": "Selemi",
      "color": "#16a085",
      "lore": "# Selemis\n\nSouthwest of the Ascarth Peninsula, across a channel narrow enough that on a clear day you can read smoke from the other shore, sits the island of Selemis: a midsized island whose position at the southern mouth of the Iberos Sea has made it, over several centuries, the most consequential single point of maritime power on the continent. The city that occupies most of its northern coast shares the island's name. The people are called the Selemi.\n\nSelemis does not have the largest navy in the Iberos Sea. It does not control the most territory. It does not produce the most goods. What it controls is position: every ship moving between the Iberos Sea and the open southern ocean passes within reach of its harbor, and every port on the sea has, at some point in the last two centuries, found itself doing business with a Selemi outpost whether it planned to or not. The Selemi have translated this geographic accident into a commercial empire built not on ownership but on presence — the carefully maintained ability to be at every significant port with something to offer and something to withdraw.\n\nThis makes them the most successful maritime traders on Azhora. It also makes them, to the Ascarth kings across the channel, an irritant of the first order.\n\n---\n\n## The Island\n\nThe island of Selemis is shaped like a crescent, its concave face turned toward the Azhoran coast, forming a natural sheltered harbor of considerable size. The city fills this crescent from headland to headland — a dense, layered settlement that has been building over itself for long enough that the oldest foundations in the lower harbor district are several stories underground and accessible only to the maintenance workers who keep the drainage systems functional.\n\nThe harbor is the city. Everything else — the residential districts climbing the island's interior hills, the warehouses and chandlers' yards, the quarter districts where foreign merchants maintain permanent residences, the schools and the meeting-halls and the archive building with its copper roof gone green — is organized around and in service to the harbor. A Selemi who cannot give you a clear account of the current tide schedule, the state of the wind, and what ships are in port knows they are going to hear about this.\n\nThe channel between Selemis and the Ascarth peninsula is narrow but not trivial — enough that a fleet can cross it but not so little that an army can wade. The Selemi have positioned their harbor fortifications to make the crossing as expensive as possible and have stationed their fastest ships at the channel's northern end, where they can intercept anything moving toward the island before it reaches the crescent harbor. These defenses have not stopped Avite conquest, historically. They have made each conquest expensive enough that it has eventually been abandoned as more trouble than it was worth.\n\n---\n\n## The Selemi\n\nThe Selemi are the most widely traveled people on the continent and they carry this fact lightly. A Selemi merchant in Nyross, in Nylon, in the ports of the Svaleen, in the Moroshé coastal towns, or in the Elodi quarter of any city has usually been in several of those places in the same season, and has adjusted their manner to each without apparent effort. They are genuinely easy to talk to, curious about other people's situations, knowledgeable about whatever the local preoccupation is, and generous with their time in the way of people who know that the investment will return.\n\nThis cosmopolitan fluency is not an affectation. The Selemi have been absorbing foreign vocabulary, foreign cooking, foreign music, and foreign religious practices for long enough that the question of what is originally Selemi culture is genuinely difficult to answer. The food in Selemis itself is, by consensus of most visitors who have eaten extensively in other Azhoran cities, the most interesting in the continent — not because it is elaborate or precious, but because it combines techniques and ingredients from a dozen different culinary traditions in combinations that should not work and do. The music is similar. The architecture of the city shows four or five distinct stylistic traditions coexisting on the same street, accumulated across the centuries of Selemi commercial reach.\n\nWhat is genuinely and distinctly Selemi is the quality of attention they bring to understanding other people. A Selemi in a new port does not merely learn the prices. They learn who sets the prices, why, who benefits from them being set at that level, and who would benefit from them being different. They learn the social geography of the city — which families are rising, which are declining, which disputes are currently active, which are superficially resolved but still generating pressure. This information is not always immediately useful. The Selemi are patient enough that this does not bother them.\n\nThe combination — charming surface, comprehensive attention, long memory — is why other Azhoran merchants sometimes find the Selemi pleasant to deal with and difficult to trust. Both assessments are accurate.\n\n---\n\n## The Outpost Network\n\nThe Selemi maintain outposts at strategic points along the coasts of the Iberos Sea and, in several cases, beyond it. These are not primarily commercial operations — there are Selemi merchants who use the outposts as bases, but that is secondary. The outposts exist to give Selemis a card to play in every major port.\n\nIn practice, this means: a Selemi outpost is a small, permanent, legally recognized Selemi presence with its own building, its own staff, its own relationship with local authorities, and its own established role in local commerce. The outpost handles Selemi ships, stores Selemi goods, and maintains the relationships that allow Selemi merchants to operate with advantages — favorable berths, early information about cargo, introductions to the people worth knowing — that outside merchants cannot easily replicate.\n\nWhen Selemis wants to apply pressure to a coastal city, it does not send a navy. It adjusts its outpost's behavior. Ships are delayed rather than expedited. Introductions are not made. Information about cargo availability becomes, suddenly, slightly less current. The goods that the outpost has been smoothly providing become subject to delays that cannot quite be pinned on anything specific. This pressure is deniable, incremental, and effective. Most coastal cities with Selemi outposts have learned what it feels like and have adjusted their policies accordingly.\n\nThe outpost network also means that the Selemi know, in near-real-time, what is happening at every significant port in their reach. This is not what the outposts are for — it is simply an inherent consequence of having well-placed, attentive people everywhere. The Selemi do not advertise this capability. They use it.\n\nThe outpost locations are chosen by a combination of strategic necessity and historical opportunity — some exist because that port controls a critical sea lane, others because a Selemi merchant a century ago established a relationship that proved durable. The network has gaps, and maintaining it requires continuous investment. Selemi diplomacy is substantially occupied with keeping outpost rights in place when local politics change, and with establishing new outpost rights in cities that are becoming important before those cities are certain they want Selemi presence.\n\n---\n\n## With Aevis\n\nThe narrow channel between Selemis and the Ascarth Peninsula is one of the most examined stretches of water in the Iberos Sea, from both sides.\n\nThe Avites and the Selemi are natural antagonists: one culture built around land, hierarchy, and the individual pursuit of martial glory; the other built around sea, lateral commercial networks, and the collective management of leverage. They have been neighbors for long enough that the antagonism has acquired the familiarity of a long argument — still genuine, still capable of producing violence, but structured by centuries of knowing exactly how the other side thinks.\n\nThe conquest history is real and sits in the Selemi cultural memory with the weight of something that must not happen again. Aevis has taken the island twice in the documented period. Both times, the Selemi response was the same: the island fell, the outpost network continued to function, and the Selemi civilization carried on from the sea. Selemi ships did not stop sailing because Aevis held the harbor. Selemi merchants did not close their outposts because an Avite garrison occupied the city. The revenue streams continued; the relationships continued; the empire of presence persisted while the physical island was temporarily someone else's.\n\nThis is the essential asymmetry: Aevis can take Selemis's island, but Selemis is not its island. The Selemi understand this about themselves. The Avites, both times, eventually understood it as well, after the cost of occupation exceeded what the occupation was producing. The occupation ended. The Selemi returned.\n\nThe current period is independent. The channel is watched. There is trade across it — Avite bronze and Selemi sea-goods, mutually useful enough that neither side wants complete disruption — and there are diplomatic missions of varying frequency and, usually, limited warmth. The Selemi outpost in Aevis is the one outpost in the network that is managed with the most careful attention to local politics, because the local politics are the most capable of turning into an army on short notice.\n\nThe Ascarth kings, for their part, are aware that the Selemi outpost in their city is watching them. Both sides find this arrangement more useful than the alternative.\n\n---\n\n## Among the Coastal Cities\n\nSelemis is known everywhere on the Iberos Sea in the way that a fact of geography is known — not because you chose to learn it but because it is simply present and relevant. Every city that trades on the sea has an opinion about the Selemi. The opinions range from genuine admiration through cautious appreciation through the particular wariness of people who have seen what happens when the outpost in their city becomes, suddenly, less helpful.\n\nNylon's relationship with Selemis is among the warmer ones on the coast. The Nyloni questori find the Selemi interesting to argue with — one of the few groups that treats negotiation as an intellectual discipline rather than a contest. The Selemi find Nylon useful as a place where information flows openly enough to be worth collecting. There is a Selemi quarter in Nylon's harbor district that is old enough to be considered part of the city's permanent texture.\n\nThe Svaleen cities deal with Selemis competently. Solis has a Selemi outpost; Enebreum has had complicated negotiations about whether it wants one (it has wanted one when it was losing trade and not wanted one when it was suspicious about what the outpost was doing). Imlamdris has no outpost and, being inland, does not need one.\n\nOutside the Iberos Sea, wherever Selemi ships have reached, the reputation travels ahead of them. The most successful maritime traders on Azhora is a description that functions as both a commercial recommendation and a social warning, depending on what you are trying to do and whether you want Selemis to know about it.\n\n---\n\n## Summary\n\nSelemis is the continent's master of maritime leverage — an island city-state whose actual power is not in its harbor or its fleet but in the distributed network of presence it maintains at every significant port in its reach. The Selemi are charming, cosmopolitan, attentive in ways that take time to notice, and constitutionally patient. Their independence from Aevis is precarious in the narrow sense that the channel can be crossed, and has been; it is secure in the broader sense that the Selemi civilization is not located on any island that can be taken. What Selemis is, the Avites have not yet found a way to hold.",
      "loreRef": "the_selemi",
      "climate": "Csa"
    },
    "Telemonia": {
      "name": "Telemonia",
      "color": "#2980b9",
      "lore": "# Telemonia\n\nBetween Pyros to the west, the base of the Legemum hills to the south, and the Galan lowlands to the east, the terrain rises into a highland country of steep ridges, narrow river valleys, and open upland pasture that the surrounding coastal and agricultural cultures have spent several centuries learning to leave alone. This is Telemonia. Its people — the Telemon — are warriors in the specific sense that the word sometimes means and more often does not: not people who fight when pressed or who celebrate fighting as a virtue, but people for whom the cultivation of combat is the primary work of a life, the way that agriculture is the primary work of a farming culture, carried on with the same systematic seriousness and the same expectation that it will be done correctly.\n\nThey are available for hire. They do not accept foreign settlement, foreign trade in the interior, or foreign politics. They have never been successfully conquered, which is a claim that has been tested enough times for the pattern to count as established.\n\n---\n\n## Geography\n\nThe highland country of Telemonia covers roughly sixty miles east to west and forty north to south — not large in absolute terms, but terrain that expands considerably when you are trying to move through it with an army. The ridgelines run northeast to southwest, creating a series of parallel valleys that funnel movement and give defenders a continuous choice of positions. The valley floors are fertile enough for grazing and subsistence agriculture; the ridges are forested in their lower reaches and rocky above the treeline; the passes between valleys are few, known to every Telemon child, and defensible by small numbers against very large forces.\n\nThe rivers that drain the highland move quickly — too quick and shallow for significant navigation, useful mainly for water supply and as obstacles. They flood unpredictably in spring. The Telemon build nothing in the flood plains.\n\nThe highland borders are not marked with walls or formal fortifications. The Telemon do not build walls around their territory. The terrain is the wall, and the people are the garrison, and every outsider who has tested this arrangement has concluded it is adequate.\n\nThe climate is cool and wet in winter, short-summer, with the kind of reliability that a pastoral people can plan around. The Telemon have planned around it for long enough to have it well understood. Their provisions are always in order.\n\n---\n\n## The Telemon\n\nThe Telemon are not a large people. The population of Telemonia has been estimated, from outside, at somewhere between sixty and a hundred thousand — enough to sustain the warrior culture, the pastoral economy, and the farms, but not enough that large numbers could go to war as mercenaries while leaving the highland undefended. The numbers work because everyone is available for defense, not because the population is large.\n\nThey are a compact, dark-complexioned, highland people who have been in these hills long enough that the hills are part of how they understand themselves. Not as metaphor — the Telemon do not speak much in metaphor — but as practical fact: they know this ground, every fold of it, every reliable water source, every ridge that provides cover and every path that turns treacherous in wet weather. This knowledge is part of training and part of inheritance. A Telemon warrior at home is a different instrument from a Telemon warrior anywhere else, and the difference is the ground.\n\nThey speak their own language — *Kellith*, in their own name for it — which has not been systematically studied by outside linguists because outside linguists have not been allowed in. What can be gathered from border-market contact and from the accounts of soldiers who have fought alongside Telemon mercenary bands is that it is grammatically unlike Mittoli, shares some sound patterns with older Pyrosi dialects, and has a vocabulary for categories of terrain, weather, and physical exertion that has no equivalent in any coastal language. Translators exist at the border markets; they are Telemon people who have chosen to serve that function, and their translations are understood by both parties to be selective.\n\nThe Telemon manner is direct to the degree that outside cultures read it as bluntness and sometimes as rudeness. It is neither. It is the communication style of a people who have found that clarity is more useful than cushioning, that an unclear statement costs more to correct later than a sharp one costs in discomfort now, and that time spent in social indirection is time not spent on something real. They are not without warmth — the accounts of soldiers who have served in Telemon mercenary bands consistently describe their camp companions as capable of deep loyalty and genuine humor — but the warmth is not performed in ways that outside cultures immediately recognize as warmth.\n\n---\n\n## The Warrior Tradition\n\nA Telemon child begins structured training at the age of seven. The training is not primarily about weapons — weapons come later. The early years are endurance: cold water, long distances, lean provisions, night navigation, extended silence. The premise is that a warrior who cannot manage their body has no use for technique. Technique is taught once the body is trustworthy.\n\nThe formal training period ends at approximately seventeen or eighteen with an individual test: several days alone in the highland, in the hardest season, returning with proof of having managed. The proof is specific and varies by tradition within different Telemon families; what is common is that the test is not witnessed by anyone else, that no help is given, and that the return is what counts. Coming back is the achievement. The account given afterward is expected to be accurate and brief.\n\nAfter the formal training period, a warrior belongs to a band — a unit of between twenty and forty fighters who train together, fight together, and whose members will be responsible for the recitation of each other's deeds at death. The band is the primary social unit. Marriage, household, kinship — these exist alongside the band but do not supersede it. A Telemon warrior's band is the audience whose judgment matters most.\n\nThere is no class of professional rememberers equivalent to the Avite *veth*. The Telemon band is its own record. The members carry the dead with them in a specific recitation — names, deeds, the manner of death — spoken at the three seasonal ceremonies that structure the Telemon year. This recitation is not performed for audiences or preserved in written form. It is for the band. An outside scholar who has sought to collect these recitations for study has found the request declined with the consistency of established policy rather than individual reluctance.\n\nThe warrior tradition does not distinguish categorically between men and women. Both train. The mercenary bands that go out to fight in others' wars are predominantly composed of men, not by rule but by convention and by the household calculation that women's presence in the highland defense during a mercenary campaign's absence is more valuable than their presence in a foreign field. When the highland is invaded, no such calculation applies. Every Telemon fights. The accounts of failed invasions make this clear.\n\n---\n\n## The Mercenary Practice\n\nTelemonia does not trade in goods. It trades in fighters.\n\nThe bands available for mercenary contract are organized through a formal process conducted at the border markets. A power seeking Telemon mercenaries sends a representative to the nearest border market site, states their need, and waits. The Telemon do not advertise; they evaluate. The representative may be kept waiting for days or may be met quickly, depending on circumstances that are not explained to them. The band leader or council of band leaders who will decide makes inquiries through the border-market translators: the nature of the conflict, the terrain, the duration, the pay, the command structure the Telemon will be expected to operate within. This last item is significant. Telemon mercenary bands serve under their own leadership on the field. They receive strategic instructions and coordinate with allied forces, but tactical command is the band leader's. Employers who cannot accept this are told so and the negotiation ends.\n\nThe terms of a Telemon mercenary contract are specific: duration, conditions, payment in goods or metal rather than political arrangement, and the departure clause. Telemon bands leave when the contract ends. Not when the war ends. Not when the employer's situation has improved. When the contract ends. This has occasionally created awkward situations for employers who allowed their planning to depend on Telemon presence beyond the agreed period. The Telemon find these situations the employer's problem.\n\nPayment comes in the form of metal goods, specific agricultural materials the highland does not produce, and occasionally worked items of value that the warrior's household can use. Political agreements, land grants, titles, and positions are refused. What the Telemon want from mercenary service is material that improves life in the highland and nothing that creates obligation to the world outside it.\n\nThe reputation is consistent enough across generations to function as currency. A contract with a Telemon band is understood, by everyone who has worked with them, to mean what it says. They will not be bribed to change sides. They will not be manipulated through the fear of losing them — when the contract ends, they leave regardless. They will not claim to have done what they did not do or to have been present where they were not. Among mercenary forces, this is more remarkable than it sounds. The Galans, who conduct most of the border-market contract negotiations, have accumulated over generations a body of practical intelligence about how to represent a client's needs to Telemon band leaders in terms that the Telemon will engage with. This is a commercial specialty, and the Galan negotiators who hold it are valued by every coastal and inland power that has needed Telemon fighters.\n\n---\n\n## The Defense of Telemonia\n\nTelemonia has been invaded. The word \"successfully\" is doing work in the claim that it has never been successfully conquered — there have been incursions, campaigns, ambitious kings who decided that a highland warrior people of manageable population was a reasonable military objective, and early-period expansions by powers that had not yet received the information.\n\nThe information, once received, is consistent: when Telemonia is invaded, the Telemon stop being the mercenaries that neighboring powers have watched in foreign service and become something else. The formal discipline is still present. The effectiveness is higher. And the terrain, which the invaders may have traveled through with some discomfort, becomes an instrument. The passes seal. The valleys produce defenders from positions that were not visible before. Supply lines for the invading force find themselves attacked by small groups that vanish. Encampments that seem secure are not.\n\nThe Telemon do not pursue invaders beyond their own borders when they withdraw. They stop at the border and return to the highland. This is not magnanimity — it is the application of the same principle that makes the defense effective: the terrain that makes Telemonia unbeatable stops at its own edge. A Telemon army pursuing a retreating force onto coastal lowland or agricultural plain is away from its advantage. The Telemon understand this and act on it. The invaders withdraw into their own territory. The Telemon return to theirs. The border markets reopen eventually.\n\nThis refusal to pursue has been misread, on more than one occasion, as weakness. Powers that have interpreted the pullback as evidence of Telemon exhaustion, limited numbers, or political constraint have occasionally concluded that a second campaign was worth attempting. The accounts of second campaigns are the most detailed available, since the commanders of forces that fail twice tend to write about it more than commanders who succeed, and the accounts share certain features: the defense was harder on the second campaign than the first, the Telemon appeared to have prepared specifically for the approach used, and the withdrawal, when it finally came, was achieved at considerable cost.\n\n---\n\n## Political Structure\n\nTelemonia is governed by a king. The kingship is hereditary within a warrior family whose lineage claim to the position is old enough to be accepted, but it is constrained by two facts: the king must be combat-capable, and the band leaders must recognize the king's authority.\n\nA king who cannot fight leads ceremonially. Real tactical command passes to the war-council of senior band leaders, who act in the king's name but according to their own judgment. This has happened; it is not considered a crisis. The Telemon do not have strong feelings about the distinction between formal authority and functional authority as long as the function is performed well.\n\nThe band leaders are the governing class in practice. They arbitrate disputes within and between bands, manage the border-market arrangements, decide collectively whether to accept a mercenary contract, and form the council that makes the few decisions requiring coordination above the band level. The king presides over this council and has the casting vote in genuine deadlock, which is rare because the band leaders find deadlock wasteful. A council that cannot agree is a council spending time on words when it could spend time on something real.\n\nThere is no written law code. The customs are old enough to be known, stable enough not to require constant arbitration, and in cases of genuine dispute, the council's decision is the resolution. This system would be inadequate for a complex commercial society. For a society organized around warrior bands and pastoral economy, it is sufficient.\n\n---\n\n## Economy\n\nTelemonia is not poor. It is deliberately self-contained.\n\nThe highland produces cattle and horses — Telemon horses are a specific highland breed, compact and sturdy and well-suited to rough terrain, that the border markets sell at prices that reflect the breed's military utility. They produce grain in the valley floors sufficient for the population, supplemented by hunting and the pastoral economy. They work iron from the highland ore deposits — not the refined craft iron of the best mainland smiths, but serviceable, and in sufficient quantity that every Telemon fighter is adequately armed without depending on imported metal. The highland timber is good for building and for charcoal.\n\nWhat they do not produce: tin (they buy it, minimally, at the border markets, from Legemum indirectly), certain cultivated foods that do not grow in the highland climate, and worked goods of the quality that coastal cities produce. These are the categories of import, conducted at the border markets in exchange for horses, cattle, and mercenary contracts.\n\nThe border markets are not Telemon institutions — they are meeting points at the highland's edge, operated by traders from Gala and Legemum who maintain the infrastructure and conduct the exchange. Telemon people come to the markets to trade; they do not operate stalls, do not reside at the market sites, and do not bring foreign traders into the interior. The distinction between \"here\" and \"inside\" is maintained with consistency. Traders who have attempted to follow the returning Telemon have found themselves turned around at the highland edge, with varying degrees of firmness.\n\n---\n\n## Among the Neighbors\n\n**Legemum** — The Telemon presence to the north of Legemum's base has functioned, practically, as a buffer between the mining peninsula and the inland powers that might otherwise covet what it produces. The Legemi did not arrange this and have not attempted to formalize it into an alliance, which is wise — the Telemon do not make alliances. The relationship is one of commercially organized mutual benefit: Legemum tin reaches Telemon border markets in small quantities, Telemon horses and occasional contract fighters pass through Legemum's trade contacts outward. Both parties understand the arrangement without discussing it.\n\n**Gala** — The Galans broker most of the Telemon mercenary contracts and conduct the bulk of the border-market trade. This is the closest thing Telemonia has to an economic relationship with an outside power. The Galan negotiators have accumulated, over generations, the expertise to represent a client's need in terms the Telemon will engage with — which requires understanding how the Telemon think about obligation, about terrain, about what a contract is and what it is not. This expertise is not easily transferred and is one of the more specific commercial assets the city of Gala holds. The Telemon, for their part, find the Galans useful and reliable in the specific sense of: they know the rules and they follow them. This is the Telemon version of a strong recommendation.\n\n**Pyros** — West Pyros shares a cultural memory of the volcanic land and a degree of mutual intelligibility between their older dialects and Kellith that suggests some ancient common history. This has not produced closeness. The Telemon and the Pyrosi are adjacent peoples who have found that their differences are more operational than their similarities. West Pyros's agricultural tradition and the Telemon pastoral-warrior economy have minimal overlap; they trade occasionally and do not interfere with each other. The Telemon have, on at least two occasions, fought as mercenaries against Pyrosi forces in other powers' wars without this appearing to significantly disturb the border relationship. The Pyrosi read this as evidence that the Telemon have no loyalties. The Telemon read this as evidence that the Pyrosi do not understand what a contract is.\n\n**Ascarth and Aevis** — The Avites and the Telemon are the two warrior cultures of the region, and their mutual assessment is the kind of guarded respect that develops between people who recognize quality in a domain they both take seriously, and who are too honest to pretend otherwise. The Avites find the Telemon's indifference to glory philosophically incorrect. The Telemon find the Avite elaboration of warrior culture — the epics, the bronze aesthetics, the public performance of heroic identity — an expenditure of energy on display that could be spent on capability. Neither has said this to the other's face in productive terms. Telemon bands have served alongside Avite forces in shared employers' campaigns on several occasions. By every account, the operational relationship is effective and the social relationship is minimal. They fight well together and eat separately.",
      "loreRef": "telemonia",
      "climate": "BSh"
    },
    "Eer": {
      "name": "Eer",
      "color": "#8e44ad",
      "lore": "# Eer\n\nEer is the agricultural hinterland north and east of Nylon — the flatlands and low hills that run from the Lizeem's northern distributaries to the coast road, from the edge of the city's harbor districts outward to wherever the next power has most recently decided its territory ends. It grows grain, olives, river-fish in its canal systems, and orchard fruit in quantities that have fed Nylon for as long as both have existed. It has also been conquered so many times that its people have developed, across the generations, something close to a professional competence at being conquered: they know which rooms to clear, which records to present, which local officials to keep visible, and approximately how long before whoever has come this time will leave.\n\nThe standard account of Eer's strategic situation is that foreign powers seize it to starve Nylon into submission. The account is accurate as far as it goes. What it does not account for is the second half of what follows: Nylon does not submit. The city sits behind walls that have not been breached in recorded history, draws food and trade goods from the Iberos Sea, and waits. Eer changes hands; Nylon does not. Eventually the occupying power calculates what holding Eer without access to Nylon is actually worth, arrives at an unflattering answer, and withdraws. Nylon then reclaims its hinterland, rebuilds what was damaged, and the cycle resumes its next iteration.\n\nThe people of Eer are not surprised by this. They have been the piece on the board long enough to understand the game.\n\n---\n\n## Geography\n\nEer occupies the coastal plain north and east of Nylon, extending roughly forty miles inland from the sea and perhaps sixty miles along the coast from the Lizeem's northern mouth. The land is flat near the coast and rises gently toward low chalk hills in the interior, which mark the region's northern limit and separate it from the dryer Mittoli upland country beyond.\n\nThe soil is the deep alluvial loam deposited by the Lizeem's seasonal floods over millennia. It is extraordinarily productive and requires relatively little effort to work compared to the hill-farming of the Pyros uplands or the irrigation-intensive desert agriculture further south. Eer does not require heroics; it requires maintenance. This has shaped the agricultural culture of the region: methodical, experienced, oriented toward the long term in a way that makes the repeated political disruptions feel like weather — unpleasant, survivable, part of the conditions one farms in.\n\nThe road north from Nylon — the main artery connecting the city to the Mittoli interior, from which it receives timber, cloth, and the goods that do not arrive by sea — runs through the center of Eer before climbing into the hills. Whoever holds Eer can tax this traffic. This is the second reason, beyond the food supply, that foreign powers find the region worth holding: it gives them the customs revenues from Nylon's northern trade without giving them Nylon.\n\nThe coast of Eer faces the Iberos Sea from a series of low headlands and small sheltered bays, none large enough to be major harbors, all useful for fishing and small coastal traffic. The Eer fishery supplements the agricultural economy without competing with Nylon's harbor commerce — the two are in different scales.\n\n---\n\n## History of Occupation\n\nThe pattern of Eer's occupation has been consistent enough across different aggressors to suggest that it is structural rather than contingent. Any power that wishes to threaten Nylon without directly assaulting its walls finds Eer accessible, productive, and strategically positioned. The calculation is not unreasonable; it simply does not account for the walls accurately.\n\nThe walls of Nylon were not built to protect Nylon's trade interests. They were built to make the loss of Eer irrelevant. This is the conclusion that Nyloni historians have eventually reached, though the early scholarly consensus was that the walls were simply good military engineering applied to a headland site with excellent natural advantages. What made the walls extraordinary — their depth, their height, the engineering of their sea-facing sections, the integration of the harbor into a defensible perimeter — went beyond what mere natural advantage required. Whoever built them knew that the hinterland would not always be held. They built for that specific scenario. Nylon has been in that scenario many times since, and the walls have not failed it.\n\nThe occupying powers have varied over the centuries. Pyrosi highland kingdoms making one of their periodic pushes toward the coast. Iberos Sea city alliances attempting to extend commercial influence into the coastal plain. Mittoli river-city confederacies seeking control of the Lizeem mouth from both ends. Ascarth in its more expansionist periods, approaching from the southwest. No single power has held Eer for more than a generation before the combination of Nylon's sea-fed independence and the expense of maintaining an occupying garrison in uncooperative farmland has made the position untenable. Nylon, for its part, has never mobilized a significant field army to reclaim Eer — it has simply waited, maintained its harbor commerce, and reoccupied the hinterland when the occupier's withdrawal created the opportunity.\n\nThe result is that Eer's political history is less a history than a list: the names of the lords its people have paid taxes to, in the order they appeared, with notes on how long each stayed. The list is long. The notes are consistent.\n\n---\n\n## The People of Eer\n\nThe Eer do not call themselves that, exactly — the name is what the Nyloni use for the region and its people, derived from an older word for \"the outside\" or \"the outlands\" that has been in use long enough that the people of the region have adopted it themselves without enthusiasm. In their own speech, they identify first by village, second by the river branch or road their village sits on, and third by the district of Eer their sub-region falls within. Political identification — Nyloni subjects, or subjects of whoever has most recently arrived — comes a distant fourth and is adjusted as required.\n\nThis local-first identity is not insularity. Eer people travel, trade, and are well aware of the wider world. The local identity is a form of stability: when everything above the village level changes every generation, the village level is where continuity lives. The names of fields, the systems of drainage, the annual rotation of crops, the relationships between families and their specific parcels of land — these are older than any overlord's claim and will outlast the next one as they have outlasted all the previous ones.\n\nThe Eer have a reputation in Nylon for being slow to trust and slow to anger in roughly equal measure. Both qualities are accurate and both are the product of the same experience: they have seen too many confident outsiders to be moved quickly by confidence, and they have learned that anger is an expense that rarely recovers its cost. They are not passive — a farmer who lets their drainage systems deteriorate or their seed stock degrade because the current political situation seems uncertain will find, when the next political situation arrives, that the farm is worse off regardless. Continuity requires active work. The Eer understanding of this is deep.\n\nTheir hospitality is practical and real. A traveler on the north road who arrives at an Eer village is fed, sheltered, and left alone; they are not expected to be sociable, and the offer of warmth and food is not an invitation to spend the evening explaining where they're from. The Eer have quartered enough foreign soldiers to have lost any romantic interest in strangers.\n\n---\n\n## Eer and Nylon\n\nThe relationship is not simple. Nylon needs Eer in the sense that any major city needs cheap, close agricultural land — it is cheaper and more efficient to have food grow nearby than to import everything. When Nylon controls Eer, the city's food is relatively inexpensive, the north road revenue flows to Nylon's treasury, and the hinterland is a source of labor and material that allows the city to focus its maritime commerce on higher-value trade.\n\nWhen Nylon does not control Eer, all of this is absent — but Nylon survives. The walls and the harbor make survival possible. The Iberos Sea trade makes it comfortable. The city is inconvenienced, never defeated.\n\nThe Eer know this. They know that Nylon does not depend on them the way most hinterlands are depended on. There is something in the relationship — not quite resentment, not quite pride — that comes from being the farmland that a city could do without if it had to, but consistently chooses to reacquire when the opportunity presents. The Eer interpretation of this pattern is not usually flattering to Nylon, though it is delivered with the same equanimity as most Eer assessments: they come back, they always come back, they did not build those walls to leave us to whoever passes through.\n\nNylon's official view is that Eer is an integral part of the city's character and a community whose welfare the city government is responsible for. The Eer interpretation of the phrase \"integral part\" is that Nylon considers Eer integral when they can afford to and recovers their interest promptly when conditions change. Both positions are defensible.\n\n---\n\n## The Walls of Nylon\n\nNo description of Eer is complete without the walls, because the walls are the structural explanation for Eer's entire situation.\n\nThe Walls of Nylon are the reason the city has never been taken by the method of seizing its hinterland. They face landward as well as seaward; their design assumes that the city may at any point be surrounded, its agricultural supply cut, and its land approaches held by hostile forces, and that none of this must prevent the city's continuation. They are not merely tall — many cities have tall walls — but deep, with interior passages and provision stores built into the curtain wall itself, and sections that can be sealed from others so that a breach in one section does not determine the defense of the whole.\n\nThe sea-facing sections are the most unusual. The harbor is not outside the walls but integrated into them — ships can enter a protected inner harbor without passing through undefended space. This means that as long as Nylon's navy or allied vessels can maintain access, the city's sea trade continues regardless of what is happening outside the walls. An army that holds Eer has cut Nylon's land supply. It has not cut Nylon's sea supply. The city's survival arithmetic does not change.\n\nWho built the walls in their current form is a question with several scholarly answers and no conclusive one. The oldest sections are older than the oldest continuous written records in the city. The most sophisticated engineering — the harbor integration, the provision systems, the sealed-section design — is attributed by different scholars to different periods and different influences, and the question of whether the current walls are one coherent design or the accumulated result of multiple building programs is contested. The Nyloni questori, characteristically, have a formal debate tradition around this question. They consider it interesting enough to be worth arguing. The Eer consider it interesting in a different way: whoever built them built them for us, the Eer sometimes say, meaning: the walls were built specifically to make the loss of Eer survivable. Whether this is flattering is a matter of interpretation.\n\n---\n\n## Language and Culture\n\nEer speaks a Mittoli dialect that linguists categorize as coastal-transitional — closer to the standard Mittoli of the Lizeem valley communities than to the Iberos coastal variants, but with accumulated borrowings from every occupying power that has administered the region. An Eer vocabulary is a stratigraphic record: Pyrosi administrative terms from the highland kingdoms' periods of control, Iberos commercial vocabulary from the sea-alliance occupations, Ascarth-derived words for several agricultural tools (the Ascarth occupation, though unwelcome, was long enough to leave marks on the farming vocabulary), and an old layer of pre-Mittoli terms for land features, water courses, and soil types that has no clear relationship to any identified language family.\n\nThe Eer naming tradition follows Mittoli practice in broad outline but has a local specificity that visitors sometimes notice: Eer place names are almost entirely descriptive of physical geography — soil color, water quality, slope aspect — rather than commemorating persons or events. A village called Long-Drainage or Red-Clay has a name that tells you something useful about the place. A village called in honor of a long-dead ruler who happened to pass through in the fourth generation of the current occupation would require footnotes. The Eer have historically preferred the kind of name that does not require footnotes.",
      "loreRef": "eer",
      "climate": "Csa"
    },
    "North Scythe": {
      "name": "North Scythe",
      "color": "#e91e63",
