nvenes twice yearly at the Hearthstone — a broad, flat-topped rock outcrop near the island's geographic center, chosen not for any natural drama but for the fact that all three peaks are visible from it, which means the goddess can witness what is said there from each of her mountain presences. This is the stated reason for the location. The practical reason is that it belongs to no tribe and no town, and both parties accept it as neutral ground.\n\nThe Assembly has binding authority. This is the key difference from the Svaleen Conclave, which has no binding authority except on lighthouse maintenance. The Izoli Assembly's decisions bind because they are made before Izol, in the place where she watches, under the condition that those who speak take an oath in her name. Violating a decision made under this condition is not merely a political betrayal — it removes the violator from the goddess's protection. The Izoli take this consequence literally, and they note that the island is rocky, the sea is cold, and those who sail or graze or trade without the goddess's protection have a noticeably shorter history.\n\nWhether this is theology or politics or simply the force of a long tradition taken seriously, the Assembly's decisions tend to hold. In a confederation with no army and no enforcement mechanism beyond communal pressure and divine displeasure, this is considered sufficient.\n\nThe Hearthstone meeting is attended by tribal leaders, town representatives, and the keepers of the three mountain shrines. The shrine keepers do not vote; they observe, and their post-Assembly assessments — which are communicated through the tribal communities rather than through formal publication — carry their own weight in the confederation's informal opinion of how the meeting went. A decision that the shrine keepers describe as having been made in proper spirit is different from one they describe as having been made under the name of the goddess for purposes she would not recognize. T