    "South Ibenal": {
      "name": "South Ibenal",
      "color": "#2980b9",
      "lore": "# Ibenale\n\nNorthwest of Alezhor, the same pattern continues: forest pressing toward the coast, a plain caught between tree line and sea, a people who have organized their lives in the narrow space between two things that dwarf them. But where Alezhor is a collection of river-mouth cities each turned toward its own gold-bearing hills, Ibenale is something longer and thinner and differently oriented — a corridor more than a settled country, running from its warmer southwestern end toward the mountains that terminate it in the northeast, where the coastal plain pinches out against the western ranges and the forest and the high ground all arrive at the same point.\n\nThe strip runs for considerably more distance than Alezhor covers. It widens and narrows as the forest's edge and the coast's edge negotiate with each other across the length of it, and the character of the place changes as you travel its length — the southwestern end, nearest Alezhor, still warm and Mediterranean in feel, and the northeastern end, pressed between mountains and sea, cooler and wetter and built differently.\n\n---\n\n## Geography\n\nThe plain of Ibenale is defined by two parallel lines that are never quite parallel: the coastline, which bends and juts and recedes as headlands and bays interrupt it, and the forest edge, which follows its own logic of drainage and elevation and ancient growth boundary, indifferent to the settlements that have established themselves in the space between. Where the two lines come close together, the plain narrows to almost nothing — a strip of shore and road and not much else. Where they open out, there is room for towns, for farmland in the better-drained soil between the rivers, for the small industries that collect around a place where people stop.\n\nThe rivers of Ibenale come out of the forest at angles, crossing the plain and reaching the sea at intervals. None of them are large by the standard of the great Mittolo river systems, but they are regular, reliable, and useful: they carry goods down from the forest hills on rafts, provide the fresh water that the coastal settlements require, and mark the natural intervals at which communities have built themselves across the length of the corridor. The river-mouth anchorages are smaller and shallower than Alezhor's — the rivers here do not carry the freight of gold-mining operations — but they are adequate for the coastal traffic that moves between Ibenale's ports and the wider world.\n\nThe ocean here continues to be the open western sea: no sheltering islands, no protected sea-lanes, the full fetch of the western ocean arriving at the shore without interruption. The Ibenale coast is less cliff-faced than Legemum's but it is not docile; in autumn and winter the weather off the open water is serious, and the communities that have built ports here have built them with an understanding of what the sea does when it decides to make a point.\n\n---\n\n## The Two Ends\n\nIbenale is long enough that its ends are different countries in everything but name.\n\nThe **southwestern end** — nearest Alezhor, sharing its general climate — is the warmest and most Mediterranean portion of the strip. The summers are dry and long, the winters mild and manageable, and the agriculture reflects this: cultivated groves on the better slopes, productive grain land in the wider valley floors, a quality of life that does not require significant preparation for serious winter. The towns at this end are the most prosperous and most densely settled in the region, and they face both southeast toward Alezhor and northwest along the corridor, positioned to take advantage of both.\n\nThe **northeastern end**, where the plain is squeezed between the western mountains and the sea, is a different experience. The mountains arrive gradually — the terrain roughening over the last quarter of the corridor, the hills steepening, the forest descending closer to the water as the plain narrows. By the time the mountains properly assert themselves, there is barely room for a road between the base of the first real slopes and the coast. The settlement that exists at this terminus — where the coastal route ends, where the mountain passes begin — is not large but it occupies a position of considerable geographic significance: the junction between everything that moves along the Ibenale coast and everything that moves through the passes into the forest uplands beyond, and from there toward the Mittolo interior.\n\nThe climate at the northeast end is cooler, wetter, and more overcast through much of the year. The open ocean weather arrives more directly here than in the south, and the mountains create their own local conditions — afternoon clouds building against the high ground, moisture retained in the forest that covers the slopes above. People here dress for rain as a default rather than as an exception.\n\n---\n\n## The Corridor\n\nWhat Ibenale is, in functional terms, is a route. Goods, people, and information moving along the western coast must either travel through it or make a significantly longer journey. The overland road that runs the length of the strip — not a grand construction but a worn practical path, maintained by the communities that depend on it rather than by any central authority — is one of the older continuous roads on this portion of the continent, old enough that its routing decisions reflect knowledge of the terrain that predates any surviving documentation. It knows which stretches of the coast are unpassable in winter and skirts inland; it knows where the river crossings are most reliable in any season; it knows the places where the forest edge is close enough to require care.\n\nThe corridor function shapes the region's character in ways that a closed, self-sufficient territory does not experience. Ibenale has always had strangers in it. Merchants from Alezhor moving north; goods from the Mittolo interior coming down through the mountain passes at the northeast end; sailors calling at the coastal anchorages; travelers whose destination is somewhere else and who are stopping because Ibenale is between where they were and where they are going. The settlements along the corridor are practiced at the business of people passing through — accommodation, provisioning, the exchange of information about conditions ahead and behind — and they have developed the particular social texture of places that serve transit: broadly hospitable, alert to opportunity, and without the insularity that geography sometimes produces.\n\nThis does not mean Ibenale is without its own character. The corridor has been inhabited for long enough that the people who live here are not merely facilitators of others' movement. But the region's identity has absorbed the transient element rather than resisting it, and the result is a place that holds its own culture lightly enough to make room for whatever is passing through.\n\n---\n\n## The Forest\n\nThe great southwestern forest runs alongside Ibenale's entire length. It does not change in character as Ibenale's climate shifts from southwest to northeast — the old growth is old everywhere, the tree line is dense everywhere, the substrate vocabulary of the Forest Mittoli speakers who live inside it is equally present at both ends of the corridor. What changes is the relationship between the forest and the plain.\n\nIn the south, the forest is relatively far from the coast and the plain is wide enough that the forest reads as background — present, significant, but not immediately pressing. The settlements of the southern corridor are oriented toward the sea and the road, and the forest is the thing behind them, the thing from which the rivers emerge, the thing that travelers who leave the road enter and do not always return from on schedule.\n\nIn the north, where the plain narrows and the mountains push in from the other side, the forest comes closer. The tree line and the road are nearly adjacent in places. The forest here is not background; it is immediate, and the communities of the northern corridor have a more present relationship with it — not more comfortable, necessarily, but more immediate. Certain of the Forest Mittoli substrate words that appear only in the margins of the vocabulary in the south are common speech in the north. The vocabulary of old-growth proximity accumulates.\n\nThe forest communities — those who live permanently inside the old growth rather than at its edge — are present along the whole length of Ibenale but are most visible, most accessible to the corridor settlements, in the northeast. The mountain-end position creates a natural meeting point: the forest comes down from the slopes, the coastal plain is narrow, and the mountain-pass terminus gathers everyone who is moving between the interior and the coast. The forest peoples have a presence at the northeastern settlement that they do not maintain at the southern ports, and the town has adjusted to this in ways that affect its character.\n\n---\n\n## Language\n\nIbenale speaks Forest Mittoli along its length, with variation between the warmth-softened southwestern dialect — which shows more Alezhor contact and more Standard Mittoli influence from the corridor trade — and the compressed, consonant-heavy northeastern speech, which has absorbed more substrate vocabulary and sounds noticeably different to ears accustomed to the standard. A merchant from Caeras who can follow the southwestern speech without too much difficulty will find the northeastern end harder, not because the language has changed but because the layer of old-growth vocabulary sits closer to the surface, and the specific words it offers for specific conditions — forest conditions, mountain conditions, the quality of certain silences — are not in the dictionaries that Caeras scholars maintain.\n\nThe corridor itself, and the mixing that transit produces, has created a contact register used along the road — a practical language of provisioning and negotiation that pulls from Forest Mittoli, Standard Mittoli, and whatever commercial vocabulary the traders from other regions have deposited over the centuries. It is not a language anyone learns as a first tongue, but it is a language that any experienced traveler on the western corridor recognizes and can work with.",
      "loreRef": "ibenale",
      "climate": "Csb"
    },
    "North Ibenal": {
      "name": "North Ibenal",
      "color": "#8e44ad",
      "lore": "# Ibenale\n\nNorthwest of Alezhor, the same pattern continues: forest pressing toward the coast, a plain caught between tree line and sea, a people who have organized their lives in the narrow space between two things that dwarf them. But where Alezhor is a collection of river-mouth cities each turned toward its own gold-bearing hills, Ibenale is something longer and thinner and differently oriented — a corridor more than a settled country, running from its warmer southwestern end toward the mountains that terminate it in the northeast, where the coastal plain pinches out against the western ranges and the forest and the high ground all arrive at the same point.\n\nThe strip runs for considerably more distance than Alezhor covers. It widens and narrows as the forest's edge and the coast's edge negotiate with each other across the length of it, and the character of the place changes as you travel its length — the southwestern end, nearest Alezhor, still warm and Mediterranean in feel, and the northeastern end, pressed between mountains and sea, cooler and wetter and built differently.\n\n---\n\n## Geography\n\nThe plain of Ibenale is defined by two parallel lines that are never quite parallel: the coastline, which bends and juts and recedes as headlands and bays interrupt it, and the forest edge, which follows its own logic of drainage and elevation and ancient growth boundary, indifferent to the settlements that have established themselves in the space between. Where the two lines come close together, the plain narrows to almost nothing — a strip of shore and road and not much else. Where they open out, there is room for towns, for farmland in the better-drained soil between the rivers, for the small industries that collect around a place where people stop.\n\nThe rivers of Ibenale come out of the forest at angles, crossing the plain and reaching the sea at intervals. None of them are large by the standard of the great Mittolo river systems, but they are regular, reliable, and useful: they carry goods down from the forest hills on rafts, provide the fresh water that the coastal settlements require, and mark the natural intervals at which communities have built themselves across the length of the corridor. The river-mouth anchorages are smaller and shallower than Alezhor's — the rivers here do not carry the freight of gold-mining operations — but they are adequate for the coastal traffic that moves between Ibenale's ports and the wider world.\n\nThe ocean here continues to be the open western sea: no sheltering islands, no protected sea-lanes, the full fetch of the western ocean arriving at the shore without interruption. The Ibenale coast is less cliff-faced than Legemum's but it is not docile; in autumn and winter the weather off the open water is serious, and the communities that have built ports here have built them with an understanding of what the sea does when it decides to make a point.\n\n---\n\n## The Two Ends\n\nIbenale is long enough that its ends are different countries in everything but name.\n\nThe **southwestern end** — nearest Alezhor, sharing its general climate — is the warmest and most Mediterranean portion of the strip. The summers are dry and long, the winters mild and manageable, and the agriculture reflects this: cultivated groves on the better slopes, productive grain land in the wider valley floors, a quality of life that does not require significant preparation for serious winter. The towns at this end are the most prosperous and most densely settled in the region, and they face both southeast toward Alezhor and northwest along the corridor, positioned to take advantage of both.\n\nThe **northeastern end**, where the plain is squeezed between the western mountains and the sea, is a different experience. The mountains arrive gradually — the terrain roughening over the last quarter of the corridor, the hills steepening, the forest descending closer to the water as the plain narrows. By the time the mountains properly assert themselves, there is barely room for a road between the base of the first real slopes and the coast. The settlement that exists at this terminus — where the coastal route ends, where the mountain passes begin — is not large but it occupies a position of considerable geographic significance: the junction between everything that moves along the Ibenale coast and everything that moves through the passes into the forest uplands beyond, and from there toward the Mittolo interior.\n\nThe climate at the northeast end is cooler, wetter, and more overcast through much of the year. The open ocean weather arrives more directly here than in the south, and the mountains create their own local conditions — afternoon clouds building against the high ground, moisture retained in the forest that covers the slopes above. People here dress for rain as a default rather than as an exception.\n\n---\n\n## The Corridor\n\nWhat Ibenale is, in functional terms, is a route. Goods, people, and information moving along the western coast must either travel through it or make a significantly longer journey. The overland road that runs the length of the strip — not a grand construction but a worn practical path, maintained by the communities that depend on it rather than by any central authority — is one of the older continuous roads on this portion of the continent, old enough that its routing decisions reflect knowledge of the terrain that predates any surviving documentation. It knows which stretches of the coast are unpassable in winter and skirts inland; it knows where the river crossings are most reliable in any season; it knows the places where the forest edge is close enough to require care.\n\nThe corridor function shapes the region's character in ways that a closed, self-sufficient territory does not experience. Ibenale has always had strangers in it. Merchants from Alezhor moving north; goods from the Mittolo interior coming down through the mountain passes at the northeast end; sailors calling at the coastal anchorages; travelers whose destination is somewhere else and who are stopping because Ibenale is between where they were and where they are going. The settlements along the corridor are practiced at the business of people passing through — accommodation, provisioning, the exchange of information about conditions ahead and behind — and they have developed the particular social texture of places that serve transit: broadly hospitable, alert to opportunity, and without the insularity that geography sometimes produces.\n\nThis does not mean Ibenale is without its own character. The corridor has been inhabited for long enough that the people who live here are not merely facilitators of others' movement. But the region's identity has absorbed the transient element rather than resisting it, and the result is a place that holds its own culture lightly enough to make room for whatever is passing through.\n\n---\n\n## The Forest\n\nThe great southwestern forest runs alongside Ibenale's entire length. It does not change in character as Ibenale's climate shifts from southwest to northeast — the old growth is old everywhere, the tree line is dense everywhere, the substrate vocabulary of the Forest Mittoli speakers who live inside it is equally present at both ends of the corridor. What changes is the relationship between the forest and the plain.\n\nIn the south, the forest is relatively far from the coast and the plain is wide enough that the forest reads as background — present, significant, but not immediately pressing. The settlements of the southern corridor are oriented toward the sea and the road, and the forest is the thing behind them, the thing from which the rivers emerge, the thing that travelers who leave the road enter and do not always return from on schedule.\n\nIn the north, where the plain narrows and the mountains push in from the other side, the forest comes closer. The tree line and the road are nearly adjacent in places. The forest here is not background; it is immediate, and the communities of the northern corridor have a more present relationship with it — not more comfortable, necessarily, but more immediate. Certain of the Forest Mittoli substrate words that appear only in the margins of the vocabulary in the south are common speech in the north. The vocabulary of old-growth proximity accumulates.\n\nThe forest communities — those who live permanently inside the old growth rather than at its edge — are present along the whole length of Ibenale but are most visible, most accessible to the corridor settlements, in the northeast. The mountain-end position creates a natural meeting point: the forest comes down from the slopes, the coastal plain is narrow, and the mountain-pass terminus gathers everyone who is moving between the interior and the coast. The forest peoples have a presence at the northeastern settlement that they do not maintain at the southern ports, and the town has adjusted to this in ways that affect its character.\n\n---\n\n## Language\n\nIbenale speaks Forest Mittoli along its length, with variation between the warmth-softened southwestern dialect — which shows more Alezhor contact and more Standard Mittoli influence from the corridor trade — and the compressed, consonant-heavy northeastern speech, which has absorbed more substrate vocabulary and sounds noticeably different to ears accustomed to the standard. A merchant from Caeras who can follow the southwestern speech without too much difficulty will find the northeastern end harder, not because the language has changed but because the layer of old-growth vocabulary sits closer to the surface, and the specific words it offers for specific conditions — forest conditions, mountain conditions, the quality of certain silences — are not in the dictionaries that Caeras scholars maintain.\n\nThe corridor itself, and the mixing that transit produces, has created a contact register used along the road — a practical language of provisioning and negotiation that pulls from Forest Mittoli, Standard Mittoli, and whatever commercial vocabulary the traders from other regions have deposited over the centuries. It is not a language anyone learns as a first tongue, but it is a language that any experienced traveler on the western corridor recognizes and can work with.",
      "loreRef": "north_ibenal",
      "climate": "Csc"
    },
