    "Ovesos": {
      "name": "Ovesos",
      "color": "#6d4c41",
      "lore": "# Ovesos\n\nThe Oveth is one of five rivers that descend from the upland country north of Telemonia and west of the main Lizeem plain, gathering drainage from a rolling, half-forested transitional zone before joining the Lizeem's upper reaches some forty miles above Minora. Where the Oveth crosses its widest valley — a stretch of bottomland the people of the region call the Sorten, \"the wide seat\" — the kingdom of Ovesos has stood for as long as the valley has been farmed.\n\nThe name follows the river, as inland Mittoli names often do. *Oves-* traces to the root *oves*, meaning lower valley, the wide place where hill country flattens into cultivable ground; *-os* is the Mittoli collective for a settled people or political body. Ovesos: the people of the wide valley bottom. The Galans who buy Ovesian apples and barley use the name without particular feeling. The Minorans who receive Ovesian water-rights petitions use it with the slight flatness of officials who have received many such petitions and found most of them premature. The Ovesians use it with the unselfconscious specificity of people who have lived in one valley long enough that naming the valley and naming themselves is the same act.\n\n---\n\n## Geography\n\nThe Oveth is not the Lizeem. It rises in the upland between Telemonia's northern ridgelines and the more open interior plateau, fed by springs, hill-streams, and the snow-melt of several elevated ridges. By the time it reaches the Sorten, it is navigable for light boats and substantial enough for irrigation. Below the Sorten it narrows, drops through a rocky lower section, and joins the Lizeem approaches above Minora. The Sorten itself is roughly twelve miles of valley floor where the Oveth slows, widens, and deposits what it has carried from the upland — not as deep or as dark as the Mithala sediment, but productive enough to support grain cultivation, orchards on the gentle slopes above the floodline, and pastoral farming on the ridges above.\n\nNo mountain wall marks Ovesos's borders to the north, west, or south. The terrain simply rises — into the plateau country shared with interior peoples to the north, into the Telemon highland edge to the south, into the forested upland to the west. What defines the kingdom's limits in these directions is not geography but water. The line where Ovesian water rights end and another polity's begin is the real border, and it is the source of most of the kingdom's external politics.\n\nThe climate is temperate and wet, sharing the Mittolo character without the main plain's scale. Winters are cold, short-summer, with spring floods that are more localized and more variable in timing than the main Lizeem's predictable annual rise. The Oveth floods quickly, recedes quickly, and does not deliver the renewing sediment sheets that make the Mithala plain what it is. What it delivers is reliable water, good gradient for mills, and a valley microclimate that produces fruit at a latitude where the main plain cannot.\n\n---\n\n## Agriculture and Economy\n\nOvesos feeds itself and exports the difference. The Sorten produces barley, hard wheat, and minor grains in quantities that exceed local need in most years; the surplus travels south-east toward the Lizeem and on to Minora and Nylon, and south through the Telemon edge markets to Gala. The orchard slopes above the Sorten's floodline are the kingdom's distinctive product. The apples of the Sorten — dense, acid, long-keeping, suited to pressing or storing through winter — have a commercial reputation that has traveled further than Ovesos itself. The Galans have been buying them long enough to know the specific character of trees grown from the valley's mineral-heavy, slightly damp soil conditions. The pear varieties are less exported, more local: eaten fresh by the valley communities and pressed for a winter drink that visitors from the downstream cities find either excellent or strange depending on whether they expected wine.\n\nThe upper Oveth's gradient drives mills — grain mills and fulling mills — that the flat Mithala cannot match. Mill sites on the upper river are significant enough that their ownership has been part of noble-family disputes for several generations. Several of the most productive mills sit in or near the Middle Reach, which gives the boundary dispute its economic dimension alongside its political one.\n\nLivestock on the upland ridges — cattle and short-legged sheep adapted to rolling terrain — provide wool, meat, and the secondary agricultural economy the valley bottomland cannot accommodate. Upland herders move seasonally between summer plateau pasture and winter valley-edge grazing, and they carry a distinct culture and dialect variant from the Sorten farmers. They are simultaneously Ovesian and somewhat separate, as pastoral people adjacent to settled kingdoms tend to be.\n\n---\n\n## The Five Branch Countries\n\nOvesos is one of five polities that recognize themselves, and are recognized from outside, as the inner-branch countries — the kingdoms of the tributaries that feed the Lizeem above Minora. The phrase appears in Minoran water-rights records (*vel-thris-kael*, \"the returning inner tributaries\") and in Galan diplomatic correspondence and means, in practice, a political geography rather than a natural one: these five polities share an upstream position, a dependence on branch-river water, a set of ongoing competition with each other, and the common downstream relationship with Minoran authority that competition has forced them to navigate.\n\n**Nescor** sits upstream of Ovesos on the same Oveth system — smaller, higher, more pastoral than agricultural. It controls the headwaters and the upper mill sites. It is Ovesos's traditional adversary; the current boundary dispute is the latest iteration of a competition that has produced three wars in the past five generations and two treaties whose language both sides have found adequate grounds for continuing the dispute.\n\n**Ovesos** occupies the middle Oveth — the most productive stretch by agricultural output and the most populous of the five.\n\n**Isoruous** sits at the northern edge of the system, where the main Lizeem channel runs above Minora. It is the northernmost of the five and the one with the most fraught external relationship — effectively a Minoran client state, its politics divided between factions oriented upstream and downstream. Ovesos's arrangement with Isoruous — Ovesian goods pass through the Isoruous staging grounds on terms negotiated with the Upstream Faction of Isor's River Council — is the most important external relationship the kingdom has, and the most precarious, because the arrangement depends on which faction currently holds enough council seats to honor it.\n\n**Caricas** occupies the eastern inner branch, a separate river and a separate valley system. The oldest dispute in the inner-branch country is between Ovesos and Caricas; the original grievance is not remembered with any precision by either side, which has made it remarkably durable. Ovesos and Caricas have been at formal peace for forty years and share a boundary that neither side is satisfied with. Caricas and Nescor have periodically cooperated against Ovesos; this cooperation has never produced decisive results but has shaped Ovesian external policy, which tends toward preoccupation with the two-front problem.\n\n**Nethereum** occupies the northwestern inner branch — a flood country built around the Nethermere, a seasonally-expanding basin lake. Its economy runs on fish, wet-adapted crops, and post-flood cattle pasture rather than orchard and grain. Governed by a Flood Council of distributed ridge communities; no single capital, no binding interlocutor. Currently maintaining a workmanlike commercial relationship with Ovesos — neither ally nor active rival, but present in each other's calculations whenever the inner-branch alignment shifts.\n\nThese five polities have no formal federation, no shared institution, and no agreement that has lasted more than a generation. What they share is the Lizeem's upper water system and the downstream obligations Minora imposes on all who use it. The Branch Courts can theoretically adjudicate inner-branch disputes; in practice, Branch Court decisions are only enforced when the party against whom they are decided is weaker than the enforcing party. This is the basic structure of all legal systems. Here it is simply more visible.\n\n---\n\n## The Boundary Dispute\n\nThe Middle Reach is approximately four miles of bottomland where the Oveth bends westward before turning south into the Sorten. The bend creates a wide deposit — not as productive as the Sorten itself, but prime bottomland by the standards of a country that does not have much of it. It also contains two of the most productive mill sites on the upper river.\n\nA treaty concluded three generations ago, after the second of the three Nescoran wars, assigned the Middle Reach to Ovesos. A later treaty, after the third war, contained language that Nescor reads as restoring the stretch to joint administration and Ovesos reads as clarifying the first treaty without altering it. Both readings are in the original document. Both sides commissioned expert opinions; the experts agreed that the language was genuinely ambiguous, which the experts found intellectually interesting and both courts found useless.\n\nCurrent status: the Middle Reach is farmed by Ovesian households under military protection during planting and harvest. Nescoran herders use the upland margins of the same area in summer. Both activities are conducted with the elaborate mutual awareness of people who know precisely where the other side is and what each side is capable of. Full war would be expensive for both and the king of Ovesos would prefer to settle; Nescor's council would prefer the land. What both sides want is a Minoran Branch Court ruling in their favor. The ongoing proceeding, now in its eleventh year, continues. Isoruous controls which Branch Court calendar items are prioritized, through its position as the transit point between Ovesos and Minora and the informal leverage this gives its River Council over which petitions move quickly. Both sides are aware of this and continue to pay attention to the Isoruous relationship accordingly.\n\nThe border communities along the Middle Reach itself — the farming villages that occupy both sides of the contested stretch — have family connections across the boundary and conduct the actual trade that makes the border functional. Their assessment of the dispute, when asked, is that the lawyers are keeping it alive because the lawyers are paid by duration and the farmers are losing by the same measure. This assessment has not been submitted as evidence to the Branch Court.\n\n---\n\n## Political Structure\n\nOvesos is a monarchy. The current king — Melos of the house Oveth-Hold — took a throne that was contested when his family won it, after the third Nescor war destroyed the previous ruling family's credibility. The present house consolidated by competence in the war's aftermath: distributing the Middle Reach allocation efficiently, settling the soldier-debt owed to families whose service had gone unrewarded, and making the Isoruous passage arrangement that gave Ovesos more commercial stability than it had seen in fifty years. They are not beloved in the heroic sense and are not expected to be.\n\nMelos himself is cautious in the way that men who inherited contested thrones become cautious. He spent his early years in the Branch Court proceedings before he was crown-relevant and knows the treaty documents better than any living lawyer on either side of the dispute. His primary domestic challenge is not Nescor but the Water Council faction that believes the Isoruous arrangement undervalues Ovesian leverage and that Ovesos should press for renegotiation. This faction is probably correct on the merits. The king's position is that it is correct about the wrong moment; Ovesos cannot afford to lose the arrangement if the Isoruous River Council's Downstream Faction loses enough seats to honor it. Both sides of this argument make it at every Water Council session. It has not been resolved.\n\nThe **Water Council** is the governing body beneath the monarchy. Its membership is the noble families whose political standing is directly tied to their water rights — each major family holds title to a stretch of the Oveth or one of its feeder channels, and the title describes not only land ownership but allocation priority during dry years. The Council arbitrates disputes between families, advises the king on external affairs, and acts as legislative body for changes to water-right arrangements. Representation is by seniority of water-right claim, not by population; the oldest grants sit at the top. The friction between formal seniority and economic weight is the routine source of Water Council politics, because several of the most productive agricultural families in the Sorten hold relatively recent grants.\n\nBelow the noble families are the farming communities: freehold farmers with their own small water-right grants, tenant farmers working noble-family land under varied arrangements, and the craftspeople and laborers of the three main market towns. The upland herding communities sit somewhat outside this structure — they have land use agreements rather than water rights, they pay different obligations, and they have a tradition of providing military service that gives them practical leverage without formal standing.\n\n---\n\n## Language\n\nInner-branch Mittoli. A variant of Standard Mittoli fully intelligible to any downstream speaker, differing in vocabulary for valley terrain, water administration, and the specific practices of the branch-country environment. Key regional terms:\n\n*Oves* — lower valley, the flat where hill country opens into farming ground; the place-name root.\n\n*Thris-kael* — inner tributary; literally \"the returning inside water.\" The Standard Mittoli term used in Branch Court proceedings; Ovesian speakers use it when writing to Minora and something locally specific when talking to each other.\n\n*Vel-sorten* — bottomland allocation; the distribution of flood-renewed ground across farming claims after the annual Oveth rise. The process is managed by Water Council officials and generates the bulk of internal legal disputes.\n\n*Osk-milis* — water-right seniority; the hierarchy by which families claim allocation in dry years. The phrase is old enough to appear in the oldest Ovesian land records and has no clear etymology in Standard Mittoli; it may be a survival of an earlier dialect form from before the Compact-period standardization.\n\nOvesian formal legal language differs from Minoran formal language in a recognizable way. Where Minoran legal speech tends toward the technical and precise — current measurements, certified weights, dated inspection records — Ovesian legal speech tends toward the genealogical and historical. The most important questions in a water-rights dispute are always: who was here first, and what did the document say. Documents are quoted at length. Ancestors are named. The Minoran Branch Court clerks who transcribe inner-branch proceedings have noted in their own administrative records that Ovesian submissions are typically twice as long as Minoran submissions and contain three times the genealogical material, a pattern consistent enough to have been listed in the Branch Court's standard intake guidance.\n\n---\n\n## Relations with Neighbors\n\n**Gala** — Ovesian orchard and grain exports reach Gala through two routes: river (south-east via Isoruous, down the Lizeem approaches, south to the coast) and overland (south through the Telemon edge markets, into the Galan lowlands). The Galans know Ovesos as a steady supplier of quality apples and acceptable grain. The Guild of Assessors has twice been asked to arbitrate inner-branch disputes, both times at Minora's request; both times the Assessors produced decisions that no party found satisfying, which the Galans consider evidence of good arbitration.\n\n**Telemonia** — South of Ovesos, adjacent but not warm. Ovesos has hired Telemon mercenary bands three times in the past five generations: twice for the Nescor wars, once for the Caricas border realignment. The Telemon performed the contracted work and left when the contract ended. Ovesos paid what was agreed and found the experience impressive and not particularly comfortable. The Telemon do not linger, do not become familiar, and do not improve the odds of a campaign without changing the character of the force they augment. The Ovesian military tradition — adequate for valley defense, insufficient for sustained aggressive campaigning — accepts Telemon augmentation with clear eyes about what it means and what it does not.\n\n**Minora** — The downstream authority and the arbitration body Ovesos most frequently invokes and most frequently finds frustrating. Branch Court rulings on inner-branch disputes are legally sound and practically enforced only when both parties agree to enforce them. Ovesos's factors in Minora are among the more experienced inner-branch lobbyists in the Branch Court system — respected for their documentary preparation, not listened to except when their interests align with what the Branch Courts want to decide anyway. The First Divider's office receives Ovesian petitions with the patience of an institution that has received similar petitions for two centuries. Ovesos knows this and continues submitting.\n\n**Isoruous** — The most important external relationship the kingdom has. The Isoruous passage arrangement — negotiated with the Upstream Faction of the Isor River Council, which controls the staging grounds at Vel-Oss where Ovesian goods transfer to Lizeem barges — is the commercial artery through which Ovesian products reach the downstream market. Its periodic renegotiation is the event that Ovesian commercial policy organizes around. When the arrangement is stable, the kingdom is stable. When it is under pressure, everything downstream of it is under pressure. The Isoruous Upstream Faction knows this, prices accordingly, and maintains enough goodwill to keep the arrangement worth having while charging enough to make it the subject of Water Council complaints every other year. The arrangement's precariousness is structural: it holds only while the Upstream Faction holds enough River Council seats to honor it.\n\n---\n\n## Character\n\nOvesos is not a place that expects to be famous. Its self-image is agricultural and managerial: a kingdom that grows food in a valley, manages water carefully, and prosecutes its boundary claims through the correct legal channels because the alternative is a war that neither the grain nor the orchards can survive without damage.\n\nThe Ovesian character, as neighbors describe it, is careful, long-memoried, and slightly aggrieved. Long-memoried because the water-rights tradition requires it — you cannot argue for a claim that predates your grandfather without knowing your grandfather's documents. Slightly aggrieved because the inner-branch countries are visibly secondary to the downstream civilization, and this is noticeable to anyone who has traveled from the Sorten to Minora and back. Careful because in a place where the political stakes are perpetually bottomland and mill sites, the cost of a mistake is not abstract.\n\nThe valley festivals are agricultural and specific: the Planting Feast before the Oveth's spring rise, the Allocation Settlement when flood-renewed ground is divided among farming claims, and the Harvest Close when the Water Council meets to reconcile the year's water usage against the recorded allocations. The Harvest Close is the closest thing Ovesos has to a civic ceremony — a formal accounting conducted in public, before the full Council and whatever observers attend, in which the year's water management is reviewed and the claims for the following year are registered. It is not entertaining. It is very well attended.",
      "loreRef": "ovesos",
      "climate": "BSh"
    },
    "Nethereum": {
      "name": "Nethereum",
      "color": "#00897b",
      "lore": "# Nethereum\n\nThe Neth flows from the northwestern uplands through country that does not hold its shape the way the Oveth does. Where the Oveth cuts a valley and stays there, the Neth spreads. It reaches the middle section of its course and finds low, poorly-drained ground — a broad depression in the interior plateau where multiple hill-streams converge and the water has no efficient route to the main Lizeem. Here it slows, fans out, and creates the Nethermere: a body of standing water that in dry years is a large shallow lake with productive margins and in flood years is a flat inland sea that covers everything below the ridgelines and does not recede until well into summer. The land within and around the Nethermere, and the communities built on the rises that remain above it, constitute the country called Nethereum.\n\nThe name is a Caeras scribal form — the Caeras compilers who first documented the inner-branch region wrote *Nethraneum* in the oldest records and shortened it in later administrative use. The people of the region call themselves the **Nethrani** and call their country *Nethran*, \"the deep water country,\" which is what the scribal form was trying to render. The distinction does not matter in Branch Court proceedings. It matters in the Nethrani's view of how much outside authorities understand about where they live.\n\n---\n\n## Geography\n\nThe Nethermere sits at the low point of a natural drainage basin roughly twenty miles across. No single river created it; the Neth is the largest input, but several smaller hill-streams feed the depression from different directions, and the basin's flat bottom means water that enters has nowhere urgent to go. The Neth itself exits through a narrow channel to the southeast, where it drops off the plateau edge and joins the Lizeem approaches below — a short, fast lower section that gives the Neth a split character: gentle and spreading in the middle country, quick and navigable in its lower reach.\n\nThe Nethermere is not the same body of water from year to year. In a dry year it is a lake of three to four miles across, with marshland extending well beyond it into the basin margins, and drier ground at the outer edges of the basin where hill-stream communities have established themselves. In a flood year, it expands until the basin margins disappear and only the ridgelines and the higher hummocks within the basin remain as islands. The line between \"lake\" and \"flooded plain\" does not exist in the Nethermere as a fixed thing. What exists is a gradient of reliable ground, and the Nethrani have mapped every foot of it.\n\nThe communities of Nethereum are built on elevated points: the ridgelines around the basin perimeter, the higher hummocks within it, and the natural levees along the Neth's course above the Nethermere. These sites were not chosen by planners but by survival; settlements that occupied lower ground were moved or destroyed by high flood years until what remained was what could not be drowned. The result is a kingdom with no continuous territory — a constellation of elevated communities distributed across a landscape that is, for several months each year, largely water.\n\nThe climate is wetter than Ovesos and much wetter than the main Mithala plain. The basin's water moderates temperature somewhat — summers are less hot than the open plateau, winters slightly milder — but the chronic dampness shapes everything: the crops, the buildings, the health traditions, the emotional register of public life. The sky over the Nethermere is often overcast. The light has a quality that travelers describe as muffled. The Nethrani describe it as the right light for seeing clearly.\n\n---\n\n## Agriculture, Fishery, and Economy\n\nNethereum produces differently from Ovesos. Where the Sorten's valley floor supports grain farming and orchard slopes, the Nethermere supports what grows in and around standing water. The division is not poverty — in good years the basin is extraordinarily productive — but the productivity operates on different principles and in a different category of risk.\n\n**Grain**: Elevated ground around the basin perimeter is farmed for barley and oats — crops suited to the damp soils and shorter reliable growing season. The yields per acre are lower than Ovesian bottomland grain; the Nethrani compensate by farming the elevated margins intensively and relying on other food sources for the rest. In high flood years even the perimeter farms are affected: the crop that was planted in spring may be inundated before harvest if the Nethermere rises late.\n\n**Reed-grain and wet crops**: In the shallower margins of the Nethermere, the Nethrani cultivate water-adapted crops — primarily a variety of marsh grain that the Minorans classify with the reed-rice family but the Nethrani insist is different. It grows in standing water of up to a foot, harvested by wading, and produces a dense, slightly bitter grain that stores extremely well and commands a modest but consistent market from traders who know its properties. It is the Nethrani's most distinctive agricultural product and the one the Galans are least sure what to do with commercially.\n\n**Fishery**: The Nethermere supports a large population of river fish that move into the basin during flood season and are harvested as the waters recede. The Nethrani have developed extensive weir and trap systems at the Nethermere's outlet points — constructions that channel the retreating water through harvesting structures and produce, in good years, quantities of preserved fish (smoked, salted, dried) that travel as far as Minora and occasionally Nylon. The fish trade is the Nethrani's most commercially reliable export and the one on which their Minoran factor relationships are primarily built.\n\n**Cattle**: The basin margins after flood recession are carpeted with the fast-growing grass that the retreating water deposits. Nethrani cattle — a compact, short-legged breed adapted to wet ground — are moved onto this post-flood pasture as soon as the footing is reliable. This grazing window is not long, but the grass's quality during it is high enough that the Nethrani cattle are notably well-conditioned by late summer. The Galan meat market has known Nethrani cattle for two generations.\n\n**Reed and marsh goods**: The Nethermere produces materials that drier countries must import — specifically the tall marsh reeds used for thatch, basketry, rope fiber, and structural framing for lighter construction. Reed goods are a secondary export, more local in reach than the fish and cattle trade, but reliable enough that several Nethrani communities specialize in them almost entirely.\n\nIn aggregate, Nethereum feeds its own population from a combination of sources that no single one of them could sustain. In a year when grain fails, fish and wet-crop carry the country. In a year when the flood is too severe for the fish weirs, grain stores and cattle are the reserve. The interdependence of multiple food systems is not an explicit policy; it is the accumulated practice of a population that has learned, over generations, that the basin does not fail the same way twice.\n\n---\n\n## The Flood\n\nThe flood is not the enemy. This is the most important thing to understand about the Nethrani and the hardest thing for outsiders to accept.\n\nThe flood makes the post-flood pasture. It deposits the silt that maintains the marsh grain's productivity. It drives the fish into the basin where they can be harvested. It fills the water stores that Nethrani households maintain in the elevated settlements. Without the flood, the Nethermere is a smaller lake with reduced margins, reduced productivity, and reduced water availability for the communities that depend on it. The Nethrani know this. Their traditions encode it. Their word for the flood — *nethvel*, \"the return of the deep water\" — carries no negative valence. The flood returns. This is the nature of the place.\n\nWhat the flood takes is also acknowledged directly. It takes crops planted in the low margins in optimistic years. It takes buildings constructed below the reliable line. It takes animals not moved to higher ground in time. It takes people, occasionally — children who did not learn the water, the old who cannot be moved, travelers who did not know the signs. In a bad year it takes more: the high flood of three generations ago, called the Long Water in Nethrani memory, remained above normal for six weeks and destroyed structures in communities that had not flooded in living memory. These losses are remembered.\n\nThe Nethrani memorial tradition for flood loss is one of the most developed among the inner-branch countries. The lost are named in the annual **Flood Recall** — a ceremony held as the Nethermere rises each spring, in which the previous year's losses are spoken aloud and added to the running community record. The record is oral and extremely long; older communities have Flood Recall lists going back thirty or forty generations, maintained by designated memory-keepers who carry them without writing. The oldest entries are names without context — no family, no place, only the name and the approximate flood year. The lists do not distinguish between the losses of floods in different years; they are cumulative. A community's Flood Recall is its complete account of what the water has taken, delivered annually before the water takes anything more.\n\nOutsiders who have witnessed a Flood Recall describe the experience as both solemn and matter-of-fact — the tone does not vary between an old loss and a recent one; the names are spoken the same way because the water does not distinguish. Several Mittoli scholars have compared the Flood Recall to the Telemon band recitation of the dead, which the Nethrani find mildly amusing; they consider the Telemon tradition to be a Flood Recall for violence, which is a more specific and therefore more manageable category of loss.\n\n---\n\n## Political Structure\n\nNethereum has no single capital and no clean hierarchy. This is not a condition it is trying to remedy.\n\nThe communities of the inner basin and the ridge settlements of the perimeter each govern themselves, organized around the elevated ground their founding families or lineages claimed and have held. The scale of self-governance varies: the largest ridge communities are small towns with their own councils and defined customary law; the smaller hummock communities are extended family groups with a senior who mediates disputes and speaks for the group in regional affairs.\n\nWhat connects them is the **Flood Council** — a body of community representatives that meets on the Neth levee above the Nethermere, twice yearly, to coordinate flood management, adjudicate cross-community disputes, and make collective decisions about external relations. The Flood Council has no permanent membership beyond its coordination function; representatives come from each recognized community, and a community's weight in deliberation is roughly proportional to what it contributes to the shared flood works. Communities that maintain the largest sections of the levee system or operate the most important fish weirs speak first and longest.\n\nThe Flood Council does not produce a king. It produces consensus, slowly, with considerable argument, and the consensus lasts as long as it serves the majority of communities better than non-compliance. In flood emergencies, the coordination function activates and the process is faster — the Nethrani have extensive practice with emergency decisions and are notably quicker at them than at routine ones. An outside observer who watched a multi-day deliberation on fish weir taxation and then watched the same body coordinate an emergency levee repair in an hour would conclude they were watching a different institution. They are watching the same institution under different conditions.\n\nMinora finds the Flood Council difficult to deal with because there is no single interlocutor with binding authority. A Branch Court ruling against \"Nethereum\" requires enforcement across every Nethrani community that does not want to comply, and different communities have different interests. The Caeras legal tradition has a general principle that treaty obligations bind a polity, not its component parts; the Nethrani have a general principle that the Flood Council's agreement binds the communities that agreed and not the ones that didn't, and that consensus is not unanimity. These two principles have not yet produced a resolution in any active proceedings.\n\n---\n\n## Relations with the Other Inner-Branch Countries\n\n**Ovesos** — Northwest boundary neighbors and, by the standards of the inner-branch competition, neither allies nor active rivals. Ovesos's water-rights obsession and Nethereum's flood-management orientation produce different political languages; they compete in the Minoran Branch Court for different things and rarely find themselves in direct opposition. The Ovesian Water Council finds the Flood Council's structure frustrating — there is no equivalent body to negotiate with, no binding agreement mechanism. The Nethrani find the Ovesian fixation on documented claims slightly absurd in the context of a landscape that periodically resets. The practical relationship is workmanlike: Nethrani fish and Ovesian grain move through shared border markets; neither polity spends significant political capital on the other.\n\n**Isoruous** — The most important relationship in the inner-branch system for Nethereum, since Isoruous controls the staging grounds at Vel-Oss where Nethrani fish and marsh goods transfer to Lizeem barges for the downstream market. The Nethrani have a passage arrangement with Isoruous, maintained separately from Ovesos's arrangement and on different terms — the Nethrani deal primarily with the Upstream Faction of the Isor River Council, which values the fish trade as an asset independent of the Minoran commercial relationship. Isorani factors at Vel-Oss have been buying preserved Nethrani fish for long enough that there is a standing commercial relationship beneath the political one; in the rare event that the Flood Council fails to reach agreement and the Isor River Council must decide who to favor, the fish trade tends to inform the decision.\n\n**Nescor** — Upstream of Ovesos and further removed from Nethereum. The Nethrani have periodic disputes with Nescor over the western upper tributaries where the two river systems' catchments overlap; when Nescor diverts water upstream it affects the Neth's flow into the Nethermere. These disputes are slower-developing and less intense than the Ovesos-Nescor boundary conflict, because changes to the Nethermere's water level are gradual and ambiguous in cause. The Nethrani do track the correlation. They simply cannot prove it to Branch Court standards.\n\n**Caricas** — The eastern inner branch, separated from Nethereum by Ovesos. The two polities have no direct relationship. They appear in each other's political calculations only when the inner-branch alliances shift enough that an Caricas-Nescor alignment threatens to isolate Ovesos and leave Nethereum with a difficult downstream partner.\n\n---\n\n## Language\n\nNethrani is an inner-branch Mittoli variant, recognizable to any Standard Mittoli speaker, with a vocabulary shaped by the basin environment and the flood tradition. Key terms:\n\n*Nethvel* — the flood; \"the return of the deep water.\" The term for the annual rise. Distinguished from *nethmorr* (a damaging flood; one that exceeds normal bounds) and *nethgell* (a low year; a flood that fails to reach its expected level, which is its own kind of problem).\n\n*Vel-nethran* — the post-flood; the season after the Nethermere recedes. The most productive season for pasture and fishing; also the season of repair, when communities assess and rebuild what the water took.\n\n*Haethoss* — the reliable line; the elevation above which a community's core structures are built. Not a legal term; a practical one. A family that builds below the haethoss is building in the flood. The haethoss of each community shifts slightly over generations as flood behavior changes.\n\n*Kael-nethvel* — to address the flood in elevated register; specifically, to speak the Flood Recall formula. The Pyrosi register-borrowing (*kael-*) did not arrive through direct Pyrosi contact but through the intermediate Mittoli trade dialect. That the Nethrani use Pyrosi elevated register for their most significant ceremonial address has been noted by linguists as a remarkable borrowing; the Nethrani explanation is that the flood has the standing of the land, and speaking to the land requires elevation. This is structurally identical to the Pyrosi explanation for using *kael-* at fumarole sites.\n\n*Nethoss* — the deep basin; the lowest point of the Nethermere. Used figuratively for any situation that has reached its lowest possible state. \"We are in the nethoss of this\" means: it cannot get worse; the only direction now is up.\n\n---\n\n## Character\n\nThe Nethrani are, by outside description, pragmatic about loss in a way that the other inner-branch countries find either admirable or unsettling depending on temperament. The observation is accurate as far as it goes. A Nethrani household that has watched the flood take a granary and rebuilt it in the same location the following spring is not a household that lacks feeling about the loss. It is a household that has decided that the location is worth the risk, that the loss is predictable and survivable, and that the alternative — moving permanently to the ridgeline — is worse. This is not resignation. It is the considered position of people who know their landscape.\n\nThe phrase that summarizes the Nethrani outlook, used internally and occasionally externally, is: *nethoss vel-kael* — \"the deep basin is where the good ground is.\" What the flood covers, it also enriches. The most productive soil in the basin is the lowest, because the lowest receives the most sediment. Building above the haethoss is safe and less fertile. Building below it is dangerous and more fertile. This is a real choice that real households make each generation, and the culture built around making it is not naive about the cost.\n\nThe festivals of Nethereum are flood-organized: the Flood Recall before the rise, the Post-Flood Settlement when communities assess and apportion the post-flood margins, and the Drying Festival at the end of the fishing season, when the Nethermere reaches its lowest point and the communities hold a market across the exposed basin floor. The Drying Festival market is the one annual occasion when the entire inner landscape of the basin is accessible; communities that are separated by water for most of the year meet in the center, on ground that will be underwater again within months. The market has been described by visitors as the strangest fair they have attended, conducted on lakeside that is simultaneously open and temporary, the whole gathering organized around the knowledge that the ground it is standing on will shortly not be available.",
      "loreRef": "nethereum",
      "climate": "Cfa"
    },
