    "Elagos": {
      "name": "Elagos",
      "color": "#16a085",
      "lore": "# Elagos\n\nThe name outsiders use — the Lake Lands — is not wrong, but it describes the surface of the place rather than its consequence. Elagos is lake country, yes: a landscape of interconnected freshwater bodies so numerous and so large that the land between them feels more like a byproduct of the water than the other way around. Canoe from any point in Elagos and you can reach almost any other point without more than a short portage. The watershed is continuous, the lakes feed each other, and everything flows eventually to the southern outlets where the drainage begins its descent toward the Moros plain. This is the geography that made Ambron. The city did not make itself.\n\n---\n\n## Geography\n\nElagos occupies the northern shelf of eastern Azhora — the broad, elevated terrain between the cold uplands that face the northern sea and the flat sediment of Moros below. It is not a plateau; it has hills, ridges, and the complicated topography of a landscape that was carved and deposited by glacial processes older than any Azhoran writing and then left to fill with water. The lakes formed where the ice had been. They are old, deep, cold even in summer below their sun-warmed surface layers, and extraordinarily clear.\n\nThere are dozens of named lakes in Elagos and many more unnamed ones. The major bodies — eight or ten, depending on how you count the connected basins — are large enough to generate their own weather, their own wave patterns, and their own distinct lacustrine climates on their shores. The largest, **Lake Ela**, is the dominant feature of the landscape in every sense: visible from the surrounding hills as a silver expanse that confuses the eye about where water ends and sky begins, productive in the way of cold northern lakes whose depths hold food that surface fishing only hints at, and positioned at the center of the whole drainage system such that whoever holds the narrows at its southern end holds the key to everything that moves through the Lake Lands.\n\nAmbron holds those narrows. It has for most of recorded history.\n\nThe other major lakes — **Lake Brul**, to the northeast, cold and deep and known for its storms; **the Thelas chain**, three connected basins in the west that drain into Ela through a river the lake communities call simply the Link; **Lake Ossen**, in the southeast, shallow and warm-watered and exceptionally productive — each have their own communities and their own characters. Together they form a network whose total extent is greater than many principalities, and whose internal transport efficiency — by boat in summer, by ice-road in winter — makes any unified power that controls it capable of moving people and goods faster than anything comparable on the surrounding continent.\n\nThe **southern outlets** are where the lake system meets the plain. Two rivers carry most of the drainage: the Ela-south, which feeds the upper Ros and is navigable as far as the Moros grain towns, and the Bruloss, a shorter and faster river that reaches the Iberos Sea coast north of Eer's chalk hills. Ambron straddles the Ela-south at the point where the narrows open into a wider navigable reach — close enough to Lake Ela to control the lake's outflow, close enough to the Moros plain to dominate the trade that passes between them.\n\n---\n\n## Climate\n\nThe lakes moderate the climate of Elagos in ways that make it substantially more livable than the northern uplands just to the north and more consistently productive than the exposed Moros plain to the south. In summer, the lake water retains heat longer than the land does; in winter, the same stored warmth delays the onset of the hardest cold and extends the growing season at the lake margins. The agricultural calendar of Elagos runs two to three weeks longer than Moros on average, and considerably longer than the northern coast.\n\nThis does not mean mild. The winters are cold, the lakes freeze — partially, in most years; completely, in hard winters — and the snowfall on the western shore of Lake Ela during lake-effect conditions can exceed anything the Moros plain experiences. Summer thunderstorms come north from Moros with their violence intact and are amplified by the warm lake surfaces they cross. The climate of Elagos is not gentle; it is productive, which is different.\n\nThe lake-effect snow that falls in the cold months has practical consequences that the Elagosi have spent many generations incorporating into their material and institutional culture. Roads become impossible and ice-roads become available at roughly the same point in the seasonal calendar. The transition is known, planned for, and managed with the same engineering attention that Mittoli plain communities give to river flooding. Elagos does not fight its climate; it schedules around it.\n\n---\n\n## Ambron\n\nThe city is at the southern gateway of the lake system, where Lake Ela's outflow narrows before dropping toward the plain. Every canoe, every barge, every timber raft, every shipment of dried fish or lake-country grain that moves south must pass through the narrows. Every trade good coming north from Moros, from the Iberos coast, from the Pyrosi transit routes — everything that enters the lake system enters through the same passage. Ambron did not choose this position. The position chose the settlement that eventually became Ambron, and Ambron has spent its existence becoming equal to the position's requirements.\n\nThe city is large by Azhoran standards — larger than Minora by most measurements when it has its full population, though population fluctuates with the empire's extent and the quality of the most recent century. At its peaks it draws administrators, merchants, craftspeople, and subject peoples from across the continent. At its diminished periods it is still the largest city in the Lake Lands by enough margin that the question is not interesting. The lake communities do not produce alternative centers capable of competing with Ambron's geographic advantage; geography at this scale does not produce accidents.\n\nThe city's physical character reflects its institutional history: it has been built, damaged, rebuilt, expanded, contracted, and rebuilt again many times. Ambron does not have a single architectural period. It has layers — the old lake-stone construction of the earliest settlement visible in the foundations of buildings that have been repurposed every few generations, the ambitious construction of the high imperial periods, the pragmatic repairs of the contraction periods, the new building of recoveries. The city as it stands at any given moment is a cross-section of its own history, which is also a cross-section of the history of a large part of the continent.\n\n**The narrows control** is the foundation of Ambron's wealth. Toll income from the lake-to-plain passage funds the military establishment that enforces the toll, which funds the administrative apparatus that manages the empire in its expansive periods, which generates the tax revenue that funds the construction that makes the city impressive enough to attract the skilled people the empire needs to function. This is a virtuous cycle in good periods and a fragile one in bad ones; when the trade through the narrows declines — due to war, plague, a major drought in Moros that reduces northbound grain-merchant traffic — the whole system faces pressure simultaneously.\n\n---\n\n## The Empire\n\nAmbron rules an empire. This is said as a fact rather than a boast in most parts of Azhora because it has been true for most of the time anyone can remember, in the sense that even Ambron's non-imperial periods tend to be described as \"between empires\" rather than as periods of permanent normalcy. The empire waxes and wanes; Ambron's centrality to the lake system is effectively permanent.\n\nThe empire at its greatest extent has reached south through Moros to the chalk hills above Eer — not usually Nylon itself, whose walls resist the same calculus that has frustrated every other power, but the hinterland and the road traffic from the Lizeem interior. It has reached east along the Iberos coast, incorporating the estuary ports through which Elagosi fish and grain move. It has extended north into the upland communities, exacting tribute from the pastoral clans in the form of cattle and labor rather than coin they do not use. Westward, toward the inner-branch rivers, Ambronite influence is weaker — highland terrain suits local defensibility more than imperial projection — though there are historical periods when the inner-branch arbitration proceedings referenced Ambronite administrative precedent as persuasive.\n\nThe mechanism of empire is administrative more than military, though military capacity underlies the administration. Ambron does not maintain permanent garrisons across its full extent in the strong imperial periods; it maintains a lake fleet that can move forces quickly, a road system that connects the major subject towns, and a system of prefects who collect taxes, maintain order, and ensure that Ambronite commercial interests are protected. The subject territories retain their own internal governance — the arbitration assembly at the Ros crossing continues to function under Ambronite supervision, not replacement — and the practical cost of empire to a well-governed subject territory is tax payment and the occasional demand for soldiers. This is not comfortable, but it is not catastrophic, and communities that have experienced the alternatives tend to note that Ambronite administration, while extractive, is at least consistent.\n\nWhen the empire contracts — which it does, on a rough cycle that the scholars of Ambron have studied without arriving at a prediction method — it tends to lose the outer provinces first. Moros reverts to its own management when Ambronite military presence thins and the arbitration assembly no longer bothers to phrase its rulings in Ambronite administrative language. The coast ports reclaim their commercial independence when the lake fleet is reduced to local patrol. The inland communities stop paying tribute when the prefects stop being able to enforce collection. The core — the Lake Lands themselves, Ambron's direct territory — holds through contraction periods and provides the base from which the empire rebuilds.\n\nWhat triggers the contractions varies. Military overextension in a particularly ambitious emperor's generation. A succession crisis that splits the city's governing council into factions. A decade of poor lake-fishery years that depletes the food surplus the military machine runs on. Plague. A combination of these in sufficient proximity that the outer provinces calculate that the cost of tribute to a weakened Ambron exceeds the benefit of Ambronite order. The calculation tips, and the empire is suddenly smaller. Ambron reconsolidates, spends a generation or two in regional dominance rather than continental projection, and then a new generation decides it is time to reassert.\n\nThe subject territories have absorbed this cycle into their institutional memory. The arbitration assembly at the Ros crossing maintains two sets of legal forms: the Ambronite-format ones used during empire periods, and the local-format ones used when Ambron is not watching. The transition between them is managed without drama.\n\n---\n\n## The Lake Communities\n\nOutside Ambron's direct administrative zone, the Lake Lands are home to communities that have been organized around the specific character of their lake for as long as anyone has records. Each major lake has produced somewhat different communities depending on its depth, its fish population, its storm patterns, and its position in the drainage network.\n\nThe communities of Lake Brul — cold, storm-prone, its fishery dominated by species that live in deep water and surface only at specific seasons — are among the most technically sophisticated fishers on the continent. They have developed boat designs, net types, and preservation methods adapted to conditions that would defeat the fishing traditions of the southern lakes. Their knowledge of northern lake behavior includes weather-reading systems that rival the sky-watching of the Moros plain in depth and exceed it in the specific application to lake meteorology. Ambron taxes their catch. It does not try to improve their methods.\n\nThe Thelas chain communities are the most politically fractious in Elagos, possibly because the three connected basins produce three distinct lake cultures that share a drainage system but not much else, and who have been arguing over water rights and portage passage since before the oldest caull record on the Moros plain. Ambron administers the Thelas chain through a separate prefectural arrangement that essentially consists of having an official available to adjudicate the chain's internal disputes so they do not require Ambron's personal attention every season. In the non-imperial periods the three basins manage their disputes themselves, which historically produces more drama and roughly the same outcomes.\n\nLake Ossen's communities are the closest to Ambron in character and the most integrated into its administrative structure — the shallow warm water produces fish in quantities that feed Ambron directly, and the proximity means Ossen communities experience empire not as a distant fact but as a constant municipal relationship. The Ossen towns are also where Ambronite administrative practice is most visible in the built environment: road surfacing, granary architecture, bridge engineering, public record-keeping traditions that all derive from Ambronite institutional precedent and persist through contraction periods because they work.\n\n---\n\n## Economy\n\nThe lake fishery is the foundation of Elagos's wealth in the way that grain is the foundation of Moros's. The lakes are extraordinarily productive — cold, clear, deep water supporting populations of large fish that dried, salted, or fermented provide both local nutrition and an export commodity with shelf life long enough to reach markets that fresh fish cannot. Elagosi dried fish appears in Minoran markets, Nylon harbor docks, and Pyrosi highland winter stores. In years when the southern grain harvest is poor, Elagosi fish is not a supplement to the diet of the Iberos coast trading cities; it is a substantial part of it.\n\nGrain is also produced, in the fertile lake-margin soils that benefit from centuries of aquatic sediment. Elagosi grain is not the principal production of the lake country — the lakes themselves are more productive per unit of labor than the fields — but the combination of fish and grain makes Elagos one of the few regions of Azhora capable of feeding a large military establishment from local production without severe trade dependency. This matters for empire. An empire that can feed its army from home territory does not have the same vulnerabilities as one that must maintain supply lines.\n\nTimber is significant. The lake-country forests produce the specific dense-grained wood from which the Elagosi lake fleet is built — long-lived, resistant to freshwater saturation, and capable of bearing the combination of load and weather that lake transport requires. The fleet is large enough that during the high imperial periods, Ambronite shipwrights have supplied vessels to Iberos coast allies, which is a form of commercial relationship that tends to complicate the political ones.\n\n---\n\n## Language\n\nElagos does not speak Mittoli. The lake country's language is its own — not related to the Mittoli family, not related to Pyrosi, possibly not related to anything else on Azhora in ways that comparative linguists have firmly established. The Caeras Valley Academy has a department for it and a long-standing argument about its origins.\n\nThe practical consequence of this is that Elagosi has functioned, in the strong imperial periods, as a prestige administrative language across a substantial portion of eastern Azhora. Subject elites learn it to interact with Ambronite prefects and courts. Commercial records in the narrows trade are kept in it. Legal forms in the Ros crossing assembly are transliterated through it in the imperial format. When the empire contracts, the Elagosi administrative vocabulary retreats with it — but not entirely. The Moros plain's eastern dialects contain Elagosi administrative loanwords for concepts the Mittoli vocabulary did not previously have: specific legal-status terms, tax categories, categories of commercial contract. These persist in the local speech long after the prefects have gone, the way the ghost of a building remains in the foundation line after the structure above has been torn down.\n\nIn the Lake Lands themselves, the language is uniform enough across the major lakes to serve as a single community of speech, with dialectal variation at the margins — the Brul northern communities have absorbed some influence from the northern Ganoss upland speech, and the Ossen southern communities have taken in Mittoli commercial vocabulary through centuries of Moros contact. But an Ambronite administrator speaking to a Thelas chain fisher is intelligible without translation, which is not something that can be said between a Caeras standard Mittoli speaker and a Ganoss upland clansman.\n\n---\n\n## Character\n\nElagos has the self-assurance of a place that has held power long enough to find it unremarkable. Ambron does not trumpet its imperial status in the way a newly powerful city-state might; it administers, collects, and maintains with the matter-of-fact efficiency of an institution that has been doing the same work for a very long time and expects to continue doing it after the current difficulties are resolved.\n\nThe lake communities outside Ambron share a version of this self-assurance that is less administrative and more environmental — the confidence of people who understand their landscape deeply enough to work with it rather than against it, who know the lakes in the specific way that comes from living beside them across generations, and who regard outside powers including Ambron as a weather condition to be managed rather than a destiny to be submitted to.\n\nThe relationship between Ambron and the lake communities is not simple. The communities provide the fish, the timber, the soldiers, and the surplus that makes the empire possible; Ambron provides the administrative order, the military protection, and the trade connections that move lake-country goods to the southern markets. Both sides are aware that they need each other. Neither side, in the contraction periods, pretends otherwise, and in the expansion periods, Ambron's administrative machinery tends to avoid the kind of overreach that would convert a manageable mutual relationship into a genuine resentment that outlasts the empire that generated it. The lake communities have been part of this system long enough to know that the alternative is worse, which is not enthusiasm, exactly, but it is a stable basis for a relationship.\n\nWhat unites the Lake Lands — more than any political arrangement, more than any imperial administration — is the water. The lakes are old and the communities living on them know it. The fish-reading and weather-reading and ice-reading traditions of Elagos are older than Ambron, older than the empire concept, older than any named political fact. They are the knowledge of people who have been living on these specific bodies of water long enough that the water itself is part of the cognitive inheritance — not the background to life in Elagos but the medium it takes place in.\n\nAmbron builds empires. The lakes have been here longer.",
      "loreRef": "elagos",
      "climate": "Cfa"
    },
    "Drent": {
      "name": "Drent",
      "color": "#2980b9",
      "lore": "# Drent\n\nThe lords of Drent have never agreed on anything long enough to matter. This is not for lack of trying; there have been Drentish unification projects in most generations, some of them producing impressive results for a decade or two before the coalition exhausts itself on a succession question or a river-toll dispute and the individual lordships drift back to their natural state of armed commercial competition. The land does not encourage unity. Every significant river in Drent has its own mouth and its own lord, and each river-mouth port is valuable enough to sustain an independent ambition and not valuable enough to compel deference to anyone else's. The result is a region of genuine wealth — the rivers are productive, the coast is rich, the Pebbles fishery is extraordinary — that has never successfully converted its wealth into the kind of unified power that might have kept Ambron out.\n\nAmbron is consequently in, more often than not.\n\n---\n\n## Geography\n\nDrent occupies the northeastern coastal slope of Azhora, east of the Lake Lands and north of where the coast begins its long turn toward the warmer Iberos Sea latitudes. The terrain descends from the uplands shared with Elagos's eastern edge through a series of river valleys — a half-dozen significant rivers and many more minor ones, all running east or southeast to the sea. None of them is as large as the Ros or the Lizeem; all of them are navigable far enough inland to give the lords of the river towns meaningful reach into the agricultural hinterland.\n\nThe coast itself is low. Where the northern Azhoran coast has cliffs and hard headlands, Drent's coast flattens as it goes south, the rivers spreading into shallow estuaries, the land losing its argument with the sea in a long contested fringe of marsh, sandbank, and beach. This is where the Pebbles come from — the same accumulation of longshore sediment that the rivers deposit, shaped over centuries by the prevailing currents into the long thin chains of barrier islands that parallel the coast half a mile to a mile offshore.\n\nBetween the Pebbles and the Drent mainland lies the **Stills** — the collective name for the sheltered water of the sounds, shallow and calm and bearing almost no resemblance to the sea on the Pebbles' open face. Fishing boats work the Stills in weather that would keep them off the coast entirely. Flat-bottomed transport barges move river cargo between ports along the sound without needing to risk the approaches. The Stills are a secondary transportation network, slow but reliable, that the Drent river-mouth communities use when the outer coast is difficult and the roads through the hinterland are worse.\n\nThe main rivers of Drent — the **Avrel**, the **Torn**, the **Caloss**, and the smaller ones — are each associated with a lordship, sometimes one per river and sometimes two or three competing for the best landing sites. The fertile valley floors support the same kind of grain and orchard agriculture as Moros to the south, though the climate is somewhat wetter and more variable, and the flood behavior of Drent's rivers is less predictable than the Ros's steady seasonal rise. The upland between rivers is forested, difficult, and the territory of no lord in particular, which has historically made it useful for moving things and people that lords are not supposed to know about.\n\n---\n\n## The Pebbles\n\nThe barrier islands run for nearly eighty miles along the Drent coast, interrupted in three places by the navigable inlets that connect the Stills to the open sea. At a distance they look like what they are named for: a scatter of pale stones dropped on the water, low enough that the sea regularly overwashes the smaller ones in storm season, shifting the sand and remaking the landform slowly but continuously. No Drentish lord holds the Pebbles in any meaningful sense. The islands do not stay still long enough for a fortification to become permanent, and the communities that have lived on them for as long as anyone remembers are not the kind of communities that a lordship manages well.\n\nThe Pebble communities — scattered across a dozen of the larger, more stable islands — are fishers and pilots and salvagers, and they are one of these more than the others. The channels through the barrier islands that connect the Stills to the Iberos Sea approaches are navigable in good weather by anyone with a competent eye and in bad weather by almost no one, with the exception of people who grew up reading the particular color of the water over the bars, the specific behavior of the current at each marked channel, the way the light falls differently on safe water and shoal water at a particular hour of a particular kind of day. This knowledge is old and it is not written down. It lives in the Pebble families and nowhere else.\n\nThe consequence is that anyone wishing to bring a substantial vessel into the Drent coast — a trading ship with any significant draft, a Elagosi lake barge refit for coastal work, anything that cannot afford to strike a bar in moderate swell — must take on a pilot. The pilots come from the Pebbles, and the pilots set their fees. This is the quiet foundation of the islands' independence: no lord's authority is worth more than the pilot's knowledge, and the pilots have never seen a reason to fix that arrangement. They are not hostile to the mainland lords; they are simply not subject to them in the way that people who need something from you are not quite subject to you regardless of what the formal hierarchy says.\n\nThe Pebble fishing grounds — the channels and the open approaches just east of the islands — are among the most productive on the northeastern coast. The migratory fish that transit the waters off Drent's coast pass through a narrowing between the open sea and the barrier island chain that concentrates them in ways that fishers elsewhere cannot replicate. The catch is salted and dried on the islands and moved through the Stills to the river-mouth markets and from there into the broader trade network. In years when the migratory shoals come through in volume, the Pebble catch exceeds what all the Drent river fisheries combined produce.\n\nThe salvage operations are not publicized. Ships that miss the channels and strike the bars become property of the nearest Pebble community by a custom older than any Drentish lordship. The mainstream position is that the pilots prevent wrecks. The Pebble communities' position is that they prevent wrecks whenever they are hired to, and that the bars do what bars do regardless. Both positions are accurate.\n\n---\n\n## The Lords of Drent\n\nDrent is governed by a loose collection of river-mouth lords, each controlling a port town and the agricultural hinterland of its river valley, and each related to the others through a web of marriage alliances, trade agreements, historical grudges, and competitive commercial relationships that outside observers have consistently found too complicated to summarize without getting something wrong.\n\nThe number of significant lordships has varied across the recorded history of the region. At any given time there are between six and a dozen who matter, with the Avrel and Torn lordships typically the largest — the Avrel because its river valley is the widest and most fertile, the Torn because its mouth is positioned where the main coastal trade route runs along the Stills and any ship moving between the Pebble approaches tends to pass it. The lesser lords hold smaller valleys, inland castles above the flood line, and the sort of defensive positions that the Drent terrain — not spectacular, but usable — provides.\n\nThe great Drentish political project, attempted in some form every two or three generations, is a congress of lords sufficiently unified to act as a single authority — to set common tariffs, maintain a shared coastal defense, negotiate with outside powers from something approaching a collective position. Every such congress has produced results. None has held together for more than a generation. The obstacles are structural: the lords' interests are too immediately commercial to sustain the compromises that collective governance requires, and any lord who grows powerful enough within the congress to make it function efficiently has grown powerful enough that the others begin calculating whether the arrangement still serves them.\n\nWhen no congress exists and no outside power is present, Drent's political reality is a series of bilateral agreements between individual lords, renegotiated constantly, honored approximately as often as they are convenient, and supplemented by the Pebble pilots' practical authority over the coast. This is not stable in a way that administrative scholars find satisfying, but it has been the default condition of the region for most of its history, which suggests that it is stable in some other sense that administrative scholars have not found a good framework for.\n\n---\n\n## Ambron's Dominion\n\nAmbron finds Drent useful. The northeastern coast provides harbors that the Elagosi lake fleet can use when it needs access to the open sea rather than the lake system. Drent's river valleys produce grain, timber, and fishing catch that supplement the lake country's domestic production. The Pebble approaches give Ambronite commercial interests access to the Iberos Sea trade routes without having to negotiate passage through the Svaleen Confederation or the Nylon harbor authorities. These interests are consistent and have driven Ambronite attention toward Drent through every empire cycle.\n\nThe conquest of Drent is not militarily difficult. The decentralized lords cannot field a combined force capable of resisting a substantial Ambronite mobilization, and the Elagosi lake fleet can suppress coastal resistance that might otherwise sustain a prolonged campaign. What individual lords can do — and consistently do — is negotiate their own submission before the army arrives, accept Ambronite overlordship on terms they find acceptable, and continue governing their own rivers in exchange for tribute and nominal fealty. Ambron's administrative preference is exactly this: the lords remain in place, the river-mouth ports continue to function, the Pebble pilots continue to pilot, and Ambronite tax collectors appear annually to collect the empire's share. The disruption of conquest is minimized because both sides understand that what Ambron actually wants is revenue, not direct administration of fishing villages.\n\nThe formal arrangement during empire periods is a **Lord Protectorate**: an Ambronite-appointed official, typically from a family with Drentish and Elagosi connections both, who sits at the Torn mouth as the empire's representative, hears disputes between lords that the individual lordships cannot resolve, and ensures that the tribute flows. The Lord Protector's actual authority over the individual lords depends on how much Ambron is paying attention at any given moment. In the strong imperial periods, the Protectorate has genuine teeth — Ambronite forces within reach, trade concessions withheld as leverage. In the periods of imperial distraction, the Protectorate becomes a formality that the lords observe punctiliously in language and ignore substantially in practice.\n\nWhen Ambron contracts, the Lord Protectorate is the first institution to lose substance. The official may remain in residence for years after the actual Ambronite military presence has withdrawn, presiding over congresses that he can no longer compel anyone to attend. Eventually the position either ceases to exist or becomes a ceremonial title that one of the major lords absorbs as a prestige addition to their own styling. The records of Drentish congress minutes from the late contraction periods contain entries like \"the Lord Protector was unable to attend and sent his compliments\" filed across thirty-odd consecutive years — a monument to the polite fiction that the structure still functions while everyone present acts as if it does not.\n\nWhen the cycle turns and Ambron expands again, the new Protectorate begins with a period of energetic reassertion — old tribute debts renegotiated, roads and harbor facilities that fell into disrepair during the gap brought back to standard, lords who accumulated private power during the free period reminded in material terms of what Ambronite backing costs. This period is uncomfortable. It does not last. Within a generation the arrangement normalizes into the standard extractive equilibrium, and Drent returns to being a productive and loosely governed province that Ambron prefers to having a problem at its eastern approaches.\n\n---\n\n## Economy\n\nThe river valleys produce grain and orchard fruit with the reliability of well-watered fertile soil. The Avrel valley in particular has been called the best agricultural land in northeastern Azhora by sources who may be exaggerating but are not inventing it. Timber from the forested upland between rivers provides raw material for ship construction — Drentish oak and fir are specifically valued for coastal and sea-going work, and the Pebbles pilots' own boats are built from local timber by local craftspeople who have never been surpassed at the specific task of building something that can spend years in the intersection of sea spray, salt wind, and hard use.\n\nThe coastal and river fisheries together make Drent one of the most productive food-producing regions per capita in eastern Azhora. Much of this production moves south through the Iberos Sea trade or north along the coast to the markets of Northern Azhora, rather than inland — the sea route is faster and cheaper than overland travel through the forested upland, and the lords of Drent have historically found that keeping trade maritime keeps their revenues higher and their dependence on Elagosi road infrastructure lower.\n\nThe Stills support a specific trade in the shellfish and estuarine species that the sounds produce in quantities the open coast cannot match. Drent oysters and the specific species of flat-water crab that lives in the Stills' southern reaches are known by name in Solis and Enebreum markets. The Pebble pilots carry these in their own small trading vessels as a sideline, which is one of several commercial activities the pilots conduct that mainland lords are aware of and choose not to involve themselves in.\n\n---\n\n## Character\n\nDrent is not ambitious in the way that Ambron or the Svaleen cities are ambitious. Its ambitions are local — this river-mouth against that one, this lord's tolls against his neighbor's, this congress arrangement versus last generation's congress arrangement — and they are pursued with considerable energy and ingenuity at the local scale without ever quite rising to a scale that would change the region's fundamental situation. This is not a cultural failure. It is a structural condition of terrain and commerce that the lords of Drent inhabit rather than choosing.\n\nThe people of the river valleys and the people of the Pebbles are recognizably from the same general northeastern coastal culture and are specifically very different from each other in the way that people who live on opposite sides of a sound and interact mainly for commercial reasons become different. Mainland Drentish culture is agricultural and feudal and suspicious of the sea in the specific way of people who need the sea for trade but do not trust it for living. Pebble culture is marine and communal and suspicious of the mainland in the specific way of people whose independence depends on a skill that the mainland cannot replace and has periodically tried to control.\n\nWhen Ambron is present, both communities accommodate it in their own way. The lords of Drent negotiate. The Pebble pilots charge.\n\nWhen Ambron is absent, both communities return to their defaults. The lords of Drent congress, fail to agree, and compete. The Pebble pilots pilot, fish, and salvage, and no one asks too carefully about the third part.\n\n---\n\n## Language\n\nDrentish is not Elagosi and it is not the eastern Mittoli of the Moros plain. It is its own thing — related to Elagosi in the way that neighboring peoples who have been in commercial and political contact for centuries tend to develop similarities without becoming the same. An Elagosi speaker and a Drentish speaker can achieve mutual understanding with effort and goodwill; they cannot follow each other's formal proceedings without translation. The Pebble islanders speak a variant of Drentish that has diverged further through relative isolation, and which mainland Drentish speakers describe as fast and folded in on itself — the same vocabulary, but compressed in ways that suggest a community that does not waste words on people who already know what they mean.\n\nAmbronite administrative presence has introduced Elagosi loanwords into the formal and commercial registers of Drentish that persist through the contraction periods. The congress minutes of Drent, regardless of which generation wrote them, contain Elagosi-derived terms for tribute categories, dispute procedures, and property classifications. Like the walls Ambron builds on the Torn and the road surfaces it lays along the Avrel valley, the administrative vocabulary survives the empire that produced it. It is useful; it stays.",
      "loreRef": "drent",
      "climate": "Cfa"
    },
    "Amod": {
      "name": "Amod",
      "color": "#8e44ad",
      "lore": "# Amod\n\nAmod is the country south of the Lotharn Mountains, lying in the long belt of foothills where the old forested range descends into warmer, more open country. It is not a mountain region in the Lotharn sense: the ridges are lower, the valleys wider, the roads more forgiving, and the sky less often hidden by trees. It is also not Pyros, though Pyros lies close enough to shape it. Amod is the slope between them, the place where mountain water, pass trade, old stone, and southern heat meet.\n\nThe name *Amod* is used by outsiders for the country as a whole. Amodians use it too, but with the usual foothill qualification that the whole country is less real than the valley, town, terrace, road, or household one is actually standing in. A person from Amod will call themselves Amodian when speaking to foreigners and then immediately become more specific when speaking to anyone who might understand the distinction.\n\n## Geography\n\nAmod runs along the southern foot of the Lotharn in an uneven crescent. To the north, the land rises into the forested Lotharn slopes. To the south and southeast, the foothills descend gradually into the lake country of Elagos — the transition from terraced hillside to lake-margin flat is one of the great visual moments of northeastern Azhora, visible from the upper Amodian passes as a shining interruption in the distance. To the west, the country opens toward the Mittoli river plains and eventually Pyros. Its western end is greener and more deeply wooded, catching rain that spills around the mountain shoulders. Its eastern end is drier, stonier, and more open, with pale grasses between terrace walls and villages built around springs.\n\nThe land is made of descending ridges. Each ridge throws a valley southward; each valley carries a stream out of the Lotharn; each stream has been made to work. Amodian fields are terraced wherever the slope allows it and irrigated wherever the stream can be persuaded. The terraces are the country's most visible human mark: long stone ribs following contour lines across the hills, patched and rebuilt by the same families for generations.\n\nThe upper foothills are chestnut, oak, beech, and walnut country. Lower down, the slopes carry orchards, vines, pulses, grazing goats, and the small hardy grains that tolerate thin soil and uncertain rain. Amod is not one of Azhora's great bread regions, but it is rarely hungry. It has too many elevations, exposures, and microclimates for a single bad season to ruin everything at once.\n\nThe rivers are small but reliable. They come cold from the Lotharn, gather warmth on the south-facing slopes, and drain in two directions depending on the valley: the western rivers run toward the Mittoli plain network, the eastern and southern rivers feed into the lake system of Elagos. A watershed divide runs through Amod without anyone having formally agreed where it is, which has generated its own category of boundary dispute. This makes water rights the central fact of Amodian law. Land can be inherited, bought, married into, or abandoned. Water must be remembered correctly.\n\n## Climate\n\nAmod has four seasons, but the mountains bend them. Spring starts at the lower terraces while snowmelt is still feeding the upper streams. Summer is hot in the lower country and mild in the upper villages, with afternoon winds that fall from the Lotharn after sunset. Autumn is the Amodian season: dry air, clear ridgelines, chestnut smoke, grape harvest, and the great movement of animals down from the high grazing.\n\nWinter is not harsh by northern standards, but it is complicated. Cold air drains through the valleys at night, frost gathers in pockets, and a village halfway up a slope may be warmer than one below it. Amodian farmers read frost the way sailors read current: by direction, shape, and habit rather than by a number on a calendar.\n\n## Settlements\n\nAmodian towns are built on shoulders of land above valley floors, high enough to avoid flood and low enough to reach water without wasting half a day. They are compact, stone-built, and vertically organized: storage and animals below, households above, drying rooms and work lofts under steep roofs. Streets tend to follow contour rather than compass direction, curving around the hill in ways that confuse lowland visitors and make complete sense to anyone carrying a load.\n\nThe capital, if Amod can be said to have one, is **Mavren**, a terraced town at the meeting of three valleys near the central passes. Mavren is where the valley councils meet, where pass tolls are reconciled, where outside envoys are received, and where disputes large enough to involve more than one watershed are argued until everyone is too tired to continue being elegant. Amodians do not describe Mavren as ruling the country. They describe it as \"where the ledgers meet.\"\n\nOther towns matter in their own domains:\n\n**Kelmod** guards the western road and is known for timber, chestnuts, and mule breeding.\n\n**Sareth-am-Vel** sits above the central south road and controls the best descent from the Lotharn pass inns into Amod.\n\n**Ostel** is the eastern dry-slope town, famous for stonecutters and hard white wine that outsiders pretend to enjoy before learning to actually enjoy it.\n\n**Tir Amel** is less a town than a chain of spring villages sharing one water court. Its legal records are among the most carefully maintained in the country, because a missed spring-right clause can start a feud that outlives the scribe.\n\n## Government\n\nAmod is a country without a single crown. Its governing structure is the **Terrace Compact**, a federation of valley councils, water courts, and pass authorities that has existed in recognizable form for at least eight centuries. The Compact is not democratic in the modern philosophical sense, but it is participatory in the practical Amodian sense: anyone whose field, spring, mill, herd, road, or terrace wall is affected by a decision has standing to be heard, and failing to hear them makes the decision unenforceable in practice.\n\nThe fundamental unit of governance is the **water court**. Every stream system has one. It records rights of diversion, mill time, flood repairs, terrace obligations, and the order in which fields receive water during shortage. The court's memory is partly written and partly embodied in specialists called **measure-keepers**, who know the channels, gates, and seasonal behavior of the stream by inspection. A measure-keeper's testimony can override a written record if the record describes a watercourse that no longer behaves as written.\n\nPass tolls are administered separately by road houses and pass families, many of whom have kin in the Lotharn valleys above them. This gives Amod unusual leverage over trade moving south out of the mountains. The Lotharn communities control the heights; Amod controls the descent, the mule changes, the lower warehouses, and the first markets where mountain goods become lowland goods.\n\n## Economy\n\nAmod sells what slopes produce well: chestnuts, dried fruit, goat cheese, wool, timber, charcoal, wine, medicinal herbs from the upper ridges, and stone worked finely enough to be used in door lintels and irrigation gates throughout the south. Its iron is limited compared with the central Lotharn mines, but Amodian smiths are known for tools rather than weapons: pruning hooks, channel knives, hinge-work, mill fittings, and the small precise metal pieces that keep water gates honest.\n\nThe country is also a passage economy. Goods from the Lotharn and Mittoli interior come down through Amod before turning toward East Pyros, the Plains, or the Iberos routes. Amodians are not grand merchants in the Nyrossi style. They are handlers, measurers, insurers, mule-breeders, warehouse keepers, road cooks, and toll accountants. Their genius is not making a trade route glamorous. Their genius is making it arrive with fewer losses than expected.\n\nThis has made them quietly wealthy. Not visibly wealthy; Amodian architecture spends money on walls, drains, roofs, and springhouses before it spends money on display. But the ledgers are good, the granaries are rarely empty, and an Amodian village that looks plain from the road may have three generations of credit extended across half the eastern trade network.\n\n## Culture\n\nAmodian culture is built around maintenance. Terraces fail if they are not repaired. Channels clog if they are not cleared. Roads wash out if no one watches the culverts. Orchards require pruning before they reward anyone. This produces a people who are suspicious of brilliance when it has no maintenance plan attached.\n\nThe central virtue is **keeping grade**: holding the correct slope, neither too steep nor too flat. Literally, this refers to irrigation channels and roads. Socially, it means proportion, continuity, and the refusal to let urgency ruin a system that must last after the urgent people have gone home. An Amodian compliment that translates poorly is \"they hold grade in weather.\" It means someone can be trusted when conditions change.\n\nHospitality is practical and exact. A guest is fed, dried, placed near heat, and asked where they are going only after the first needs are met. A guest who volunteers to help with an obvious task without being asked is remembered well. A guest who compliments the view while standing next to a broken retaining wall has misunderstood the situation.\n\n## Religion and Custom\n\nAmod shares religious vocabulary with both the Lotharn valleys and Pyros, but its practice is its own. Springs, culverts, old terrace walls, and pass stones receive small offerings at seasonal transitions. These are not usually grand ceremonies. A sprig of herb in a water gate, the first chestnuts left at a spring, a cup of wine poured on a repaired wall: the customs are modest because the relationship is ongoing rather than theatrical.\n\nThe dead are buried above the villages, never below them. This is partly practical — the lower terraces are too valuable — and partly conceptual. Ancestors should be uphill. They look down the watercourse. They see what is being neglected.\n\n## Language\n\nAmodian speech is a Mittoli-derived foothill dialect with heavy Lotharn substrate in place names, water vocabulary, and terrace terms. Pyrosi influence is strongest in the south and east, especially in agricultural and hospitality vocabulary. An Amodian merchant can usually make themselves understood in East Pyros and in the Lotharn pass communities, though both sides will hear the slope in the speech immediately.\n\nThe dialect preserves a set of water-measure words that Standard Mittoli lacks. These words describe not only quantity but behavior: water that cuts, water that seeps, water that can be trusted overnight, water that looks abundant but will vanish into gravel before reaching the lower fields. Lowland administrators translate all of these as \"flow\" and then wonder why Amodian contracts become long.\n\nSeveral scholars have argued that the name *Amod* is not Mittoli. Proposed derivations from Mittoli roots are unconvincing, and Pyrosi explanations fare no better. The oldest Lotharn substrate lists include related forms meaning \"south slope\" or \"held water,\" but the evidence is fragmentary. Amodians have a simpler answer: Amod is what the country is called because that is what it was called when their grandparents learned where the roads went.\n\n## Relations\n\nWith the Lotharn, Amod has kinship, trade, and irritation. Mountain communities think Amodians are lowlanders who talk too much about walls. Amodians think mountain communities are admirable people who would forget half their income if no one downhill wrote it down. The relationship works because both claims are partly true.\n\nWith Elagos, Amod has its most commercially consequential relationship. The lake country below is the primary destination for Amodian exports — chestnuts, wine, stonework, dried fruit, and the pass-refined goods that come down through Mavren and reach the Elagosi lake markets by the southern river routes. The Elagosi lake towns are large enough to absorb Amodian production volumes that no other regional market can, and the return trade — lake fish, lowland grain in poor years, Elagosi metalwork — fills gaps that the hills cannot fill from their own resources. Amod and Elagos have been commercially interlocked long enough that the ledgers in Mavren contain Elagosi-derived accounting forms that arrived through trade and stayed.\n\nAmbron is a political fact that Amod has navigated in most generations rather than resolved. The empire that controls Elagos wants the Lotharn passes — the routes through Amod connect the lake system to the Mittoli interior and to the northern mountains, and whoever holds Elagos finds Amod's cooperation worth purchasing or, periodically, compelling. In the strong imperial periods Amod has paid tribute to Ambron and received the usual imperial package in return: road maintenance, trade protection, military presence that functions as both security and occupation depending on the moment. The Terrace Compact's institutional depth makes Amod harder to directly administer than the Moros plain or even Drent — the water courts, measure-keepers, and pass families all have legitimate authority that Ambronite prefects cannot simply override without breaking the systems they need to function. Ambron has learned this in practice more than once. The characteristic resolution is a negotiated tribute arrangement that leaves the Compact nominally intact, which suits both parties well enough to persist across contraction cycles. Amod does not keep two sets of legal forms the way Drent does; the Terrace Compact is too old and too embedded to be replaced. It simply continues, with or without an Ambronite representative in Mavren, and the presence or absence of that representative is noted and adjusted for.\n\nWith Pyros, to the west, Amod maintains older commercial ties that predate the Elagosi connections. The Pyrosi admire Amodian hospitality and find Amodian councils exhausting. Amodians admire Pyrosi formality and find Pyrosi latent certainty difficult to invoice. Trade between them is real but slower than the Elagosi connection — overland through the Mittoli plain country, with the usual friction of moving goods between highland and lowland economies without a convenient lake network to do it on.\n\nWith the Plains peoples, Amod's relationship is seasonal. Herding groups use the lower foothill grazing in certain years, and the older agreements governing that access are maintained through Amodian water courts and Grassic oral law in parallel. The two systems do not translate cleanly, but they have coexisted for long enough that both sides know which parts must not be simplified.\n\n## Amodian Saying\n\n*A wall remembers every winter.*\n\nSaid when repairing something before it fails; said when refusing a convenient compromise; said, with particular dryness, to young officials from outside who explain that this year's emergency requires ignoring old maintenance obligations.",
      "loreRef": "amod",
      "climate": "Cfa"
    },
    "Feradom": {
      "name": "Feradom",
      "color": "#e91e63",
      "lore": "# Feradom\n\nFeradom is what Drent would be if the terrain gave it the option. The same culture in most of its essentials — decentralized lords, coastal orientation, fishing and timber and commercial pragmatism — but sitting behind a forested ridge line that turns an Ambronite campaign from a negotiation conducted from military dominance into an actual war. The lords of Feradom have refused Ambron before. Some of them have survived it. This is a different kind of knowledge than the Drent lords possess, and it produces a different kind of lord.\n\n---\n\n## Geography\n\nNorth of the Drent coastal plain, the terrain rises abruptly into the **barrier hills** — a forested ridge system running along the country's inland edge, not dramatic in height but dense in tree cover and limited in crossing points. A half-dozen usable passes exist, none of them gentle in autumn and most of them impassable under hard winter snow. The hills are not the Oremindi; a determined force can move through them. What the hills do is constrain that movement to specific corridors where small defending forces have known advantages, which is more than sufficient for the purpose.\n\nNorth of the hills, the coast reasserts itself in a different register from Drent's flat delta shore. Rocky headlands, sheltered inlets, harbors tucked into coves between cliff faces — the first stretches of coast that feel northern rather than Mediterranean. The sea here is colder and the currents less forgiving, and the fishing it supports requires different methods from the channel-work of the Pebbles. Feradom's northern shore faces the open water that leads toward the Bay of Lol approaches; its eastern coast catches the sea as it transitions out of the warmth of the full Iberos reach. The country is shaped like something turning its back on the south, which in certain historical contexts is not merely a geographic description.\n\nThe passes through the barrier hills are Feradom's strategic heart. Each pass capable of carrying an army is held by a fortified position — not always impressive in architecture, but placed with generations of accumulated knowledge about exactly where a small force stops a large one. A tower above the narrows. A castle at the point where the valley behind a pass opens enough to permit a staging defense. Cleared ground in front, walls at the back, and lords who have been thinking about that specific problem since they inherited the title. The pass-lords are the most politically significant figures in Feradom regardless of how small their immediate territories are, because their fortifications are what distinguishes Feradom defensible from Feradom merely defended.\n\n---\n\n## The Lords of Feradom\n\nThe structural tendency of the lordship culture is the same as Drent's — individual river valleys, harbor towns, no natural dominant center, commercial competition substituting for political unity in quiet periods. But the passes add a variable that Drent's coast lacks. A coastal lord who controls a major inlet can be bypassed by land if you're willing to pay for it. A pass-lord who controls the route through the barrier hills cannot be bypassed at all if you need to move a force in or out of Feradom from the south. This gives the pass-lords leverage over every southern campaign, and correspondingly more standing in the councils that the Feradom lords convene when outside pressure requires coordination.\n\nThe councils of Feradom are less frequent than Drent's congresses and more likely to produce decisions when they meet. The subject matter tends to be survival rather than toll disputes, and a council convened because Ambron has mobilized is working from a different premise than a congress convened to renegotiate river-passage fees. Not every council produces useful results — there have been famously unsuccessful ones — but the tradition of councils-that-matter has shaped the Feradom lords into people who understand they need each other in specific circumstances. This is more than the Drent lords have ever consistently internalized.\n\nThe pass-lords' role in these councils is structural: their cooperation is necessary for any collective defense, which means their concerns must be addressed before anything binding can be agreed. A Feradom council that fails to satisfy the pass-lords on the terms of their military contribution tends to produce nominal agreements that dissolve when the actual fighting begins. The lords who have held the passes longest tend to know this and to use it, with varying degrees of grace, to extract terms that their landholdings would not otherwise command.\n\n---\n\n## Ambron's Relationship with Feradom\n\nAmbron wants Feradom when it is strong. The northern harbors give the Elagosi lake fleet access to the coastal routes that hug the northern shore, and the barrier hills' forests produce the best shipbuilding timber available in the northeast. What Ambron must pay for these things in Feradom is different from what it pays in Drent. In Drent, the campaign is a negotiation from a position of overwhelming military advantage — individual lords submit because the calculation is obvious and the terrain offers no alternative. In Feradom, the campaign is a campaign. Forces move through passes against prepared positions, or land on a rocky coast where the defenders have had centuries to identify the good ground, and the Feradom lords have contingency plans for both approaches.\n\nFeradom does fall. The conditions are specific. A divided Feradom — lords at active war with each other, a succession dispute producing factions that genuinely cannot resolve without outside arbitration — can be entered by an Ambronite force that arrives as an arbiter and departs as an overlord. This is the most reliable mechanism, and the Feradom lords know it well enough that a lord seen opening communication with Ambron during a domestic dispute is making an announcement. The rest of the lords notice. The resulting social pressure has aborted several potential Ambronite interventions at the communication stage, which is not guaranteed but is more effective than it sounds.\n\nWhen Ambron does hold Feradom, the occupation requires more permanent military presence than Drent demands. The same passes used to enter are the passes through which a resurgent Feradom can cut supply lines in winter. An Ambronite garrison in a northern harbor town that cannot be relieved overland between the first snowfall and the spring thaw knows exactly what its situation is. The occupation is sustainable when Ambron is attentive and provisioned; it begins to deteriorate faster than the Drent occupation when Ambron's attention shifts. Feradom is typically among the first territories recovered in the contraction phase of an Ambronite empire, not because resistance is heroic but because the logistics of maintaining it are the first to become untenable.\n\nWhether Feradom is currently subject to Ambron is consequently one of the reliable indicators of how the empire is doing. A strong Ambron holds Drent and Feradom both. A middling Ambron holds Drent and negotiates with Feradom. A contracting Ambron loses Feradom first and holds Drent longest, because Drent requires less effort to keep. The Feradom lords have absorbed this pattern into their political self-understanding: their independence is not merely a matter of pride but a barometer reading.\n\n---\n\n## Economy\n\nTimber is the primary export. The barrier hills' forests are old-growth in their upper reaches and actively managed on their lower slopes — the Feradom lord families have been harvesting and replanting specific forest sections for generations, not from environmental philosophy but from the practical understanding that a lord whose hills are stripped of useful timber has lost half his military advantage along with his export income. The oak and fir that come out of the Feradom hills are specifically sought by Elagosi and Drent shipbuilders for construction that must hold in northern-sea conditions. The timber trade flows regardless of political arrangements; even during Ambronite occupation, the timber moves south through the passes and the prices are negotiated between people who all want the transaction to happen.\n\nThe fishing is deepwater work — different species from Drent's channel and estuary catches, requiring larger vessels and longer voyages out past the rocky headlands into the colder reaches. Feradom fishers have historically gone further north than any other Azhoran fishing fleet for which records exist, following specific migratory species into the cold water that other fleets regard as peripheral. What they encounter at those latitudes is not extensively documented outside of the harbor communities that have been doing it for generations.\n\nAgriculture in the river valleys is productive enough to feed the country in most years, limited by the hills that break the landscape into disconnected pockets. Feradom imports grain from Moros and Drent in poor years and exports modest surplus in good ones. It has never been a grain power and does not expect to be.\n\n---\n\n## Character\n\nThe Feradom lords carry a specific kind of self-regard that the Drent lords cannot quite share. The genealogy of resistance — particular lords who refused Ambron at particular moments and survived the refusal — functions as a living standard of reference in Feradom's aristocratic culture. Current lords are measured against it, sometimes by themselves and always by each other. This does not produce recklessness; the Feradom councils are capable of choosing accommodation when accommodation is the right calculation. What it produces is a framework in which the calculation occurs differently than it does in Drent.\n\nA Drent lord asks: what does submission to Ambron cost me, and is that less than the cost of resistance? A Feradom lord asks that question and also a second one: what does this decision make me? Both questions are real. Only one of them appears in Drent political deliberations with any regularity.\n\nThe coastal communities of Feradom share a variant of this character at a different social register — not aristocratic honor but maritime independence, the self-reliance of communities that work rougher seas further from port in conditions that require their own competence and cannot be delegated. The harbor towns of Feradom are harder places than Drent's estuary ports in the specific way of places where the sea is less forgiving. The people are not unfriendly; they are precisely as friendly as they have reason to be, which in good conditions is genuinely warm and in ambiguous conditions is watchful in ways that strangers without context sometimes misread as hostility.\n\nThe relationship between the lordship culture and the maritime communities is not frictionless — lords and harbor towns have the usual disagreements about tolls, obligations, and military service — but it rests on a shared premise that Drent's equivalent relationship partially lacks: both know that if things go badly enough, they will need each other in ways that cannot be replaced by outside assistance. The hills close off Feradom from easy relief. Whatever happens here is settled largely by the people here. This is, depending on the situation, either a burden or a foundation.\n\n---\n\n## Language\n\nRelated to Drentish in the way of long-separated cousins — recognizably from the same source, significantly diverged. The -dom of Feradom is the same root as the Drentish word for a lordly holding or domain, which may mean the name derives from the same political concept that organizes both cultures: Feradom as \"the domain country,\" the territory of the domains. The specific terrain vocabulary diverges considerably — Feradom has a rich vocabulary for passes, ridge terrain, and the specific conditions of northern sea approaches that Drentish does not need and does not have. The two vocabularies for sea conditions are largely different, reflecting different waters.\n\nElagosi loanwords appear in the formal registers, as they do in Drent, but fewer of them — Ambronite occupation has been shorter and less continuous in Feradom, which means the administrative vocabulary arrived in smaller doses and some of it did not take root. A Feradom legal document from a strong Ambronite occupation period looks substantially like its Drent equivalent. A Feradom legal document from a period of independence looks more purely Drentish-derived, which is its own kind of record.",
      "loreRef": "feradom",
      "climate": "Csb"
    },
