    "Yunethre": {
      "name": "Yunethre",
      "color": "#5e35b1",
      "lore": "# Yunethre\n\nFrom the high ground at the center of Yunethre — the spinal ridge that the old road follows from north to south — both ranges are visible at once. To the west, the Oremindi. To the east, the Lotharn. Two mountain systems that have nothing to do with each other geologically, one young and catastrophic in scale, one ancient and settled into its final form, facing each other across a corridor that by the logic of the continent should not exist. The Oremindi run north to south along the western spine. The Lotharn runs roughly east to west across the south of the plains. They do not meet. The gap between their nearest approaches is Yunethre, and every army, caravan, migration, and great commercial enterprise that has needed to move between the Mittolo heartland and the south has passed through it.\n\nBeing the gate between two mountain ranges is a specific kind of situation. It is not the same as being between two armies, though that has also happened here many times. It means that the terrain itself is on your side in a way that has nothing to do with your military power — not because you can defend from above, as pass-lords defend, but because you are the gap, and the only alternative to dealing with you is crossing one of the two flanking ranges, and neither range invites crossing. The Oremindi are what they are. The Lotharn's passes are possible but deliberate. The powers that have come to take Yunethre have, in most cases, already demonstrated that they understand geography by arriving at all.\n\nYunethre knows this about itself with the complete equanimity of a country that has had centuries to become accustomed to being strategically necessary.\n\n---\n\n## Geography\n\nThe corridor is not flat. The plain country of Celder to the north does not extend into Yunethre; the terrain here has been roughened by its proximity to two mountain systems, the foothills of both pressing inward from opposite sides and meeting in the middle in a landscape of short ridges, cupped valleys, and blunt hills that give the country its visual character. It is not dramatic terrain — neither range is present as peaks here, only as the rising ground at each edge — but it is terrain with opinions, the kind that makes roads wind and makes the traveler always slightly above or below where they expect to be.\n\nThe Lizeem has its upper reaches in Yunethre's southwestern corner, fed by the meltwater of the Oremindi's eastern slopes. Here the river is not what it becomes in Celder or Mithala: it is fast and cold, running in a defined channel with enough gradient to have cut notches in the valley floors, clear in the way that young mountain rivers are clear. The tributaries that feed it from the Lotharn's western face add their own volume and character. This is the Lizeem before it becomes the Lizeem — before the spreading and slowing that makes the lower river what the lower river is — and the communities along its upper reaches in Yunethre know it in this earlier form.\n\nThe southern boundary is the Lizeem itself and, beyond it, the Ibenwood. The forest does not press into Yunethre; the river marks the line with a clarity that is unusual in Azhoran geography. The Ibenwood's edge begins on the river's southern bank in the lower reaches of the corridor, and the quality difference between standing in Yunethre and standing at the Ibenwood's margin is the difference between a country you understand and a threshold you think carefully about crossing. The northern Yunethran communities use the river for everything that requires the south — trade with the forest-edge communities, the particular goods that come out of the Ibenwood's accessible margins — but they do not cross casually.\n\nThe eastern edge of the corridor, where the foothills of the Lotharn's western end press in, is the less traveled and less governed side of Yunethre. The Lotharn's valley communities extend their influence west along the lower passes into the corridor's eastern margin; there are Yunethran communities in the eastern foothills that speak Lotharn-derived dialects and maintain cultural affiliations more Lotharn than corridor, and the boundary between Yunethre and the Lotharn's western reaches is negotiated in practice rather than established in principle.\n\n---\n\n## The Narrows\n\nThe corridor has a narrowest point. Between the Oremindi's southeastern toe and the Lotharn's closest westward extension, the corridor closes to a distance of perhaps twelve miles of traversable ground — flanked by difficult foothills on both sides, centered on a valley through which the old road has run since the earliest period of settled use. This is the Narrows: not a pass in the mountain sense, not a dramatic constriction, but a defined chokepoint that anyone moving a significant force through Yunethre must use or pay heavily to avoid.\n\nThe city of **Vaundrel** sits at the Narrows. It is not a beautiful city and it does not aspire to be. It is a fortified city, built across the valley floor at the narrowest point, with a citadel on the ridge above that commands the road and the walls that close the valley between the ridge faces on either side. The architecture is accumulated defense — each period of Vaundrel's history visible in the different building styles of its walls, older stone courses below, newer extensions above, the whole record of who held Vaundrel and what they were afraid of visible in the layers. The city changes hands; the walls remain and the new holders repair what needs repairing and add what they find necessary. This process has continued for a very long time.\n\nBelow the citadel, Vaundrel is a road city. The main street is the road, widened and stabilized, flanked by the commercial infrastructure that passage requires: inns, stables, warehouses, exchange houses, the offices of the toll authority, the headquarters of the various brokers and agents who make their livings managing what moves through the Narrows. The city smells like horses and stored grain and the specific mix of cooking traditions that a transit city produces when travelers from a dozen directions have been eating in its establishments for generations. It is comfortable in the way that a place built to be used is comfortable — not refined, not memorable for its own character, but equipped for whatever you need when you arrive.\n\nThe toll authority at Vaundrel is the oldest continuously functioning institution in Yunethre. Its records, maintained in the archive that occupies one of the older sections of the citadel, are the best documentation of Azhoran north-south trade that exists anywhere — a meticulous century-by-century account of what moved through the Narrows, in what volumes, at what prices, from which direction. Scholars who have obtained access to the archive describe it as the continent's commercial spine made visible. The toll authority describes it as their ledger and requests that visitors not remove pages.\n\n---\n\n## Governance\n\nYunethre is governed by whoever controls Vaundrel — in practice, the lord of the Narrows, who holds the citadel and the toll authority and therefore holds the gate. The title has varied across Yunethre's history: gate-lord, high warden, lord of the corridor, bridge-count during one period when a significant river crossing was the revenue source, simply \"the Vaundrel\" during a period when the title became the name. The current form is **Gate Lord**, which has been stable for approximately three centuries and is generally understood to mean whoever controls the toll and the citadel and can back that control with enough military presence to discourage challenges.\n\nThe Gate Lord is not an absolute ruler of Yunethre. The communities of the corridor valleys — particularly in the eastern foothills, where Lotharn cultural affiliations give them an alternative orientation — have historically maintained considerable practical autonomy, contributing to the Gate Lord's authority mainly by not actively contesting it. The western communities along the upper Lizeem are more directly under Vaundrel's administration, because the river roads are easier to police and because the commercial traffic of the corridor passes through their territory and makes their cooperation worth maintaining. The eastern communities are governed by negotiation and tradition rather than by enforcement, and the Gate Lords who have tried to change this have found the cost of changing it higher than the benefit.\n\nThe external powers that have held Vaundrel and called themselves lord of Yunethre have generally found this local distribution of authority more persistent than expected. Controlling the Narrows means controlling the toll income and the military position. It does not mean that the valley communities change their language, their land management practices, or their effective self-governance. Several powers that have held Vaundrel for extended periods have eventually reached the same pragmatic conclusion that local institutions reached long before them: the corridor is manageable, and the corridor's communities are not the same problem as the corridor's choke point.\n\n---\n\n## History\n\nThe history of Yunethre is largely a list of who held the Narrows and for how long, interspersed with the periods when no one held it completely, which the corridor communities tend to describe as the normal condition and outside powers tend to describe as disorder. The list is long, and several of the names on it are names that appear elsewhere in Azhoran history for other reasons — the powers that wanted the gate have generally been the powers that mattered.\n\nThe pattern is consistent enough to be almost mechanical: a strong outside power takes Vaundrel, collects the toll income, garrisons the citadel, and administers the corridor as a revenue source; the power contracts or is succeeded or overextends elsewhere; the Gate Lord position reverts to local control; the local authorities that never stopped functioning reassert themselves formally; a generation passes; another outside power arrives. The Vaundrel archive reflects this pattern in its varying administrative formats — the record-keeping systems change with each change of controlling authority, and then revert to the local form during the intervals, and then change again. Archivists have used this stylistic variation to date disputed entries more reliably than the entries' own internal evidence would permit.\n\nWhat the corridor communities have preserved across all of these transitions is the toll authority itself. Every power that has held the Narrows has understood that the toll is why the gate matters, and has therefore maintained the infrastructure that makes the toll collectable. The archive, the weigh stations, the measurement standards, the dispute resolution procedures that traders rely on — these have survived every transfer of power because every holder of power needed them to survive. The toll authority is therefore the oldest thing in Yunethre, older than any political structure that has claimed it, and its institutional memory is correspondingly deep.\n\nThe one category of holding power that Yunethre has consistently resisted is permanent absorption. The corridor communities that would need to stop being corridor communities and become provincial subjects of a distant center have never fully made this transition. They cooperate with the authorities that hold the gate; they do not become those authorities' people in the sense of losing their own orientation. Travelers who have passed through Yunethre under different administrations and returned decades later remark on this consistency. The language changes slightly in the educated registers as the prestige dialect shifts with the controlling power. The life of the valley communities continues in recognizable form regardless.\n\n---\n\n## The Ibenwood Relationship\n\nSouth of the Lizeem, on the far bank from Yunethre's lower communities, the Ibenwood begins. This proximity has shaped Yunethre's southern edge in ways that are not always legible from the north. The communities along the river's southern bank in Yunethre's lower section — those with the water between them and the forest rather than with the forest behind them — maintain the relationship with the Ibenwood's edge that such proximity requires: careful, specific, structured around what the edge of the Iben offers and what it demands.\n\nThe Ibenwood's accessible margins provide products that enter Yunethre's commercial traffic and pass north through the corridor into the Mittolo plain economy: specific timbers, specific medicinal and craft materials, certain goods that the forest-edge communities produce from forest products in ways that are not replicable outside the margin zone. These goods move north through the toll stations at Vaundrel and have been accounted in the archive for as long as the archive has been kept. The Ibenwood does not sell its interior; it sells what the edge provides.\n\nThe southern Yunethran communities also serve as the primary point of contact between the Ibenwood's edge peoples and the commercial world to the north. This position is understood on both sides as delicate. The forest-edge communities do not trade with everyone; they trade through the Yunethran communities they have established long relationships with, and those relationships are not transferable to outside commercial agents without their mediation. The outside commercial agents who have attempted to shortcut this arrangement have uniformly found that there is no shortcut. Several of them have found this out in ways that the archive records carefully and does not elaborate on.\n\nWhat the Yunethran gateway communities who work this southern trade share with the Ibenwood communities they work with is a matter of speculation outside the corridor. That they know something the rest of Yunethre does not, and that the rest of Yunethre has a long-standing practice of not pressing them on what it is, is as close to a formal consensus as the corridor has reached on the subject.\n\n---\n\n## Economy\n\nThe toll is primary. The income from the passage of goods and travelers through the Narrows has funded every significant construction in Yunethre, every military campaign to defend the gate, and the comfortable standard of living that the gate-lord households have maintained across the various holding powers. The toll rate schedule maintained by the Vaundrel archive is one of the most sophisticated documents of its kind in Azhoran commercial history — a two-hundred-page calibration of rates by goods category, season, weight, declared destination, and the holder's current political relationships with the major producing and consuming regions. Understanding it requires several years of apprenticeship in the toll authority, which is why the apprenticeship exists.\n\nBelow the toll income, the corridor produces what foothills between two mountain systems produce: sheep on the upper grazing, timber on the managed hillside slopes, some iron and lead ore in the Lotharn-facing eastern margins, and the agricultural output of the valley floors, which are fertile enough where the mountain streams have spread their sediment and sheltered enough in the corridor valleys to be productive without being exceptional. Yunethre does not feed other regions. It feeds itself adequately and concentrates its surplus into the specialized goods that make corridor life work: stabling, provisioning, storage, transport logistics, currency exchange, and the commercial mediation that travelers from many different trade traditions require when they encounter each other at a choke point.\n\n---\n\n## Character\n\nYunethre people know, with the certainty that comes from watching it happen for many generations, that the powers that arrive to hold the gate eventually leave. This knowledge does not produce passivity. It produces a particular kind of patient precision: the ability to cooperate fully with the current authority while maintaining the structures that will persist when the current authority is gone. The valley communities that have outlasted a dozen holding powers do not do this through hidden resistance. They do it by being excellent at the things that every holding power needs them to be excellent at, and by declining to be excellent at things that no holding power has yet found a use for but that are theirs.\n\nThe corridor culture is cosmopolitan in the specific way of transit countries: deeply experienced with outsiders, capable of navigating many different commercial and diplomatic conventions, genuinely curious about what travelers carry (in the intellectual sense as well as the commercial), and thoroughly clear about the line between knowing the world and belonging to any part of it. Yunethran merchants and toll officials can negotiate in multiple commercial traditions, quote precedent from the archives of four different administrations, and produce exactly the documentation that the current holding power's format requires. They cannot be mistaken for the people who wrote those formats. They are recognizable as the people who have been here through all of them.\n\nThe specific virtue that Yunethre's own traditions emphasize — said in a dozen forms, none of them the same, all meaning approximately the same thing — is *knowing what passes and what remains*. The things that pass are armies, administrations, fashions, the priorities of outside powers, and the high prices of specific goods during specific periods. The things that remain are the corridor, the gate, the families that know the valley, and the archive. Making sure you are one of the things that remains rather than one of the things that passes is the fundamental orientation of Yunethran culture. It is not cynical. It is the result of paying very close attention to what the corridor's history actually demonstrates.\n\n---\n\n## Language\n\nYunethran speech is a Mittoli-derived dialect with a substrate layer that the Academy's linguistic division classifies alongside the Mithala and Canerd pre-Mittoli names: the same older phonological system, appearing most densely in the landscape terms — valley names, ridge names, the specific words for different kinds of corridor terrain that standard Mittoli must describe in phrases. The name Yunethre itself is from this substrate. The proposed derivations from Mittoli roots are unconvincing, and the form has no satisfying analysis in any Azhoran language family. It is, like Canerd and the old Mithala place names, from whatever was spoken here before Mittoli came.\n\nThe trade register of the corridor is extensive and multilingual in a practical rather than a scholarly sense. Toll officials in Vaundrel work in Standard Mittoli for documentation, in the commercial dialects of the Iberos coast for southbound trade, in the upper-plain Mittoli of Celder for northbound horse and grain traffic, and in a pidgin that has no formal name but that experienced traders recognize as the Narrows trade language — a functional composite that has been developing at the choke point for long enough to have grammar of its own. Outside linguists find the Narrows trade language interesting. Toll officials find it useful.\n\nThe one vocabulary area where Yunethran dialect is most specifically developed is the categorization of passage: the different types of movement through the corridor, the different reasons for it, the different relationships that different travelers have to the gate. There are words for the traveler who has been through before and is returning, for the one who passed through ten years ago and is different now, for the merchant in transit versus the merchant looking for a position, for the army that is moving to somewhere versus the army that is retreating from somewhere. These distinctions are visible in the archive's record-keeping and are commercially practical. They are also, corridor people will note if pressed, accurate. You can tell. Once you've stood at the gate long enough, you can tell.",
      "loreRef": "yunethre",
      "climate": "Dfa"
    },
    "West Endevor": {
      "name": "West Endevor",
      "color": "#f06292",
      "lore": "# Endevor\n\nNorthwest of the Acorwood, where the Oremindi's northern terminus descends into lower ground and the air begins to carry the cold salt weight of the bay, the hills of Endevor fill the space between things. Not quite the upland — the Ganoss plateau is harder and higher, to the north. Not quite the coast — the Bay of Lol is a day's descent to the northwest. Not quite the mountain country, though the Oremindi makes the southern skyline and certain clear mornings let the peaks show through. Endevor is the hilly country in between, and it has organized itself around that position for as long as anyone has recorded it.\n\nThe name has no agreed derivation. The leading proposal in Academy language files is that *Endevor* is a contracted Northern Mittoli compound, perhaps *ond* (broad water) + *vel* (return) + an archaic locative marker — \"the place where the water turns back,\" referring to the watershed ridge where the hill streams divide between draining northwest toward the bay and north toward the Ganoss. A minority view holds that the name predates Northern Mittoli entirely and that the Mittoli speakers who arrived here simply kept the word that was already there. Both camps cite the same evidence in their favor, which the Academy has noted without resolving.\n\nThe outside world, if it thinks of Endevor at all, thinks of fell-sheep wool and reliable inns and the road toward the Bay of Lol. It does not think of the barrows on the ridgelines, which are older than the wool trade, and more numerous, and full of kings.\n\n---\n\n## Geography\n\nThe Endevor hills are old, rounded, and wet. They do not have the dramatic vertical of the Oremindi or the bleak exposed sweep of the Ganoss uplands; they are the kind of hills that fold into one another, valley after valley, each with its own stream, each valley floor broad enough for a strip of farmland before the slope comes up again. The highest ground is along the eastern ridge — the long slope that faces the Acorwood — which catches the worst of the weather coming in from the north and west and wrings it out before the clouds continue inland.\n\nThe result is that Endevor is wet without being waterlogged. The streams are reliable and numerous. The valley floors are damp and dark-soiled. The upper slopes are peat-moorland and short-grassed heath, suitable for grazing and not much else. The lower slopes are where most of Endevor's population lives, in the long ribbon settlements that follow the valley floors between the farmland and the hill pasture above.\n\nThe eastern ridge is wooded on its lower face — a mix of native northern species, hardy and not especially tall, transitioning as you approach the Acorwood's margin into something denser and older. Exactly where Endevor's edge-forest ends and the Acorwood begins is not a line that appears on any map; the local communities know it by feel and by the quality of the light, which changes at a threshold that most people can locate within a few hundred yards but cannot explain as a specific visible marker.\n\nThe Oremindi's northern end shapes the southern horizon and does more than frame the view. The meltwater coming off the range's northernmost snowfields feeds several of Endevor's main rivers, making them flashier in spring than their size would suggest and keeping them running in the driest summers. The passes through the Oremindi's northern extension are few and severe — harder and more seasonal than those further south — and are used primarily by the Sevron contact route and by occasional extraordinary travelers with specific purpose. Endevor has never been a mountain-crossing gateway in the way that the southern Oremindi communities are; the passes behind it are not worth the effort for ordinary commerce.\n\n---\n\n## The People\n\nEndevor is pastoral first. The hill communities keep sheep on the upper slopes and cattle in the valley bottoms, and have done this long enough that the organization of the land around those two requirements is baked into everything else: the placement of settlements, the timing of the year, the structure of disputes, the vocabulary of status. A family's standing in any Endevor valley is legible from its position on the hill — how far up the slope their summer grazing runs, how many animals winter in their valley barns, and whether the barns themselves are stone or timber, which says something about how long the family has been in that particular valley.\n\nThe characteristic animal of the Endevor hills is the **fell-sheep** — a breed whose name is a Northern Mittoli word meaning roughly \"the hill-animal\" or \"the high-pasture one,\" and which has been in these hills long enough that no records mention its arrival. The fell-sheep is not impressive beside the great animals of other regions. It is compact, short-legged, and deeply shaggy, with a fleece that produces more lanolin than any comparable breed — a natural oil that makes the wool water-resistant in the fleece and almost waterproof once spun and prepared. In a climate that is wet for most of the year, this matters. Endevor wool is traded throughout northern Azhora and down the coast road specifically because it performs in conditions that other wool cannot. The Bay of Lol fishing communities buy it for sea gear. The Ganoss upland clans have their own animals but trade for Endevor spun goods when they can get them. The Orched Hills mining settlements are regular purchasers of Endevor wool in preference to anything grown closer.\n\nThe fell-sheep also live their entire year outside, which is not true of most northern livestock. They know the upper hills the way the hills know themselves, and they will come down of their own accord before weather turns that their shepherds have not yet noticed. Endevor farmers pay attention to this. Where the sheep are on a given morning is information about the day.\n\nThe social unit of Endevor is the valley community — a loose collection of households sharing a river system, with informal but understood obligations around water rights, common hill access, and dispute resolution. Disputes between valleys are resolved at **stone-shelters** — low circular enclosures of dry-laid stone set at the ridgelines between valleys, old enough that no one remembers who built them, used as neutral meeting ground when valley communities need to negotiate. The stone-shelters are not impressive structures; they are simply very old and unmoved, which in Endevor is a form of authority. A resolution reached at a stone-shelter has a different weight from one reached anywhere else, because the shelter has been the resolution site before and will be again, and its walls have heard the settlement's terms and will keep them the same way they keep the wind off.\n\nThe shelters have one specific physical property that the Vornheld tradition says is why they were placed where they were placed and not anywhere else: speech spoken inside them at ordinary volume is heard simultaneously and with equal clarity by everyone in the circle, regardless of wind. The Academy attributes this to circular construction and the reflection properties of dry-laid stone at these specific angles. The Vornheld say this is what the sites were chosen for — that whoever built them tested the acoustics before placing the stone, and selected positions where the ground itself held the sound. A resolution reached inside a stone-shelter is heard by all parties in the same moment. This is not a formality. It is the physical condition that makes the resolution binding. You cannot claim you did not hear it.\n\nThe main market settlement is **Stroven** — positioned at the convergence of the three largest valley routes, where goods from the upper hills and goods coming northwest from the Bay of Lol approaches meet before separating again into the valley network. Stroven is not large; it is consequential because of where it is. The permanent population is several hundred, swelling in trading seasons. It has a covered market structure, a handful of inns accustomed to through-travelers, and the only standing archive in Endevor — a collection of land records and valley boundary agreements maintained by a family of hereditary record-keepers who have been doing this work long enough to hold opinions about disputes from four generations ago and will share those opinions if asked. The record-keepers are not impartial, exactly; they are the memory, and memory has its own loyalties. This is broadly understood and accounted for in how disputes proceed.\n\n---\n\n## The Acorwood's Western Face\n\nEvery Endevor community faces east toward the Acorwood. From the higher ridgelines it is visible as a dark line — darker than the surrounding hill forest, perceptibly different in color and density — that marks the edge of navigable country. The Ganoss upland communities on the Acorwood's northern margin and the Orched Hills settlements to the south have their own longstanding relationship with the forest's accessible edges. Endevor's relationship is with the western face, and it is different in a specific way: the morning sun rises behind the Acorwood, and from Endevor's hills at dawn the forest is lit from within — the edge trees stand in silhouette against the sky while the interior remains dark, the margin visible as a precise line between what is and is not entered. This is the Acorwood as Endevor knows it.\n\nThe edge timber harvest here is modest. The accessible trees on the Acorwood's northwestern fringe are similar in character to the edge timber on the northern and southern margins — large hardwoods that can be taken from the outer tree lines without going deep — but the Endevor communities have never developed a major edge-timber trade. The Bay of Lol coastal communities do not require much inland timber, having their own supplies from the coastal strips. The Ganoss use the northern margins. Endevor's edge harvest is primarily for local use: charcoal, building timber, occasional mast in good years.\n\nWhat the Endevor communities have developed, across many generations, is a practice of **smoke-reading**.\n\nThe Acorwood occasionally produces smoke from its interior. The Orched Hills records note this with uncertainty and do not follow it up. The Ganoss communities acknowledge it and have made their peace with not explaining it. Endevor has spent enough seasons watching the western and northwestern face — the face that catches the afternoon light, visible from the high sheepwalks for hours at a time — that their communities have accumulated an unusual amount of observational detail.\n\nThe fell-sheep shepherds in the high pastures are positioned to watch the Acorwood's margin across long hours, with nothing pressing them to look elsewhere. Across generations, this has produced a tradition of noting, without explaining, what the smoke does. There is vocabulary for it in the Endevor dialect — words that do not appear in other Northern Mittoli varieties — for the smoke that rises thin and straight in still air, the smoke that spreads at canopy height and moves with the wind before dispersing, and the specific, acknowledged category of smoke that appears with no wind and dissipates downward rather than upward, which the Endevor fell-sheep shepherds call by a substrate word that translates into Standard Mittoli as something between \"breathing-down\" and \"the forest taking something in.\"\n\nThe smoke-reading tradition is practical in two uses that have been confirmed across several generations. First: smoke from the Acorwood interior in late summer correlates imperfectly but consistently with good mast years; the shepherds who track this will predict mast yield before the edge trees show it in their growth. Second: the quality of smoke — color, height, behavior — appears to predict weather shifts in the way that cloud formations over the Oremindi also predict weather shifts, and is used the same way. Neither use requires an explanation of what the smoke is. The Endevor communities have not produced one.\n\nWhen Academy scholars have visited Stroven and asked about the smoke, they receive accurate and detailed information about what the smoke does and what it predicts, and no information about what causes it. The question of cause is not treated as something the Endevor communities are withholding. It is treated as a question that belongs to a different kind of inquiry than the one they practice. The smoke behaves; they read it; that is the relationship; cause is for someone with more time and less sheep.\n\n---\n\n## History\n\nThe pastoral Endevor is recent, as these things go.\n\nFor several centuries before the current era, Endevor was the center of the most extensive political authority in northern Azhora. The source of that authority was gold — placer gold in the eastern hill streams, and beneath it the deep vein gold that the streams had been eroding for thousands of years before anyone thought to follow them uphill. When the Endevor mine-kings found those veins, the northern world reorganized around them.\n\nAt its height the Endevor realm extended further north than the Bay of Lol, incorporating the coastal port communities and the Ganoss upland clans as tributaries. It extended south through the Orched Hills and, in its largest period, pressed claims as far as the northern Mittolo margins — the first and last time a northern power came close to bridging the cultural divide that the Acorwood and the Orched Hills maintain between north and south. The mine-kings did not rule these territories as the later Mittoli city-states governed their regions, through administrators and written law; they held them through the distribution of gold, the weight of tribute obligation, and the personal prestige of a line that had been producing wealth long enough for wealth and legitimacy to become indistinguishable. The Ganoss clans supplied livestock and manpower. The Bay of Lol communities supplied ships and navigators. The Orched Hills supplied iron and copper. The gold at the center moved all of it.\n\nFor roughly two hundred and fifty years at the empire's peak — the period the Vornheld poetry calls the *Eld-Gold*, the time of gold — the mine-kings of Endevor were the most significant power in northern Azhora. Their burials are still in the hills. The ridgelines are scattered with them.\n\nThe tradition names thirty-eight kings in the mine-king period. Not all are equally present in the *Eld-Keth* — the earliest are named briefly, the middle kings described at length, the late-period kings compressed as the tradition seems to contract around the decline. Three stand apart.\n\n**Keld** is the first-opener: the king who followed the stream gold uphill and found the vein. His *Eld-Keth* naming-poem is among the shortest in the body — he is recorded as a beginning rather than an achievement, and the tradition treats him accordingly, with respect but without ornamentation. A king who opens something gets the short poem. The gold that follows him speaks for itself.\n\n**Orrven** is the peak. His naming-poem is the longest in the *Eld-Keth* body and is structured differently from all others: a call-and-answer form in which the first voice names what Orrven gave and the second names what was returned, eight exchanges moving from the Bay of Lol communities to the Ganoss clans to the Orched Hills to what the tradition calls simply \"the deep ground.\" The effect, which Endevor audiences hear differently from how outside scholars read it, is of weight in motion — a redistribution so large that the land itself leaned under it. A Vornheld practitioner, when pressed by an Academy visitor to summarize Orrven's significance, said: \"His word was the weight gold makes before anyone counts it.\" The Academy visitor wrote this down, which the Vornheld probably anticipated.\n\n**Helm** is the surveyor — the king who sent Endevor's only recorded expedition into the Acorwood's interior, in the middle-late period when the eastern veins were thinning and the tradition of looking further was still recent enough to seem reasonable. Helm had observed, from fell-sheep shepherds watching the Acorwood's western face, that the breathing-down smoke concentrated most reliably above the Deepworks area. He concluded, or commissioned the conclusion, that the smoke was the Acorwood's surface indication of something below: an extension of the same hydrothermal system that had produced the vein gold, deeper and further east. Seventeen men entered the Acorwood's northwestern margin. Twelve returned. The five who did not are recorded by name in the *Eld-Keth*; that poem is recited at their stone at the barrow dedication and has not been offered at a public Stroven gathering in anyone's living memory.\n\nThe twelve who came back brought a word: *vel-keth-eld*. The Vornheld have not reached consensus on its meaning in the three or four generations since the expedition. Two translations circulate within the tradition: \"the forest's memory of fire\" and \"what the dark knows that we left behind.\" The first suggests the Acorwood has a relationship to the ancient hydrothermal period — that the forest grew in or around or from the same geological event that made the gold. The second suggests that whatever the expedition encountered inside remembered the mine-kings in some form. Helm's own interpretation was not recorded. He did not seal the Deepworks — that was Veld, a generation later — but he did not send another expedition.\n\nThe mines did not run out all at once. That was the particular difficulty.\n\nThe gold declined over several generations, each one producing less than the last, each one also the one that the current king believed was the bottom before a new vein would open. The shaft-heads went deeper. The spoil heaps grew. The streams were panned harder. The veins were followed further into the Oremindi foothills, into ground that was more expensive and more dangerous, and which produced less. The gold was finite because it had been deposited by a period of ancient hydrothermal activity in the range that had long since ended; there was no new gold forming. What was there was what had been there, and when it was taken, the taking was over.\n\nThe kings who presided over the decline maintained their tributary relationships through prestige long after the gold couldn't sustain them economically. This worked while the living memory of abundance lasted. When the generation that had known the peak died out and the next knew only the decline, the calculations changed. The Ganoss clans stopped sending cattle and said they were using them at home. The Bay of Lol communities began trading directly with the Orched Hills, bypassing the tribute routing. The Orched Hills settlements, which had their own ore wealth and their own long memories, assessed the situation and stopped paying.\n\nThe last king of the Endevor realm was named Veld — a Northern Mittoli name meaning simply \"return,\" which was understood at the time as a statement of ambition and in retrospect reads as something else. He held the realm together for eighteen years through the force of his personal authority and is described in the Vornheld tradition as the greatest of the later kings, which is not exactly praise: the greatest of the later kings could not undo what the geology had done. Veld died in his valley, and after him, the tributaries did not return.\n\nWhat followed was not collapse in the dramatic sense. There were no invasions, no great battles for succession, no famine. The population stayed. The valley communities kept their land. The stone-shelters kept their function. What changed was scope: Endevor contracted back to Endevor, and the wider world that had organized around its gold reorganized around other things, and eventually forgot there had been an organizing.\n\nThe raiding period that the northern Azhoran regional record describes as coming after this was partly a consequence: without the empire's redistributive economy, the coastal communities went back to taking by force what they had previously received through the gold-network. The Endevor hills were the logistical hinterland of that raiding culture's staging, not its origin. The mine-kings' empire was before the raiding; the raiding filled the vacuum after.\n\nBy the time the north's transition to trade began, Endevor had been quiet pastoral hill country for long enough that neither the coastal communities nor the Orched Hills settlements remembered what it had been. No Mittoli scholar wrote down the Endevor empire while it existed; the region was too far outside the Mittoli world for systematic record-keeping. The Vornheld tradition preserved everything, and the Vornheld tradition is oral, in archaic Northern Mittoli, inaccessible to outsiders who do not seek it out.\n\nThose who seek it out and can read what they find tend not to believe it — the scale is too large, the gold too much, the empire too extensive, for a country presently famous for waterproof sheep. This is a known difficulty with oral traditions from regions without outside corroboration. The barrows corroborate. The shaft-heads corroborate. Most outside visitors see hills and do not read them as texts.\n\n---\n\n## The Mines\n\nThe evidence for the Endevor gold is still in the hills if you know where to look.\n\nThe eastern hill streams — those draining toward the Acorwood's margin, fed by Oremindi snowfields — carry trace gold even now, at concentrations too low to bother with but detectable to anyone who knows what color to watch for in the pan. These are the runoff from the same deposits that built the empire: the deep vein gold in the Oremindi foothills, which the ancient hydrothermal activity of the range concentrated and which the long slow erosion of the hills has been dispersing into the streams for longer than anyone has been here to observe. At the mining peak, those concentrations were high enough that stream-panning alone produced meaningful yield. They have been diluting since the veins were broken open, and what remains is memory rather than resource.\n\nThe shaft-heads are more visible than the streams. The eastern faces of the three valley systems nearest the Acorwood — which the Vornheld tradition calls the **Eld-Valleys** — are marked with collapsed mine entrances at irregular intervals from near the valley floor to well above the current sheepwalks. Some are obvious: squared stonework around a shaft-mouth, subsidence depressions where fill has settled over generations. Others are subtle enough that a new arrival walking the sheep paths will occasionally put a foot through the turf into the edge of old roof-stone. The communities closest to the Eld-Valleys know where the dangerous ground is and avoid it without drama; it is simply part of the landscape.\n\nThe Vornheld tradition preserves names for seventeen distinct mine-workings, of which fourteen can be approximately located on the eastern ridge. The other three — the poems call them the **Deepworks** — are either collapsed without surface sign or are in the transition zone between the Endevor hills and the Acorwood's northwestern margin, where the Endevor communities do not regularly go. Whether the Deepworks still have accessible chambers is not known. The tradition says they were the richest of the veins, opened last and exhausted fastest, and sealed by Veld's order when the workers kept not returning from the deeper levels. The sealing instructions and the reason for the non-returns are subjects the Vornheld will discuss at different lengths depending on who is asking and what they appear to intend.\n\nSilver was mined alongside gold in the middle period — a secondary ore from the same hydrothermal deposits, found at greater depth once the gold concentrations thinned. Silver mining continued for two or three generations after gold effectively ended, providing enough revenue to maintain something that looked, from the outside, like a functioning economy while the empire's political structure dissolved. The last silver was exhausted within living memory of the decline's final phase, and the silver shafts are visible in the northern valley systems, shallower and less numerous than the gold workings.\n\nThe Deepworks have a specific entry in the sealing record preserved by the Vornheld: Veld's decree is noted in archaic Northern Mittoli as *oss nil-vel-keth* — \"mark: no return-speech\" — a designation the tradition treats as distinct from ordinary closure. Other shafts were sealed when they were worked out. The Deepworks were closed *nil-vel-keth*, without the response that would indicate emptiness. The formulation implies something was there to not-answer.\n\nThe mine-worker tradition, preserved fragmentarily in the *Eld-Keth* naming-poems for the last men to work the Deepworks, refers to a phenomenon called *llos-eld* — roughly \"gold-voice,\" though \"the ore that speaks\" is the translation Vornheld practitioners prefer when they feel precise. The *llos-eld* was a resonance audible in the rock at the deepest working levels: described variously as a low vibration, a harmonic in the stone that had no identifiable external source, a sound that seemed to originate not from above or below but from the rock around the worker. Experienced mine-foremen tracked it the way fell-sheep shepherds track the Acorwood's smoke — as information whose practical use was clear and whose underlying meaning was not. High *llos-eld* correlated with structural stability in the workings. The specific signature recorded in the Deepworks naming-poems is different from the stable reading, but what it was different toward is the thing the *Eld-Keth* does not say directly.\n\nWhether the *llos-eld* and the Acorwood's breathing-down smoke are related is a question the mine-workers and the shepherds never appear to have addressed together — the workers were underground and the shepherds were watching the forest above, and their separate communities had different relationships to the relevant facts. What the Vornheld tradition notes, without drawing a conclusion, is that the breathing-down smoke is most frequently reported by Endevor shepherds above the ground that corresponds to the Deepworks' approximate location. The observation is in the tradition. No resolution has been attached to it that the tradition records.\n\nThe mines are not worked. No new vein has been found in eight generations of looking, which has not entirely stopped people from looking. There are people in every generation of Endevor history who believe the Deepworks are not exhausted — that Veld sealed something rather than nothing, that the gold is still in the ground and waiting. The Vornheld have opinions about this. They express them when asked and reserve their most specific opinions for those who have already decided to listen.\n\n---\n\n## The Barrows\n\nThe ridgelines of Endevor are not bare. They are covered with sheep and with the dead.\n\nThe barrow-building tradition spans the full mine-king period and reads in the landscape as a slow record of decline. The oldest barrows are the highest — the first mine-kings claimed the skyline, and the mounds they raised are the largest, some visible for miles in clear weather, their grass-covered profiles the most distinctive feature of Endevor's upper silhouette. The Vornheld tradition describes these burials with objects that make outside scholars careful about skepticism: gold plate, gold ornament, ceremonial vessels, weapons edged with gold — the vocabulary of a household that operated at a scale that required gold to describe itself.\n\nThe barrows descend through the centuries and through the hill. Middle-period kings are buried at the ridgeline's lower edge. These barrows are smaller, the grave goods richer in silver and bronze, with gold present but diminished. Then the late-period burials in the upper valley sides, below the crest entirely: modest mounds that a traveler might pass without noticing, holding kings who went into the ground with bronze and the memory of what their fathers had.\n\nVeld's barrow is in the floor of the main Stroven valley — the lowest position of any king in the Vornheld record. The tradition notes that this was not a dishonoring but a request. Veld asked to be buried below rather than above. One school of Vornheld interpretation holds that this was a gesture of humility, acknowledging what had been lost on his watch. Another says the valley-floor placement was a statement of intention: a king buried facing upward rather than outward, not surveying what he once had but waiting for what would come to him. The debate has not been resolved in several generations of argument and probably will not be.\n\nVeld's mound is unmarked. The Vornheld know where it is. Certain Stroven families, by long tradition, know where it is. Occasional outside scholars come looking and either find a different mound or find nothing useful. Whether this concealment is protective of the burial or of some other property of the location is a question whose answer, if there is one, belongs to the Vornheld.\n\nThe barrows in general are the community's ancestors, not its archaeological record. Disturbing them carries no explicit legal prohibition because the community's understanding of what the barrows are does not require explicit prohibition: they are the dead kings, their possessions are the dead kings' possessions, and what belongs to the dead kings belongs to the community's memory of itself rather than to anyone currently living. Outside treasure-seekers have attempted barrows in northern Azhora before. There are at least two documented cases in the Bay of Lol's coastal records of attempts at Endevor-adjacent mounds. In both cases the attempts were unsuccessful — one because the mound opened was not a barrow, and one because the attempt was interrupted in circumstances the coastal record describes with some vagueness and the Endevor account of the same events does not describe at all.\n\nThe second case involves three men from a Bay of Lol coastal family with documented commercial interests in the northern Orched Hills, who came to Endevor, in the account of a northern merchant who tracked the sequence of events, \"to see what was in the old mounds.\" They opened what they believed was a late-period barrow on the upper eastern ridge, in the Eld-Valleys area. The coastal record notes their subsequent departure from Endevor as rapid and without what they came for, and that one of the three did not complete the return journey to the coast. The coastal narrative is vague on the circumstances that produced these outcomes. The Vornheld record of the same period — which does not describe what happened on the eastern ridge in any account that has been shared with outside inquirers — notes in its margin-notation system a designation applied afterward to the incident location: *nil-vel-gall nil-vel-keth*. The compound is not common. *Nil-vel-keth* appears as Veld's designation for the Deepworks: no return-speech. *Nil-vel-gall* is rarer still, appearing only twice in the tradition before this notation, both in contexts the Vornheld describe as \"the closure of something that was not supposed to be opened.\" The combined designation places the site outside the category of locations where ordinary community protocols apply. The valley community that grazes the hill above the site now routes its sheep differently than before. No one has explained this to outside observers in a context where they have found explanation appropriate.\n\n---\n\n## The Vornheld and the Epic Tradition\n\nThe Vornheld are the keepers of the Endevor epic tradition. The name is a Northern Mittoli compound, meaning roughly \"the ancestor-holders\" or \"those who hold the old ones\" — from *vorn* (the sky-pivot; north; the ancestor-direction) and *held* (hold; maintenance; keeping). The title is old enough to be uncertain in its own derivation. By now it names the role as much as it describes it.\n\nA Vornheld is not trained like a craft apprentice. The tradition enters through family — almost all practitioners come from households that have produced Vornheld before — but the selection is not simply inheritance. A child who shows the specific kind of memory the tradition requires is cultivated into it; a child who does not, even from a Vornheld household, is not. What the tradition requires is something between musical memory and structural memory: the capacity to hold long, metrically complex poems in reproducible form, and to hold the relationships between poems — the web of cross-references, the moment where one poem's allusion answers another's question — as a single navigable structure rather than a list. Knowing the poems is not knowing the tradition. The tradition is the map between them.\n\nThe Vornheld perform at the stone-shelters when gatherings are significant enough to require it — the major inter-valley assemblies, the seasonal land-right confirmations, the resolution of disputes that touch on ancestry, which in Endevor can mean almost any dispute between old families. The recitation at these events is not ornamental. The Vornheld's knowledge of ancestral claims is evidence. When a family asserts a right to a stretch of hill pasture based on an ancestor's service to a mine-king three hundred years ago, the Vornheld knows whether that claim is in the tradition, what the tradition says about it, and whether anything in the tradition speaks to the counter-claim. This is why Endevor dispute-resolution requires a Vornheld present for any proceeding invoking ancestry: the record-keepers in Stroven hold land agreements; the Vornheld hold lineages. The two archives do not duplicate each other.\n\nThe Vornheld lineages currently active number seven, each associated with a particular valley system and, by extension, with a particular portion of the tradition. Lineage specialization is not formalized — there is no rule stipulating that the Stroven valley lineage holds the Veld poems — but it has become de facto over several generations of practice, as each lineage has tended to recite its own valley's ancestral claims most often and therefore knows them most deeply. Full knowledge of all three bodies is the stated goal; full knowledge of the complete tradition in reproducible form is what distinguishes a master Vornheld from a capable one, and in any given generation there are perhaps two or three practitioners who can honestly claim it.\n\nThe most consequential current lineage disagreement runs between the family associated with the Keld poems and the family associated with the Orrven poems — the tradition of the first-opener against the tradition of the peak. The dispute is not about the poems themselves, which are settled, but about the primacy of their subjects: whether the origin is more essential to the tradition's meaning than the achievement, or whether the peak defines the entire arc and makes the opening merely prelude. In practical terms the disagreement affects how several disputed ancestral claims are weighted when they touch on the first-opener period versus the peak period. The dispute has an analog in a longstanding property disagreement between the two lineages' associated valley communities that has been argued at stone-shelters four times in the past century without permanent resolution. Everyone in Stroven understands the land question and the Vornheld question to be the same argument in different registers.\n\nThe most discussed recent intervention of Vornheld authority in living memory concerns an episode known in Stroven as the Drevos incident, or, among those who find the name insufficient, the salted-pan dispute. The Drevos family, a mining-descended household in the northern valley system, had for three generations asserted a right to a seasonal pasture on the basis of an ancestor's service to a late-period king. The counter-claim, from an older hill family, held that the same ancestor's service was in the *Eld-Keth* but attributed to a different king — which, if correct, would mean the pasture assignment had been made under misattributed authority and could be revisited. The dispute reached a stone-shelter proceeding with two Vornheld present from different lineages, who disagreed on the attribution. Resolution required a third Vornheld to recite the relevant naming-poem in full. This third Vornheld, who was elderly and had not recited the poem publicly in twelve years, did so without pause, in the original archaic Northern Mittoli, before an audience that included the Stroven record-keepers and approximately forty community members who had come specifically because word had gone around that the recitation would happen. The attribution was established. The pasture question was settled. The elderly Vornheld's unassisted recitation of a poem whose text two younger practitioners had disputed is still referenced in Stroven as a lesson about what the tradition requires of its keepers. What the salted-pan element of the name refers to — whether it is the name of the pasture, a detail of that day's proceedings, or something else — no one who was present has found it necessary to clarify.\n\nThe epic tradition is divided into three bodies.\n\n**The *Eld-Keth*** (\"the old-speech,\" the words from before): poems of the mine-king period, naming kings, campaigns, tributary arrangements, and the workings. The most extensive body and the least often publicly recited — they require context to hear correctly, and much of what they say is addressed to the Endevor community rather than to any outside listener. The *Eld-Keth* includes the naming-poems of the barrows: a short piece for each buried king describing the burial goods and the terms of his life. These are recited at the barrows on specific seasonal occasions and nowhere else.\n\n**The *Vel-Keth*** (\"the return-speech,\" the words of transition): poems of the decline — the closing mines, the dissolving tributaries, Veld's reign and burial. The most frequently performed body in public settings, because it does what narrative does best: it names a recognizable arc of rise and fall, and its audiences understand the shape. The *Vel-Keth* is what a traveler in Stroven who witnesses a Vornheld performance is most likely to hear. Fragments have circulated outside Endevor — two Orched Hills records and one Bay of Lol coastal archive preserve passages, apparently transcribed by travelers who heard a Vornheld recite and wrote what they could catch. These fragments are classified in the Academy's northern archive as \"miscellaneous oral material, provenance unclear.\"\n\n**The *Vel-Eld***: the renewal poem. Treated separately because it is not, in the Vornheld tradition, the same category of thing as the other bodies. The *Eld-Keth* and *Vel-Keth* describe what was. The *Vel-Eld* describes what will be — or what may be — or what the tradition holds is latent in the ground the way the gold was latent in the ground, waiting for its conditions.\n\n---\n\n## The Vel-Eld\n\nThe *Vel-Eld* — the Old Return, or the Ancient Coming-Back, or the Return That Was Always There — carries a title that is itself one of the tradition's genuinely contested problems. The Northern Mittoli word *vel* functions, in the archaic form the poem uses, as both \"return\" and \"was-already.\" The poem is about something coming back, or about something that was always present and is finally becoming visible, or both, and the language declines to resolve the question.\n\nIts subject, in plain summary: the restoration of the Endevor realm and the reopening of the mines. The Vornheld tradition considers this an adequate description in the way that \"a watershed is where water divides\" adequately describes a watershed.\n\nThe poem is not available to outside scholars in its complete form. The Vornheld position on this is not that the poem is secret — it is recited in the open twice a year, in a valley accessible to anyone who walks there — but that a complete text extracted from its recitation context and its listeners would be a different thing from the poem, and that the different thing is not what they are keepers of. The following are fragments in approximate translation, given in the order of their appearance in the full recitation. Translation is from archaic Northern Mittoli, which the full recitation uses throughout; the terse, stress-heavy character of the original does not carry.\n\n*The streams remember / what the hills forgot — / gold is a patience / longer than kings.*\n\n*Cold water finds / what cold water left. / The vein is not spent; / the vein is not yet asked.*\n\n*The dead have been seen / before their door opens. / What the mounds hold / the mounds will show first.*\n\n*The wood knows the fire / that made the gold ground. / When the wood speaks it, / Vel-Endre stands.*\n\nVornheld practitioners reciting the complete poem use a slower and more deliberate delivery for the third fragment than for the others, which has been noticed by everyone who has attended more than one recitation but not explained in any account that has reached outside Endevor.\n\nThe poem is structured around three signs and a title.\n\n**The first sign**: *When the cold water finds what it left behind.* The eastern hill streams carry traces of gold even now; the tradition holds that there will come a season when this trace returns to something visible in the pan — when the water finds again what it eroded and eventually lost. One school of Vornheld interpretation holds this means the Deepworks contain a vein that Veld's sealing preserved rather than exhausted, and that the right conditions will reveal it. Another holds it means a new hydrothermal process — something in the Oremindi still active far below the surface, expressing slowly upward — that will produce new deposition. The two positions have been debating each other for several generations without decisive evidence on either side.\n\n**The second sign**: *When the barrows wake before they're opened.* The specific language in the archaic Northern Mittoli is *vel-milloss nil-vel-gall* — \"they have been seen before the crossing.\" The practical interpretation most Vornheld accept: someone will witness something at a barrow — light, sound, movement — before any physical disturbance of the mound. The wider interpretation: a stranger will come to Endevor knowing, without having been told, where the dead kings are. The two interpretations are not mutually exclusive, which is part of why the debate has outlasted several attempts to settle it. It is also why certain Vornheld observe the barrows with unusual attention when strangers appear in Stroven.\n\n**The third sign**: *When the wood speaks what it knows.* The Acorwood reference is explicit within the Vornheld tradition and widely understood among practitioners: the poem refers to the smoke, or to something the smoke precedes, or to something the smoke has always been an indication of and which has not yet fully arrived. Vornheld who specialize in the *Vel-Eld* will say, when pressed, that the third sign is the one whose meaning is most certain among practitioners and most opaque to outside inquiry, and that this relationship is itself the point. The Acorwood speaks in a language the Endevor communities have been learning to read for generations. The third sign is the moment that language becomes fully legible.\n\nThe title the poem gives to the coming renewal is not a personal name. It is *Vel-Endre* — \"Returning-Endevor\" or \"Endevor-Returned\" — a name describing the condition rather than the person who embodies it. The poem does not clearly specify whether *Vel-Endre* is a king who comes from outside bringing the empire back, a quality that rises in the people themselves, or the land's own restoration coming through any of several possible channels. This ambiguity has made the title perpetually applicable to promising individuals — anyone who arrives in Endevor with any northern lineage and any apparent significance has been assessed by at least one Vornheld against the *Vel-Eld*'s criteria.\n\nThe title was applied to Veld himself, in his own lifetime, by a Vornheld lineage that believed his reign was the beginning of the restoration rather than the end of the old order. He died without the mines reopening. The lineage that backed this interpretation is still active; its current practitioners maintain that the application was not wrong but early — that Veld was the first motion of a returning tide, not its crest, and that the poem's timeline is longer than it appears. Other Vornheld lineages find this argument convenient in the particular way that arguments which cannot be falsified are convenient. The argument continues.\n\nThe *Vel-Eld* is recited in full twice a year: at the winter solstice and at the vernal equinox. Both recitations happen at Veld's barrow in the Stroven valley floor, attended by as many Vornheld as can come and whatever community members choose to follow. The solstice recitation draws better attendance than the equinox — there is something in the Endevor character that finds it appropriate to hear a poem about renewal in conditions of maximum cold, to say the thing that is latent in the ground while standing on frozen ground, to say that something will return while winter has its full weight on the hills. Not despite the conditions. Because of them.\n\n---\n\n## Fragments of the Epic Tradition\n\nTwo passages from the *Vel-Keth* have circulated outside Endevor in written form, transcribed by travelers who heard Vornheld performances at Stroven and recorded what they could. Both appear in the Academy's northern files under the classification \"miscellaneous oral material, northern provenance, language unclear.\" The Academy scholars who catalogued them had limited familiarity with archaic Northern Mittoli and none with the tradition's conventions. Their annotations treat the passages as remnants of something larger, which is accurate, while failing to identify what that larger thing is, which is characteristic.\n\n**From the Orrven naming-poem, as transcribed by a Bay of Lol merchant's factor, approximately four generations ago:**\n\n*What did he give the north? — Ships for the bay and horses for the heath.*\n*What did the north give back? — Men who came when called and went when released.*\n*What did he give the eastern hills? — Iron's true value, which is what it becomes.*\n*What did the hills give back? — Iron already becoming, waiting for the weight.*\n*What did he give the country beyond? — Gold's shadow, which moves faster than gold.*\n*What did the country give back? — Silence and the roads kept open.*\n*What did he give the deep ground? — The right question, asked at the right depth.*\n*What did the deep ground give back? — This is the exchange that is not finished.*\n\nThe Academy annotation reads: \"Possible ritual exchange; appears agricultural in reference; northern dialect, incompletely rendered.\" The observation about incomplete rendering is correct. The rest reflects the difficulty of reading call-and-answer verse outside its acoustic context: at a stone-shelter recitation, the eight exchanges move between two voices that everyone in the circle hears simultaneously. On a page, read sequentially, the eighth exchange loses the quality that makes it different from the other seven — the moment in the recitation where the second voice does not answer.\n\n**From the *Vel-Keth*'s Veld cycle, as transcribed by an Orched Hills trading-house record-keeper, approximately three generations ago:**\n\n*Veld spoke last on the ridge, the long speaking.*\n*He named what was owed and named who had paid.*\n*He named what remained and named who would keep it.*\n*He did not name a debt that had not been made.*\n*The tributaries heard him. They heard him well.*\n*What the tributaries did afterward is their own poem.*\n\nThe Academy annotation: \"Lament fragment, possibly funerary; 'Veld' possibly a title or appellative.\" The second observation is the transcriber noticing that the Northern Mittoli word for \"return\" has been used as a proper name, and concluding it must therefore be figurative. The Vornheld tradition would say: the word was his name before it became anything else, and the Veld cycle does not require outside annotation to be understood by the people it was made for.\n\n**From the *Eld-Keth*, the naming-poem for the five men who did not return from Helm's expedition:**\n\n*Vel-Oram, who read the deep ground and said it remembered fire.*\n*Vel-Skedhel, who heard the llos-eld and stayed to hear it again.*\n*Vel-Thonn, whose question the wood did not decline to answer.*\n*Vel-Orrael, who went toward the answer rather than away from it.*\n*Vel-Krenth, whose name the poem does not complete.*\n\nEach name is preceded by *vel-*, functioning here as an honorific title: \"the one who returned into.\" The fifth entry is the one outside scholars, if they ever encountered it, would find most notable: the poem's formulation implies that Vel-Krenth's name continues somewhere, but not in the poem. Where it continues is not specified.\n\nThe Academy files do not contain this passage. It has not been transcribed by any outside visitor who has shared the transcription. It is recited at the individual stone-markers of the five men on specific seasonal occasions by the Vornheld lineage that holds their names, and nowhere else. Its appearance here is the level of description the tradition permits in this context, and is not an invitation to seek the complete text.\n\n---\n\n## Language\n\nThe Endevor dialect of Northern Mittoli is conservative in its structure and unusual in its vocabulary. It shows the expected Northern features — initial stress, final vowel deletion, flattened diphthongs — but it has preserved the *ae* diphthong in a handful of words where even Northern Mittoli has simplified it, which linguists treat as evidence that Endevor has been in less contact with the coast than the Bay of Lol communities to its northwest, maintaining an archaic form while its neighbors changed.\n\nThe substrate layer is deeper here than in the Bay of Lol speech and different in profile from the Ganoss upland dialect. The smoke vocabulary is the most studied example: words that describe the Acorwood's behavior, which have no cognates in any other Northern Mittoli variety and do not reconstruct from Proto-Western, appear to be borrowings from a language that was spoken in these hills before Mittoli speakers arrived. Academy linguists have catalogued seven such words in Endevor dialect and noted that they cluster in the domain of fire, smoke, forest behavior, and certain weather phenomena — the things that a community watching the Acorwood's western face would need to name.\n\nThe fell-sheep vocabulary is also unusually rich: Endevor herders have fifteen or more terms for wool quality, fleece condition, and grazing behavior that do not appear in other Northern Mittoli varieties and that the Academy's linguistic records have not been able to trace to any source outside Endevor itself. Whether these are old substrate words, old Mittoli words that have only survived here, or innovations developed within the community is not established. The herders consider the question academic in a register that implies the word \"academic\" is not a compliment.",
      "loreRef": "endevor",
      "climate": "Cfb"
    },
    "East Endevor": {
      "name": "East Endevor",
      "color": "#c0392b",
      "lore": "# Endevor\n\nNorthwest of the Acorwood, where the Oremindi's northern terminus descends into lower ground and the air begins to carry the cold salt weight of the bay, the hills of Endevor fill the space between things. Not quite the upland — the Ganoss plateau is harder and higher, to the north. Not quite the coast — the Bay of Lol is a day's descent to the northwest. Not quite the mountain country, though the Oremindi makes the southern skyline and certain clear mornings let the peaks show through. Endevor is the hilly country in between, and it has organized itself around that position for as long as anyone has recorded it.\n\nThe name has no agreed derivation. The leading proposal in Academy language files is that *Endevor* is a contracted Northern Mittoli compound, perhaps *ond* (broad water) + *vel* (return) + an archaic locative marker — \"the place where the water turns back,\" referring to the watershed ridge where the hill streams divide between draining northwest toward the bay and north toward the Ganoss. A minority view holds that the name predates Northern Mittoli entirely and that the Mittoli speakers who arrived here simply kept the word that was already there. Both camps cite the same evidence in their favor, which the Academy has noted without resolving.\n\nThe outside world, if it thinks of Endevor at all, thinks of fell-sheep wool and reliable inns and the road toward the Bay of Lol. It does not think of the barrows on the ridgelines, which are older than the wool trade, and more numerous, and full of kings.\n\n---\n\n## Geography\n\nThe Endevor hills are old, rounded, and wet. They do not have the dramatic vertical of the Oremindi or the bleak exposed sweep of the Ganoss uplands; they are the kind of hills that fold into one another, valley after valley, each with its own stream, each valley floor broad enough for a strip of farmland before the slope comes up again. The highest ground is along the eastern ridge — the long slope that faces the Acorwood — which catches the worst of the weather coming in from the north and west and wrings it out before the clouds continue inland.\n\nThe result is that Endevor is wet without being waterlogged. The streams are reliable and numerous. The valley floors are damp and dark-soiled. The upper slopes are peat-moorland and short-grassed heath, suitable for grazing and not much else. The lower slopes are where most of Endevor's population lives, in the long ribbon settlements that follow the valley floors between the farmland and the hill pasture above.\n\nThe eastern ridge is wooded on its lower face — a mix of native northern species, hardy and not especially tall, transitioning as you approach the Acorwood's margin into something denser and older. Exactly where Endevor's edge-forest ends and the Acorwood begins is not a line that appears on any map; the local communities know it by feel and by the quality of the light, which changes at a threshold that most people can locate within a few hundred yards but cannot explain as a specific visible marker.\n\nThe Oremindi's northern end shapes the southern horizon and does more than frame the view. The meltwater coming off the range's northernmost snowfields feeds several of Endevor's main rivers, making them flashier in spring than their size would suggest and keeping them running in the driest summers. The passes through the Oremindi's northern extension are few and severe — harder and more seasonal than those further south — and are used primarily by the Sevron contact route and by occasional extraordinary travelers with specific purpose. Endevor has never been a mountain-crossing gateway in the way that the southern Oremindi communities are; the passes behind it are not worth the effort for ordinary commerce.\n\n---\n\n## The People\n\nEndevor is pastoral first. The hill communities keep sheep on the upper slopes and cattle in the valley bottoms, and have done this long enough that the organization of the land around those two requirements is baked into everything else: the placement of settlements, the timing of the year, the structure of disputes, the vocabulary of status. A family's standing in any Endevor valley is legible from its position on the hill — how far up the slope their summer grazing runs, how many animals winter in their valley barns, and whether the barns themselves are stone or timber, which says something about how long the family has been in that particular valley.\n\nThe characteristic animal of the Endevor hills is the **fell-sheep** — a breed whose name is a Northern Mittoli word meaning roughly \"the hill-animal\" or \"the high-pasture one,\" and which has been in these hills long enough that no records mention its arrival. The fell-sheep is not impressive beside the great animals of other regions. It is compact, short-legged, and deeply shaggy, with a fleece that produces more lanolin than any comparable breed — a natural oil that makes the wool water-resistant in the fleece and almost waterproof once spun and prepared. In a climate that is wet for most of the year, this matters. Endevor wool is traded throughout northern Azhora and down the coast road specifically because it performs in conditions that other wool cannot. The Bay of Lol fishing communities buy it for sea gear. The Ganoss upland clans have their own animals but trade for Endevor spun goods when they can get them. The Orched Hills mining settlements are regular purchasers of Endevor wool in preference to anything grown closer.\n\nThe fell-sheep also live their entire year outside, which is not true of most northern livestock. They know the upper hills the way the hills know themselves, and they will come down of their own accord before weather turns that their shepherds have not yet noticed. Endevor farmers pay attention to this. Where the sheep are on a given morning is information about the day.\n\nThe social unit of Endevor is the valley community — a loose collection of households sharing a river system, with informal but understood obligations around water rights, common hill access, and dispute resolution. Disputes between valleys are resolved at **stone-shelters** — low circular enclosures of dry-laid stone set at the ridgelines between valleys, old enough that no one remembers who built them, used as neutral meeting ground when valley communities need to negotiate. The stone-shelters are not impressive structures; they are simply very old and unmoved, which in Endevor is a form of authority. A resolution reached at a stone-shelter has a different weight from one reached anywhere else, because the shelter has been the resolution site before and will be again, and its walls have heard the settlement's terms and will keep them the same way they keep the wind off.\n\nThe shelters have one specific physical property that the Vornheld tradition says is why they were placed where they were placed and not anywhere else: speech spoken inside them at ordinary volume is heard simultaneously and with equal clarity by everyone in the circle, regardless of wind. The Academy attributes this to circular construction and the reflection properties of dry-laid stone at these specific angles. The Vornheld say this is what the sites were chosen for — that whoever built them tested the acoustics before placing the stone, and selected positions where the ground itself held the sound. A resolution reached inside a stone-shelter is heard by all parties in the same moment. This is not a formality. It is the physical condition that makes the resolution binding. You cannot claim you did not hear it.\n\nThe main market settlement is **Stroven** — positioned at the convergence of the three largest valley routes, where goods from the upper hills and goods coming northwest from the Bay of Lol approaches meet before separating again into the valley network. Stroven is not large; it is consequential because of where it is. The permanent population is several hundred, swelling in trading seasons. It has a covered market structure, a handful of inns accustomed to through-travelers, and the only standing archive in Endevor — a collection of land records and valley boundary agreements maintained by a family of hereditary record-keepers who have been doing this work long enough to hold opinions about disputes from four generations ago and will share those opinions if asked. The record-keepers are not impartial, exactly; they are the memory, and memory has its own loyalties. This is broadly understood and accounted for in how disputes proceed.\n\n---\n\n## The Acorwood's Western Face\n\nEvery Endevor community faces east toward the Acorwood. From the higher ridgelines it is visible as a dark line — darker than the surrounding hill forest, perceptibly different in color and density — that marks the edge of navigable country. The Ganoss upland communities on the Acorwood's northern margin and the Orched Hills settlements to the south have their own longstanding relationship with the forest's accessible edges. Endevor's relationship is with the western face, and it is different in a specific way: the morning sun rises behind the Acorwood, and from Endevor's hills at dawn the forest is lit from within — the edge trees stand in silhouette against the sky while the interior remains dark, the margin visible as a precise line between what is and is not entered. This is the Acorwood as Endevor knows it.\n\nThe edge timber harvest here is modest. The accessible trees on the Acorwood's northwestern fringe are similar in character to the edge timber on the northern and southern margins — large hardwoods that can be taken from the outer tree lines without going deep — but the Endevor communities have never developed a major edge-timber trade. The Bay of Lol coastal communities do not require much inland timber, having their own supplies from the coastal strips. The Ganoss use the northern margins. Endevor's edge harvest is primarily for local use: charcoal, building timber, occasional mast in good years.\n\nWhat the Endevor communities have developed, across many generations, is a practice of **smoke-reading**.\n\nThe Acorwood occasionally produces smoke from its interior. The Orched Hills records note this with uncertainty and do not follow it up. The Ganoss communities acknowledge it and have made their peace with not explaining it. Endevor has spent enough seasons watching the western and northwestern face — the face that catches the afternoon light, visible from the high sheepwalks for hours at a time — that their communities have accumulated an unusual amount of observational detail.\n\nThe fell-sheep shepherds in the high pastures are positioned to watch the Acorwood's margin across long hours, with nothing pressing them to look elsewhere. Across generations, this has produced a tradition of noting, without explaining, what the smoke does. There is vocabulary for it in the Endevor dialect — words that do not appear in other Northern Mittoli varieties — for the smoke that rises thin and straight in still air, the smoke that spreads at canopy height and moves with the wind before dispersing, and the specific, acknowledged category of smoke that appears with no wind and dissipates downward rather than upward, which the Endevor fell-sheep shepherds call by a substrate word that translates into Standard Mittoli as something between \"breathing-down\" and \"the forest taking something in.\"\n\nThe smoke-reading tradition is practical in two uses that have been confirmed across several generations. First: smoke from the Acorwood interior in late summer correlates imperfectly but consistently with good mast years; the shepherds who track this will predict mast yield before the edge trees show it in their growth. Second: the quality of smoke — color, height, behavior — appears to predict weather shifts in the way that cloud formations over the Oremindi also predict weather shifts, and is used the same way. Neither use requires an explanation of what the smoke is. The Endevor communities have not produced one.\n\nWhen Academy scholars have visited Stroven and asked about the smoke, they receive accurate and detailed information about what the smoke does and what it predicts, and no information about what causes it. The question of cause is not treated as something the Endevor communities are withholding. It is treated as a question that belongs to a different kind of inquiry than the one they practice. The smoke behaves; they read it; that is the relationship; cause is for someone with more time and less sheep.\n\n---\n\n## History\n\nThe pastoral Endevor is recent, as these things go.\n\nFor several centuries before the current era, Endevor was the center of the most extensive political authority in northern Azhora. The source of that authority was gold — placer gold in the eastern hill streams, and beneath it the deep vein gold that the streams had been eroding for thousands of years before anyone thought to follow them uphill. When the Endevor mine-kings found those veins, the northern world reorganized around them.\n\nAt its height the Endevor realm extended further north than the Bay of Lol, incorporating the coastal port communities and the Ganoss upland clans as tributaries. It extended south through the Orched Hills and, in its largest period, pressed claims as far as the northern Mittolo margins — the first and last time a northern power came close to bridging the cultural divide that the Acorwood and the Orched Hills maintain between north and south. The mine-kings did not rule these territories as the later Mittoli city-states governed their regions, through administrators and written law; they held them through the distribution of gold, the weight of tribute obligation, and the personal prestige of a line that had been producing wealth long enough for wealth and legitimacy to become indistinguishable. The Ganoss clans supplied livestock and manpower. The Bay of Lol communities supplied ships and navigators. The Orched Hills supplied iron and copper. The gold at the center moved all of it.\n\nFor roughly two hundred and fifty years at the empire's peak — the period the Vornheld poetry calls the *Eld-Gold*, the time of gold — the mine-kings of Endevor were the most significant power in northern Azhora. Their burials are still in the hills. The ridgelines are scattered with them.\n\nThe tradition names thirty-eight kings in the mine-king period. Not all are equally present in the *Eld-Keth* — the earliest are named briefly, the middle kings described at length, the late-period kings compressed as the tradition seems to contract around the decline. Three stand apart.\n\n**Keld** is the first-opener: the king who followed the stream gold uphill and found the vein. His *Eld-Keth* naming-poem is among the shortest in the body — he is recorded as a beginning rather than an achievement, and the tradition treats him accordingly, with respect but without ornamentation. A king who opens something gets the short poem. The gold that follows him speaks for itself.\n\n**Orrven** is the peak. His naming-poem is the longest in the *Eld-Keth* body and is structured differently from all others: a call-and-answer form in which the first voice names what Orrven gave and the second names what was returned, eight exchanges moving from the Bay of Lol communities to the Ganoss clans to the Orched Hills to what the tradition calls simply \"the deep ground.\" The effect, which Endevor audiences hear differently from how outside scholars read it, is of weight in motion — a redistribution so large that the land itself leaned under it. A Vornheld practitioner, when pressed by an Academy visitor to summarize Orrven's significance, said: \"His word was the weight gold makes before anyone counts it.\" The Academy visitor wrote this down, which the Vornheld probably anticipated.\n\n**Helm** is the surveyor — the king who sent Endevor's only recorded expedition into the Acorwood's interior, in the middle-late period when the eastern veins were thinning and the tradition of looking further was still recent enough to seem reasonable. Helm had observed, from fell-sheep shepherds watching the Acorwood's western face, that the breathing-down smoke concentrated most reliably above the Deepworks area. He concluded, or commissioned the conclusion, that the smoke was the Acorwood's surface indication of something below: an extension of the same hydrothermal system that had produced the vein gold, deeper and further east. Seventeen men entered the Acorwood's northwestern margin. Twelve returned. The five who did not are recorded by name in the *Eld-Keth*; that poem is recited at their stone at the barrow dedication and has not been offered at a public Stroven gathering in anyone's living memory.\n\nThe twelve who came back brought a word: *vel-keth-eld*. The Vornheld have not reached consensus on its meaning in the three or four generations since the expedition. Two translations circulate within the tradition: \"the forest's memory of fire\" and \"what the dark knows that we left behind.\" The first suggests the Acorwood has a relationship to the ancient hydrothermal period — that the forest grew in or around or from the same geological event that made the gold. The second suggests that whatever the expedition encountered inside remembered the mine-kings in some form. Helm's own interpretation was not recorded. He did not seal the Deepworks — that was Veld, a generation later — but he did not send another expedition.\n\nThe mines did not run out all at once. That was the particular difficulty.\n\nThe gold declined over several generations, each one producing less than the last, each one also the one that the current king believed was the bottom before a new vein would open. The shaft-heads went deeper. The spoil heaps grew. The streams were panned harder. The veins were followed further into the Oremindi foothills, into ground that was more expensive and more dangerous, and which produced less. The gold was finite because it had been deposited by a period of ancient hydrothermal activity in the range that had long since ended; there was no new gold forming. What was there was what had been there, and when it was taken, the taking was over.\n\nThe kings who presided over the decline maintained their tributary relationships through prestige long after the gold couldn't sustain them economically. This worked while the living memory of abundance lasted. When the generation that had known the peak died out and the next knew only the decline, the calculations changed. The Ganoss clans stopped sending cattle and said they were using them at home. The Bay of Lol communities began trading directly with the Orched Hills, bypassing the tribute routing. The Orched Hills settlements, which had their own ore wealth and their own long memories, assessed the situation and stopped paying.\n\nThe last king of the Endevor realm was named Veld — a Northern Mittoli name meaning simply \"return,\" which was understood at the time as a statement of ambition and in retrospect reads as something else. He held the realm together for eighteen years through the force of his personal authority and is described in the Vornheld tradition as the greatest of the later kings, which is not exactly praise: the greatest of the later kings could not undo what the geology had done. Veld died in his valley, and after him, the tributaries did not return.\n\nWhat followed was not collapse in the dramatic sense. There were no invasions, no great battles for succession, no famine. The population stayed. The valley communities kept their land. The stone-shelters kept their function. What changed was scope: Endevor contracted back to Endevor, and the wider world that had organized around its gold reorganized around other things, and eventually forgot there had been an organizing.\n\nThe raiding period that the northern Azhoran regional record describes as coming after this was partly a consequence: without the empire's redistributive economy, the coastal communities went back to taking by force what they had previously received through the gold-network. The Endevor hills were the logistical hinterland of that raiding culture's staging, not its origin. The mine-kings' empire was before the raiding; the raiding filled the vacuum after.\n\nBy the time the north's transition to trade began, Endevor had been quiet pastoral hill country for long enough that neither the coastal communities nor the Orched Hills settlements remembered what it had been. No Mittoli scholar wrote down the Endevor empire while it existed; the region was too far outside the Mittoli world for systematic record-keeping. The Vornheld tradition preserved everything, and the Vornheld tradition is oral, in archaic Northern Mittoli, inaccessible to outsiders who do not seek it out.\n\nThose who seek it out and can read what they find tend not to believe it — the scale is too large, the gold too much, the empire too extensive, for a country presently famous for waterproof sheep. This is a known difficulty with oral traditions from regions without outside corroboration. The barrows corroborate. The shaft-heads corroborate. Most outside visitors see hills and do not read them as texts.\n\n---\n\n## The Mines\n\nThe evidence for the Endevor gold is still in the hills if you know where to look.\n\nThe eastern hill streams — those draining toward the Acorwood's margin, fed by Oremindi snowfields — carry trace gold even now, at concentrations too low to bother with but detectable to anyone who knows what color to watch for in the pan. These are the runoff from the same deposits that built the empire: the deep vein gold in the Oremindi foothills, which the ancient hydrothermal activity of the range concentrated and which the long slow erosion of the hills has been dispersing into the streams for longer than anyone has been here to observe. At the mining peak, those concentrations were high enough that stream-panning alone produced meaningful yield. They have been diluting since the veins were broken open, and what remains is memory rather than resource.\n\nThe shaft-heads are more visible than the streams. The eastern faces of the three valley systems nearest the Acorwood — which the Vornheld tradition calls the **Eld-Valleys** — are marked with collapsed mine entrances at irregular intervals from near the valley floor to well above the current sheepwalks. Some are obvious: squared stonework around a shaft-mouth, subsidence depressions where fill has settled over generations. Others are subtle enough that a new arrival walking the sheep paths will occasionally put a foot through the turf into the edge of old roof-stone. The communities closest to the Eld-Valleys know where the dangerous ground is and avoid it without drama; it is simply part of the landscape.\n\nThe Vornheld tradition preserves names for seventeen distinct mine-workings, of which fourteen can be approximately located on the eastern ridge. The other three — the poems call them the **Deepworks** — are either collapsed without surface sign or are in the transition zone between the Endevor hills and the Acorwood's northwestern margin, where the Endevor communities do not regularly go. Whether the Deepworks still have accessible chambers is not known. The tradition says they were the richest of the veins, opened last and exhausted fastest, and sealed by Veld's order when the workers kept not returning from the deeper levels. The sealing instructions and the reason for the non-returns are subjects the Vornheld will discuss at different lengths depending on who is asking and what they appear to intend.\n\nSilver was mined alongside gold in the middle period — a secondary ore from the same hydrothermal deposits, found at greater depth once the gold concentrations thinned. Silver mining continued for two or three generations after gold effectively ended, providing enough revenue to maintain something that looked, from the outside, like a functioning economy while the empire's political structure dissolved. The last silver was exhausted within living memory of the decline's final phase, and the silver shafts are visible in the northern valley systems, shallower and less numerous than the gold workings.\n\nThe Deepworks have a specific entry in the sealing record preserved by the Vornheld: Veld's decree is noted in archaic Northern Mittoli as *oss nil-vel-keth* — \"mark: no return-speech\" — a designation the tradition treats as distinct from ordinary closure. Other shafts were sealed when they were worked out. The Deepworks were closed *nil-vel-keth*, without the response that would indicate emptiness. The formulation implies something was there to not-answer.\n\nThe mine-worker tradition, preserved fragmentarily in the *Eld-Keth* naming-poems for the last men to work the Deepworks, refers to a phenomenon called *llos-eld* — roughly \"gold-voice,\" though \"the ore that speaks\" is the translation Vornheld practitioners prefer when they feel precise. The *llos-eld* was a resonance audible in the rock at the deepest working levels: described variously as a low vibration, a harmonic in the stone that had no identifiable external source, a sound that seemed to originate not from above or below but from the rock around the worker. Experienced mine-foremen tracked it the way fell-sheep shepherds track the Acorwood's smoke — as information whose practical use was clear and whose underlying meaning was not. High *llos-eld* correlated with structural stability in the workings. The specific signature recorded in the Deepworks naming-poems is different from the stable reading, but what it was different toward is the thing the *Eld-Keth* does not say directly.\n\nWhether the *llos-eld* and the Acorwood's breathing-down smoke are related is a question the mine-workers and the shepherds never appear to have addressed together — the workers were underground and the shepherds were watching the forest above, and their separate communities had different relationships to the relevant facts. What the Vornheld tradition notes, without drawing a conclusion, is that the breathing-down smoke is most frequently reported by Endevor shepherds above the ground that corresponds to the Deepworks' approximate location. The observation is in the tradition. No resolution has been attached to it that the tradition records.\n\nThe mines are not worked. No new vein has been found in eight generations of looking, which has not entirely stopped people from looking. There are people in every generation of Endevor history who believe the Deepworks are not exhausted — that Veld sealed something rather than nothing, that the gold is still in the ground and waiting. The Vornheld have opinions about this. They express them when asked and reserve their most specific opinions for those who have already decided to listen.\n\n---\n\n## The Barrows\n\nThe ridgelines of Endevor are not bare. They are covered with sheep and with the dead.\n\nThe barrow-building tradition spans the full mine-king period and reads in the landscape as a slow record of decline. The oldest barrows are the highest — the first mine-kings claimed the skyline, and the mounds they raised are the largest, some visible for miles in clear weather, their grass-covered profiles the most distinctive feature of Endevor's upper silhouette. The Vornheld tradition describes these burials with objects that make outside scholars careful about skepticism: gold plate, gold ornament, ceremonial vessels, weapons edged with gold — the vocabulary of a household that operated at a scale that required gold to describe itself.\n\nThe barrows descend through the centuries and through the hill. Middle-period kings are buried at the ridgeline's lower edge. These barrows are smaller, the grave goods richer in silver and bronze, with gold present but diminished. Then the late-period burials in the upper valley sides, below the crest entirely: modest mounds that a traveler might pass without noticing, holding kings who went into the ground with bronze and the memory of what their fathers had.\n\nVeld's barrow is in the floor of the main Stroven valley — the lowest position of any king in the Vornheld record. The tradition notes that this was not a dishonoring but a request. Veld asked to be buried below rather than above. One school of Vornheld interpretation holds that this was a gesture of humility, acknowledging what had been lost on his watch. Another says the valley-floor placement was a statement of intention: a king buried facing upward rather than outward, not surveying what he once had but waiting for what would come to him. The debate has not been resolved in several generations of argument and probably will not be.\n\nVeld's mound is unmarked. The Vornheld know where it is. Certain Stroven families, by long tradition, know where it is. Occasional outside scholars come looking and either find a different mound or find nothing useful. Whether this concealment is protective of the burial or of some other property of the location is a question whose answer, if there is one, belongs to the Vornheld.\n\nThe barrows in general are the community's ancestors, not its archaeological record. Disturbing them carries no explicit legal prohibition because the community's understanding of what the barrows are does not require explicit prohibition: they are the dead kings, their possessions are the dead kings' possessions, and what belongs to the dead kings belongs to the community's memory of itself rather than to anyone currently living. Outside treasure-seekers have attempted barrows in northern Azhora before. There are at least two documented cases in the Bay of Lol's coastal records of attempts at Endevor-adjacent mounds. In both cases the attempts were unsuccessful — one because the mound opened was not a barrow, and one because the attempt was interrupted in circumstances the coastal record describes with some vagueness and the Endevor account of the same events does not describe at all.\n\nThe second case involves three men from a Bay of Lol coastal family with documented commercial interests in the northern Orched Hills, who came to Endevor, in the account of a northern merchant who tracked the sequence of events, \"to see what was in the old mounds.\" They opened what they believed was a late-period barrow on the upper eastern ridge, in the Eld-Valleys area. The coastal record notes their subsequent departure from Endevor as rapid and without what they came for, and that one of the three did not complete the return journey to the coast. The coastal narrative is vague on the circumstances that produced these outcomes. The Vornheld record of the same period — which does not describe what happened on the eastern ridge in any account that has been shared with outside inquirers — notes in its margin-notation system a designation applied afterward to the incident location: *nil-vel-gall nil-vel-keth*. The compound is not common. *Nil-vel-keth* appears as Veld's designation for the Deepworks: no return-speech. *Nil-vel-gall* is rarer still, appearing only twice in the tradition before this notation, both in contexts the Vornheld describe as \"the closure of something that was not supposed to be opened.\" The combined designation places the site outside the category of locations where ordinary community protocols apply. The valley community that grazes the hill above the site now routes its sheep differently than before. No one has explained this to outside observers in a context where they have found explanation appropriate.\n\n---\n\n## The Vornheld and the Epic Tradition\n\nThe Vornheld are the keepers of the Endevor epic tradition. The name is a Northern Mittoli compound, meaning roughly \"the ancestor-holders\" or \"those who hold the old ones\" — from *vorn* (the sky-pivot; north; the ancestor-direction) and *held* (hold; maintenance; keeping). The title is old enough to be uncertain in its own derivation. By now it names the role as much as it describes it.\n\nA Vornheld is not trained like a craft apprentice. The tradition enters through family — almost all practitioners come from households that have produced Vornheld before — but the selection is not simply inheritance. A child who shows the specific kind of memory the tradition requires is cultivated into it; a child who does not, even from a Vornheld household, is not. What the tradition requires is something between musical memory and structural memory: the capacity to hold long, metrically complex poems in reproducible form, and to hold the relationships between poems — the web of cross-references, the moment where one poem's allusion answers another's question — as a single navigable structure rather than a list. Knowing the poems is not knowing the tradition. The tradition is the map between them.\n\nThe Vornheld perform at the stone-shelters when gatherings are significant enough to require it — the major inter-valley assemblies, the seasonal land-right confirmations, the resolution of disputes that touch on ancestry, which in Endevor can mean almost any dispute between old families. The recitation at these events is not ornamental. The Vornheld's knowledge of ancestral claims is evidence. When a family asserts a right to a stretch of hill pasture based on an ancestor's service to a mine-king three hundred years ago, the Vornheld knows whether that claim is in the tradition, what the tradition says about it, and whether anything in the tradition speaks to the counter-claim. This is why Endevor dispute-resolution requires a Vornheld present for any proceeding invoking ancestry: the record-keepers in Stroven hold land agreements; the Vornheld hold lineages. The two archives do not duplicate each other.\n\nThe Vornheld lineages currently active number seven, each associated with a particular valley system and, by extension, with a particular portion of the tradition. Lineage specialization is not formalized — there is no rule stipulating that the Stroven valley lineage holds the Veld poems — but it has become de facto over several generations of practice, as each lineage has tended to recite its own valley's ancestral claims most often and therefore knows them most deeply. Full knowledge of all three bodies is the stated goal; full knowledge of the complete tradition in reproducible form is what distinguishes a master Vornheld from a capable one, and in any given generation there are perhaps two or three practitioners who can honestly claim it.\n\nThe most consequential current lineage disagreement runs between the family associated with the Keld poems and the family associated with the Orrven poems — the tradition of the first-opener against the tradition of the peak. The dispute is not about the poems themselves, which are settled, but about the primacy of their subjects: whether the origin is more essential to the tradition's meaning than the achievement, or whether the peak defines the entire arc and makes the opening merely prelude. In practical terms the disagreement affects how several disputed ancestral claims are weighted when they touch on the first-opener period versus the peak period. The dispute has an analog in a longstanding property disagreement between the two lineages' associated valley communities that has been argued at stone-shelters four times in the past century without permanent resolution. Everyone in Stroven understands the land question and the Vornheld question to be the same argument in different registers.\n\nThe most discussed recent intervention of Vornheld authority in living memory concerns an episode known in Stroven as the Drevos incident, or, among those who find the name insufficient, the salted-pan dispute. The Drevos family, a mining-descended household in the northern valley system, had for three generations asserted a right to a seasonal pasture on the basis of an ancestor's service to a late-period king. The counter-claim, from an older hill family, held that the same ancestor's service was in the *Eld-Keth* but attributed to a different king — which, if correct, would mean the pasture assignment had been made under misattributed authority and could be revisited. The dispute reached a stone-shelter proceeding with two Vornheld present from different lineages, who disagreed on the attribution. Resolution required a third Vornheld to recite the relevant naming-poem in full. This third Vornheld, who was elderly and had not recited the poem publicly in twelve years, did so without pause, in the original archaic Northern Mittoli, before an audience that included the Stroven record-keepers and approximately forty community members who had come specifically because word had gone around that the recitation would happen. The attribution was established. The pasture question was settled. The elderly Vornheld's unassisted recitation of a poem whose text two younger practitioners had disputed is still referenced in Stroven as a lesson about what the tradition requires of its keepers. What the salted-pan element of the name refers to — whether it is the name of the pasture, a detail of that day's proceedings, or something else — no one who was present has found it necessary to clarify.\n\nThe epic tradition is divided into three bodies.\n\n**The *Eld-Keth*** (\"the old-speech,\" the words from before): poems of the mine-king period, naming kings, campaigns, tributary arrangements, and the workings. The most extensive body and the least often publicly recited — they require context to hear correctly, and much of what they say is addressed to the Endevor community rather than to any outside listener. The *Eld-Keth* includes the naming-poems of the barrows: a short piece for each buried king describing the burial goods and the terms of his life. These are recited at the barrows on specific seasonal occasions and nowhere else.\n\n**The *Vel-Keth*** (\"the return-speech,\" the words of transition): poems of the decline — the closing mines, the dissolving tributaries, Veld's reign and burial. The most frequently performed body in public settings, because it does what narrative does best: it names a recognizable arc of rise and fall, and its audiences understand the shape. The *Vel-Keth* is what a traveler in Stroven who witnesses a Vornheld performance is most likely to hear. Fragments have circulated outside Endevor — two Orched Hills records and one Bay of Lol coastal archive preserve passages, apparently transcribed by travelers who heard a Vornheld recite and wrote what they could catch. These fragments are classified in the Academy's northern archive as \"miscellaneous oral material, provenance unclear.\"\n\n**The *Vel-Eld***: the renewal poem. Treated separately because it is not, in the Vornheld tradition, the same category of thing as the other bodies. The *Eld-Keth* and *Vel-Keth* describe what was. The *Vel-Eld* describes what will be — or what may be — or what the tradition holds is latent in the ground the way the gold was latent in the ground, waiting for its conditions.\n\n---\n\n## The Vel-Eld\n\nThe *Vel-Eld* — the Old Return, or the Ancient Coming-Back, or the Return That Was Always There — carries a title that is itself one of the tradition's genuinely contested problems. The Northern Mittoli word *vel* functions, in the archaic form the poem uses, as both \"return\" and \"was-already.\" The poem is about something coming back, or about something that was always present and is finally becoming visible, or both, and the language declines to resolve the question.\n\nIts subject, in plain summary: the restoration of the Endevor realm and the reopening of the mines. The Vornheld tradition considers this an adequate description in the way that \"a watershed is where water divides\" adequately describes a watershed.\n\nThe poem is not available to outside scholars in its complete form. The Vornheld position on this is not that the poem is secret — it is recited in the open twice a year, in a valley accessible to anyone who walks there — but that a complete text extracted from its recitation context and its listeners would be a different thing from the poem, and that the different thing is not what they are keepers of. The following are fragments in approximate translation, given in the order of their appearance in the full recitation. Translation is from archaic Northern Mittoli, which the full recitation uses throughout; the terse, stress-heavy character of the original does not carry.\n\n*The streams remember / what the hills forgot — / gold is a patience / longer than kings.*\n\n*Cold water finds / what cold water left. / The vein is not spent; / the vein is not yet asked.*\n\n*The dead have been seen / before their door opens. / What the mounds hold / the mounds will show first.*\n\n*The wood knows the fire / that made the gold ground. / When the wood speaks it, / Vel-Endre stands.*\n\nVornheld practitioners reciting the complete poem use a slower and more deliberate delivery for the third fragment than for the others, which has been noticed by everyone who has attended more than one recitation but not explained in any account that has reached outside Endevor.\n\nThe poem is structured around three signs and a title.\n\n**The first sign**: *When the cold water finds what it left behind.* The eastern hill streams carry traces of gold even now; the tradition holds that there will come a season when this trace returns to something visible in the pan — when the water finds again what it eroded and eventually lost. One school of Vornheld interpretation holds this means the Deepworks contain a vein that Veld's sealing preserved rather than exhausted, and that the right conditions will reveal it. Another holds it means a new hydrothermal process — something in the Oremindi still active far below the surface, expressing slowly upward — that will produce new deposition. The two positions have been debating each other for several generations without decisive evidence on either side.\n\n**The second sign**: *When the barrows wake before they're opened.* The specific language in the archaic Northern Mittoli is *vel-milloss nil-vel-gall* — \"they have been seen before the crossing.\" The practical interpretation most Vornheld accept: someone will witness something at a barrow — light, sound, movement — before any physical disturbance of the mound. The wider interpretation: a stranger will come to Endevor knowing, without having been told, where the dead kings are. The two interpretations are not mutually exclusive, which is part of why the debate has outlasted several attempts to settle it. It is also why certain Vornheld observe the barrows with unusual attention when strangers appear in Stroven.\n\n**The third sign**: *When the wood speaks what it knows.* The Acorwood reference is explicit within the Vornheld tradition and widely understood among practitioners: the poem refers to the smoke, or to something the smoke precedes, or to something the smoke has always been an indication of and which has not yet fully arrived. Vornheld who specialize in the *Vel-Eld* will say, when pressed, that the third sign is the one whose meaning is most certain among practitioners and most opaque to outside inquiry, and that this relationship is itself the point. The Acorwood speaks in a language the Endevor communities have been learning to read for generations. The third sign is the moment that language becomes fully legible.\n\nThe title the poem gives to the coming renewal is not a personal name. It is *Vel-Endre* — \"Returning-Endevor\" or \"Endevor-Returned\" — a name describing the condition rather than the person who embodies it. The poem does not clearly specify whether *Vel-Endre* is a king who comes from outside bringing the empire back, a quality that rises in the people themselves, or the land's own restoration coming through any of several possible channels. This ambiguity has made the title perpetually applicable to promising individuals — anyone who arrives in Endevor with any northern lineage and any apparent significance has been assessed by at least one Vornheld against the *Vel-Eld*'s criteria.\n\nThe title was applied to Veld himself, in his own lifetime, by a Vornheld lineage that believed his reign was the beginning of the restoration rather than the end of the old order. He died without the mines reopening. The lineage that backed this interpretation is still active; its current practitioners maintain that the application was not wrong but early — that Veld was the first motion of a returning tide, not its crest, and that the poem's timeline is longer than it appears. Other Vornheld lineages find this argument convenient in the particular way that arguments which cannot be falsified are convenient. The argument continues.\n\nThe *Vel-Eld* is recited in full twice a year: at the winter solstice and at the vernal equinox. Both recitations happen at Veld's barrow in the Stroven valley floor, attended by as many Vornheld as can come and whatever community members choose to follow. The solstice recitation draws better attendance than the equinox — there is something in the Endevor character that finds it appropriate to hear a poem about renewal in conditions of maximum cold, to say the thing that is latent in the ground while standing on frozen ground, to say that something will return while winter has its full weight on the hills. Not despite the conditions. Because of them.\n\n---\n\n## Fragments of the Epic Tradition\n\nTwo passages from the *Vel-Keth* have circulated outside Endevor in written form, transcribed by travelers who heard Vornheld performances at Stroven and recorded what they could. Both appear in the Academy's northern files under the classification \"miscellaneous oral material, northern provenance, language unclear.\" The Academy scholars who catalogued them had limited familiarity with archaic Northern Mittoli and none with the tradition's conventions. Their annotations treat the passages as remnants of something larger, which is accurate, while failing to identify what that larger thing is, which is characteristic.\n\n**From the Orrven naming-poem, as transcribed by a Bay of Lol merchant's factor, approximately four generations ago:**\n\n*What did he give the north? — Ships for the bay and horses for the heath.*\n*What did the north give back? — Men who came when called and went when released.*\n*What did he give the eastern hills? — Iron's true value, which is what it becomes.*\n*What did the hills give back? — Iron already becoming, waiting for the weight.*\n*What did he give the country beyond? — Gold's shadow, which moves faster than gold.*\n*What did the country give back? — Silence and the roads kept open.*\n*What did he give the deep ground? — The right question, asked at the right depth.*\n*What did the deep ground give back? — This is the exchange that is not finished.*\n\nThe Academy annotation reads: \"Possible ritual exchange; appears agricultural in reference; northern dialect, incompletely rendered.\" The observation about incomplete rendering is correct. The rest reflects the difficulty of reading call-and-answer verse outside its acoustic context: at a stone-shelter recitation, the eight exchanges move between two voices that everyone in the circle hears simultaneously. On a page, read sequentially, the eighth exchange loses the quality that makes it different from the other seven — the moment in the recitation where the second voice does not answer.\n\n**From the *Vel-Keth*'s Veld cycle, as transcribed by an Orched Hills trading-house record-keeper, approximately three generations ago:**\n\n*Veld spoke last on the ridge, the long speaking.*\n*He named what was owed and named who had paid.*\n*He named what remained and named who would keep it.*\n*He did not name a debt that had not been made.*\n*The tributaries heard him. They heard him well.*\n*What the tributaries did afterward is their own poem.*\n\nThe Academy annotation: \"Lament fragment, possibly funerary; 'Veld' possibly a title or appellative.\" The second observation is the transcriber noticing that the Northern Mittoli word for \"return\" has been used as a proper name, and concluding it must therefore be figurative. The Vornheld tradition would say: the word was his name before it became anything else, and the Veld cycle does not require outside annotation to be understood by the people it was made for.\n\n**From the *Eld-Keth*, the naming-poem for the five men who did not return from Helm's expedition:**\n\n*Vel-Oram, who read the deep ground and said it remembered fire.*\n*Vel-Skedhel, who heard the llos-eld and stayed to hear it again.*\n*Vel-Thonn, whose question the wood did not decline to answer.*\n*Vel-Orrael, who went toward the answer rather than away from it.*\n*Vel-Krenth, whose name the poem does not complete.*\n\nEach name is preceded by *vel-*, functioning here as an honorific title: \"the one who returned into.\" The fifth entry is the one outside scholars, if they ever encountered it, would find most notable: the poem's formulation implies that Vel-Krenth's name continues somewhere, but not in the poem. Where it continues is not specified.\n\nThe Academy files do not contain this passage. It has not been transcribed by any outside visitor who has shared the transcription. It is recited at the individual stone-markers of the five men on specific seasonal occasions by the Vornheld lineage that holds their names, and nowhere else. Its appearance here is the level of description the tradition permits in this context, and is not an invitation to seek the complete text.\n\n---\n\n## Language\n\nThe Endevor dialect of Northern Mittoli is conservative in its structure and unusual in its vocabulary. It shows the expected Northern features — initial stress, final vowel deletion, flattened diphthongs — but it has preserved the *ae* diphthong in a handful of words where even Northern Mittoli has simplified it, which linguists treat as evidence that Endevor has been in less contact with the coast than the Bay of Lol communities to its northwest, maintaining an archaic form while its neighbors changed.\n\nThe substrate layer is deeper here than in the Bay of Lol speech and different in profile from the Ganoss upland dialect. The smoke vocabulary is the most studied example: words that describe the Acorwood's behavior, which have no cognates in any other Northern Mittoli variety and do not reconstruct from Proto-Western, appear to be borrowings from a language that was spoken in these hills before Mittoli speakers arrived. Academy linguists have catalogued seven such words in Endevor dialect and noted that they cluster in the domain of fire, smoke, forest behavior, and certain weather phenomena — the things that a community watching the Acorwood's western face would need to name.\n\nThe fell-sheep vocabulary is also unusually rich: Endevor herders have fifteen or more terms for wool quality, fleece condition, and grazing behavior that do not appear in other Northern Mittoli varieties and that the Academy's linguistic records have not been able to trace to any source outside Endevor itself. Whether these are old substrate words, old Mittoli words that have only survived here, or innovations developed within the community is not established. The herders consider the question academic in a register that implies the word \"academic\" is not a compliment.",
      "loreRef": "endevor",
      "climate": "Cfb"
    },
    "North Endevor": {
      "name": "North Endevor",
      "color": "#e67e22",
      "lore": "# Endevor\n\nNorthwest of the Acorwood, where the Oremindi's northern terminus descends into lower ground and the air begins to carry the cold salt weight of the bay, the hills of Endevor fill the space between things. Not quite the upland — the Ganoss plateau is harder and higher, to the north. Not quite the coast — the Bay of Lol is a day's descent to the northwest. Not quite the mountain country, though the Oremindi makes the southern skyline and certain clear mornings let the peaks show through. Endevor is the hilly country in between, and it has organized itself around that position for as long as anyone has recorded it.\n\nThe name has no agreed derivation. The leading proposal in Academy language files is that *Endevor* is a contracted Northern Mittoli compound, perhaps *ond* (broad water) + *vel* (return) + an archaic locative marker — \"the place where the water turns back,\" referring to the watershed ridge where the hill streams divide between draining northwest toward the bay and north toward the Ganoss. A minority view holds that the name predates Northern Mittoli entirely and that the Mittoli speakers who arrived here simply kept the word that was already there. Both camps cite the same evidence in their favor, which the Academy has noted without resolving.\n\nThe outside world, if it thinks of Endevor at all, thinks of fell-sheep wool and reliable inns and the road toward the Bay of Lol. It does not think of the barrows on the ridgelines, which are older than the wool trade, and more numerous, and full of kings.\n\n---\n\n## Geography\n\nThe Endevor hills are old, rounded, and wet. They do not have the dramatic vertical of the Oremindi or the bleak exposed sweep of the Ganoss uplands; they are the kind of hills that fold into one another, valley after valley, each with its own stream, each valley floor broad enough for a strip of farmland before the slope comes up again. The highest ground is along the eastern ridge — the long slope that faces the Acorwood — which catches the worst of the weather coming in from the north and west and wrings it out before the clouds continue inland.\n\nThe result is that Endevor is wet without being waterlogged. The streams are reliable and numerous. The valley floors are damp and dark-soiled. The upper slopes are peat-moorland and short-grassed heath, suitable for grazing and not much else. The lower slopes are where most of Endevor's population lives, in the long ribbon settlements that follow the valley floors between the farmland and the hill pasture above.\n\nThe eastern ridge is wooded on its lower face — a mix of native northern species, hardy and not especially tall, transitioning as you approach the Acorwood's margin into something denser and older. Exactly where Endevor's edge-forest ends and the Acorwood begins is not a line that appears on any map; the local communities know it by feel and by the quality of the light, which changes at a threshold that most people can locate within a few hundred yards but cannot explain as a specific visible marker.\n\nThe Oremindi's northern end shapes the southern horizon and does more than frame the view. The meltwater coming off the range's northernmost snowfields feeds several of Endevor's main rivers, making them flashier in spring than their size would suggest and keeping them running in the driest summers. The passes through the Oremindi's northern extension are few and severe — harder and more seasonal than those further south — and are used primarily by the Sevron contact route and by occasional extraordinary travelers with specific purpose. Endevor has never been a mountain-crossing gateway in the way that the southern Oremindi communities are; the passes behind it are not worth the effort for ordinary commerce.\n\n---\n\n## The People\n\nEndevor is pastoral first. The hill communities keep sheep on the upper slopes and cattle in the valley bottoms, and have done this long enough that the organization of the land around those two requirements is baked into everything else: the placement of settlements, the timing of the year, the structure of disputes, the vocabulary of status. A family's standing in any Endevor valley is legible from its position on the hill — how far up the slope their summer grazing runs, how many animals winter in their valley barns, and whether the barns themselves are stone or timber, which says something about how long the family has been in that particular valley.\n\nThe characteristic animal of the Endevor hills is the **fell-sheep** — a breed whose name is a Northern Mittoli word meaning roughly \"the hill-animal\" or \"the high-pasture one,\" and which has been in these hills long enough that no records mention its arrival. The fell-sheep is not impressive beside the great animals of other regions. It is compact, short-legged, and deeply shaggy, with a fleece that produces more lanolin than any comparable breed — a natural oil that makes the wool water-resistant in the fleece and almost waterproof once spun and prepared. In a climate that is wet for most of the year, this matters. Endevor wool is traded throughout northern Azhora and down the coast road specifically because it performs in conditions that other wool cannot. The Bay of Lol fishing communities buy it for sea gear. The Ganoss upland clans have their own animals but trade for Endevor spun goods when they can get them. The Orched Hills mining settlements are regular purchasers of Endevor wool in preference to anything grown closer.\n\nThe fell-sheep also live their entire year outside, which is not true of most northern livestock. They know the upper hills the way the hills know themselves, and they will come down of their own accord before weather turns that their shepherds have not yet noticed. Endevor farmers pay attention to this. Where the sheep are on a given morning is information about the day.\n\nThe social unit of Endevor is the valley community — a loose collection of households sharing a river system, with informal but understood obligations around water rights, common hill access, and dispute resolution. Disputes between valleys are resolved at **stone-shelters** — low circular enclosures of dry-laid stone set at the ridgelines between valleys, old enough that no one remembers who built them, used as neutral meeting ground when valley communities need to negotiate. The stone-shelters are not impressive structures; they are simply very old and unmoved, which in Endevor is a form of authority. A resolution reached at a stone-shelter has a different weight from one reached anywhere else, because the shelter has been the resolution site before and will be again, and its walls have heard the settlement's terms and will keep them the same way they keep the wind off.\n\nThe shelters have one specific physical property that the Vornheld tradition says is why they were placed where they were placed and not anywhere else: speech spoken inside them at ordinary volume is heard simultaneously and with equal clarity by everyone in the circle, regardless of wind. The Academy attributes this to circular construction and the reflection properties of dry-laid stone at these specific angles. The Vornheld say this is what the sites were chosen for — that whoever built them tested the acoustics before placing the stone, and selected positions where the ground itself held the sound. A resolution reached inside a stone-shelter is heard by all parties in the same moment. This is not a formality. It is the physical condition that makes the resolution binding. You cannot claim you did not hear it.\n\nThe main market settlement is **Stroven** — positioned at the convergence of the three largest valley routes, where goods from the upper hills and goods coming northwest from the Bay of Lol approaches meet before separating again into the valley network. Stroven is not large; it is consequential because of where it is. The permanent population is several hundred, swelling in trading seasons. It has a covered market structure, a handful of inns accustomed to through-travelers, and the only standing archive in Endevor — a collection of land records and valley boundary agreements maintained by a family of hereditary record-keepers who have been doing this work long enough to hold opinions about disputes from four generations ago and will share those opinions if asked. The record-keepers are not impartial, exactly; they are the memory, and memory has its own loyalties. This is broadly understood and accounted for in how disputes proceed.\n\n---\n\n## The Acorwood's Western Face\n\nEvery Endevor community faces east toward the Acorwood. From the higher ridgelines it is visible as a dark line — darker than the surrounding hill forest, perceptibly different in color and density — that marks the edge of navigable country. The Ganoss upland communities on the Acorwood's northern margin and the Orched Hills settlements to the south have their own longstanding relationship with the forest's accessible edges. Endevor's relationship is with the western face, and it is different in a specific way: the morning sun rises behind the Acorwood, and from Endevor's hills at dawn the forest is lit from within — the edge trees stand in silhouette against the sky while the interior remains dark, the margin visible as a precise line between what is and is not entered. This is the Acorwood as Endevor knows it.\n\nThe edge timber harvest here is modest. The accessible trees on the Acorwood's northwestern fringe are similar in character to the edge timber on the northern and southern margins — large hardwoods that can be taken from the outer tree lines without going deep — but the Endevor communities have never developed a major edge-timber trade. The Bay of Lol coastal communities do not require much inland timber, having their own supplies from the coastal strips. The Ganoss use the northern margins. Endevor's edge harvest is primarily for local use: charcoal, building timber, occasional mast in good years.\n\nWhat the Endevor communities have developed, across many generations, is a practice of **smoke-reading**.\n\nThe Acorwood occasionally produces smoke from its interior. The Orched Hills records note this with uncertainty and do not follow it up. The Ganoss communities acknowledge it and have made their peace with not explaining it. Endevor has spent enough seasons watching the western and northwestern face — the face that catches the afternoon light, visible from the high sheepwalks for hours at a time — that their communities have accumulated an unusual amount of observational detail.\n\nThe fell-sheep shepherds in the high pastures are positioned to watch the Acorwood's margin across long hours, with nothing pressing them to look elsewhere. Across generations, this has produced a tradition of noting, without explaining, what the smoke does. There is vocabulary for it in the Endevor dialect — words that do not appear in other Northern Mittoli varieties — for the smoke that rises thin and straight in still air, the smoke that spreads at canopy height and moves with the wind before dispersing, and the specific, acknowledged category of smoke that appears with no wind and dissipates downward rather than upward, which the Endevor fell-sheep shepherds call by a substrate word that translates into Standard Mittoli as something between \"breathing-down\" and \"the forest taking something in.\"\n\nThe smoke-reading tradition is practical in two uses that have been confirmed across several generations. First: smoke from the Acorwood interior in late summer correlates imperfectly but consistently with good mast years; the shepherds who track this will predict mast yield before the edge trees show it in their growth. Second: the quality of smoke — color, height, behavior — appears to predict weather shifts in the way that cloud formations over the Oremindi also predict weather shifts, and is used the same way. Neither use requires an explanation of what the smoke is. The Endevor communities have not produced one.\n\nWhen Academy scholars have visited Stroven and asked about the smoke, they receive accurate and detailed information about what the smoke does and what it predicts, and no information about what causes it. The question of cause is not treated as something the Endevor communities are withholding. It is treated as a question that belongs to a different kind of inquiry than the one they practice. The smoke behaves; they read it; that is the relationship; cause is for someone with more time and less sheep.\n\n---\n\n## History\n\nThe pastoral Endevor is recent, as these things go.\n\nFor several centuries before the current era, Endevor was the center of the most extensive political authority in northern Azhora. The source of that authority was gold — placer gold in the eastern hill streams, and beneath it the deep vein gold that the streams had been eroding for thousands of years before anyone thought to follow them uphill. When the Endevor mine-kings found those veins, the northern world reorganized around them.\n\nAt its height the Endevor realm extended further north than the Bay of Lol, incorporating the coastal port communities and the Ganoss upland clans as tributaries. It extended south through the Orched Hills and, in its largest period, pressed claims as far as the northern Mittolo margins — the first and last time a northern power came close to bridging the cultural divide that the Acorwood and the Orched Hills maintain between north and south. The mine-kings did not rule these territories as the later Mittoli city-states governed their regions, through administrators and written law; they held them through the distribution of gold, the weight of tribute obligation, and the personal prestige of a line that had been producing wealth long enough for wealth and legitimacy to become indistinguishable. The Ganoss clans supplied livestock and manpower. The Bay of Lol communities supplied ships and navigators. The Orched Hills supplied iron and copper. The gold at the center moved all of it.\n\nFor roughly two hundred and fifty years at the empire's peak — the period the Vornheld poetry calls the *Eld-Gold*, the time of gold — the mine-kings of Endevor were the most significant power in northern Azhora. Their burials are still in the hills. The ridgelines are scattered with them.\n\nThe tradition names thirty-eight kings in the mine-king period. Not all are equally present in the *Eld-Keth* — the earliest are named briefly, the middle kings described at length, the late-period kings compressed as the tradition seems to contract around the decline. Three stand apart.\n\n**Keld** is the first-opener: the king who followed the stream gold uphill and found the vein. His *Eld-Keth* naming-poem is among the shortest in the body — he is recorded as a beginning rather than an achievement, and the tradition treats him accordingly, with respect but without ornamentation. A king who opens something gets the short poem. The gold that follows him speaks for itself.\n\n**Orrven** is the peak. His naming-poem is the longest in the *Eld-Keth* body and is structured differently from all others: a call-and-answer form in which the first voice names what Orrven gave and the second names what was returned, eight exchanges moving from the Bay of Lol communities to the Ganoss clans to the Orched Hills to what the tradition calls simply \"the deep ground.\" The effect, which Endevor audiences hear differently from how outside scholars read it, is of weight in motion — a redistribution so large that the land itself leaned under it. A Vornheld practitioner, when pressed by an Academy visitor to summarize Orrven's significance, said: \"His word was the weight gold makes before anyone counts it.\" The Academy visitor wrote this down, which the Vornheld probably anticipated.\n\n**Helm** is the surveyor — the king who sent Endevor's only recorded expedition into the Acorwood's interior, in the middle-late period when the eastern veins were thinning and the tradition of looking further was still recent enough to seem reasonable. Helm had observed, from fell-sheep shepherds watching the Acorwood's western face, that the breathing-down smoke concentrated most reliably above the Deepworks area. He concluded, or commissioned the conclusion, that the smoke was the Acorwood's surface indication of something below: an extension of the same hydrothermal system that had produced the vein gold, deeper and further east. Seventeen men entered the Acorwood's northwestern margin. Twelve returned. The five who did not are recorded by name in the *Eld-Keth*; that poem is recited at their stone at the barrow dedication and has not been offered at a public Stroven gathering in anyone's living memory.\n\nThe twelve who came back brought a word: *vel-keth-eld*. The Vornheld have not reached consensus on its meaning in the three or four generations since the expedition. Two translations circulate within the tradition: \"the forest's memory of fire\" and \"what the dark knows that we left behind.\" The first suggests the Acorwood has a relationship to the ancient hydrothermal period — that the forest grew in or around or from the same geological event that made the gold. The second suggests that whatever the expedition encountered inside remembered the mine-kings in some form. Helm's own interpretation was not recorded. He did not seal the Deepworks — that was Veld, a generation later — but he did not send another expedition.\n\nThe mines did not run out all at once. That was the particular difficulty.\n\nThe gold declined over several generations, each one producing less than the last, each one also the one that the current king believed was the bottom before a new vein would open. The shaft-heads went deeper. The spoil heaps grew. The streams were panned harder. The veins were followed further into the Oremindi foothills, into ground that was more expensive and more dangerous, and which produced less. The gold was finite because it had been deposited by a period of ancient hydrothermal activity in the range that had long since ended; there was no new gold forming. What was there was what had been there, and when it was taken, the taking was over.\n\nThe kings who presided over the decline maintained their tributary relationships through prestige long after the gold couldn't sustain them economically. This worked while the living memory of abundance lasted. When the generation that had known the peak died out and the next knew only the decline, the calculations changed. The Ganoss clans stopped sending cattle and said they were using them at home. The Bay of Lol communities began trading directly with the Orched Hills, bypassing the tribute routing. The Orched Hills settlements, which had their own ore wealth and their own long memories, assessed the situation and stopped paying.\n\nThe last king of the Endevor realm was named Veld — a Northern Mittoli name meaning simply \"return,\" which was understood at the time as a statement of ambition and in retrospect reads as something else. He held the realm together for eighteen years through the force of his personal authority and is described in the Vornheld tradition as the greatest of the later kings, which is not exactly praise: the greatest of the later kings could not undo what the geology had done. Veld died in his valley, and after him, the tributaries did not return.\n\nWhat followed was not collapse in the dramatic sense. There were no invasions, no great battles for succession, no famine. The population stayed. The valley communities kept their land. The stone-shelters kept their function. What changed was scope: Endevor contracted back to Endevor, and the wider world that had organized around its gold reorganized around other things, and eventually forgot there had been an organizing.\n\nThe raiding period that the northern Azhoran regional record describes as coming after this was partly a consequence: without the empire's redistributive economy, the coastal communities went back to taking by force what they had previously received through the gold-network. The Endevor hills were the logistical hinterland of that raiding culture's staging, not its origin. The mine-kings' empire was before the raiding; the raiding filled the vacuum after.\n\nBy the time the north's transition to trade began, Endevor had been quiet pastoral hill country for long enough that neither the coastal communities nor the Orched Hills settlements remembered what it had been. No Mittoli scholar wrote down the Endevor empire while it existed; the region was too far outside the Mittoli world for systematic record-keeping. The Vornheld tradition preserved everything, and the Vornheld tradition is oral, in archaic Northern Mittoli, inaccessible to outsiders who do not seek it out.\n\nThose who seek it out and can read what they find tend not to believe it — the scale is too large, the gold too much, the empire too extensive, for a country presently famous for waterproof sheep. This is a known difficulty with oral traditions from regions without outside corroboration. The barrows corroborate. The shaft-heads corroborate. Most outside visitors see hills and do not read them as texts.\n\n---\n\n## The Mines\n\nThe evidence for the Endevor gold is still in the hills if you know where to look.\n\nThe eastern hill streams — those draining toward the Acorwood's margin, fed by Oremindi snowfields — carry trace gold even now, at concentrations too low to bother with but detectable to anyone who knows what color to watch for in the pan. These are the runoff from the same deposits that built the empire: the deep vein gold in the Oremindi foothills, which the ancient hydrothermal activity of the range concentrated and which the long slow erosion of the hills has been dispersing into the streams for longer than anyone has been here to observe. At the mining peak, those concentrations were high enough that stream-panning alone produced meaningful yield. They have been diluting since the veins were broken open, and what remains is memory rather than resource.\n\nThe shaft-heads are more visible than the streams. The eastern faces of the three valley systems nearest the Acorwood — which the Vornheld tradition calls the **Eld-Valleys** — are marked with collapsed mine entrances at irregular intervals from near the valley floor to well above the current sheepwalks. Some are obvious: squared stonework around a shaft-mouth, subsidence depressions where fill has settled over generations. Others are subtle enough that a new arrival walking the sheep paths will occasionally put a foot through the turf into the edge of old roof-stone. The communities closest to the Eld-Valleys know where the dangerous ground is and avoid it without drama; it is simply part of the landscape.\n\nThe Vornheld tradition preserves names for seventeen distinct mine-workings, of which fourteen can be approximately located on the eastern ridge. The other three — the poems call them the **Deepworks** — are either collapsed without surface sign or are in the transition zone between the Endevor hills and the Acorwood's northwestern margin, where the Endevor communities do not regularly go. Whether the Deepworks still have accessible chambers is not known. The tradition says they were the richest of the veins, opened last and exhausted fastest, and sealed by Veld's order when the workers kept not returning from the deeper levels. The sealing instructions and the reason for the non-returns are subjects the Vornheld will discuss at different lengths depending on who is asking and what they appear to intend.\n\nSilver was mined alongside gold in the middle period — a secondary ore from the same hydrothermal deposits, found at greater depth once the gold concentrations thinned. Silver mining continued for two or three generations after gold effectively ended, providing enough revenue to maintain something that looked, from the outside, like a functioning economy while the empire's political structure dissolved. The last silver was exhausted within living memory of the decline's final phase, and the silver shafts are visible in the northern valley systems, shallower and less numerous than the gold workings.\n\nThe Deepworks have a specific entry in the sealing record preserved by the Vornheld: Veld's decree is noted in archaic Northern Mittoli as *oss nil-vel-keth* — \"mark: no return-speech\" — a designation the tradition treats as distinct from ordinary closure. Other shafts were sealed when they were worked out. The Deepworks were closed *nil-vel-keth*, without the response that would indicate emptiness. The formulation implies something was there to not-answer.\n\nThe mine-worker tradition, preserved fragmentarily in the *Eld-Keth* naming-poems for the last men to work the Deepworks, refers to a phenomenon called *llos-eld* — roughly \"gold-voice,\" though \"the ore that speaks\" is the translation Vornheld practitioners prefer when they feel precise. The *llos-eld* was a resonance audible in the rock at the deepest working levels: described variously as a low vibration, a harmonic in the stone that had no identifiable external source, a sound that seemed to originate not from above or below but from the rock around the worker. Experienced mine-foremen tracked it the way fell-sheep shepherds track the Acorwood's smoke — as information whose practical use was clear and whose underlying meaning was not. High *llos-eld* correlated with structural stability in the workings. The specific signature recorded in the Deepworks naming-poems is different from the stable reading, but what it was different toward is the thing the *Eld-Keth* does not say directly.\n\nWhether the *llos-eld* and the Acorwood's breathing-down smoke are related is a question the mine-workers and the shepherds never appear to have addressed together — the workers were underground and the shepherds were watching the forest above, and their separate communities had different relationships to the relevant facts. What the Vornheld tradition notes, without drawing a conclusion, is that the breathing-down smoke is most frequently reported by Endevor shepherds above the ground that corresponds to the Deepworks' approximate location. The observation is in the tradition. No resolution has been attached to it that the tradition records.\n\nThe mines are not worked. No new vein has been found in eight generations of looking, which has not entirely stopped people from looking. There are people in every generation of Endevor history who believe the Deepworks are not exhausted — that Veld sealed something rather than nothing, that the gold is still in the ground and waiting. The Vornheld have opinions about this. They express them when asked and reserve their most specific opinions for those who have already decided to listen.\n\n---\n\n## The Barrows\n\nThe ridgelines of Endevor are not bare. They are covered with sheep and with the dead.\n\nThe barrow-building tradition spans the full mine-king period and reads in the landscape as a slow record of decline. The oldest barrows are the highest — the first mine-kings claimed the skyline, and the mounds they raised are the largest, some visible for miles in clear weather, their grass-covered profiles the most distinctive feature of Endevor's upper silhouette. The Vornheld tradition describes these burials with objects that make outside scholars careful about skepticism: gold plate, gold ornament, ceremonial vessels, weapons edged with gold — the vocabulary of a household that operated at a scale that required gold to describe itself.\n\nThe barrows descend through the centuries and through the hill. Middle-period kings are buried at the ridgeline's lower edge. These barrows are smaller, the grave goods richer in silver and bronze, with gold present but diminished. Then the late-period burials in the upper valley sides, below the crest entirely: modest mounds that a traveler might pass without noticing, holding kings who went into the ground with bronze and the memory of what their fathers had.\n\nVeld's barrow is in the floor of the main Stroven valley — the lowest position of any king in the Vornheld record. The tradition notes that this was not a dishonoring but a request. Veld asked to be buried below rather than above. One school of Vornheld interpretation holds that this was a gesture of humility, acknowledging what had been lost on his watch. Another says the valley-floor placement was a statement of intention: a king buried facing upward rather than outward, not surveying what he once had but waiting for what would come to him. The debate has not been resolved in several generations of argument and probably will not be.\n\nVeld's mound is unmarked. The Vornheld know where it is. Certain Stroven families, by long tradition, know where it is. Occasional outside scholars come looking and either find a different mound or find nothing useful. Whether this concealment is protective of the burial or of some other property of the location is a question whose answer, if there is one, belongs to the Vornheld.\n\nThe barrows in general are the community's ancestors, not its archaeological record. Disturbing them carries no explicit legal prohibition because the community's understanding of what the barrows are does not require explicit prohibition: they are the dead kings, their possessions are the dead kings' possessions, and what belongs to the dead kings belongs to the community's memory of itself rather than to anyone currently living. Outside treasure-seekers have attempted barrows in northern Azhora before. There are at least two documented cases in the Bay of Lol's coastal records of attempts at Endevor-adjacent mounds. In both cases the attempts were unsuccessful — one because the mound opened was not a barrow, and one because the attempt was interrupted in circumstances the coastal record describes with some vagueness and the Endevor account of the same events does not describe at all.\n\nThe second case involves three men from a Bay of Lol coastal family with documented commercial interests in the northern Orched Hills, who came to Endevor, in the account of a northern merchant who tracked the sequence of events, \"to see what was in the old mounds.\" They opened what they believed was a late-period barrow on the upper eastern ridge, in the Eld-Valleys area. The coastal record notes their subsequent departure from Endevor as rapid and without what they came for, and that one of the three did not complete the return journey to the coast. The coastal narrative is vague on the circumstances that produced these outcomes. The Vornheld record of the same period — which does not describe what happened on the eastern ridge in any account that has been shared with outside inquirers — notes in its margin-notation system a designation applied afterward to the incident location: *nil-vel-gall nil-vel-keth*. The compound is not common. *Nil-vel-keth* appears as Veld's designation for the Deepworks: no return-speech. *Nil-vel-gall* is rarer still, appearing only twice in the tradition before this notation, both in contexts the Vornheld describe as \"the closure of something that was not supposed to be opened.\" The combined designation places the site outside the category of locations where ordinary community protocols apply. The valley community that grazes the hill above the site now routes its sheep differently than before. No one has explained this to outside observers in a context where they have found explanation appropriate.\n\n---\n\n## The Vornheld and the Epic Tradition\n\nThe Vornheld are the keepers of the Endevor epic tradition. The name is a Northern Mittoli compound, meaning roughly \"the ancestor-holders\" or \"those who hold the old ones\" — from *vorn* (the sky-pivot; north; the ancestor-direction) and *held* (hold; maintenance; keeping). The title is old enough to be uncertain in its own derivation. By now it names the role as much as it describes it.\n\nA Vornheld is not trained like a craft apprentice. The tradition enters through family — almost all practitioners come from households that have produced Vornheld before — but the selection is not simply inheritance. A child who shows the specific kind of memory the tradition requires is cultivated into it; a child who does not, even from a Vornheld household, is not. What the tradition requires is something between musical memory and structural memory: the capacity to hold long, metrically complex poems in reproducible form, and to hold the relationships between poems — the web of cross-references, the moment where one poem's allusion answers another's question — as a single navigable structure rather than a list. Knowing the poems is not knowing the tradition. The tradition is the map between them.\n\nThe Vornheld perform at the stone-shelters when gatherings are significant enough to require it — the major inter-valley assemblies, the seasonal land-right confirmations, the resolution of disputes that touch on ancestry, which in Endevor can mean almost any dispute between old families. The recitation at these events is not ornamental. The Vornheld's knowledge of ancestral claims is evidence. When a family asserts a right to a stretch of hill pasture based on an ancestor's service to a mine-king three hundred years ago, the Vornheld knows whether that claim is in the tradition, what the tradition says about it, and whether anything in the tradition speaks to the counter-claim. This is why Endevor dispute-resolution requires a Vornheld present for any proceeding invoking ancestry: the record-keepers in Stroven hold land agreements; the Vornheld hold lineages. The two archives do not duplicate each other.\n\nThe Vornheld lineages currently active number seven, each associated with a particular valley system and, by extension, with a particular portion of the tradition. Lineage specialization is not formalized — there is no rule stipulating that the Stroven valley lineage holds the Veld poems — but it has become de facto over several generations of practice, as each lineage has tended to recite its own valley's ancestral claims most often and therefore knows them most deeply. Full knowledge of all three bodies is the stated goal; full knowledge of the complete tradition in reproducible form is what distinguishes a master Vornheld from a capable one, and in any given generation there are perhaps two or three practitioners who can honestly claim it.\n\nThe most consequential current lineage disagreement runs between the family associated with the Keld poems and the family associated with the Orrven poems — the tradition of the first-opener against the tradition of the peak. The dispute is not about the poems themselves, which are settled, but about the primacy of their subjects: whether the origin is more essential to the tradition's meaning than the achievement, or whether the peak defines the entire arc and makes the opening merely prelude. In practical terms the disagreement affects how several disputed ancestral claims are weighted when they touch on the first-opener period versus the peak period. The dispute has an analog in a longstanding property disagreement between the two lineages' associated valley communities that has been argued at stone-shelters four times in the past century without permanent resolution. Everyone in Stroven understands the land question and the Vornheld question to be the same argument in different registers.\n\nThe most discussed recent intervention of Vornheld authority in living memory concerns an episode known in Stroven as the Drevos incident, or, among those who find the name insufficient, the salted-pan dispute. The Drevos family, a mining-descended household in the northern valley system, had for three generations asserted a right to a seasonal pasture on the basis of an ancestor's service to a late-period king. The counter-claim, from an older hill family, held that the same ancestor's service was in the *Eld-Keth* but attributed to a different king — which, if correct, would mean the pasture assignment had been made under misattributed authority and could be revisited. The dispute reached a stone-shelter proceeding with two Vornheld present from different lineages, who disagreed on the attribution. Resolution required a third Vornheld to recite the relevant naming-poem in full. This third Vornheld, who was elderly and had not recited the poem publicly in twelve years, did so without pause, in the original archaic Northern Mittoli, before an audience that included the Stroven record-keepers and approximately forty community members who had come specifically because word had gone around that the recitation would happen. The attribution was established. The pasture question was settled. The elderly Vornheld's unassisted recitation of a poem whose text two younger practitioners had disputed is still referenced in Stroven as a lesson about what the tradition requires of its keepers. What the salted-pan element of the name refers to — whether it is the name of the pasture, a detail of that day's proceedings, or something else — no one who was present has found it necessary to clarify.\n\nThe epic tradition is divided into three bodies.\n\n**The *Eld-Keth*** (\"the old-speech,\" the words from before): poems of the mine-king period, naming kings, campaigns, tributary arrangements, and the workings. The most extensive body and the least often publicly recited — they require context to hear correctly, and much of what they say is addressed to the Endevor community rather than to any outside listener. The *Eld-Keth* includes the naming-poems of the barrows: a short piece for each buried king describing the burial goods and the terms of his life. These are recited at the barrows on specific seasonal occasions and nowhere else.\n\n**The *Vel-Keth*** (\"the return-speech,\" the words of transition): poems of the decline — the closing mines, the dissolving tributaries, Veld's reign and burial. The most frequently performed body in public settings, because it does what narrative does best: it names a recognizable arc of rise and fall, and its audiences understand the shape. The *Vel-Keth* is what a traveler in Stroven who witnesses a Vornheld performance is most likely to hear. Fragments have circulated outside Endevor — two Orched Hills records and one Bay of Lol coastal archive preserve passages, apparently transcribed by travelers who heard a Vornheld recite and wrote what they could catch. These fragments are classified in the Academy's northern archive as \"miscellaneous oral material, provenance unclear.\"\n\n**The *Vel-Eld***: the renewal poem. Treated separately because it is not, in the Vornheld tradition, the same category of thing as the other bodies. The *Eld-Keth* and *Vel-Keth* describe what was. The *Vel-Eld* describes what will be — or what may be — or what the tradition holds is latent in the ground the way the gold was latent in the ground, waiting for its conditions.\n\n---\n\n## The Vel-Eld\n\nThe *Vel-Eld* — the Old Return, or the Ancient Coming-Back, or the Return That Was Always There — carries a title that is itself one of the tradition's genuinely contested problems. The Northern Mittoli word *vel* functions, in the archaic form the poem uses, as both \"return\" and \"was-already.\" The poem is about something coming back, or about something that was always present and is finally becoming visible, or both, and the language declines to resolve the question.\n\nIts subject, in plain summary: the restoration of the Endevor realm and the reopening of the mines. The Vornheld tradition considers this an adequate description in the way that \"a watershed is where water divides\" adequately describes a watershed.\n\nThe poem is not available to outside scholars in its complete form. The Vornheld position on this is not that the poem is secret — it is recited in the open twice a year, in a valley accessible to anyone who walks there — but that a complete text extracted from its recitation context and its listeners would be a different thing from the poem, and that the different thing is not what they are keepers of. The following are fragments in approximate translation, given in the order of their appearance in the full recitation. Translation is from archaic Northern Mittoli, which the full recitation uses throughout; the terse, stress-heavy character of the original does not carry.\n\n*The streams remember / what the hills forgot — / gold is a patience / longer than kings.*\n\n*Cold water finds / what cold water left. / The vein is not spent; / the vein is not yet asked.*\n\n*The dead have been seen / before their door opens. / What the mounds hold / the mounds will show first.*\n\n*The wood knows the fire / that made the gold ground. / When the wood speaks it, / Vel-Endre stands.*\n\nVornheld practitioners reciting the complete poem use a slower and more deliberate delivery for the third fragment than for the others, which has been noticed by everyone who has attended more than one recitation but not explained in any account that has reached outside Endevor.\n\nThe poem is structured around three signs and a title.\n\n**The first sign**: *When the cold water finds what it left behind.* The eastern hill streams carry traces of gold even now; the tradition holds that there will come a season when this trace returns to something visible in the pan — when the water finds again what it eroded and eventually lost. One school of Vornheld interpretation holds this means the Deepworks contain a vein that Veld's sealing preserved rather than exhausted, and that the right conditions will reveal it. Another holds it means a new hydrothermal process — something in the Oremindi still active far below the surface, expressing slowly upward — that will produce new deposition. The two positions have been debating each other for several generations without decisive evidence on either side.\n\n**The second sign**: *When the barrows wake before they're opened.* The specific language in the archaic Northern Mittoli is *vel-milloss nil-vel-gall* — \"they have been seen before the crossing.\" The practical interpretation most Vornheld accept: someone will witness something at a barrow — light, sound, movement — before any physical disturbance of the mound. The wider interpretation: a stranger will come to Endevor knowing, without having been told, where the dead kings are. The two interpretations are not mutually exclusive, which is part of why the debate has outlasted several attempts to settle it. It is also why certain Vornheld observe the barrows with unusual attention when strangers appear in Stroven.\n\n**The third sign**: *When the wood speaks what it knows.* The Acorwood reference is explicit within the Vornheld tradition and widely understood among practitioners: the poem refers to the smoke, or to something the smoke precedes, or to something the smoke has always been an indication of and which has not yet fully arrived. Vornheld who specialize in the *Vel-Eld* will say, when pressed, that the third sign is the one whose meaning is most certain among practitioners and most opaque to outside inquiry, and that this relationship is itself the point. The Acorwood speaks in a language the Endevor communities have been learning to read for generations. The third sign is the moment that language becomes fully legible.\n\nThe title the poem gives to the coming renewal is not a personal name. It is *Vel-Endre* — \"Returning-Endevor\" or \"Endevor-Returned\" — a name describing the condition rather than the person who embodies it. The poem does not clearly specify whether *Vel-Endre* is a king who comes from outside bringing the empire back, a quality that rises in the people themselves, or the land's own restoration coming through any of several possible channels. This ambiguity has made the title perpetually applicable to promising individuals — anyone who arrives in Endevor with any northern lineage and any apparent significance has been assessed by at least one Vornheld against the *Vel-Eld*'s criteria.\n\nThe title was applied to Veld himself, in his own lifetime, by a Vornheld lineage that believed his reign was the beginning of the restoration rather than the end of the old order. He died without the mines reopening. The lineage that backed this interpretation is still active; its current practitioners maintain that the application was not wrong but early — that Veld was the first motion of a returning tide, not its crest, and that the poem's timeline is longer than it appears. Other Vornheld lineages find this argument convenient in the particular way that arguments which cannot be falsified are convenient. The argument continues.\n\nThe *Vel-Eld* is recited in full twice a year: at the winter solstice and at the vernal equinox. Both recitations happen at Veld's barrow in the Stroven valley floor, attended by as many Vornheld as can come and whatever community members choose to follow. The solstice recitation draws better attendance than the equinox — there is something in the Endevor character that finds it appropriate to hear a poem about renewal in conditions of maximum cold, to say the thing that is latent in the ground while standing on frozen ground, to say that something will return while winter has its full weight on the hills. Not despite the conditions. Because of them.\n\n---\n\n## Fragments of the Epic Tradition\n\nTwo passages from the *Vel-Keth* have circulated outside Endevor in written form, transcribed by travelers who heard Vornheld performances at Stroven and recorded what they could. Both appear in the Academy's northern files under the classification \"miscellaneous oral material, northern provenance, language unclear.\" The Academy scholars who catalogued them had limited familiarity with archaic Northern Mittoli and none with the tradition's conventions. Their annotations treat the passages as remnants of something larger, which is accurate, while failing to identify what that larger thing is, which is characteristic.\n\n**From the Orrven naming-poem, as transcribed by a Bay of Lol merchant's factor, approximately four generations ago:**\n\n*What did he give the north? — Ships for the bay and horses for the heath.*\n*What did the north give back? — Men who came when called and went when released.*\n*What did he give the eastern hills? — Iron's true value, which is what it becomes.*\n*What did the hills give back? — Iron already becoming, waiting for the weight.*\n*What did he give the country beyond? — Gold's shadow, which moves faster than gold.*\n*What did the country give back? — Silence and the roads kept open.*\n*What did he give the deep ground? — The right question, asked at the right depth.*\n*What did the deep ground give back? — This is the exchange that is not finished.*\n\nThe Academy annotation reads: \"Possible ritual exchange; appears agricultural in reference; northern dialect, incompletely rendered.\" The observation about incomplete rendering is correct. The rest reflects the difficulty of reading call-and-answer verse outside its acoustic context: at a stone-shelter recitation, the eight exchanges move between two voices that everyone in the circle hears simultaneously. On a page, read sequentially, the eighth exchange loses the quality that makes it different from the other seven — the moment in the recitation where the second voice does not answer.\n\n**From the *Vel-Keth*'s Veld cycle, as transcribed by an Orched Hills trading-house record-keeper, approximately three generations ago:**\n\n*Veld spoke last on the ridge, the long speaking.*\n*He named what was owed and named who had paid.*\n*He named what remained and named who would keep it.*\n*He did not name a debt that had not been made.*\n*The tributaries heard him. They heard him well.*\n*What the tributaries did afterward is their own poem.*\n\nThe Academy annotation: \"Lament fragment, possibly funerary; 'Veld' possibly a title or appellative.\" The second observation is the transcriber noticing that the Northern Mittoli word for \"return\" has been used as a proper name, and concluding it must therefore be figurative. The Vornheld tradition would say: the word was his name before it became anything else, and the Veld cycle does not require outside annotation to be understood by the people it was made for.\n\n**From the *Eld-Keth*, the naming-poem for the five men who did not return from Helm's expedition:**\n\n*Vel-Oram, who read the deep ground and said it remembered fire.*\n*Vel-Skedhel, who heard the llos-eld and stayed to hear it again.*\n*Vel-Thonn, whose question the wood did not decline to answer.*\n*Vel-Orrael, who went toward the answer rather than away from it.*\n*Vel-Krenth, whose name the poem does not complete.*\n\nEach name is preceded by *vel-*, functioning here as an honorific title: \"the one who returned into.\" The fifth entry is the one outside scholars, if they ever encountered it, would find most notable: the poem's formulation implies that Vel-Krenth's name continues somewhere, but not in the poem. Where it continues is not specified.\n\nThe Academy files do not contain this passage. It has not been transcribed by any outside visitor who has shared the transcription. It is recited at the individual stone-markers of the five men on specific seasonal occasions by the Vornheld lineage that holds their names, and nowhere else. Its appearance here is the level of description the tradition permits in this context, and is not an invitation to seek the complete text.\n\n---\n\n## Language\n\nThe Endevor dialect of Northern Mittoli is conservative in its structure and unusual in its vocabulary. It shows the expected Northern features — initial stress, final vowel deletion, flattened diphthongs — but it has preserved the *ae* diphthong in a handful of words where even Northern Mittoli has simplified it, which linguists treat as evidence that Endevor has been in less contact with the coast than the Bay of Lol communities to its northwest, maintaining an archaic form while its neighbors changed.\n\nThe substrate layer is deeper here than in the Bay of Lol speech and different in profile from the Ganoss upland dialect. The smoke vocabulary is the most studied example: words that describe the Acorwood's behavior, which have no cognates in any other Northern Mittoli variety and do not reconstruct from Proto-Western, appear to be borrowings from a language that was spoken in these hills before Mittoli speakers arrived. Academy linguists have catalogued seven such words in Endevor dialect and noted that they cluster in the domain of fire, smoke, forest behavior, and certain weather phenomena — the things that a community watching the Acorwood's western face would need to name.\n\nThe fell-sheep vocabulary is also unusually rich: Endevor herders have fifteen or more terms for wool quality, fleece condition, and grazing behavior that do not appear in other Northern Mittoli varieties and that the Academy's linguistic records have not been able to trace to any source outside Endevor itself. Whether these are old substrate words, old Mittoli words that have only survived here, or innovations developed within the community is not established. The herders consider the question academic in a register that implies the word \"academic\" is not a compliment.",
      "loreRef": "endevor",
      "climate": "Cfb"
    },
    "South Endevor": {
      "name": "South Endevor",
      "color": "#d4ac0d",
      "lore": "# Endevor\n\nNorthwest of the Acorwood, where the Oremindi's northern terminus descends into lower ground and the air begins to carry the cold salt weight of the bay, the hills of Endevor fill the space between things. Not quite the upland — the Ganoss plateau is harder and higher, to the north. Not quite the coast — the Bay of Lol is a day's descent to the northwest. Not quite the mountain country, though the Oremindi makes the southern skyline and certain clear mornings let the peaks show through. Endevor is the hilly country in between, and it has organized itself around that position for as long as anyone has recorded it.\n\nThe name has no agreed derivation. The leading proposal in Academy language files is that *Endevor* is a contracted Northern Mittoli compound, perhaps *ond* (broad water) + *vel* (return) + an archaic locative marker — \"the place where the water turns back,\" referring to the watershed ridge where the hill streams divide between draining northwest toward the bay and north toward the Ganoss. A minority view holds that the name predates Northern Mittoli entirely and that the Mittoli speakers who arrived here simply kept the word that was already there. Both camps cite the same evidence in their favor, which the Academy has noted without resolving.\n\nThe outside world, if it thinks of Endevor at all, thinks of fell-sheep wool and reliable inns and the road toward the Bay of Lol. It does not think of the barrows on the ridgelines, which are older than the wool trade, and more numerous, and full of kings.\n\n---\n\n## Geography\n\nThe Endevor hills are old, rounded, and wet. They do not have the dramatic vertical of the Oremindi or the bleak exposed sweep of the Ganoss uplands; they are the kind of hills that fold into one another, valley after valley, each with its own stream, each valley floor broad enough for a strip of farmland before the slope comes up again. The highest ground is along the eastern ridge — the long slope that faces the Acorwood — which catches the worst of the weather coming in from the north and west and wrings it out before the clouds continue inland.\n\nThe result is that Endevor is wet without being waterlogged. The streams are reliable and numerous. The valley floors are damp and dark-soiled. The upper slopes are peat-moorland and short-grassed heath, suitable for grazing and not much else. The lower slopes are where most of Endevor's population lives, in the long ribbon settlements that follow the valley floors between the farmland and the hill pasture above.\n\nThe eastern ridge is wooded on its lower face — a mix of native northern species, hardy and not especially tall, transitioning as you approach the Acorwood's margin into something denser and older. Exactly where Endevor's edge-forest ends and the Acorwood begins is not a line that appears on any map; the local communities know it by feel and by the quality of the light, which changes at a threshold that most people can locate within a few hundred yards but cannot explain as a specific visible marker.\n\nThe Oremindi's northern end shapes the southern horizon and does more than frame the view. The meltwater coming off the range's northernmost snowfields feeds several of Endevor's main rivers, making them flashier in spring than their size would suggest and keeping them running in the driest summers. The passes through the Oremindi's northern extension are few and severe — harder and more seasonal than those further south — and are used primarily by the Sevron contact route and by occasional extraordinary travelers with specific purpose. Endevor has never been a mountain-crossing gateway in the way that the southern Oremindi communities are; the passes behind it are not worth the effort for ordinary commerce.\n\n---\n\n## The People\n\nEndevor is pastoral first. The hill communities keep sheep on the upper slopes and cattle in the valley bottoms, and have done this long enough that the organization of the land around those two requirements is baked into everything else: the placement of settlements, the timing of the year, the structure of disputes, the vocabulary of status. A family's standing in any Endevor valley is legible from its position on the hill — how far up the slope their summer grazing runs, how many animals winter in their valley barns, and whether the barns themselves are stone or timber, which says something about how long the family has been in that particular valley.\n\nThe characteristic animal of the Endevor hills is the **fell-sheep** — a breed whose name is a Northern Mittoli word meaning roughly \"the hill-animal\" or \"the high-pasture one,\" and which has been in these hills long enough that no records mention its arrival. The fell-sheep is not impressive beside the great animals of other regions. It is compact, short-legged, and deeply shaggy, with a fleece that produces more lanolin than any comparable breed — a natural oil that makes the wool water-resistant in the fleece and almost waterproof once spun and prepared. In a climate that is wet for most of the year, this matters. Endevor wool is traded throughout northern Azhora and down the coast road specifically because it performs in conditions that other wool cannot. The Bay of Lol fishing communities buy it for sea gear. The Ganoss upland clans have their own animals but trade for Endevor spun goods when they can get them. The Orched Hills mining settlements are regular purchasers of Endevor wool in preference to anything grown closer.\n\nThe fell-sheep also live their entire year outside, which is not true of most northern livestock. They know the upper hills the way the hills know themselves, and they will come down of their own accord before weather turns that their shepherds have not yet noticed. Endevor farmers pay attention to this. Where the sheep are on a given morning is information about the day.\n\nThe social unit of Endevor is the valley community — a loose collection of households sharing a river system, with informal but understood obligations around water rights, common hill access, and dispute resolution. Disputes between valleys are resolved at **stone-shelters** — low circular enclosures of dry-laid stone set at the ridgelines between valleys, old enough that no one remembers who built them, used as neutral meeting ground when valley communities need to negotiate. The stone-shelters are not impressive structures; they are simply very old and unmoved, which in Endevor is a form of authority. A resolution reached at a stone-shelter has a different weight from one reached anywhere else, because the shelter has been the resolution site before and will be again, and its walls have heard the settlement's terms and will keep them the same way they keep the wind off.\n\nThe shelters have one specific physical property that the Vornheld tradition says is why they were placed where they were placed and not anywhere else: speech spoken inside them at ordinary volume is heard simultaneously and with equal clarity by everyone in the circle, regardless of wind. The Academy attributes this to circular construction and the reflection properties of dry-laid stone at these specific angles. The Vornheld say this is what the sites were chosen for — that whoever built them tested the acoustics before placing the stone, and selected positions where the ground itself held the sound. A resolution reached inside a stone-shelter is heard by all parties in the same moment. This is not a formality. It is the physical condition that makes the resolution binding. You cannot claim you did not hear it.\n\nThe main market settlement is **Stroven** — positioned at the convergence of the three largest valley routes, where goods from the upper hills and goods coming northwest from the Bay of Lol approaches meet before separating again into the valley network. Stroven is not large; it is consequential because of where it is. The permanent population is several hundred, swelling in trading seasons. It has a covered market structure, a handful of inns accustomed to through-travelers, and the only standing archive in Endevor — a collection of land records and valley boundary agreements maintained by a family of hereditary record-keepers who have been doing this work long enough to hold opinions about disputes from four generations ago and will share those opinions if asked. The record-keepers are not impartial, exactly; they are the memory, and memory has its own loyalties. This is broadly understood and accounted for in how disputes proceed.\n\n---\n\n## The Acorwood's Western Face\n\nEvery Endevor community faces east toward the Acorwood. From the higher ridgelines it is visible as a dark line — darker than the surrounding hill forest, perceptibly different in color and density — that marks the edge of navigable country. The Ganoss upland communities on the Acorwood's northern margin and the Orched Hills settlements to the south have their own longstanding relationship with the forest's accessible edges. Endevor's relationship is with the western face, and it is different in a specific way: the morning sun rises behind the Acorwood, and from Endevor's hills at dawn the forest is lit from within — the edge trees stand in silhouette against the sky while the interior remains dark, the margin visible as a precise line between what is and is not entered. This is the Acorwood as Endevor knows it.\n\nThe edge timber harvest here is modest. The accessible trees on the Acorwood's northwestern fringe are similar in character to the edge timber on the northern and southern margins — large hardwoods that can be taken from the outer tree lines without going deep — but the Endevor communities have never developed a major edge-timber trade. The Bay of Lol coastal communities do not require much inland timber, having their own supplies from the coastal strips. The Ganoss use the northern margins. Endevor's edge harvest is primarily for local use: charcoal, building timber, occasional mast in good years.\n\nWhat the Endevor communities have developed, across many generations, is a practice of **smoke-reading**.\n\nThe Acorwood occasionally produces smoke from its interior. The Orched Hills records note this with uncertainty and do not follow it up. The Ganoss communities acknowledge it and have made their peace with not explaining it. Endevor has spent enough seasons watching the western and northwestern face — the face that catches the afternoon light, visible from the high sheepwalks for hours at a time — that their communities have accumulated an unusual amount of observational detail.\n\nThe fell-sheep shepherds in the high pastures are positioned to watch the Acorwood's margin across long hours, with nothing pressing them to look elsewhere. Across generations, this has produced a tradition of noting, without explaining, what the smoke does. There is vocabulary for it in the Endevor dialect — words that do not appear in other Northern Mittoli varieties — for the smoke that rises thin and straight in still air, the smoke that spreads at canopy height and moves with the wind before dispersing, and the specific, acknowledged category of smoke that appears with no wind and dissipates downward rather than upward, which the Endevor fell-sheep shepherds call by a substrate word that translates into Standard Mittoli as something between \"breathing-down\" and \"the forest taking something in.\"\n\nThe smoke-reading tradition is practical in two uses that have been confirmed across several generations. First: smoke from the Acorwood interior in late summer correlates imperfectly but consistently with good mast years; the shepherds who track this will predict mast yield before the edge trees show it in their growth. Second: the quality of smoke — color, height, behavior — appears to predict weather shifts in the way that cloud formations over the Oremindi also predict weather shifts, and is used the same way. Neither use requires an explanation of what the smoke is. The Endevor communities have not produced one.\n\nWhen Academy scholars have visited Stroven and asked about the smoke, they receive accurate and detailed information about what the smoke does and what it predicts, and no information about what causes it. The question of cause is not treated as something the Endevor communities are withholding. It is treated as a question that belongs to a different kind of inquiry than the one they practice. The smoke behaves; they read it; that is the relationship; cause is for someone with more time and less sheep.\n\n---\n\n## History\n\nThe pastoral Endevor is recent, as these things go.\n\nFor several centuries before the current era, Endevor was the center of the most extensive political authority in northern Azhora. The source of that authority was gold — placer gold in the eastern hill streams, and beneath it the deep vein gold that the streams had been eroding for thousands of years before anyone thought to follow them uphill. When the Endevor mine-kings found those veins, the northern world reorganized around them.\n\nAt its height the Endevor realm extended further north than the Bay of Lol, incorporating the coastal port communities and the Ganoss upland clans as tributaries. It extended south through the Orched Hills and, in its largest period, pressed claims as far as the northern Mittolo margins — the first and last time a northern power came close to bridging the cultural divide that the Acorwood and the Orched Hills maintain between north and south. The mine-kings did not rule these territories as the later Mittoli city-states governed their regions, through administrators and written law; they held them through the distribution of gold, the weight of tribute obligation, and the personal prestige of a line that had been producing wealth long enough for wealth and legitimacy to become indistinguishable. The Ganoss clans supplied livestock and manpower. The Bay of Lol communities supplied ships and navigators. The Orched Hills supplied iron and copper. The gold at the center moved all of it.\n\nFor roughly two hundred and fifty years at the empire's peak — the period the Vornheld poetry calls the *Eld-Gold*, the time of gold — the mine-kings of Endevor were the most significant power in northern Azhora. Their burials are still in the hills. The ridgelines are scattered with them.\n\nThe tradition names thirty-eight kings in the mine-king period. Not all are equally present in the *Eld-Keth* — the earliest are named briefly, the middle kings described at length, the late-period kings compressed as the tradition seems to contract around the decline. Three stand apart.\n\n**Keld** is the first-opener: the king who followed the stream gold uphill and found the vein. His *Eld-Keth* naming-poem is among the shortest in the body — he is recorded as a beginning rather than an achievement, and the tradition treats him accordingly, with respect but without ornamentation. A king who opens something gets the short poem. The gold that follows him speaks for itself.\n\n**Orrven** is the peak. His naming-poem is the longest in the *Eld-Keth* body and is structured differently from all others: a call-and-answer form in which the first voice names what Orrven gave and the second names what was returned, eight exchanges moving from the Bay of Lol communities to the Ganoss clans to the Orched Hills to what the tradition calls simply \"the deep ground.\" The effect, which Endevor audiences hear differently from how outside scholars read it, is of weight in motion — a redistribution so large that the land itself leaned under it. A Vornheld practitioner, when pressed by an Academy visitor to summarize Orrven's significance, said: \"His word was the weight gold makes before anyone counts it.\" The Academy visitor wrote this down, which the Vornheld probably anticipated.\n\n**Helm** is the surveyor — the king who sent Endevor's only recorded expedition into the Acorwood's interior, in the middle-late period when the eastern veins were thinning and the tradition of looking further was still recent enough to seem reasonable. Helm had observed, from fell-sheep shepherds watching the Acorwood's western face, that the breathing-down smoke concentrated most reliably above the Deepworks area. He concluded, or commissioned the conclusion, that the smoke was the Acorwood's surface indication of something below: an extension of the same hydrothermal system that had produced the vein gold, deeper and further east. Seventeen men entered the Acorwood's northwestern margin. Twelve returned. The five who did not are recorded by name in the *Eld-Keth*; that poem is recited at their stone at the barrow dedication and has not been offered at a public Stroven gathering in anyone's living memory.\n\nThe twelve who came back brought a word: *vel-keth-eld*. The Vornheld have not reached consensus on its meaning in the three or four generations since the expedition. Two translations circulate within the tradition: \"the forest's memory of fire\" and \"what the dark knows that we left behind.\" The first suggests the Acorwood has a relationship to the ancient hydrothermal period — that the forest grew in or around or from the same geological event that made the gold. The second suggests that whatever the expedition encountered inside remembered the mine-kings in some form. Helm's own interpretation was not recorded. He did not seal the Deepworks — that was Veld, a generation later — but he did not send another expedition.\n\nThe mines did not run out all at once. That was the particular difficulty.\n\nThe gold declined over several generations, each one producing less than the last, each one also the one that the current king believed was the bottom before a new vein would open. The shaft-heads went deeper. The spoil heaps grew. The streams were panned harder. The veins were followed further into the Oremindi foothills, into ground that was more expensive and more dangerous, and which produced less. The gold was finite because it had been deposited by a period of ancient hydrothermal activity in the range that had long since ended; there was no new gold forming. What was there was what had been there, and when it was taken, the taking was over.\n\nThe kings who presided over the decline maintained their tributary relationships through prestige long after the gold couldn't sustain them economically. This worked while the living memory of abundance lasted. When the generation that had known the peak died out and the next knew only the decline, the calculations changed. The Ganoss clans stopped sending cattle and said they were using them at home. The Bay of Lol communities began trading directly with the Orched Hills, bypassing the tribute routing. The Orched Hills settlements, which had their own ore wealth and their own long memories, assessed the situation and stopped paying.\n\nThe last king of the Endevor realm was named Veld — a Northern Mittoli name meaning simply \"return,\" which was understood at the time as a statement of ambition and in retrospect reads as something else. He held the realm together for eighteen years through the force of his personal authority and is described in the Vornheld tradition as the greatest of the later kings, which is not exactly praise: the greatest of the later kings could not undo what the geology had done. Veld died in his valley, and after him, the tributaries did not return.\n\nWhat followed was not collapse in the dramatic sense. There were no invasions, no great battles for succession, no famine. The population stayed. The valley communities kept their land. The stone-shelters kept their function. What changed was scope: Endevor contracted back to Endevor, and the wider world that had organized around its gold reorganized around other things, and eventually forgot there had been an organizing.\n\nThe raiding period that the northern Azhoran regional record describes as coming after this was partly a consequence: without the empire's redistributive economy, the coastal communities went back to taking by force what they had previously received through the gold-network. The Endevor hills were the logistical hinterland of that raiding culture's staging, not its origin. The mine-kings' empire was before the raiding; the raiding filled the vacuum after.\n\nBy the time the north's transition to trade began, Endevor had been quiet pastoral hill country for long enough that neither the coastal communities nor the Orched Hills settlements remembered what it had been. No Mittoli scholar wrote down the Endevor empire while it existed; the region was too far outside the Mittoli world for systematic record-keeping. The Vornheld tradition preserved everything, and the Vornheld tradition is oral, in archaic Northern Mittoli, inaccessible to outsiders who do not seek it out.\n\nThose who seek it out and can read what they find tend not to believe it — the scale is too large, the gold too much, the empire too extensive, for a country presently famous for waterproof sheep. This is a known difficulty with oral traditions from regions without outside corroboration. The barrows corroborate. The shaft-heads corroborate. Most outside visitors see hills and do not read them as texts.\n\n---\n\n## The Mines\n\nThe evidence for the Endevor gold is still in the hills if you know where to look.\n\nThe eastern hill streams — those draining toward the Acorwood's margin, fed by Oremindi snowfields — carry trace gold even now, at concentrations too low to bother with but detectable to anyone who knows what color to watch for in the pan. These are the runoff from the same deposits that built the empire: the deep vein gold in the Oremindi foothills, which the ancient hydrothermal activity of the range concentrated and which the long slow erosion of the hills has been dispersing into the streams for longer than anyone has been here to observe. At the mining peak, those concentrations were high enough that stream-panning alone produced meaningful yield. They have been diluting since the veins were broken open, and what remains is memory rather than resource.\n\nThe shaft-heads are more visible than the streams. The eastern faces of the three valley systems nearest the Acorwood — which the Vornheld tradition calls the **Eld-Valleys** — are marked with collapsed mine entrances at irregular intervals from near the valley floor to well above the current sheepwalks. Some are obvious: squared stonework around a shaft-mouth, subsidence depressions where fill has settled over generations. Others are subtle enough that a new arrival walking the sheep paths will occasionally put a foot through the turf into the edge of old roof-stone. The communities closest to the Eld-Valleys know where the dangerous ground is and avoid it without drama; it is simply part of the landscape.\n\nThe Vornheld tradition preserves names for seventeen distinct mine-workings, of which fourteen can be approximately located on the eastern ridge. The other three — the poems call them the **Deepworks** — are either collapsed without surface sign or are in the transition zone between the Endevor hills and the Acorwood's northwestern margin, where the Endevor communities do not regularly go. Whether the Deepworks still have accessible chambers is not known. The tradition says they were the richest of the veins, opened last and exhausted fastest, and sealed by Veld's order when the workers kept not returning from the deeper levels. The sealing instructions and the reason for the non-returns are subjects the Vornheld will discuss at different lengths depending on who is asking and what they appear to intend.\n\nSilver was mined alongside gold in the middle period — a secondary ore from the same hydrothermal deposits, found at greater depth once the gold concentrations thinned. Silver mining continued for two or three generations after gold effectively ended, providing enough revenue to maintain something that looked, from the outside, like a functioning economy while the empire's political structure dissolved. The last silver was exhausted within living memory of the decline's final phase, and the silver shafts are visible in the northern valley systems, shallower and less numerous than the gold workings.\n\nThe Deepworks have a specific entry in the sealing record preserved by the Vornheld: Veld's decree is noted in archaic Northern Mittoli as *oss nil-vel-keth* — \"mark: no return-speech\" — a designation the tradition treats as distinct from ordinary closure. Other shafts were sealed when they were worked out. The Deepworks were closed *nil-vel-keth*, without the response that would indicate emptiness. The formulation implies something was there to not-answer.\n\nThe mine-worker tradition, preserved fragmentarily in the *Eld-Keth* naming-poems for the last men to work the Deepworks, refers to a phenomenon called *llos-eld* — roughly \"gold-voice,\" though \"the ore that speaks\" is the translation Vornheld practitioners prefer when they feel precise. The *llos-eld* was a resonance audible in the rock at the deepest working levels: described variously as a low vibration, a harmonic in the stone that had no identifiable external source, a sound that seemed to originate not from above or below but from the rock around the worker. Experienced mine-foremen tracked it the way fell-sheep shepherds track the Acorwood's smoke — as information whose practical use was clear and whose underlying meaning was not. High *llos-eld* correlated with structural stability in the workings. The specific signature recorded in the Deepworks naming-poems is different from the stable reading, but what it was different toward is the thing the *Eld-Keth* does not say directly.\n\nWhether the *llos-eld* and the Acorwood's breathing-down smoke are related is a question the mine-workers and the shepherds never appear to have addressed together — the workers were underground and the shepherds were watching the forest above, and their separate communities had different relationships to the relevant facts. What the Vornheld tradition notes, without drawing a conclusion, is that the breathing-down smoke is most frequently reported by Endevor shepherds above the ground that corresponds to the Deepworks' approximate location. The observation is in the tradition. No resolution has been attached to it that the tradition records.\n\nThe mines are not worked. No new vein has been found in eight generations of looking, which has not entirely stopped people from looking. There are people in every generation of Endevor history who believe the Deepworks are not exhausted — that Veld sealed something rather than nothing, that the gold is still in the ground and waiting. The Vornheld have opinions about this. They express them when asked and reserve their most specific opinions for those who have already decided to listen.\n\n---\n\n## The Barrows\n\nThe ridgelines of Endevor are not bare. They are covered with sheep and with the dead.\n\nThe barrow-building tradition spans the full mine-king period and reads in the landscape as a slow record of decline. The oldest barrows are the highest — the first mine-kings claimed the skyline, and the mounds they raised are the largest, some visible for miles in clear weather, their grass-covered profiles the most distinctive feature of Endevor's upper silhouette. The Vornheld tradition describes these burials with objects that make outside scholars careful about skepticism: gold plate, gold ornament, ceremonial vessels, weapons edged with gold — the vocabulary of a household that operated at a scale that required gold to describe itself.\n\nThe barrows descend through the centuries and through the hill. Middle-period kings are buried at the ridgeline's lower edge. These barrows are smaller, the grave goods richer in silver and bronze, with gold present but diminished. Then the late-period burials in the upper valley sides, below the crest entirely: modest mounds that a traveler might pass without noticing, holding kings who went into the ground with bronze and the memory of what their fathers had.\n\nVeld's barrow is in the floor of the main Stroven valley — the lowest position of any king in the Vornheld record. The tradition notes that this was not a dishonoring but a request. Veld asked to be buried below rather than above. One school of Vornheld interpretation holds that this was a gesture of humility, acknowledging what had been lost on his watch. Another says the valley-floor placement was a statement of intention: a king buried facing upward rather than outward, not surveying what he once had but waiting for what would come to him. The debate has not been resolved in several generations of argument and probably will not be.\n\nVeld's mound is unmarked. The Vornheld know where it is. Certain Stroven families, by long tradition, know where it is. Occasional outside scholars come looking and either find a different mound or find nothing useful. Whether this concealment is protective of the burial or of some other property of the location is a question whose answer, if there is one, belongs to the Vornheld.\n\nThe barrows in general are the community's ancestors, not its archaeological record. Disturbing them carries no explicit legal prohibition because the community's understanding of what the barrows are does not require explicit prohibition: they are the dead kings, their possessions are the dead kings' possessions, and what belongs to the dead kings belongs to the community's memory of itself rather than to anyone currently living. Outside treasure-seekers have attempted barrows in northern Azhora before. There are at least two documented cases in the Bay of Lol's coastal records of attempts at Endevor-adjacent mounds. In both cases the attempts were unsuccessful — one because the mound opened was not a barrow, and one because the attempt was interrupted in circumstances the coastal record describes with some vagueness and the Endevor account of the same events does not describe at all.\n\nThe second case involves three men from a Bay of Lol coastal family with documented commercial interests in the northern Orched Hills, who came to Endevor, in the account of a northern merchant who tracked the sequence of events, \"to see what was in the old mounds.\" They opened what they believed was a late-period barrow on the upper eastern ridge, in the Eld-Valleys area. The coastal record notes their subsequent departure from Endevor as rapid and without what they came for, and that one of the three did not complete the return journey to the coast. The coastal narrative is vague on the circumstances that produced these outcomes. The Vornheld record of the same period — which does not describe what happened on the eastern ridge in any account that has been shared with outside inquirers — notes in its margin-notation system a designation applied afterward to the incident location: *nil-vel-gall nil-vel-keth*. The compound is not common. *Nil-vel-keth* appears as Veld's designation for the Deepworks: no return-speech. *Nil-vel-gall* is rarer still, appearing only twice in the tradition before this notation, both in contexts the Vornheld describe as \"the closure of something that was not supposed to be opened.\" The combined designation places the site outside the category of locations where ordinary community protocols apply. The valley community that grazes the hill above the site now routes its sheep differently than before. No one has explained this to outside observers in a context where they have found explanation appropriate.\n\n---\n\n## The Vornheld and the Epic Tradition\n\nThe Vornheld are the keepers of the Endevor epic tradition. The name is a Northern Mittoli compound, meaning roughly \"the ancestor-holders\" or \"those who hold the old ones\" — from *vorn* (the sky-pivot; north; the ancestor-direction) and *held* (hold; maintenance; keeping). The title is old enough to be uncertain in its own derivation. By now it names the role as much as it describes it.\n\nA Vornheld is not trained like a craft apprentice. The tradition enters through family — almost all practitioners come from households that have produced Vornheld before — but the selection is not simply inheritance. A child who shows the specific kind of memory the tradition requires is cultivated into it; a child who does not, even from a Vornheld household, is not. What the tradition requires is something between musical memory and structural memory: the capacity to hold long, metrically complex poems in reproducible form, and to hold the relationships between poems — the web of cross-references, the moment where one poem's allusion answers another's question — as a single navigable structure rather than a list. Knowing the poems is not knowing the tradition. The tradition is the map between them.\n\nThe Vornheld perform at the stone-shelters when gatherings are significant enough to require it — the major inter-valley assemblies, the seasonal land-right confirmations, the resolution of disputes that touch on ancestry, which in Endevor can mean almost any dispute between old families. The recitation at these events is not ornamental. The Vornheld's knowledge of ancestral claims is evidence. When a family asserts a right to a stretch of hill pasture based on an ancestor's service to a mine-king three hundred years ago, the Vornheld knows whether that claim is in the tradition, what the tradition says about it, and whether anything in the tradition speaks to the counter-claim. This is why Endevor dispute-resolution requires a Vornheld present for any proceeding invoking ancestry: the record-keepers in Stroven hold land agreements; the Vornheld hold lineages. The two archives do not duplicate each other.\n\nThe Vornheld lineages currently active number seven, each associated with a particular valley system and, by extension, with a particular portion of the tradition. Lineage specialization is not formalized — there is no rule stipulating that the Stroven valley lineage holds the Veld poems — but it has become de facto over several generations of practice, as each lineage has tended to recite its own valley's ancestral claims most often and therefore knows them most deeply. Full knowledge of all three bodies is the stated goal; full knowledge of the complete tradition in reproducible form is what distinguishes a master Vornheld from a capable one, and in any given generation there are perhaps two or three practitioners who can honestly claim it.\n\nThe most consequential current lineage disagreement runs between the family associated with the Keld poems and the family associated with the Orrven poems — the tradition of the first-opener against the tradition of the peak. The dispute is not about the poems themselves, which are settled, but about the primacy of their subjects: whether the origin is more essential to the tradition's meaning than the achievement, or whether the peak defines the entire arc and makes the opening merely prelude. In practical terms the disagreement affects how several disputed ancestral claims are weighted when they touch on the first-opener period versus the peak period. The dispute has an analog in a longstanding property disagreement between the two lineages' associated valley communities that has been argued at stone-shelters four times in the past century without permanent resolution. Everyone in Stroven understands the land question and the Vornheld question to be the same argument in different registers.\n\nThe most discussed recent intervention of Vornheld authority in living memory concerns an episode known in Stroven as the Drevos incident, or, among those who find the name insufficient, the salted-pan dispute. The Drevos family, a mining-descended household in the northern valley system, had for three generations asserted a right to a seasonal pasture on the basis of an ancestor's service to a late-period king. The counter-claim, from an older hill family, held that the same ancestor's service was in the *Eld-Keth* but attributed to a different king — which, if correct, would mean the pasture assignment had been made under misattributed authority and could be revisited. The dispute reached a stone-shelter proceeding with two Vornheld present from different lineages, who disagreed on the attribution. Resolution required a third Vornheld to recite the relevant naming-poem in full. This third Vornheld, who was elderly and had not recited the poem publicly in twelve years, did so without pause, in the original archaic Northern Mittoli, before an audience that included the Stroven record-keepers and approximately forty community members who had come specifically because word had gone around that the recitation would happen. The attribution was established. The pasture question was settled. The elderly Vornheld's unassisted recitation of a poem whose text two younger practitioners had disputed is still referenced in Stroven as a lesson about what the tradition requires of its keepers. What the salted-pan element of the name refers to — whether it is the name of the pasture, a detail of that day's proceedings, or something else — no one who was present has found it necessary to clarify.\n\nThe epic tradition is divided into three bodies.\n\n**The *Eld-Keth*** (\"the old-speech,\" the words from before): poems of the mine-king period, naming kings, campaigns, tributary arrangements, and the workings. The most extensive body and the least often publicly recited — they require context to hear correctly, and much of what they say is addressed to the Endevor community rather than to any outside listener. The *Eld-Keth* includes the naming-poems of the barrows: a short piece for each buried king describing the burial goods and the terms of his life. These are recited at the barrows on specific seasonal occasions and nowhere else.\n\n**The *Vel-Keth*** (\"the return-speech,\" the words of transition): poems of the decline — the closing mines, the dissolving tributaries, Veld's reign and burial. The most frequently performed body in public settings, because it does what narrative does best: it names a recognizable arc of rise and fall, and its audiences understand the shape. The *Vel-Keth* is what a traveler in Stroven who witnesses a Vornheld performance is most likely to hear. Fragments have circulated outside Endevor — two Orched Hills records and one Bay of Lol coastal archive preserve passages, apparently transcribed by travelers who heard a Vornheld recite and wrote what they could catch. These fragments are classified in the Academy's northern archive as \"miscellaneous oral material, provenance unclear.\"\n\n**The *Vel-Eld***: the renewal poem. Treated separately because it is not, in the Vornheld tradition, the same category of thing as the other bodies. The *Eld-Keth* and *Vel-Keth* describe what was. The *Vel-Eld* describes what will be — or what may be — or what the tradition holds is latent in the ground the way the gold was latent in the ground, waiting for its conditions.\n\n---\n\n## The Vel-Eld\n\nThe *Vel-Eld* — the Old Return, or the Ancient Coming-Back, or the Return That Was Always There — carries a title that is itself one of the tradition's genuinely contested problems. The Northern Mittoli word *vel* functions, in the archaic form the poem uses, as both \"return\" and \"was-already.\" The poem is about something coming back, or about something that was always present and is finally becoming visible, or both, and the language declines to resolve the question.\n\nIts subject, in plain summary: the restoration of the Endevor realm and the reopening of the mines. The Vornheld tradition considers this an adequate description in the way that \"a watershed is where water divides\" adequately describes a watershed.\n\nThe poem is not available to outside scholars in its complete form. The Vornheld position on this is not that the poem is secret — it is recited in the open twice a year, in a valley accessible to anyone who walks there — but that a complete text extracted from its recitation context and its listeners would be a different thing from the poem, and that the different thing is not what they are keepers of. The following are fragments in approximate translation, given in the order of their appearance in the full recitation. Translation is from archaic Northern Mittoli, which the full recitation uses throughout; the terse, stress-heavy character of the original does not carry.\n\n*The streams remember / what the hills forgot — / gold is a patience / longer than kings.*\n\n*Cold water finds / what cold water left. / The vein is not spent; / the vein is not yet asked.*\n\n*The dead have been seen / before their door opens. / What the mounds hold / the mounds will show first.*\n\n*The wood knows the fire / that made the gold ground. / When the wood speaks it, / Vel-Endre stands.*\n\nVornheld practitioners reciting the complete poem use a slower and more deliberate delivery for the third fragment than for the others, which has been noticed by everyone who has attended more than one recitation but not explained in any account that has reached outside Endevor.\n\nThe poem is structured around three signs and a title.\n\n**The first sign**: *When the cold water finds what it left behind.* The eastern hill streams carry traces of gold even now; the tradition holds that there will come a season when this trace returns to something visible in the pan — when the water finds again what it eroded and eventually lost. One school of Vornheld interpretation holds this means the Deepworks contain a vein that Veld's sealing preserved rather than exhausted, and that the right conditions will reveal it. Another holds it means a new hydrothermal process — something in the Oremindi still active far below the surface, expressing slowly upward — that will produce new deposition. The two positions have been debating each other for several generations without decisive evidence on either side.\n\n**The second sign**: *When the barrows wake before they're opened.* The specific language in the archaic Northern Mittoli is *vel-milloss nil-vel-gall* — \"they have been seen before the crossing.\" The practical interpretation most Vornheld accept: someone will witness something at a barrow — light, sound, movement — before any physical disturbance of the mound. The wider interpretation: a stranger will come to Endevor knowing, without having been told, where the dead kings are. The two interpretations are not mutually exclusive, which is part of why the debate has outlasted several attempts to settle it. It is also why certain Vornheld observe the barrows with unusual attention when strangers appear in Stroven.\n\n**The third sign**: *When the wood speaks what it knows.* The Acorwood reference is explicit within the Vornheld tradition and widely understood among practitioners: the poem refers to the smoke, or to something the smoke precedes, or to something the smoke has always been an indication of and which has not yet fully arrived. Vornheld who specialize in the *Vel-Eld* will say, when pressed, that the third sign is the one whose meaning is most certain among practitioners and most opaque to outside inquiry, and that this relationship is itself the point. The Acorwood speaks in a language the Endevor communities have been learning to read for generations. The third sign is the moment that language becomes fully legible.\n\nThe title the poem gives to the coming renewal is not a personal name. It is *Vel-Endre* — \"Returning-Endevor\" or \"Endevor-Returned\" — a name describing the condition rather than the person who embodies it. The poem does not clearly specify whether *Vel-Endre* is a king who comes from outside bringing the empire back, a quality that rises in the people themselves, or the land's own restoration coming through any of several possible channels. This ambiguity has made the title perpetually applicable to promising individuals — anyone who arrives in Endevor with any northern lineage and any apparent significance has been assessed by at least one Vornheld against the *Vel-Eld*'s criteria.\n\nThe title was applied to Veld himself, in his own lifetime, by a Vornheld lineage that believed his reign was the beginning of the restoration rather than the end of the old order. He died without the mines reopening. The lineage that backed this interpretation is still active; its current practitioners maintain that the application was not wrong but early — that Veld was the first motion of a returning tide, not its crest, and that the poem's timeline is longer than it appears. Other Vornheld lineages find this argument convenient in the particular way that arguments which cannot be falsified are convenient. The argument continues.\n\nThe *Vel-Eld* is recited in full twice a year: at the winter solstice and at the vernal equinox. Both recitations happen at Veld's barrow in the Stroven valley floor, attended by as many Vornheld as can come and whatever community members choose to follow. The solstice recitation draws better attendance than the equinox — there is something in the Endevor character that finds it appropriate to hear a poem about renewal in conditions of maximum cold, to say the thing that is latent in the ground while standing on frozen ground, to say that something will return while winter has its full weight on the hills. Not despite the conditions. Because of them.\n\n---\n\n## Fragments of the Epic Tradition\n\nTwo passages from the *Vel-Keth* have circulated outside Endevor in written form, transcribed by travelers who heard Vornheld performances at Stroven and recorded what they could. Both appear in the Academy's northern files under the classification \"miscellaneous oral material, northern provenance, language unclear.\" The Academy scholars who catalogued them had limited familiarity with archaic Northern Mittoli and none with the tradition's conventions. Their annotations treat the passages as remnants of something larger, which is accurate, while failing to identify what that larger thing is, which is characteristic.\n\n**From the Orrven naming-poem, as transcribed by a Bay of Lol merchant's factor, approximately four generations ago:**\n\n*What did he give the north? — Ships for the bay and horses for the heath.*\n*What did the north give back? — Men who came when called and went when released.*\n*What did he give the eastern hills? — Iron's true value, which is what it becomes.*\n*What did the hills give back? — Iron already becoming, waiting for the weight.*\n*What did he give the country beyond? — Gold's shadow, which moves faster than gold.*\n*What did the country give back? — Silence and the roads kept open.*\n*What did he give the deep ground? — The right question, asked at the right depth.*\n*What did the deep ground give back? — This is the exchange that is not finished.*\n\nThe Academy annotation reads: \"Possible ritual exchange; appears agricultural in reference; northern dialect, incompletely rendered.\" The observation about incomplete rendering is correct. The rest reflects the difficulty of reading call-and-answer verse outside its acoustic context: at a stone-shelter recitation, the eight exchanges move between two voices that everyone in the circle hears simultaneously. On a page, read sequentially, the eighth exchange loses the quality that makes it different from the other seven — the moment in the recitation where the second voice does not answer.\n\n**From the *Vel-Keth*'s Veld cycle, as transcribed by an Orched Hills trading-house record-keeper, approximately three generations ago:**\n\n*Veld spoke last on the ridge, the long speaking.*\n*He named what was owed and named who had paid.*\n*He named what remained and named who would keep it.*\n*He did not name a debt that had not been made.*\n*The tributaries heard him. They heard him well.*\n*What the tributaries did afterward is their own poem.*\n\nThe Academy annotation: \"Lament fragment, possibly funerary; 'Veld' possibly a title or appellative.\" The second observation is the transcriber noticing that the Northern Mittoli word for \"return\" has been used as a proper name, and concluding it must therefore be figurative. The Vornheld tradition would say: the word was his name before it became anything else, and the Veld cycle does not require outside annotation to be understood by the people it was made for.\n\n**From the *Eld-Keth*, the naming-poem for the five men who did not return from Helm's expedition:**\n\n*Vel-Oram, who read the deep ground and said it remembered fire.*\n*Vel-Skedhel, who heard the llos-eld and stayed to hear it again.*\n*Vel-Thonn, whose question the wood did not decline to answer.*\n*Vel-Orrael, who went toward the answer rather than away from it.*\n*Vel-Krenth, whose name the poem does not complete.*\n\nEach name is preceded by *vel-*, functioning here as an honorific title: \"the one who returned into.\" The fifth entry is the one outside scholars, if they ever encountered it, would find most notable: the poem's formulation implies that Vel-Krenth's name continues somewhere, but not in the poem. Where it continues is not specified.\n\nThe Academy files do not contain this passage. It has not been transcribed by any outside visitor who has shared the transcription. It is recited at the individual stone-markers of the five men on specific seasonal occasions by the Vornheld lineage that holds their names, and nowhere else. Its appearance here is the level of description the tradition permits in this context, and is not an invitation to seek the complete text.\n\n---\n\n## Language\n\nThe Endevor dialect of Northern Mittoli is conservative in its structure and unusual in its vocabulary. It shows the expected Northern features — initial stress, final vowel deletion, flattened diphthongs — but it has preserved the *ae* diphthong in a handful of words where even Northern Mittoli has simplified it, which linguists treat as evidence that Endevor has been in less contact with the coast than the Bay of Lol communities to its northwest, maintaining an archaic form while its neighbors changed.\n\nThe substrate layer is deeper here than in the Bay of Lol speech and different in profile from the Ganoss upland dialect. The smoke vocabulary is the most studied example: words that describe the Acorwood's behavior, which have no cognates in any other Northern Mittoli variety and do not reconstruct from Proto-Western, appear to be borrowings from a language that was spoken in these hills before Mittoli speakers arrived. Academy linguists have catalogued seven such words in Endevor dialect and noted that they cluster in the domain of fire, smoke, forest behavior, and certain weather phenomena — the things that a community watching the Acorwood's western face would need to name.\n\nThe fell-sheep vocabulary is also unusually rich: Endevor herders have fifteen or more terms for wool quality, fleece condition, and grazing behavior that do not appear in other Northern Mittoli varieties and that the Academy's linguistic records have not been able to trace to any source outside Endevor itself. Whether these are old substrate words, old Mittoli words that have only survived here, or innovations developed within the community is not established. The herders consider the question academic in a register that implies the word \"academic\" is not a compliment.",
      "loreRef": "endevor",
      "climate": "Cfb"
    },
    "West Acorwood": {
      "name": "West Acorwood",
      "color": "#27ae60",
      "lore": "# The Acorwood\n\nThe Acorwood lies in the narrowed neck of land east of the Oremindi's northern terminus, filling the corridor between the cold Ganoss uplands and the temperate interior of central Azhora. This is the ground that separates Northern Azhora from the rest of the continent — not the bay to the west, which is a water crossing, and not the mountains to the west, which are a climb, but the forest, which is something else.\n\nA forest that is simply dense is an obstacle. The Acorwood is not described as an obstacle. It is described as a place, and the distinction matters in the accounts of people who have entered it and returned, and in the more numerous accounts of people who have stood at its margin and decided not to.\n\nThe word that appears in every account — in every season, from travelers crossing north to south and travelers crossing south to north, from the Northern Azhoran coastal records and the Caeras Valley Academy files and the trade-house logs of the Orched Hills merchants — is *dark*. The Acorwood is dark. This observation, invariably made, does not exhaust what the place is. But it is where every account begins.\n\n---\n\n## Geography\n\nThe Acorwood occupies the transitional zone that connects the northern landmass extension to the main body of the continent. To its west: the final northward reach of the Oremindi, where the range descends and meets the bay's eastern shore. To its north: the lower Ganoss upland margins, where the forest thins into open heath within a day's walk. To its south: the transitional country of the Orched Hills, cleared and settled and crossed by roads that the Acorwood shadows without entering. To its east: the coast road and, beyond it, the sea.\n\nThe forest is not large by Azhoran standards. The Ibenwood is larger by an order of magnitude; even the Orched Hills' wooded valleys cover comparable ground. Measured at its margins — which is how the Academy has measured it, since interior surveys have not produced reliable numbers — the Acorwood is perhaps four to six days' travel at ordinary pace from its northern edge to its southern. Call it forty miles, call it fifty. On a map it appears as a green patch in a narrow corridor, surrounded on two sides by regions that have been described in detail and on two sides by geographic features that prevent circumnavigation except by sea.\n\nThe terrain beneath the forest is hilly — ridge-and-valley country, extension of the transitional ground on either side, with streams draining east to the coast and the occasional marsh where a valley has flattened and the drainage has slowed. The valleys are the natural routes of travel through. They are where travelers attempt passage. They are also where the canopy is thickest, because it is in the valley soils that the acor trees grow largest.\n\nThe acor tree — the species that names the forest and forms its dominant canopy — is a broad-crowned deciduous of unusual size and considerable age. The largest specimens are not as tall as the great trees of the Ibenwood's deep interior, but they are wide: a mature acor spreads its crown over a radius that exceeds any comparable temperate species outside the southwest forest. It bears mast in cycles of three to seven years — large, oil-rich nuts that drop in quantities that were, in good mast years, historically harvested by the northern communities from the forest's accessible margins. The Caeras Valley Academy's forest classification notes the acor as closely related to the broad-leafed species of the Orched Hills, which are themselves related to the interior deciduous trees of northern Mittolo. The relationship is not disputed. What has not been explained is why the Acorwood specimens are substantially larger than their relatives a day's walk to the south, or why the canopy they form has the quality that travelers describe.\n\nThe quality is the problem. The acor, when its canopy is established at sufficient density, interlocks crown to crown with its neighbors and with the secondary species beneath it — a second canopy of shade-tolerant trees that have grown where the acor's leaf-out permits, which is very little direct light, and which have therefore grown into the residual. The result is a layered roof of interleaved foliage through which light enters and is then scattered through so many surfaces that it arrives at the forest floor without a discernible direction.\n\nIn spring, before the leaf-out, the Acorwood admits full light. It is open in the way any northern deciduous forest is open before the canopy closes. Travelers who enter before the leaves are out report nothing unusual. After leaf-out, from late spring through the first frosts of autumn: the quality that everyone who enters in summer describes.\n\n---\n\n## The Dark\n\n\"Dark\" is inadequate and used anyway because nothing better has been found.\n\nThe Caeras Valley Academy's most detailed record of the Acorwood interior is a geographer's account of eight days' travel from the southern margin to the northern, written two generations ago and not repeated. The geographer was a methodical observer — her work on the Orched Hills ore deposits and the northern coastal survey are still the Academy's reference accounts for those regions — and her Acorwood record is among the most careful documents in the Academy's geography files. It does not resolve what the Acorwood is. It characterizes it, which is not the same thing.\n\nHer specific term is \"directionally nullified light.\" The mechanism, as she describes it: light enters the Acorwood. It illuminates. Objects at the forest floor are visible with reasonable clarity in daytime. What is absent is the sense of direction that light normally carries — the shadowing that tells a traveler which way the sun stands, the angles that allow navigation by sky position. At midday in the Acorwood interior on a clear summer day, the light falls equally from all sides. The shadows, where they exist at all, point nowhere consistently. The sun's position cannot be determined from inside the forest.\n\nThe practical consequence: sun navigation is impossible. The geographer maintained direction by compass and by the streams, which still drain east regardless of what the light is doing. She notes that travelers who enter without a compass and without the habit of reading drainage direction will turn in the Acorwood. Not because the forest disorients in any other way — she does not report the distances being wrong, as has been recorded in the Ibenwood interior — but because the one constant navigational reference available in open country is absent, and the ambient light offers no replacement.\n\nWhether this is entirely a function of the acor canopy at this density — the scattering and re-scattering of light through multiple layers of interleaved leaf until it loses its directionality — or whether something else contributes is a question the geographer declines to answer. She notes that the effect is present in the Acorwood and absent in the Orched Hills, which have comparable tree cover but not comparable species or density. She notes that the deepest sections of the Ibenwood have been described in terms that share certain qualities with the Acorwood — particularly the properties of light — but that she has not personally visited the Ibenwood's deep interior and cannot make the comparison from first-hand observation. She leaves the question open in the way that thorough scholars leave things that are genuinely open: without asserting a resolution.\n\nThe northern peoples who live at the Ganoss and the Orched Hills margins do not distinguish between mechanism and effect. The Acorwood is dark. You will not know north from south unless you know before you enter. These are facts in the same register as the mast cycle and the spring flood.\n\n---\n\n## The Barrier\n\nThe Acorwood's role as the boundary between Northern Azhora and the rest of the continent is not a political designation. No power has declared it a border. No treaty names it as a line. It is a boundary in the geographic sense: the thing that is in between.\n\nArmies moving between north and south have always faced the Acorwood as a logistical fact. The options are the forest itself, which is navigable in the right season with experienced guides and reliable compasses and does not allow the movement of large formations without an extended and costly period of route-clearing; the Oremindi passes to the west, which are severe at the range's northern end and seasonal in the best years; or the coast road to the east, which is narrow, easily watched, and the obvious route for any force large enough to matter. The coast road has therefore been the site of most northern conflicts. It has been fortified and contested and changed hands more times than the Orched Hills miners can reliably count.\n\nThe Acorwood has never been fortified because it cannot be held. It is not hostile to defenders. It is merely indifferent to everyone equally, which produces the same strategic result.\n\nThe consequence is that Northern Azhora and the rest of the continent have developed in partial separation for most of their recorded history. The separation is not total — the coast road exists, the bay is crossable, merchants move — but it is enough to make the north feel, from the south, like a different civilization, and the south feel, from the north, like a warmer and more complicated place that requires the coast road and the Acorwood crossing to reach. Both assessments are approximately correct.\n\n---\n\n## The People\n\nWhether anyone lives inside the Acorwood is not established.\n\nThe communities on the northern margin — the lower Ganoss settlements and the edge farms that push against the forest from the heath country — know the Acorwood's accessible margins in the way that communities know the edge of a forest they use without entering. They harvest mast in good mast years, working the outer tree lines. They cut edge timber where the canopy thins and the acor specimens are smaller and less covered. The forest products they take are all from the first quarter-mile; there is no evidence of any settled harvest practice deeper than that.\n\nThe communities on the southern margin, in the northern Orched Hills, maintain a similar relationship. They have been taking edge timber and occasional mast for as long as their records extend. They tell the same thing the northern communities tell: they do not go deep, the forest is dark, and their grandfathers did not go deep either, and their grandfathers said their grandfathers had not.\n\nWhether this represents an ancient decision or simply accumulated practice is not clear. No prohibition exists, in any documented form, against entering the Acorwood. No record of a community that tried to settle the interior and failed. The shallowness of the use is not explained; it simply is.\n\nOne account — a trading-house record from the northern Orched Hills, perhaps three generations old — notes smoke seen rising from the Acorwood interior in autumn of an unspecified year. The recorder treats it as notable but not alarming, and offers no further information. The Academy has no record of following up. The northern communities, when asked about it, say that smoke can come from inside without anyone they know having put it there, and that this is one of the things about the Acorwood that they have made their peace with.\n\nThe name for the Acorwood's interior in the northern Ganoss margin speech is a compound that translates approximately as \"the inside that knows itself.\" The southern Orched Hills communities use a different phrase in their dialect that produces, in Standard Mittoli, something like \"what belongs to the inside.\" Both translations have been noted in Academy language files. Neither has been pursued.\n\n---\n\n## Fauna\n\nThe Acorwood is not quiet. The travelers who have entered it consistently note that the bird life is dense — canopy birds, predominantly, species adapted to the light conditions and the mast crop, in numbers that seem high relative to the forest's apparent density. The mast cycles drive them: in a good mast year, the sound from the Acorwood's upper canopy is audible at the margins as a persistent layered noise, the production of a very large number of animals dealing with a very large food surplus.\n\nWhat lives below the canopy has been observed less directly. The geographer's account describes tracks and sign that she attributes to a large ungulate — she mentions hoof impressions in soft valley soil of a size that exceeds any cervid she knows from the northern regions, and what she interprets as browsing damage to upper-canopy growth by something considerably taller than any cervid she has seen. She does not see the animal itself. She notes that the browse damage is not recent — it is the weathered mark of a long-established pattern, not a recent passing.\n\nThe northern communities have a name for something in the Acorwood's interior. The name does not translate directly; it renders into Standard Mittoli as something between \"the tall one\" and \"the one that does not see you\" — the second part being a statement about attention rather than vision, indicating an animal that is not afraid of what it notices. They do not describe it in detail to outside questioners, not from reluctance but from the evident impression that a detailed description would not produce useful understanding. They know it is there. They do not go where it is. This is the relevant information.\n\nThe Academy naturalists who have seen the Cape Thalmagar materials have noted the browse damage description in the geographer's account and the description of the Long-Back from Thalmagar's forest margins in the same session. The notation in the Academy's comparison file reads: \"possible range extension, or related form, or the same tradition of large browsing ungulates in forests isolated long enough to preserve them.\" The notation is marked for follow-up. The follow-up has not occurred.\n\n---\n\n## The Acorwood and the Ibenwood\n\nThe Forest Mittoli communities of the southwest hold that the Dark Groves of the Mittolo heartland — the dense, anomalous patches of ancient woodland scattered through the interior, where maps disagree with themselves and distances behave strangely — are not anomalies. They are the Iben. The great southwestern forest once covered more of Azhora than it does now. What remains in the heartland are fragments of that earlier extent, cut off by clearing and cultivation but continuous with the main body in everything except space.\n\nOutside scholars who have encountered this position have generally found it interesting and difficult to evaluate. The Forest Mittoli do not present it as a historical claim in the sense that requires proof. They present it as a description of what the Iben is — not something that was larger and shrank, but something that is present in more of the continent than its current mapped extent suggests, and that the spaces between apparent fragments are not absences but misreadings.\n\nThe Academy has not, in any file, asked whether the Acorwood is related to this account. The Academy has Ibenwood files and Acorwood files and they are in different sections. No scholar appears to have placed them next to each other and asked what the light quality in the Ibenwood edge and the light quality in the Acorwood interior have in common. The Dark Groves file mentions the Acorwood once, in a list of northern forests to be investigated, and does not return to it.\n\nWhether this is an oversight or a consequence of the Academy's organizational structure or the inherent difficulty of the question is not established. The question is available to anyone who reads both files. The Forest Mittoli, if they have been asked about the Acorwood directly, have not given answers that appear in any Academy record. The Acorwood does not have Forest Mittoli communities the way the Ibenwood does — or at least none that have introduced themselves. Whether it has something else is the question the smoke and the tall-one's tracks make difficult to set aside entirely.\n\n---\n\n## Trade and the Margins\n\nThe Acorwood is commercially present to both the northern and southern communities primarily as mast. Acorn mast in a good year is a significant food source for both human communities and their animals — pigs pastured at the forest margins during mast years are a standard practice on both sides, well-documented in agricultural records. The mast cycle's irregularity is tracked as weather is tracked: noted, anticipated, corrected for when wrong. A good mast year shapes the following winter's herd numbers.\n\nBeyond mast: edge timber, where the acor specimens are smaller and accessible. The acor is a durable hardwood, preferred for specific applications in northern shipbuilding and for the wheel stock used in the Orched Hills' mine cart tradition. Edge harvesting is technically sustainable — the trees are large enough that margin cutting produces useful timber while leaving enough canopy to regenerate — and has been practiced on both sides long enough that the edge communities have developed their own protocols for what can be taken and what cannot.\n\nNo claim of political sovereignty over the Acorwood has been maintained for any extended period. Northern Azhoran city-states have asserted it in theory; Orched Hills mining communities have asserted it in theory. Neither has found a way to make the assertion meaningful in practice. The Acorwood belongs to no one in the sense that the forest products at its margins belong to whoever is there to collect them, and the interior belongs to whatever is in there, and the two conditions have coexisted without visible friction since the earliest records.\n\nThe coast road east of the Acorwood is the primary north-south route. It is not a pleasant road — the ground between the forest's edge and the sea is narrow, wet in wet seasons, liable to storm surge where it passes the coastal headlands — but it is a road that can be walked and mapped and garrisoned. It is the road by which most commerce between north and south actually moves, by which travelers and armies and ambassadors make their passage, and by which the two halves of the continent maintain the connection that the Acorwood does not prevent but does not facilitate.\n\nThe forest stands between them, dark in summer, open in the weeks before and after leaf-out, and present in every account as the fact that both sides have organized around: the thing you go around because going through requires more than most trips are worth, and in which the light comes from no direction, and which, in autumn, occasionally exhales a thread of smoke from somewhere it should not.",
      "loreRef": "acorwood",
      "climate": "Dfb"
    },
    "North Acorwood": {
      "name": "North Acorwood",
      "color": "#16a085",
      "lore": "# The Acorwood\n\nThe Acorwood lies in the narrowed neck of land east of the Oremindi's northern terminus, filling the corridor between the cold Ganoss uplands and the temperate interior of central Azhora. This is the ground that separates Northern Azhora from the rest of the continent — not the bay to the west, which is a water crossing, and not the mountains to the west, which are a climb, but the forest, which is something else.\n\nA forest that is simply dense is an obstacle. The Acorwood is not described as an obstacle. It is described as a place, and the distinction matters in the accounts of people who have entered it and returned, and in the more numerous accounts of people who have stood at its margin and decided not to.\n\nThe word that appears in every account — in every season, from travelers crossing north to south and travelers crossing south to north, from the Northern Azhoran coastal records and the Caeras Valley Academy files and the trade-house logs of the Orched Hills merchants — is *dark*. The Acorwood is dark. This observation, invariably made, does not exhaust what the place is. But it is where every account begins.\n\n---\n\n## Geography\n\nThe Acorwood occupies the transitional zone that connects the northern landmass extension to the main body of the continent. To its west: the final northward reach of the Oremindi, where the range descends and meets the bay's eastern shore. To its north: the lower Ganoss upland margins, where the forest thins into open heath within a day's walk. To its south: the transitional country of the Orched Hills, cleared and settled and crossed by roads that the Acorwood shadows without entering. To its east: the coast road and, beyond it, the sea.\n\nThe forest is not large by Azhoran standards. The Ibenwood is larger by an order of magnitude; even the Orched Hills' wooded valleys cover comparable ground. Measured at its margins — which is how the Academy has measured it, since interior surveys have not produced reliable numbers — the Acorwood is perhaps four to six days' travel at ordinary pace from its northern edge to its southern. Call it forty miles, call it fifty. On a map it appears as a green patch in a narrow corridor, surrounded on two sides by regions that have been described in detail and on two sides by geographic features that prevent circumnavigation except by sea.\n\nThe terrain beneath the forest is hilly — ridge-and-valley country, extension of the transitional ground on either side, with streams draining east to the coast and the occasional marsh where a valley has flattened and the drainage has slowed. The valleys are the natural routes of travel through. They are where travelers attempt passage. They are also where the canopy is thickest, because it is in the valley soils that the acor trees grow largest.\n\nThe acor tree — the species that names the forest and forms its dominant canopy — is a broad-crowned deciduous of unusual size and considerable age. The largest specimens are not as tall as the great trees of the Ibenwood's deep interior, but they are wide: a mature acor spreads its crown over a radius that exceeds any comparable temperate species outside the southwest forest. It bears mast in cycles of three to seven years — large, oil-rich nuts that drop in quantities that were, in good mast years, historically harvested by the northern communities from the forest's accessible margins. The Caeras Valley Academy's forest classification notes the acor as closely related to the broad-leafed species of the Orched Hills, which are themselves related to the interior deciduous trees of northern Mittolo. The relationship is not disputed. What has not been explained is why the Acorwood specimens are substantially larger than their relatives a day's walk to the south, or why the canopy they form has the quality that travelers describe.\n\nThe quality is the problem. The acor, when its canopy is established at sufficient density, interlocks crown to crown with its neighbors and with the secondary species beneath it — a second canopy of shade-tolerant trees that have grown where the acor's leaf-out permits, which is very little direct light, and which have therefore grown into the residual. The result is a layered roof of interleaved foliage through which light enters and is then scattered through so many surfaces that it arrives at the forest floor without a discernible direction.\n\nIn spring, before the leaf-out, the Acorwood admits full light. It is open in the way any northern deciduous forest is open before the canopy closes. Travelers who enter before the leaves are out report nothing unusual. After leaf-out, from late spring through the first frosts of autumn: the quality that everyone who enters in summer describes.\n\n---\n\n## The Dark\n\n\"Dark\" is inadequate and used anyway because nothing better has been found.\n\nThe Caeras Valley Academy's most detailed record of the Acorwood interior is a geographer's account of eight days' travel from the southern margin to the northern, written two generations ago and not repeated. The geographer was a methodical observer — her work on the Orched Hills ore deposits and the northern coastal survey are still the Academy's reference accounts for those regions — and her Acorwood record is among the most careful documents in the Academy's geography files. It does not resolve what the Acorwood is. It characterizes it, which is not the same thing.\n\nHer specific term is \"directionally nullified light.\" The mechanism, as she describes it: light enters the Acorwood. It illuminates. Objects at the forest floor are visible with reasonable clarity in daytime. What is absent is the sense of direction that light normally carries — the shadowing that tells a traveler which way the sun stands, the angles that allow navigation by sky position. At midday in the Acorwood interior on a clear summer day, the light falls equally from all sides. The shadows, where they exist at all, point nowhere consistently. The sun's position cannot be determined from inside the forest.\n\nThe practical consequence: sun navigation is impossible. The geographer maintained direction by compass and by the streams, which still drain east regardless of what the light is doing. She notes that travelers who enter without a compass and without the habit of reading drainage direction will turn in the Acorwood. Not because the forest disorients in any other way — she does not report the distances being wrong, as has been recorded in the Ibenwood interior — but because the one constant navigational reference available in open country is absent, and the ambient light offers no replacement.\n\nWhether this is entirely a function of the acor canopy at this density — the scattering and re-scattering of light through multiple layers of interleaved leaf until it loses its directionality — or whether something else contributes is a question the geographer declines to answer. She notes that the effect is present in the Acorwood and absent in the Orched Hills, which have comparable tree cover but not comparable species or density. She notes that the deepest sections of the Ibenwood have been described in terms that share certain qualities with the Acorwood — particularly the properties of light — but that she has not personally visited the Ibenwood's deep interior and cannot make the comparison from first-hand observation. She leaves the question open in the way that thorough scholars leave things that are genuinely open: without asserting a resolution.\n\nThe northern peoples who live at the Ganoss and the Orched Hills margins do not distinguish between mechanism and effect. The Acorwood is dark. You will not know north from south unless you know before you enter. These are facts in the same register as the mast cycle and the spring flood.\n\n---\n\n## The Barrier\n\nThe Acorwood's role as the boundary between Northern Azhora and the rest of the continent is not a political designation. No power has declared it a border. No treaty names it as a line. It is a boundary in the geographic sense: the thing that is in between.\n\nArmies moving between north and south have always faced the Acorwood as a logistical fact. The options are the forest itself, which is navigable in the right season with experienced guides and reliable compasses and does not allow the movement of large formations without an extended and costly period of route-clearing; the Oremindi passes to the west, which are severe at the range's northern end and seasonal in the best years; or the coast road to the east, which is narrow, easily watched, and the obvious route for any force large enough to matter. The coast road has therefore been the site of most northern conflicts. It has been fortified and contested and changed hands more times than the Orched Hills miners can reliably count.\n\nThe Acorwood has never been fortified because it cannot be held. It is not hostile to defenders. It is merely indifferent to everyone equally, which produces the same strategic result.\n\nThe consequence is that Northern Azhora and the rest of the continent have developed in partial separation for most of their recorded history. The separation is not total — the coast road exists, the bay is crossable, merchants move — but it is enough to make the north feel, from the south, like a different civilization, and the south feel, from the north, like a warmer and more complicated place that requires the coast road and the Acorwood crossing to reach. Both assessments are approximately correct.\n\n---\n\n## The People\n\nWhether anyone lives inside the Acorwood is not established.\n\nThe communities on the northern margin — the lower Ganoss settlements and the edge farms that push against the forest from the heath country — know the Acorwood's accessible margins in the way that communities know the edge of a forest they use without entering. They harvest mast in good mast years, working the outer tree lines. They cut edge timber where the canopy thins and the acor specimens are smaller and less covered. The forest products they take are all from the first quarter-mile; there is no evidence of any settled harvest practice deeper than that.\n\nThe communities on the southern margin, in the northern Orched Hills, maintain a similar relationship. They have been taking edge timber and occasional mast for as long as their records extend. They tell the same thing the northern communities tell: they do not go deep, the forest is dark, and their grandfathers did not go deep either, and their grandfathers said their grandfathers had not.\n\nWhether this represents an ancient decision or simply accumulated practice is not clear. No prohibition exists, in any documented form, against entering the Acorwood. No record of a community that tried to settle the interior and failed. The shallowness of the use is not explained; it simply is.\n\nOne account — a trading-house record from the northern Orched Hills, perhaps three generations old — notes smoke seen rising from the Acorwood interior in autumn of an unspecified year. The recorder treats it as notable but not alarming, and offers no further information. The Academy has no record of following up. The northern communities, when asked about it, say that smoke can come from inside without anyone they know having put it there, and that this is one of the things about the Acorwood that they have made their peace with.\n\nThe name for the Acorwood's interior in the northern Ganoss margin speech is a compound that translates approximately as \"the inside that knows itself.\" The southern Orched Hills communities use a different phrase in their dialect that produces, in Standard Mittoli, something like \"what belongs to the inside.\" Both translations have been noted in Academy language files. Neither has been pursued.\n\n---\n\n## Fauna\n\nThe Acorwood is not quiet. The travelers who have entered it consistently note that the bird life is dense — canopy birds, predominantly, species adapted to the light conditions and the mast crop, in numbers that seem high relative to the forest's apparent density. The mast cycles drive them: in a good mast year, the sound from the Acorwood's upper canopy is audible at the margins as a persistent layered noise, the production of a very large number of animals dealing with a very large food surplus.\n\nWhat lives below the canopy has been observed less directly. The geographer's account describes tracks and sign that she attributes to a large ungulate — she mentions hoof impressions in soft valley soil of a size that exceeds any cervid she knows from the northern regions, and what she interprets as browsing damage to upper-canopy growth by something considerably taller than any cervid she has seen. She does not see the animal itself. She notes that the browse damage is not recent — it is the weathered mark of a long-established pattern, not a recent passing.\n\nThe northern communities have a name for something in the Acorwood's interior. The name does not translate directly; it renders into Standard Mittoli as something between \"the tall one\" and \"the one that does not see you\" — the second part being a statement about attention rather than vision, indicating an animal that is not afraid of what it notices. They do not describe it in detail to outside questioners, not from reluctance but from the evident impression that a detailed description would not produce useful understanding. They know it is there. They do not go where it is. This is the relevant information.\n\nThe Academy naturalists who have seen the Cape Thalmagar materials have noted the browse damage description in the geographer's account and the description of the Long-Back from Thalmagar's forest margins in the same session. The notation in the Academy's comparison file reads: \"possible range extension, or related form, or the same tradition of large browsing ungulates in forests isolated long enough to preserve them.\" The notation is marked for follow-up. The follow-up has not occurred.\n\n---\n\n## The Acorwood and the Ibenwood\n\nThe Forest Mittoli communities of the southwest hold that the Dark Groves of the Mittolo heartland — the dense, anomalous patches of ancient woodland scattered through the interior, where maps disagree with themselves and distances behave strangely — are not anomalies. They are the Iben. The great southwestern forest once covered more of Azhora than it does now. What remains in the heartland are fragments of that earlier extent, cut off by clearing and cultivation but continuous with the main body in everything except space.\n\nOutside scholars who have encountered this position have generally found it interesting and difficult to evaluate. The Forest Mittoli do not present it as a historical claim in the sense that requires proof. They present it as a description of what the Iben is — not something that was larger and shrank, but something that is present in more of the continent than its current mapped extent suggests, and that the spaces between apparent fragments are not absences but misreadings.\n\nThe Academy has not, in any file, asked whether the Acorwood is related to this account. The Academy has Ibenwood files and Acorwood files and they are in different sections. No scholar appears to have placed them next to each other and asked what the light quality in the Ibenwood edge and the light quality in the Acorwood interior have in common. The Dark Groves file mentions the Acorwood once, in a list of northern forests to be investigated, and does not return to it.\n\nWhether this is an oversight or a consequence of the Academy's organizational structure or the inherent difficulty of the question is not established. The question is available to anyone who reads both files. The Forest Mittoli, if they have been asked about the Acorwood directly, have not given answers that appear in any Academy record. The Acorwood does not have Forest Mittoli communities the way the Ibenwood does — or at least none that have introduced themselves. Whether it has something else is the question the smoke and the tall-one's tracks make difficult to set aside entirely.\n\n---\n\n## Trade and the Margins\n\nThe Acorwood is commercially present to both the northern and southern communities primarily as mast. Acorn mast in a good year is a significant food source for both human communities and their animals — pigs pastured at the forest margins during mast years are a standard practice on both sides, well-documented in agricultural records. The mast cycle's irregularity is tracked as weather is tracked: noted, anticipated, corrected for when wrong. A good mast year shapes the following winter's herd numbers.\n\nBeyond mast: edge timber, where the acor specimens are smaller and accessible. The acor is a durable hardwood, preferred for specific applications in northern shipbuilding and for the wheel stock used in the Orched Hills' mine cart tradition. Edge harvesting is technically sustainable — the trees are large enough that margin cutting produces useful timber while leaving enough canopy to regenerate — and has been practiced on both sides long enough that the edge communities have developed their own protocols for what can be taken and what cannot.\n\nNo claim of political sovereignty over the Acorwood has been maintained for any extended period. Northern Azhoran city-states have asserted it in theory; Orched Hills mining communities have asserted it in theory. Neither has found a way to make the assertion meaningful in practice. The Acorwood belongs to no one in the sense that the forest products at its margins belong to whoever is there to collect them, and the interior belongs to whatever is in there, and the two conditions have coexisted without visible friction since the earliest records.\n\nThe coast road east of the Acorwood is the primary north-south route. It is not a pleasant road — the ground between the forest's edge and the sea is narrow, wet in wet seasons, liable to storm surge where it passes the coastal headlands — but it is a road that can be walked and mapped and garrisoned. It is the road by which most commerce between north and south actually moves, by which travelers and armies and ambassadors make their passage, and by which the two halves of the continent maintain the connection that the Acorwood does not prevent but does not facilitate.\n\nThe forest stands between them, dark in summer, open in the weeks before and after leaf-out, and present in every account as the fact that both sides have organized around: the thing you go around because going through requires more than most trips are worth, and in which the light comes from no direction, and which, in autumn, occasionally exhales a thread of smoke from somewhere it should not.",
      "loreRef": "acorwood",
      "climate": "Dfb"
    },
