    "East Acordwood": {
      "name": "East Acordwood",
      "color": "#8e44ad",
      "lore": "# The Acorwood\n\nThe Acorwood lies in the narrowed neck of land east of the Oremindi's northern terminus, filling the corridor between the cold Ganoss uplands and the temperate interior of central Azhora. This is the ground that separates Northern Azhora from the rest of the continent — not the bay to the west, which is a water crossing, and not the mountains to the west, which are a climb, but the forest, which is something else.\n\nA forest that is simply dense is an obstacle. The Acorwood is not described as an obstacle. It is described as a place, and the distinction matters in the accounts of people who have entered it and returned, and in the more numerous accounts of people who have stood at its margin and decided not to.\n\nThe word that appears in every account — in every season, from travelers crossing north to south and travelers crossing south to north, from the Northern Azhoran coastal records and the Caeras Valley Academy files and the trade-house logs of the Orched Hills merchants — is *dark*. The Acorwood is dark. This observation, invariably made, does not exhaust what the place is. But it is where every account begins.\n\n---\n\n## Geography\n\nThe Acorwood occupies the transitional zone that connects the northern landmass extension to the main body of the continent. To its west: the final northward reach of the Oremindi, where the range descends and meets the bay's eastern shore. To its north: the lower Ganoss upland margins, where the forest thins into open heath within a day's walk. To its south: the transitional country of the Orched Hills, cleared and settled and crossed by roads that the Acorwood shadows without entering. To its east: the coast road and, beyond it, the sea.\n\nThe forest is not large by Azhoran standards. The Ibenwood is larger by an order of magnitude; even the Orched Hills' wooded valleys cover comparable ground. Measured at its margins — which is how the Academy has measured it, since interior surveys have not produced reliable numbers — the Acorwood is perhaps four to six days' travel at ordinary pace from its northern edge to its southern. Call it forty miles, call it fifty. On a map it appears as a green patch in a narrow corridor, surrounded on two sides by regions that have been described in detail and on two sides by geographic features that prevent circumnavigation except by sea.\n\nThe terrain beneath the forest is hilly — ridge-and-valley country, extension of the transitional ground on either side, with streams draining east to the coast and the occasional marsh where a valley has flattened and the drainage has slowed. The valleys are the natural routes of travel through. They are where travelers attempt passage. They are also where the canopy is thickest, because it is in the valley soils that the acor trees grow largest.\n\nThe acor tree — the species that names the forest and forms its dominant canopy — is a broad-crowned deciduous of unusual size and considerable age. The largest specimens are not as tall as the great trees of the Ibenwood's deep interior, but they are wide: a mature acor spreads its crown over a radius that exceeds any comparable temperate species outside the southwest forest. It bears mast in cycles of three to seven years — large, oil-rich nuts that drop in quantities that were, in good mast years, historically harvested by the northern communities from the forest's accessible margins. The Caeras Valley Academy's forest classification notes the acor as closely related to the broad-leafed species of the Orched Hills, which are themselves related to the interior deciduous trees of northern Mittolo. The relationship is not disputed. What has not been explained is why the Acorwood specimens are substantially larger than their relatives a day's walk to the south, or why the canopy they form has the quality that travelers describe.\n\nThe quality is the problem. The acor, when its canopy is established at sufficient density, interlocks crown to crown with its neighbors and with the secondary species beneath it — a second canopy of shade-tolerant trees that have grown where the acor's leaf-out permits, which is very little direct light, and which have therefore grown into the residual. The result is a layered roof of interleaved foliage through which light enters and is then scattered through so many surfaces that it arrives at the forest floor without a discernible direction.\n\nIn spring, before the leaf-out, the Acorwood admits full light. It is open in the way any northern deciduous forest is open before the canopy closes. Travelers who enter before the leaves are out report nothing unusual. After leaf-out, from late spring through the first frosts of autumn: the quality that everyone who enters in summer describes.\n\n---\n\n## The Dark\n\n\"Dark\" is inadequate and used anyway because nothing better has been found.\n\nThe Caeras Valley Academy's most detailed record of the Acorwood interior is a geographer's account of eight days' travel from the southern margin to the northern, written two generations ago and not repeated. The geographer was a methodical observer — her work on the Orched Hills ore deposits and the northern coastal survey are still the Academy's reference accounts for those regions — and her Acorwood record is among the most careful documents in the Academy's geography files. It does not resolve what the Acorwood is. It characterizes it, which is not the same thing.\n\nHer specific term is \"directionally nullified light.\" The mechanism, as she describes it: light enters the Acorwood. It illuminates. Objects at the forest floor are visible with reasonable clarity in daytime. What is absent is the sense of direction that light normally carries — the shadowing that tells a traveler which way the sun stands, the angles that allow navigation by sky position. At midday in the Acorwood interior on a clear summer day, the light falls equally from all sides. The shadows, where they exist at all, point nowhere consistently. The sun's position cannot be determined from inside the forest.\n\nThe practical consequence: sun navigation is impossible. The geographer maintained direction by compass and by the streams, which still drain east regardless of what the light is doing. She notes that travelers who enter without a compass and without the habit of reading drainage direction will turn in the Acorwood. Not because the forest disorients in any other way — she does not report the distances being wrong, as has been recorded in the Ibenwood interior — but because the one constant navigational reference available in open country is absent, and the ambient light offers no replacement.\n\nWhether this is entirely a function of the acor canopy at this density — the scattering and re-scattering of light through multiple layers of interleaved leaf until it loses its directionality — or whether something else contributes is a question the geographer declines to answer. She notes that the effect is present in the Acorwood and absent in the Orched Hills, which have comparable tree cover but not comparable species or density. She notes that the deepest sections of the Ibenwood have been described in terms that share certain qualities with the Acorwood — particularly the properties of light — but that she has not personally visited the Ibenwood's deep interior and cannot make the comparison from first-hand observation. She leaves the question open in the way that thorough scholars leave things that are genuinely open: without asserting a resolution.\n\nThe northern peoples who live at the Ganoss and the Orched Hills margins do not distinguish between mechanism and effect. The Acorwood is dark. You will not know north from south unless you know before you enter. These are facts in the same register as the mast cycle and the spring flood.\n\n---\n\n## The Barrier\n\nThe Acorwood's role as the boundary between Northern Azhora and the rest of the continent is not a political designation. No power has declared it a border. No treaty names it as a line. It is a boundary in the geographic sense: the thing that is in between.\n\nArmies moving between north and south have always faced the Acorwood as a logistical fact. The options are the forest itself, which is navigable in the right season with experienced guides and reliable compasses and does not allow the movement of large formations without an extended and costly period of route-clearing; the Oremindi passes to the west, which are severe at the range's northern end and seasonal in the best years; or the coast road to the east, which is narrow, easily watched, and the obvious route for any force large enough to matter. The coast road has therefore been the site of most northern conflicts. It has been fortified and contested and changed hands more times than the Orched Hills miners can reliably count.\n\nThe Acorwood has never been fortified because it cannot be held. It is not hostile to defenders. It is merely indifferent to everyone equally, which produces the same strategic result.\n\nThe consequence is that Northern Azhora and the rest of the continent have developed in partial separation for most of their recorded history. The separation is not total — the coast road exists, the bay is crossable, merchants move — but it is enough to make the north feel, from the south, like a different civilization, and the south feel, from the north, like a warmer and more complicated place that requires the coast road and the Acorwood crossing to reach. Both assessments are approximately correct.\n\n---\n\n## The People\n\nWhether anyone lives inside the Acorwood is not established.\n\nThe communities on the northern margin — the lower Ganoss settlements and the edge farms that push against the forest from the heath country — know the Acorwood's accessible margins in the way that communities know the edge of a forest they use without entering. They harvest mast in good mast years, working the outer tree lines. They cut edge timber where the canopy thins and the acor specimens are smaller and less covered. The forest products they take are all from the first quarter-mile; there is no evidence of any settled harvest practice deeper than that.\n\nThe communities on the southern margin, in the northern Orched Hills, maintain a similar relationship. They have been taking edge timber and occasional mast for as long as their records extend. They tell the same thing the northern communities tell: they do not go deep, the forest is dark, and their grandfathers did not go deep either, and their grandfathers said their grandfathers had not.\n\nWhether this represents an ancient decision or simply accumulated practice is not clear. No prohibition exists, in any documented form, against entering the Acorwood. No record of a community that tried to settle the interior and failed. The shallowness of the use is not explained; it simply is.\n\nOne account — a trading-house record from the northern Orched Hills, perhaps three generations old — notes smoke seen rising from the Acorwood interior in autumn of an unspecified year. The recorder treats it as notable but not alarming, and offers no further information. The Academy has no record of following up. The northern communities, when asked about it, say that smoke can come from inside without anyone they know having put it there, and that this is one of the things about the Acorwood that they have made their peace with.\n\nThe name for the Acorwood's interior in the northern Ganoss margin speech is a compound that translates approximately as \"the inside that knows itself.\" The southern Orched Hills communities use a different phrase in their dialect that produces, in Standard Mittoli, something like \"what belongs to the inside.\" Both translations have been noted in Academy language files. Neither has been pursued.\n\n---\n\n## Fauna\n\nThe Acorwood is not quiet. The travelers who have entered it consistently note that the bird life is dense — canopy birds, predominantly, species adapted to the light conditions and the mast crop, in numbers that seem high relative to the forest's apparent density. The mast cycles drive them: in a good mast year, the sound from the Acorwood's upper canopy is audible at the margins as a persistent layered noise, the production of a very large number of animals dealing with a very large food surplus.\n\nWhat lives below the canopy has been observed less directly. The geographer's account describes tracks and sign that she attributes to a large ungulate — she mentions hoof impressions in soft valley soil of a size that exceeds any cervid she knows from the northern regions, and what she interprets as browsing damage to upper-canopy growth by something considerably taller than any cervid she has seen. She does not see the animal itself. She notes that the browse damage is not recent — it is the weathered mark of a long-established pattern, not a recent passing.\n\nThe northern communities have a name for something in the Acorwood's interior. The name does not translate directly; it renders into Standard Mittoli as something between \"the tall one\" and \"the one that does not see you\" — the second part being a statement about attention rather than vision, indicating an animal that is not afraid of what it notices. They do not describe it in detail to outside questioners, not from reluctance but from the evident impression that a detailed description would not produce useful understanding. They know it is there. They do not go where it is. This is the relevant information.\n\nThe Academy naturalists who have seen the Cape Thalmagar materials have noted the browse damage description in the geographer's account and the description of the Long-Back from Thalmagar's forest margins in the same session. The notation in the Academy's comparison file reads: \"possible range extension, or related form, or the same tradition of large browsing ungulates in forests isolated long enough to preserve them.\" The notation is marked for follow-up. The follow-up has not occurred.\n\n---\n\n## The Acorwood and the Ibenwood\n\nThe Forest Mittoli communities of the southwest hold that the Dark Groves of the Mittolo heartland — the dense, anomalous patches of ancient woodland scattered through the interior, where maps disagree with themselves and distances behave strangely — are not anomalies. They are the Iben. The great southwestern forest once covered more of Azhora than it does now. What remains in the heartland are fragments of that earlier extent, cut off by clearing and cultivation but continuous with the main body in everything except space.\n\nOutside scholars who have encountered this position have generally found it interesting and difficult to evaluate. The Forest Mittoli do not present it as a historical claim in the sense that requires proof. They present it as a description of what the Iben is — not something that was larger and shrank, but something that is present in more of the continent than its current mapped extent suggests, and that the spaces between apparent fragments are not absences but misreadings.\n\nThe Academy has not, in any file, asked whether the Acorwood is related to this account. The Academy has Ibenwood files and Acorwood files and they are in different sections. No scholar appears to have placed them next to each other and asked what the light quality in the Ibenwood edge and the light quality in the Acorwood interior have in common. The Dark Groves file mentions the Acorwood once, in a list of northern forests to be investigated, and does not return to it.\n\nWhether this is an oversight or a consequence of the Academy's organizational structure or the inherent difficulty of the question is not established. The question is available to anyone who reads both files. The Forest Mittoli, if they have been asked about the Acorwood directly, have not given answers that appear in any Academy record. The Acorwood does not have Forest Mittoli communities the way the Ibenwood does — or at least none that have introduced themselves. Whether it has something else is the question the smoke and the tall-one's tracks make difficult to set aside entirely.\n\n---\n\n## Trade and the Margins\n\nThe Acorwood is commercially present to both the northern and southern communities primarily as mast. Acorn mast in a good year is a significant food source for both human communities and their animals — pigs pastured at the forest margins during mast years are a standard practice on both sides, well-documented in agricultural records. The mast cycle's irregularity is tracked as weather is tracked: noted, anticipated, corrected for when wrong. A good mast year shapes the following winter's herd numbers.\n\nBeyond mast: edge timber, where the acor specimens are smaller and accessible. The acor is a durable hardwood, preferred for specific applications in northern shipbuilding and for the wheel stock used in the Orched Hills' mine cart tradition. Edge harvesting is technically sustainable — the trees are large enough that margin cutting produces useful timber while leaving enough canopy to regenerate — and has been practiced on both sides long enough that the edge communities have developed their own protocols for what can be taken and what cannot.\n\nNo claim of political sovereignty over the Acorwood has been maintained for any extended period. Northern Azhoran city-states have asserted it in theory; Orched Hills mining communities have asserted it in theory. Neither has found a way to make the assertion meaningful in practice. The Acorwood belongs to no one in the sense that the forest products at its margins belong to whoever is there to collect them, and the interior belongs to whatever is in there, and the two conditions have coexisted without visible friction since the earliest records.\n\nThe coast road east of the Acorwood is the primary north-south route. It is not a pleasant road — the ground between the forest's edge and the sea is narrow, wet in wet seasons, liable to storm surge where it passes the coastal headlands — but it is a road that can be walked and mapped and garrisoned. It is the road by which most commerce between north and south actually moves, by which travelers and armies and ambassadors make their passage, and by which the two halves of the continent maintain the connection that the Acorwood does not prevent but does not facilitate.\n\nThe forest stands between them, dark in summer, open in the weeks before and after leaf-out, and present in every account as the fact that both sides have organized around: the thing you go around because going through requires more than most trips are worth, and in which the light comes from no direction, and which, in autumn, occasionally exhales a thread of smoke from somewhere it should not.",
      "climate": "Cfb"
    },
    "Cold Stones": {
      "name": "Cold Stones",
      "color": "#e91e63",
      "lore": "# Cold Stones\n\nCold Stones is an archipelago of pale, rocky islands sitting in the cold sea between Azhora's northern coast and the Ice Spear peninsula of the northern continent. It is the furthest north that most Azhoran sailors have gone, and for many expeditions it represents not a destination but the last reliable waypoint before the unknown.\n\nThe name distinguishes it from Blue Stones in Bouén — a common source of confusion in navigational records, where \"the Stones\" without qualification has sent at least two documented supply missions to entirely the wrong place.\n\n## Climate\n\nCold Stones sits at the intersection of two ocean current systems. The warm current that wraps around northern Azhora's western coast loses most of its warmth before reaching this latitude. The cold current flowing south along the eastern side of the northern continent brings Ice Spear's climate directly to the archipelago's northern edge. The result is cold in ways that defeat simple description, because temperature is not the primary fact. Wind is.\n\nStorms off the northern continent are not like Azhoran storms. Azhoran storms build gradually and offer sky-signs that experienced sailors read hours in advance. Cold Stones storms arrive quickly, often without the warning cues that Azhoran navigational training relies on. The permanent residents have at least six named categories of winter storm, distinguished by wind direction, sea surface behavior, and the quality of the cold itself — whether the cold is wet or dry, whether it comes with pressure drop or pressure rise, whether it means ice in the channel or simply misery on the shore.\n\n**Summer** lasts approximately three months. Extended daylight accompanies it, but not warmth. Average summer temperatures sit just above freezing at night and reach perhaps ten degrees above freezing at midday on the warmest days. The southern channel to Azhora clears. The northern channel toward Ice Spear opens to careful navigation. The vegetation flush is rapid and intense — coastal grass grows to knee height in the warmest weeks, lichen blooms turn the pale rock visibly green, and the seabird colonies on the outer stacks reach their fullest density. Visitors from Azhora consistently describe summer on Cold Stones as beautiful and cold. Residents do not find the description complete.\n\n**Winter** lasts approximately seven months. At the solstice, the sun rises for a few hours and does not climb high. Sea ice closes the northern channel by late autumn. By deep winter, the southern channel becomes unreliable. The islands do not typically freeze — the surrounding sea moderates the temperature — but they become wind-scoured and largely impassable in the worst storms, when visibility drops to a few feet and exposed skin freezes within minutes.\n\nThe permanent residents spend deep winter in their settlements, which are built low to the ground and partially below it. Winter is not described as hardship in the Cold Stones communities. It is described as *neth* — the local word for the cold season, unrelated to any Mittoli calendar term. What the Mithala plains calls *nilcaelith* (the dry river, winter) and what the Caeras cities mark on their astronomical calendar have nothing to do with this. *Neth* is a different thing.\n\n## The Islands\n\nCold Stones comprises roughly thirty islands of notable size and several hundred smaller outcrops and sea-stacks. The larger islands are low and wind-scoured, their surfaces covered in pale rock, hardy coastal grass, and the grey-green lichen that is one of the few things that thrives in the archipelago's conditions.\n\nThe rock itself is the feature that gave the islands their name — a pale, almost white sandstone unusual for this latitude, where darker igneous rock dominates. The pale stone is visible from considerable distance and gives the archipelago a ghostly quality in low light, particularly in the long winter dusk when the islands glow briefly before going dark.\n\nSea ice does not typically form on the islands themselves except in the worst winters, but the sea between them and Ice Spear carries drifting pack ice in all but the warmest months. The channel approach from the south, from Azhora, is clear in summer and difficult in winter. The northern approach is rarely attempted.\n\n## The Sea\n\nThe water around Cold Stones is cold, rough, and productive. The same current system that makes the climate severe makes the sea rich: cold, well-oxygenated water supports dense populations of the species on which the archipelago's food economy depends. The fish are large and numerous by Azhoran standards. The communities do not fish far from shore — the outer sea is too difficult in most conditions — but the inshore waters yield enough year-round to sustain the population without significant supplementation.\n\nSea mammals are present in numbers. Seals use the outer rock stacks as haul-out points seasonally and the residents hunt them, primarily for oil and fur rather than meat. Larger animals — something in the walrus family, never closely described in Academy records — have been reported on the northern rocks, though the residents are noncommittal about their habits and do not name them in the vocabulary they share with outsiders. Seabirds are prolific: a dozen nesting species occupy the outer stacks in summer, and the egg-harvest from accessible colonies is an important part of the spring diet. In winter most species leave; a few remain year-round, and the residents know them individually by behavior if not by name.\n\nWhales transit the deeper waters south of the archipelago. The residents do not hunt them — their boats are not built for it — but they have detailed knowledge of the transit patterns, seasons, and species composition, which they communicate to Academy expeditions in exchange for specific goods and without commentary on why the Academy wants to know.\n\n## Habitation\n\nCold Stones has been intermittently inhabited for as long as Azhorans have been sailing north. Whether it was inhabited before Azhorans arrived is an open question — certain features of the larger islands, including a series of stone enclosures on the second-largest island that predate any known Azhoran presence, suggest the answer may be yes.\n\nCurrently, Cold Stones supports two small permanent communities on the largest island — fishing families whose ancestors came from the Smunders Bay coast and who have been here long enough to constitute a distinct sub-culture. They speak a dialect of Northern Mittoli that has drifted considerably from the mainland version and has absorbed vocabulary from sources no one has identified. They are accustomed to hosting Academy expeditions with the specific combination of hospitality and mild contempt that comes from repeatedly accommodating people who think they are the first to notice something you have lived next to your entire life.\n\n### The Communities in Winter\n\nThe two communities — separated by about three miles of coastline and a ridge — maintain distinct household traditions but share resources during the hardest winter months, which function as a single extended community period. The buildings are stone-walled and partially dug into the hillside, with turf-covered roofing that sheds wind better than any pitched surface. Interior spaces are small. Multiple families share a single building during the coldest period; the arrangement is practical and unromantic about it.\n\nThe diet in winter is stored fish (dried or cold-smoked over fuel that has to be brought from Azhora, since the islands provide almost none), seal oil, preserved eggs, and a starchy root crop that grows in the thin soil in summer and stores well underground. Fresh food arrives with the first navigable days of late spring. The permanent residents do not treat the winter diet as deprivation; they treat it as the winter diet. Visitors who arrive expecting to supplement Academy supplies with local provisions are told clearly what is available and at what price.\n\nThe oil lamps burn through winter. The buildings are dark and warm and smell of fish and seal fat and people. Academy expedition members who have wintered on Cold Stones describe the experience with a range of responses, none of which the permanent residents find particularly interesting.\n\n## The Old Enclosures\n\nOn the second-largest island, a ridge of the pale sandstone running east-west has had its southern face shaped into a series of enclosures — roughly rectangular, roughly the same size, built from the same stone that forms the ridge but worked rather than natural. The working is good: the stones are fitted without mortar and have not shifted perceptibly despite centuries of frost cycles. The enclosures are open to the sky. Their interiors show no evidence of roofing and no obvious evidence of function — no hearth deposits, no occupation layer, nothing that suggests habitation, storage, or animal keeping.\n\nWhat they are for is not known. The permanent residents do not know either, or say they do not. They do not avoid the enclosures, but they do not particularly use them. They are described, in the local dialect, as *sketh-nor* — a compound that does not translate neatly but carries something like \"the placed stones\" or \"the stones that were put there,\" with an implication that the putting-there was deliberate and that the purpose of the deliberateness has not been communicated. When pressed, residents say the enclosures were here before their grandparents' grandparents came, and before whoever was here before that.\n\nThe Academy's northern expedition spent two days examining the enclosures and produced a report that describes their physical characteristics accurately and declines to speculate on their purpose. The lead archaeologist's field notes, which were not included in the official report, describe a feeling of being examined that they attribute to wind through the stone gaps and then cross out.\n\nThe alignment of the enclosures — open faces all oriented at the same bearing, slightly west of south — has been noted but not explained.\n\n## As Waypoint\n\nCold Stones is the staging ground for any serious attempt on the northern continent. Both Academy expeditions used the largest island as their winter base. The permanent residents have strong opinions about this, which they express through the pricing of supplies and the quality of information they choose to share.\n\nFrom the northern shore of Cold Stones, Ice Spear is visible on clear days as a pale presence on the horizon, roughly sixty miles away. The water between is cold, often rough, and in winter, heavily iced. The sixty miles might as well be six hundred in terms of the difficulty of crossing them reliably.\n\n## The Dialect\n\nThe variety of Northern Mittoli spoken on Cold Stones has drifted from the mainland version in ways consistent with long isolation and then absorbed vocabulary from a source that no linguist has identified. The grammar is recognizable Northern Mittoli — fixed initial stress, final vowel deletion, collapsed *ae* diphthong — but the phonological inventory includes sounds that Standard and Northern Mittoli do not have, suggesting either the influence of an older contact language or independent phonological development in isolation. The former is more likely, given that several of the unidentified vocabulary items cluster around specific semantic domains: sea ice, storm types, light conditions, and the old enclosures.\n\nSelected dialect vocabulary not traceable to any Mittoli root:\n\n*neth* — the cold season; winter. Distinguished from the Mittoli *nilcaelith* by covering both the depth of winter and the months of approach and departure on either side. *Neth* begins when the light changes, not when the temperature drops. Residents say they can feel *neth* arriving in the quality of late-summer light before any thermometric change.\n\n*sketh* — ice that is on stone or land, as opposed to sea ice. The distinction matters: *sketh* on a path is a danger, *sketh* on a roof is structural load, *sketh* on the ridge is a weather indicator. Sea ice has a different word.\n\n*droven* — sea ice; specifically floating pack ice, as opposed to ice that is anchored to the shore or fixed. The word carries the sense of the ice being in motion — *droven* that has stilled is *sketh-droven*, \"landed ice.\" Navigators use these terms precisely and find Azhoran navigational language for ice conditions imprecise.\n\n*skelver* — a storm that arrives without the usual sky precursors. The sudden type. Distinguished from storms that give warning not because the storm itself is different but because the local knowledge cannot be applied: *skelver* is what catches you out. A *skelver* in summer is unusual and discussed afterward. A *skelver* in winter is expected several times per season.\n\n*thrag* — the quality of light at the winter solstice, when the sun is present for only a few hours and never climbs above the ridge line. Not darkness; a specific dim that comes from the south, flat and cold, without casting shadows in any useful direction. Visitors find it disorienting. Residents describe it as *thrag* and leave it there.\n\n*ungvel* — the open northern sea when it is completely clear of ice; a rare condition that the residents treat as notable. Also used as a term of provisional good fortune: \"it is *ungvel* today\" carries the sense of \"conditions are favorable, do not rely on this.\"\n\nThe Academy's position is that these terms are borrowings of unknown origin. The residents' position is that they are words for things that needed naming.\n\n## Compass Anomaly\n\nNavigation within and around Cold Stones requires local knowledge because compass needles behave inconsistently in the archipelago. The deviation is not constant — it shifts across the day in ways not attributable to any identified magnetic deposit, and it worsens significantly as you approach the northern edge of the archipelago near the open channel to Ice Spear. In that area, needles have been observed to rotate slowly in a clockwise direction, to hold a bearing that is approximately ninety degrees off magnetic north, or to oscillate without settling. The pattern does not repeat predictably between visits.\n\nNavigators who rely on instruments rather than dead reckoning and star observation have run into trouble here regularly enough that all Academy expedition reports include explicit warnings. The second expedition's navigator lost two days in fog with a misbehaving compass and recorded a level of frustration that reads through the formal language of the expedition log.\n\nThe permanent residents navigate by star, current, wave pattern, and local knowledge of the channels between islands. They do not use compasses. They find the outsiders' reliance on them faintly baffling, in the specific way that people who have never needed a thing find baffling the distress of those who cannot function without it.\n\nWhether the compass anomaly is related to whatever produces the anomalous features of the enclosures on the second-largest island is a question the Academy has not formally posed. The two phenomena are documented in separate reports by separate expeditions and have not been discussed in the same paper. The residents, asked directly, say the enclosures have nothing to do with it and then change the subject to something practical.",
      "loreRef": "cold_stones",
      "climate": "Dfc"
    },
    "Central Lond": {
      "name": "Central Lond",
      "color": "#ff5722",
      "lore": "# Lond\n\nThe most important thing about Lond is the thing it doesn't look like. From the south, coming up through the Orched Hills and over the ridge that separates the transitional country from the true north, what a traveler sees is an open plain — wide, dark-soiled, unremarkable. Not impressive terrain by the standards of a continent that includes the Ibenwood and the Oremindi and the Acorwood's unanswerable dark. Just ground, and a lot of it, with the sky sitting low in every direction and the cold coming straight down from the north without the hills to break it.\n\nWhat the open ground is, taken together: the largest extent of arable land in Northern Azhora by a margin large enough that the rest of the north's agriculture, combined, does not match it. What grows in Lond's dark plateau soil feeds communities on three coasts and the Ganoss upland clans besides. The north without Lond's harvest is the north calculating what it can do without, which is not a calculation the north enjoys.\n\nAnd at the center of the plain, visible from every approach long before anything else in the landscape resolves into detail: the formation that gives the place its strategic meaning. From a distance, in the grey flat light of a northern morning, it looks like what the name says it is. It looks like a fist.\n\n---\n\n## Geography\n\nThe Lond plateau is the central inland mass of Northern Azhora — bounded to the north by the lower margins of the Ganoss uplands, where the fertile soil thins into heath and the elevation climbs into country too exposed and cold for grain; to the west by the Endevor hills, where the plateau's edge breaks into the folded valley country that descends eventually toward the Bay of Lol; to the east by the gradual slope that leads down to the Smunders Bay watershed; and to the south by the transitional ground where the highland gives way to the Orched Hills' forested ridges and the Acorwood's northwestern approaches.\n\nThe plateau is elevated but not severely so. It sits higher than the coastal country on either side — high enough that the approach from any direction involves climbing, and high enough that the winters arrive harder and sooner than at the Bay of Lol — but lower than the Ganoss uplands, and sufficiently sheltered by the upland mass to its north to avoid the worst of the open-sea winds. The result is a climate that is cold in winter, thoroughly so, and productive in summer in a way that the Ganoss, sitting fully in the wind's path, is not. The Lond plateau gets the north's short growing season and makes full use of it. The Ganoss gets the same season and cannot.\n\nThe soil is the explanation for the fertility. Millennia of accumulated organic matter — decomposed heath, compressed peat, the slow chemistry of elevation and moisture and deep cold — have produced a dark topsoil of unusual depth and density across the plateau's middle ground. Where streams cross the plain the soil is darkest and deepest, and these are the oldest-farmed strips, evident from the air in their centuries-smoothed ridges and the particular color difference that long-worked ground shows against unworked.\n\nThe plateau drains in two directions, which is the geographic fact that makes it a strategic crossroads rather than simply a productive one. The western rivers run toward the Bay of Lol watershed, ultimately joining the routes that connect the plateau's agricultural surplus to the northwest coast. The eastern drainage runs toward Smunders Bay. The ridgeline that separates these two drainages runs roughly through the center of the plain, and Stonefist sits at its highest point.\n\n---\n\n## Stonefist\n\nWhatever formed it did so a very long time before anyone needed a fortress.\n\nThe Lond plateau, in the long geological record, was once more varied than it appears now — harder and softer rock in alternating formations, the softer layers subject to the slow grinding of whatever covered this country in earlier ages. The softer rock wore. The harder rock did not. What remains above the plateau surface — what the plain wears like a knuckle showing through thin skin — is a mass of intrusive igneous stone, denser and more resistant than anything around it, which the millennia that eroded the surrounding plain have left standing alone.\n\nIt rises between eighty and a hundred feet above the plateau, depending on which face is measured. It is not a single column or a smooth dome. The rock is fractured vertically by ancient cooling stress, which has produced a cluster of irregular columns and buttresses of varying height, some standing slightly separated from the main mass by fissures wide enough to walk through, others fused into continuous face. The columns vary in height by twenty or thirty feet among themselves, which produces — from the correct angle, at sufficient distance, in the particular condition of northern light that tends to flatten and dramatize simultaneously — the impression of a hand clenched into a fist, the tallest columns as the two middle knuckles, the shorter columns on either side as the index and little finger, and the broad base descending into the ground where the igneous mass roots into the plateau's underbelly.\n\nThe name is not metaphorical. It is descriptive. Every account of the first approach to Lond from the south, from any direction that keeps the formation in view long enough to resolve, notes the shape without apparent surprise — as if the name had prepared the traveler adequately, which it had, because the name is simply what it looks like.\n\nUp close, the dramatic quality is replaced by a different one: the rock is traversable in a way that a cliff face is not. The fissures between columns provide natural passages into the formation's interior. The horizontal fractures at various heights produce ledges, some wide enough for a standing person, some wide enough for several. The internal chambers — spaces between columns where the roof has been provided by a fallen slab, or where the geometry of the surrounding rock creates an overhang — are not comfortable by any southern standard but are sheltered from wind and waterproof where the original stone has not cracked. The rock's highest accessible ledge provides a view of the entire Lond plateau and, on clear days, of the Ganoss upland margins to the north and the Endevor hill country to the west.\n\nThe work that human hands have done to Stonefist is present but minimal by design: minimal in that it has avoided adding anything the rock does not support, and significant in that it has made the rock's natural properties substantially more usable. Gaps in the outer face that might allow entry where entry is not wanted have been filled with dry-laid stone, leaving the natural vertical profile intact while converting the interior from accessible to garrisoned. The single negotiable approach — the eastern face, which is not a cliff but a steep slope of tumbled rock — has been terraced with cut stone stairs and walled at two choke points where the approach narrows naturally. Internal ledges have been floored with timber where a chamber needs to house people rather than just protect them. Three cisterns have been cut into the rock floor in the main interior chamber, fed by the thin seep of water that works through the fissure system during snowmelt and rain. The cisterns are not comfortable, but they hold enough water to outlast most sieges that any northern power has been able to mount.\n\nThe garrison that holds Stonefist can see the entire plain. An army crossing Lond is visible from the formation's upper ledge for the better part of a day before it arrives. This is not a tactical advantage in the sense of an archer's angle or a cavalry's flank; it is a strategic advantage in the sense that whoever is in Stonefist is never surprised, never uninformed about what is crossing the plateau, and never reduced to reacting rather than deciding. The difference between reaction and decision, over the generations of Lond's contested history, has been the difference between survival and otherwise more times than the Lond communities can straightforwardly count.\n\nThe formation also controls water in a practical sense. The springs that emerge at the base of the igneous mass — where the impermeable rock forces the plateau's groundwater up and out — are the most reliable water sources in the central plain. Communities on the plateau have always clustered within a day's walk of Stonefist partly for the stone and partly for the springs. A garrison in Stonefist that chose to deny the springs below it would make life on the central plateau very difficult very quickly. No garrison in recorded memory has found it advantageous to do this — the point of holding Lond is to hold its population and its harvest, not to destroy them — but the fact that it could be done is not entirely absent from negotiations about who the garrison will be.\n\n---\n\n## The People\n\nThe Lond farmers do not describe themselves in terms that outside visitors find immediately legible. They do not say they are hardy, which they are. They do not say they are practical, which they also are. What they say, when pressed to characterize themselves, is a Northern Mittoli phrase that translates most directly as \"we know where the frost goes.\"\n\nThe statement is meteorological and metaphorical at once. The frost on the Lond plateau follows the terrain: it settles in the low spots, pools along the stream courses, lingers in the hollows after the higher ground has warmed. A farmer who knows where the frost goes knows which fields to plant last and which to harvest first, which ground will hold the cold through a night that feels warm and therefore lose the crop, and which ground will shed it early and reward the risk of an early sowing. The knowledge is hyper-local, granular, and not transferable in the abstract — it lives in the body of someone who has walked these specific fields through specific seasons for long enough to have made every calibration the terrain requires.\n\nThe same phrase, applied to politics, means: we know which way trouble settles and how long it stays before it moves on. This knowledge is also hyper-local and also not abstract, and it has been earned the same way — through many seasons of watching the terrain.\n\nThe Lond plateau does not have the valley-by-valley social structure of Endevor or the clan mobility of the Ganoss. Its communities are larger, more settled, and more mixed in ancestry than either — open-plain communities that have been accumulating population from all directions for as long as the plateau has been worth farming, which is a long time. The settlements are not walled. The landscape does not permit the kind of defensible position that valley country or upland heath provides. Defense on the Lond plateau means keeping Stonefist friendly and maintaining the social arrangements that make keeping it friendly possible. This has shaped the Lond character in the direction of negotiated accommodation rather than armed resistance. When Stonefist changes hands — which it does, with some regularity, in the larger view — the Lond communities accommodate the new garrison with a competence that outside observers sometimes read as servility and that the Lond communities themselves read as sense.\n\nThe grain is planted regardless of who is in Stonefist. The harvest is brought in regardless of who is in Stonefist. The surplus goes to whoever is in Stonefist, in quantities that have been calibrated by long practice to sustain a garrison without making the communities themselves hungry. The relationship has been renegotiated in every generation, sometimes loudly, but its basic structure has persisted because both sides need it: the garrison needs to eat, and the farmers need not to be the thing the garrison is pointed at.\n\nWhether the Lond communities feel anything about this beyond pragmatic calculation is a question that comes up more readily in the asking than in the answering. The word the Lond dialect uses for Stonefist does not appear in any account prior to the current era; the older word, preserved in one archaic Northern Mittoli text, means something closer to \"the fixed thing\" — not the fist, not the rock, but the feature of the landscape that does not change while everything around it does. That the current name is also the older name read differently, depending on what the light is doing, is the kind of ambiguity the Lond communities do not explain but also do not appear to find accidental.\n\n---\n\n## Stonefist and the North's Routes\n\nThe plateau sits at the intersection of the north's overland connections in a way that no other piece of ground in the region replicates.\n\nThe east-west route between the Bay of Lol coast and the Smunders Bay coast — the two ports that together service most of the north's external trade — can be made by sea, but only in the right season, by ships that can manage the northern cape. The overland alternative crosses the Lond plateau, and the practical path across the plateau passes within a few hours of Stonefist regardless of which route a traveler takes. A garrison that chose to levy toll on this traffic would be in an essentially uncontestable position; a garrison that chose to restrict it would separate the north's two coasts from each other more effectively than weather.\n\nThe north-south route from the Ganoss upland clans to the Orched Hills and eventually the Mittolo approach passes through Lond as well. The Ganoss clans move their herds to lower pasture in autumn; the lower pasture that the plateau's margins provide is part of the seasonal movement, and the route through Lond to the Orched Hills is how they reach the winter trading grounds where they exchange livestock and wool for the goods the uplands cannot produce. Closing or tolling this route is the lever that whoever holds Stonefist can apply to the Ganoss in ways that other regional powers cannot — a source of periodic friction and periodic advantage, depending on the relationship between the current garrison and the current clan leadership.\n\nThe third route connection is the most historically significant: the corridor through Endevor toward the southern Acorwood approach. The Acorwood is navigable by experienced travelers; the coast road east of the Acorwood is the main artery. Whoever holds Stonefist and can reach this corridor controls the only northern power with a plausible route of influence into the Acorwood margins and eventually the Orched Hills. The Endevor mine-kings understood this. Their empire required Lond because without it their southern tributary relationships could be bypassed. Every subsequent power that has held Stonefist has come to the same understanding, sometimes by deliberate calculation and sometimes by making the mistake of ignoring it once.\n\nWhat this adds up to is that Stonefist is not a fortress that happens to sit in a productive plain. It is the keystone of Northern Azhora's overland network, and the plain is what makes the keystone sustainable — the logistics base that turns a defensible position into a durable power. A garrison in a rock without food within reach is a siege waiting to happen. Stonefist and Lond together are a different thing: the rock fed by the plain, the plain protected by the rock, each necessary to the other's function in a way that makes the combination more stable than either element alone.\n\n---\n\n## History\n\nStonefist has not been empty, in all of recorded northern history, for any span of time that could be measured in years rather than months. Something is always in it. This is the most reliable fact about the north.\n\nThe Endevor mine-kings held Lond during the *Eld-Gold* period — the Vornheld tradition places Lond grain in the tribute accounts, and the distribution of barrows on the Endevor ridgelines suggests a reach eastward that would have required Lond's cooperation at minimum. Whether the mine-kings built any of Stonefist's human infrastructure or simply used what was already there when they arrived is a question the Vornheld tradition does not address; the implication, in the naming-poems that mention Lond, is that the rock was already useful and already used, which suggests the improvements are older than the Endevor empire or were made too incrementally to record as a single king's achievement.\n\nAfter the Endevor decline, Stonefist entered the period of its most rapid ownership succession. The raiding communities of the Bay of Lol coast — newly freed from the mine-kings' redistributive network and looking for other ways to secure grain — attempted Stonefist four times in the two generations following the empire's dissolution. They took it twice, held it for periods ranging from three years to eleven, and were displaced both times by coalitions of Ganoss clans who had their own interest in the plateau's grain access and their own grievances about coastal levies on the upland autumn route. The Ganoss clans held it longer than the coastal powers but not permanently; the upland clan structure is not suited to sustained garrison work, and the clan leadership that took Stonefist in a given generation was rarely able to pass the holding intact to the next.\n\nThe result, across the two centuries following the Endevor collapse, was a Stonefist that changed hands approximately every decade and occasionally more often — a rhythm that the Lond farmers came to treat as a background condition of their existence, the way the Endevor hill communities treat the Acorwood's smoke. You do not prevent it. You learn to read it.\n\nThe longer periods of stable holding, when they occurred, tended to produce the most infrastructure: the terraced eastern approach dates to one such period, attributed in the northern records to a Bay of Lol coalition that held Stonefist for roughly thirty years in the middle historical period and spent the resources on engineering rather than tribute-extraction. The Lond communities remember this period as the one where the garrison behaved well and the grain went to Stonefist in good faith rather than under duress. What ended it was a succession dispute in the Bay of Lol coalition that had nothing to do with Lond and everything to do with who inherited the ships. Stonefist then changed hands three times in six years.\n\nThe Lond communities' institutional memory of all this is not written in any formal record. The Academy has found nothing in Stroven's archive comparable to the Endevor record-keeping tradition, and Lond has no Vornheld equivalent. What Lond has instead is a distributed and practical knowledge about Stonefist that lives in the farming communities themselves: who is in there, what they want, how long this kind tends to last, and what the negotiable terms of the current arrangement are. This knowledge is transmitted informally and loses nothing in transmission, because the transmission happens continuously, between people who are living inside its consequences.\n\n---\n\n## Agriculture\n\nLond grows rye and barley in quantities that define the north's grain economy. Both are cold-tolerant crops — rye in particular will germinate in soil temperatures that kill the more productive southern grains — and both have been bred here across generations for the specific conditions of a highland plain: short seasons, hard frosts, wet springs, the occasional summer storm that can flatten unprocessed crop. The varieties grown in Lond are demonstrably different from their Orched Hills or southern Mittoli cousins. How long they have been different is a question for Academy botanists who have not yet made the trip.\n\nRoot crops supplement the grain: turnips, the northern potato-root varieties, and several storage vegetables that the Lond dialect names with words that do not appear in standard Mittoli dictionaries and that Academy linguists have not yet catalogued. Storage is the critical skill. A Lond farming household that cannot keep grain and root crops edible through a winter of five or six months has miscalculated something important, and the calculus of storage — what dries, what pits, what requires covered structures, what quantity of which type survives which conditions — is knowledge that the farming communities hold with the same density and granularity as their frost knowledge.\n\nCattle are kept on the plateau margins and on the eastern slopes where the terrain breaks the worst of the wind. They are not Lond's primary product and have not been — the soil and the season favor grain over grazing — but they are present, and the herds produce enough for local consumption and a modest dairy trade with the Ganoss clans, who come down in autumn and will take butter and cheese in exchange for wool and livestock they don't need for the upland winter.\n\nThe grain surplus, in a good year, is significant. The Bay of Lol communities have bought Lond grain since the earliest coastal records. Smunders Bay uses it to supplement its fishing-based diet. The Orched Hills mining settlements, which have the ore wealth and not the agricultural space, are consistent and reliable purchasers. In bad years — a late frost, a wet summer that brings the fungal rots, the specific failure of a dry August that leaves the rye short — these same communities know the shortage is coming before it arrives, because they watch Lond the way they watch weather, and begin their own adjustments accordingly. The north's food security and Lond's harvest are understood, by everyone involved, to be the same variable.\n\n---\n\n## Language\n\nThe Lond dialect of Northern Mittoli is closer to the Bay of Lol coastal speech than to the Endevor or Ganoss varieties — less conservative than Endevor, less divergent than the Ganoss upland speech, with the flattened vowels and shortened sentences of the north but without the particular substrate elements that give Endevor its unusual vocabulary. Academy linguists classify it as Northern Mittoli standard, which in the context of northern varieties means it is the least interesting specimen in the region, and which the Lond communities would agree with if they thought the classification worth engaging.\n\nWhat the Lond dialect does have, unremarkably, is density of agricultural vocabulary — not a substrate layer in the manner of Endevor's smoke words, but a specialized lexicon developed from within the Northern Mittoli word-stock for the granular requirements of highland grain farming. There are fourteen terms for stages of rye maturity, nine for frost conditions relevant to planting decisions, a complex tense structure around harvest timing that expresses degrees of urgency not available in standard Mittoli grammar. None of this is exotic to the communities that use it; it is simply the language that the work requires.\n\nThe name \"Lond\" derives, in the leading Academy interpretation, from an archaic Northern Mittoli root for \"broad\" or \"open ground\" — *lon*, a word now obsolete in the living language but reconstructable from its cognates in the Bay of Lol coastal speech, where related words for open water survive. The compound with the locative *-d* suffix would give: \"the open place,\" \"the broad one,\" \"the flat.\" The interpretation has the quality of being plausible, consistent with the attested phonology, and entirely insufficient as a description of what the name means to the people who use it, who have been using it long enough that the word is the place and the derivation is an academic exercise.",
      "loreRef": "lond",
      "climate": "Cfb"
    },
