      "lore": "# Nonoth\n\nMost northern regions define themselves by what they produce or where they sit or what their terrain does to everyone who tries to cross it. Nonoth defines itself by whom it stands behind.\n\nThe land between the two branches of the Ond is fertile, well-watered, and clear in its borders — the rivers mark the western and eastern edges with the precision that other regions' mountains and uplands cannot match. A Nonoth community knows exactly where it is. But this geographic legibility is not what the Nonoth have made central to their identity. What they have made central is the political fact: Nonoth is the vassal of whoever holds Stonefist, and has been for as long as anyone can trace the relationship, and intends to continue. This is not resignation. It is principle, and the difference matters to them in ways that outside observers who read it as simple subordination consistently miss.\n\n---\n\n## Geography\n\nThe Ond descends from the Gorgi Mountains' southern face carrying the snowmelt of the northern interior — the accumulated drainage of a range extensive enough to maintain significant snowpack well into summer — and enters the Ganoss upland margins as a single, substantial channel. South of the upland edge, where the terrain flattens and the gradient eases, the Ond forks. The two branches that result are not equal: the western branch, which the surrounding communities call the Velond, is the larger and faster; the eastern branch, the Vornond, is slower and more prone to the seasonal flooding that makes its lower banks difficult to farm in wet years. Both are significant rivers by northern standards. Together they are the largest riverine system in Northern Azhora by volume, fed as they are by the most extensive highland snowpack on the continent's northern half.\n\nNonoth is the wedge of ground between them.\n\nThe fork is the northern point; the territory widens as the two branches diverge southward before eventually moving beyond Nonoth's settled ground and into the broader drainage basins that feed the Bay of Lol watershed to the west and the Sond-tributaries to the east. The width of the territory at its broadest — roughly where the main settlements are — is perhaps two days' travel between the riverbanks. The length from the fork to the southern edge of consistent habitation is four to five days. The shape, on any map that shows the two river courses, is unmistakable: a broadening leaf of land, water on either side, the Gorgi Mountains as its stem.\n\nThe terrain within the wedge is the product of long river influence: flat bottomland near both banks, dark-soiled and damp, transitioning to slightly higher ground in the middle strip where the soil is still deep but the drainage is better. The middle strip is where the largest Nonoth settlements have established themselves — elevated enough above both flood plains to be reliable, close enough to both rivers to use them. The bottomland near the Velond's western bank and the Vornond's eastern bank is farmed intensively where it can be managed and left as water meadow where it cannot.\n\nThe climate is colder than Lond's plateau — Nonoth sits further north, closer to the Gorgi's influence — but the river corridors moderate the worst of it, and the river valleys accumulate warmth in summer in ways the open Lond plateau does not. The growing season is shorter than Lond's, but the bottomland fertility compensates in yield per acre what it loses in weeks of cultivation time.\n\n---\n\n## The Ond and Its Branches\n\nThe Ond above the fork is navigable by small craft for a short season in late summer, when the snowmelt has settled and the channel has stabilized. The fork itself is too shallow and braided to be reliably useful for cargo. The lower Velond — south of the fork, where the gradient is gentle and the channel has deepened and clarified — is navigable for a longer season and carries modest boat traffic between the Nonoth settlements and the Bay of Lol watershed approaches to the southwest, eventually making contact with the trade routes that run through Endevor's margins.\n\nThe Vornond is less navigable than its western counterpart — the eastern branch is shallower, more prone to seasonal variation, and interrupted in places by reaches that require portage. The communities on the Vornond's eastern bank use it primarily for fishing and for the small-scale movement of goods that does not require consistent navigability. The larger commercial traffic of Nonoth moves on the Velond or overland.\n\nThe rivers are fished extensively. Cold, fast water from the Gorgi snowmelt carries the specific fish populations that the northern interior streams support — the same cold-water species that the Witherst communities harvest from the sea, here present in the upper river system in numbers that sustain a secondary food economy alongside the bottomland agriculture. Nonoth communities on both riverbanks maintain weir systems and seasonal fish camps that supplement the stored grain through winter. In a bad harvest year, the rivers feed the community; in a bad fishing year, the harvest feeds it. The two systems together provide a resilience that neither would alone.\n\nBoth rivers flood in spring, which the Nonoth manage rather than prevent. The annual flood deposits the silt and organic matter that refreshes the bottomland fertility, and the farming calendar is organized around the flood's timing — late to plant, fast to harvest, with the interval between precisely calculated by generations of observation. A family that misjudges the flood timing loses the crop. This has happened enough times in every generation that it no longer happens to the same family twice.\n\n---\n\n## The People\n\nThe Nonoth are a settled, stable, and in northern terms moderately prosperous people whose primary social identity is not the community they live in or the valley system they are part of but the political relationship they maintain. This is unusual in the north. The Lond communities know themselves by their fields. The Endevor know themselves by their valleys and their Vornheld memory. The Ganun know themselves by the river they work. The Nonoth know themselves by whom they serve.\n\nThis is not how the Nonoth would phrase it, exactly. They would say: Stonefist is the proper seat of northern authority, and Nonoth is the proper support of that seat. The framing matters. They are not the people who submit to Stonefist. They are the people who recognize Stonefist, and the recognition is a statement about the north's correct political order rather than about their own subordination.\n\nThe practical expression of this identity is a particular kind of social seriousness about political relationships. The Nonoth pay close attention to Stonefist — who holds it, how they hold it, what the current terms of the arrangement are, and whether those terms are being honored. They are not passive in their loyalty; they are attentive. A Nonoth council that has decided a new Stonefist ruler is legitimate will be among the first in the north to formally acknowledge it, and the acknowledgment carries weight precisely because it comes first and because it has historically been reliable. Conversely, if the Nonoth are slow to acknowledge a new holder, other northern powers read the hesitation as information.\n\nThey are not, and have not been, a military power in the conventional sense. The Ond's two branches do not produce the kind of armed culture that the raiding traditions of the coast or the Orgmala highland generated. The Nonoth contribution to Stonefist's authority is manpower for garrison work, agricultural surplus that supplements Lond's, and the institutional weight of a recognized and continuous loyalty that other claimants to Stonefist's authority have consistently found worth having. You can hold Stonefist against the Nonoth's resistance if you are strong enough. It is much easier to hold it with their support.\n\n---\n\n## The Vassalage\n\nThe formal relationship between Nonoth and Stonefist is the most explicit political bond in Northern Azhora outside of the Mittoli city-state system. It involves acknowledged obligations in both directions: Nonoth provides a specified share of its harvest, a specified number of men for Stonefist's garrison in call-up seasons, and the institutional recognition that legitimizes new holders. Stonefist's holders provide, in return, military protection for the river territory — specifically against Orgmala raiding from the west, against Ganoss incursions from the north, and against whatever other pressures the current era produces — and preferential access to the trade routes that Stonefist controls.\n\nThe obligations are not written in a single document. They have been renegotiated, in practice, at every major transition of Stonefist's holders, which given the frequency with which Stonefist changes hands means the Nonoth have considerable experience in the mechanics of establishing terms with new rulers who need their support. The Nonoth do not enter these negotiations as supplicants. They enter them as the party whose recognition a new holder requires, and they negotiate accordingly. The terms have not changed dramatically across recorded history — the structure is old and the values are known — but the specific form of each renegotiation reflects the current balance of need.\n\nWhen a new power takes Stonefist, the question \"have the Nonoth recognized?\" is one of the first asked by every other northern power making its own calculation about whether the transfer is durable. A Stonefist holder without Nonoth recognition is a garrison in a rock with an uncertain future. A Stonefist holder with Nonoth recognition has a functioning tributary arrangement and the beginning of legitimate authority. The difference is significant enough that Nonoth recognition has become, over time, something that new Stonefist holders seek before they have fully consolidated their position, because the act of seeking it and receiving it is itself a stabilizing signal.\n\nThe Nonoth are aware of this leverage and use it with the care that a tool of limited applications requires. Withholding recognition entirely is an option they have exercised, in recorded history, on three occasions. On two of those occasions, the holder they declined to recognize was displaced within a generation. On the third occasion, the holder they declined to recognize managed to consolidate anyway, and the subsequent relationship between that holder and Nonoth was, by all accounts, uncomfortable for both parties for the duration of that holder's time at Stonefist. The lesson the Nonoth draw from the third occasion is not that recognition can be withheld without cost, but that the conditions under which it should be withheld require very careful assessment.\n\n---\n\n## History\n\nThe origin of the Nonoth-Stonefist relationship is not precisely traceable. The earliest records in both Nonoth's own tradition and Lond's sparse historical documentation treat the relationship as established — the question of when it began is not one that either side has found answerable from any record currently available. The Vornheld tradition in Endevor mentions Nonoth in the context of the Eld-Gold period: the mine-kings' empire required Nonoth's river territory as a grain supplement to the more distant Lond plateau, and the relationship at that time was tributary in the general sense of the empire's structure rather than the specific Stonefist-vassalage it became afterward.\n\nThe transition from mine-king tributary to Stonefist vassal appears to have happened during or after the raiding period, when the Endevor empire's structure had collapsed and the north was reorganizing around the political fact of Stonefist rather than the economic fact of Endevor's gold. The Nonoth's specific alignment with Stonefist — rather than with any individual power competing for the fortress — may have developed from the observation that Stonefist itself was more durable than any particular holder of it, and that a relationship with the position was more stable than a relationship with any person.\n\nThis insight, if it was articulated consciously at some founding moment, has been maintained through many subsequent transitions. The Nonoth position is not: we support this ruler. It is: we support the authority that Stonefist represents. The current ruler exercises that authority. When the current ruler is replaced, the authority remains, and our support continues.\n\nThe three occasions on which Nonoth withheld recognition are the most discussed episodes in their political history. The first was during a contested succession in which two claimants held Stonefist simultaneously for a period — Nonoth declined to recognize either until one had definitively displaced the other, and the resulting months of formal neutrality were later described by Nonoth community records as \"the time the north was uncertain.\" The second was when a holder took Stonefist through methods that the Nonoth council assessed as incompatible with the obligations of the office — specifically, the destruction of a Lond community that had resisted transition rather than accommodation. The third was more recent and is not yet fully in historical perspective.\n\n---\n\n## Economy\n\nNonoth produces grain and fish in quantities that sustain itself and a modest surplus for trade. The bottomland agriculture is the foundation; the river fisheries are the supplement; the two together have supported the region's population through the full range of northern weather without recorded famine.\n\nThe surplus goes primarily to Stonefist's garrison as the tributary portion of the vassalage arrangement, and secondarily to the trade routes that run through the Velond corridor toward the Endevor margins and the Bay of Lol approaches. The Nonoth are not commercial traders in the sense that Ganun is commercial; they do not have a trade-house culture or a market to match Ondrath. They have the river, the grain, and the established relationships that the vassalage network provides. What they need from outside — iron, manufactured goods, the occasional specialized product from the wider northern trade system — they acquire through those relationships rather than through independent commerce.\n\nThe Velond carries more commercial traffic than the Vornond for the reasons noted: navigability, gradient, connection to the Bay of Lol watershed. The Velond-side communities are correspondingly more commercially engaged, with better access to goods moving between Endevor and the broader northern network. The Vornond-side communities are more self-sufficient, more agricultural, and slightly more isolated. The two sides of Nonoth have a gentle cultural divergence that the wider outside world does not usually notice and that the Nonoth themselves articulate as \"the Velond way\" and \"the Vornond way\" without implying that one is superior to the other. The Velond way is more connected. The Vornond way is more settled. Both are loyal.\n\n---\n\n## Language\n\nThe Nonoth dialect is Northern Mittoli with a moderate substrate layer and no particular distinguishing features that have attracted Academy attention. It falls between the conservative Endevor variety and the Lond standard, closer to Lond on most phonological measures but with a handful of vocabulary items that appear to derive from an older local layer. The name \"Nonoth\" itself is among these items: it does not reconstruct from Northern Mittoli, does not trace to Proto-Western, and is treated in the Academy's notes as a substrate toponym of unknown derivation — the region had a name before Mittoli speakers arrived, and the name survived while the language that produced it did not.\n\nThe word that distinguishes Nonoth speech to neighboring ears is the specific term they use for the loyalty relationship with Stonefist. Northern Mittoli has standard vocabulary for political subordination and for alliance. The Nonoth use neither when describing their relationship to Stonefist's authority. The word they use is a compound that translates most directly as something between \"maintained recognition\" and \"the thing that holds its shape\" — implying not passive submission but active structural maintenance, the way a wall holds its shape because each stone has been placed with intention and remains because the placement continues to be correct. The Academy notes this usage. It does not note an equivalent in any other northern dialect.",
      "loreRef": "nonoth",
      "climate": "Dwc"
    },
    "South Orsa": {
      "name": "South Orsa",
      "color": "#e67e22",
      "lore": "# Orsa\n\nThe mineral wealth of Orsa's coastal hills is not a secret. It has been known, in the south, for as long as the south has known that Orsa exists. The silver-bearing rock that breaks through the surface along the Gorgi's western slopes and the dark heavy-metal ore in the coastal cliffs near the western headlands have been described in Academy records, noted in Bay of Lol merchant logs, assessed by several expeditions that returned with samples and estimates and plans for extraction at scale.\n\nNone of those plans have been realized. The country where they would be realized has not changed. The plans have changed.\n\nThe mineral wealth is not defended by armies. Orsa does not have armies in any sense that the rest of the continent would recognize as military organization. The wealth is defended by the climate, and the climate has not lost.\n\n---\n\n## Geography\n\nOrsa occupies the western portion of the continent's northernmost coast — the stretch from the Gorgi Mountains' western terminus to the point where the coast turns southward toward the Bay of Lol's distant northern approaches. It is west of Witherst and north of Nonoth, and it is larger than either, measured in coastline and inland extent. It is not larger in population.\n\nThe terrain is what results when the geological processes that built the Gorgi range have had a very long time to express themselves at the surface without being covered over. The Gorgi's western end is not a clean descending face in the way the eastern and central sections are; it breaks apart into a series of spurs and ridges and isolated massifs that extend toward the coast, leaving between them long, narrow valleys that run roughly north from the mountain core to the sea. The valleys are the only habitable corridors — the ground between the ridges is largely exposed rock, scree, and the persistent ice fields that the western coastal weather deposits and never fully removes. The valleys themselves are narrow and cold but shelter enough from the worst of the lateral weather to support the sparse human presence that has organized itself around them for as long as anyone has been here.\n\nThe coast is rocky and exposed in a manner distinct from Witherst's fjord-cut inlets. Where Witherst's coastline is incised — deep narrow openings that provide shelter proportional to their length — Orsa's coast is broad and irregular, with headlands rather than inlets, and the shelter available between headlands is real but not the complete wind-break that an inlet provides. The bays between Orsa's headlands are workable anchorage in calm conditions and dangerous in bad ones, and the transition between the two can happen in the space of a watch.\n\nThe western sea is the explanation for this. Witherst faces north and northeast, and the storms that reach it have crossed the northern latitudes, which are cold but not wild in the same way. Orsa faces west and northwest, into the full fetch of the ocean weather that has built across open water from wherever the continent's western margin eventually gives way to sea. The storms that reach Orsa are not the Witherst wind, which is persistent and significant. They are events — weather that arrives with enough force to move stone, that deposits ice on the exposed ridges even in summer, and that can trap a ship against the coast without enough warning to work clear.\n\nThe Gorgi passes west of center are the hardest of all the Gorgi crossings. The eastern and central passes, which lead to Witherst, are difficult and seasonal. The western passes, which lead to Orsa, are more difficult and have a shorter reliable window — perhaps four to six weeks in the best summer, nothing reliable in a poor one. They are used, but the Academy's notes on the Orsa-approach routes are among the most heavily annotated in the northern files: every account of a crossing includes conditions that at least one subsequent reader has marked as unrepeatable.\n\n---\n\n## The Minerals\n\nThe silver is the most visible. On the Gorgi's western slopes and in the valley walls where water has cut through to the underlying rock, the silver-bearing veins break through as dark metalite lines in the lighter host stone that catch the flat northern light and flash it back differently. The exposure is extensive — not concentrated in one or two workings but distributed through a long section of the foothills that the Academy's descriptive maps cover with a notation that one scholar's handwriting has made read as both \"substantial\" and \"frustrating\" depending on how it is read.\n\nThe dark ore — found in the exposed coastal cliffs and the lower valley floors where the geological formations that produced the silver continue outward toward the sea — is heavy and dull-surfaced and contains lead and a secondary metal whose precise composition has not been established by any outside analyst who has had both the sample and the equipment at the same time. The lead content alone would make it commercially significant if it could be extracted and moved in quantity. That it appears to contain a secondary valuable component, imperfectly identified in several Bay of Lol merchant reports, has kept the interest of southern commercial parties alive across generations of failed extraction attempts.\n\nThere is also iron, of the cold, dense variety that northern surface rock produces — not the Orched Hills' ore-body iron, which is workable and consistent, but a surface iron from frost-fractured rock that is harder than standard smelting produces and is worked, by the small population here, into tools and implements with properties that outside metalworkers have noted with interest when specimens have made the journey south. The Orsa people's iron-working practice is small in scale and uses techniques specific to the materials they have. The products are not exported in quantity. The ones that have reached southern hands are treated as curiosities by most and as extremely useful by the few metalworkers who have tried to understand what was done to produce them.\n\nThe reason none of this wealth has been extracted at scale is not that it is inaccessible to an outsider's eye or that the Orsa people guard it. The rock is exposed. The veins are visible. The ore is present in the valley walls as clearly as in any workable deposit. The reason is that extraction at scale requires a workforce, a workforce requires housing and feeding and warming through the winter, and winter in Orsa is the problem that every extraction plan has failed to solve.\n\n---\n\n## The Climate\n\nOrsa's climate is not colder than Witherst by the measure of lowest temperature recorded in any winter. The absolute cold of the northern interior, when it descends, descends equally across the northernmost coast. What is different about Orsa is the combination: cold combined with the western weather, cold combined with unreliable sea ice that may or may not freeze a harbor before ships can leave, cold combined with the specific wind behavior where the Gorgi's western spurs funnel the mountain's cold air down the valley corridors and out across the coastal approaches.\n\nSea ice in Orsa's waters is not the predictable seasonal phenomenon it is in parts of Smunders Bay or the Witherst eastern approaches. It forms irregularly, early in bad years, and in the worst winters closes the western coast completely in a way that cannot be predicted from the autumn conditions. A ship that arrives in Orsa's bays in mid-summer on good weather and stays through three weeks of productive extraction may leave or may find, on the morning of its planned departure, that the approach has iced over and will not clear for another month, or two, or the entire winter.\n\nThis is not a metaphor. This has happened. The Bay of Lol merchant records document at least four instances in which extraction-or-trading expeditions to Orsa were trapped by early ice, with the varying outcomes that isolation through an Orsa winter produces: loss of ship, loss of crew, partial survival with the assistance of the local population, and on one occasion full survival with the assistance of the local population at a cost that the merchant family in question found, on reviewing it later, difficult to describe to their trading partners as anything other than \"everything we had and some of what we owed.\"\n\nThe local population survives Orsa's winters because they are adapted to doing so in ways that cannot be transferred to an unprepared outsider in a single season. The knowledge is not primarily technical, though the specific practices — where to shelter from which wind configuration, how to read the ice behavior on the specific coastal sections, what food stores and what sources are available in what combination through the winter — are technical in their details. The knowledge is also physical: what the body can sustain in these conditions with this preparation, and what it cannot. An outside workforce that has not grown up in Orsa does not have this calibration. They learn it, when forced to, through the winter's instruction. The winter's instruction has a significant dropout rate.\n\n---\n\n## The People\n\nThe population of Orsa is the smallest of any settled region on the continent. Not small in the way that Witherst is small — Witherst has communities of dozens, clustered at their inlet sites, with a social infrastructure that functions as a community. Orsa's human presence is more dispersed: bands of families and households moving seasonally through the valley corridors, converging at certain coastal sites in summer when the conditions allow gathering, dispersing again as winter approaches and the communities pull back to their winter sheltering sites.\n\nThere are perhaps a dozen winter sheltering sites in Orsa — locations where the terrain provides enough protection from the valley-wind, where the stored resources accumulated through summer and autumn are adequate to see a group through the worst months, and where the knowledge of how to manage that specific shelter in those specific conditions has been accumulated across multiple generations of the families who use it. The sites are not villages in the sense of fixed permanent settlement. They are the known places where survival is possible, maintained and returned to because they have worked before and because that record is the most reliable information available about survival here.\n\nThe total population that these sheltering sites can sustain is not large. Estimates based on the summer gathering accounts from Bay of Lol merchant logs — the outsiders most likely to have seen a significant proportion of the Orsa people in one place — run between several hundred and perhaps a thousand. The Academy has not produced a more precise estimate because the Academy has not deployed anyone to count.\n\nThe Orsa people know their terrain with the completeness of people whose lives are organized around terrain-knowledge as a primary competency. Every valley, every ridge, every section of coast that changes its behavior between seasons, every approach to every winter shelter, every location where the ice forms first and clears last — this knowledge is the community's most important collective possession, and it is maintained through practice and transmission the way any survival-critical skill is maintained: with the specific attention given to things that, if forgotten, produce consequences.\n\nThey are not hostile to outsiders by temperament, but they are categorical about the conditions under which outsiders can be usefully engaged with. A Bay of Lol ship that arrives in the right season, anchors in a bay they know, and announces itself through the signals that the summer contact tradition has established over generations will receive trade interaction. A Bay of Lol ship that arrives at the wrong time, anchors in the wrong bay, or attempts to move inland without a guide will receive, in order: indifference, observation from a distance, and then whatever the climate produces, which the Orsa people will not interrupt.\n\n---\n\n## External Attempts\n\nThe Bay of Lol maritime communities are the outside party with the most contact, the most investment, and the most consistent record of learning the same lesson about what is and is not possible in Orsa. The western coast is reachable from the Bay of Lol by ships that know the northern approach — not easy, not safe in the wrong season, but achievable in the summer window in a way that the Gorgi overland crossing is not. The Bay of Lol has been making this approach, in varying organizational forms, for as long as its records go back.\n\nThe forms have included: individual merchant ventures, merchant partnerships organized specifically for Orsa extraction, a sustained two-season operation backed by a consortium of Bay of Lol trading families that represents the largest single investment ever made in northern mineral access, and several quasi-military expeditions in the raiding period that found the country militarily unresisting and climatically unsurvivable and returned with that assessment.\n\nThe consortium effort is the most documented. It ran for two full years, brought more workers and more equipment than any previous attempt, and built permanent extraction structures at two valley sites and one coastal cliff location. In the first summer it extracted silver ore in quantities that, had they reached the Bay of Lol, would have justified the investment. The winter killed most of the workforce. The second summer's skeleton crew, augmented by new arrivals, continued with reduced capacity. The second winter finished what the first had started. The structures still exist; the Academy's notes on the Orsa expedition records describe them as \"presently occupied by the local population for storage purposes, which may be their most functional application.\"\n\nThe southern powers of the Mittoli city-state network have assessed Orsa at intervals as a potential mineral source. Their assessments consistently arrive at the same conclusion as the Bay of Lol's practice: the mineral wealth is real, the extraction cost in lives and resources exceeds the value of what could be extracted, and the climate's resistance is not a military problem that force can solve. Some problems cannot be solved by force. Orsa is a demonstration of this that the continent has been running for as long as the continent has been aware of the place.\n\n---\n\n## The Summer Contact\n\nIn the six to eight weeks when the western coast is reliably accessible by ship, Orsa is not entirely closed. Bay of Lol vessels make the approach, anchor in the recognized bays, and conduct the trade that both sides have found worth conducting. The Orsa bring to these encounters: silver ore in hand-selected quantities — not the bulk extraction that extraction plans dream of, but the careful surface and near-surface gathering that the small population manages through the year; samples and small amounts of the worked iron; skins and oil from the coastal animals; and information about conditions for the season, which Bay of Lol captains buy before deciding whether to go further along the coast or turn back.\n\nThe Bay of Lol brings: iron in worked form, grain in preserved quantities, tools and implements that the Orsa cannot produce locally, and the maintained relationship that makes the summer contact possible at all. The relationship is not equal in commercial terms — the Orsa are trading luxury-category goods in small quantities for basic necessities — but it is sustained by mutual need and by the specific knowledge, on the Bay of Lol side, that the Orsa's familiarity with the western coast is the only thing standing between an ambitious extraction attempt and its inevitable result.\n\nThe Bay of Lol captains who make the Orsa run are a specific subset of the coastal fleet: experienced, conservative about weather, unwilling to stay past the established departure window, and in possession of accumulated knowledge about the Orsa coast that is not written in any public record because the captains who know it have not found it advantageous to share. This knowledge — the specific anchorages, the ice behavior at specific locations, the signals that the Orsa use at the recognized trading bays — is the intellectual property of the families and vessels that have maintained the route. It is inherited, trained, and occasionally sold to parties who subsequently discover whether the price was fair.\n\n---\n\n## Language\n\nThe Orsa speech is not a single variety. The population is too sparse and too seasonally dispersed for a single uniform dialect to maintain itself; the different valley communities and family bands have drifted from a shared base across the generations of their relative isolation from each other, producing something that the Academy describes as a language cluster rather than a language — varieties that are mutually comprehensible to their speakers, who encounter each other at summer gatherings often enough to maintain a common core, but that differ in enough vocabulary and pronunciation to be distinguishable to a careful listener.\n\nThe shared core is very old and very different from Northern Mittoli. The substrate layer in Orsa speech is deeper than in Witherst, deeper than in the Ganoss upland dialects, deeper than anything the Academy has documented elsewhere on the continent. The Pro-Western reconstruction provides essentially nothing useful for Orsa's core vocabulary. The words for rock formations, weather conditions, and seasonal phenomena — the words that any community in this terrain would need first — do not trace to any language family the Academy has identified. The working hypothesis, marked explicitly as a hypothesis, is that the Gorgi Mountains isolated a pre-Northern population long enough for their language to develop independently of the Mittoli expansion that eventually reached their neighbors to the south and east, and that this pre-Northern language is what Orsa predominantly speaks in everything that matters.\n\nThe Northern Mittoli overlay exists in the trade vocabulary and in the words for things that came from the south: the names of southern goods, the terms for the Bay of Lol trading practices, the phrases that allow a summer contact to function between parties who do not otherwise share a language base. This layer is thin and sits visibly on top of the older material. The Orsa do not appear to find this stratification notable. The old words are for what was always here. The new words are for what arrived. The distribution seems correct to them.",
      "loreRef": "orsa",
      "climate": "Dwc"
    },
