      "lore": "# Ganun\n\nEvery region of Northern Azhora produces something and needs to move it. Lond produces grain. Endevor produces wool. The Ganoss clans produce livestock. The Bay of Lol produces fish and ships. What Ganun produces, more than anything it grows or raises, is access: the fact that it sits at the head of the Sond, and the Sond flows southeast, and southeast in the Azhoran north means toward the Orched Hills and the Mittoli river network and the continent's larger commercial world.\n\nThe north has always had surplus. What it has lacked, for most of its history, is a way to move that surplus somewhere that will pay for it. The coast road east of the Acorwood is the overland route, and it is slow, narrow, and subject to everything a road in the north is subject to: mud, ice, contested toll points, and the limitation that a wagon moves at a fraction of a boat's pace. The Bay of Lol communities provide ocean access, but ocean access requires going west before going anywhere, and the open northern sea is only reliable in the sailing seasons. The Sond provides something different: a river going south, navigable for much of the year, with enough volume to carry loaded boats and enough course to reach the transitional country where the northern trade network and the Mittoli trade network finally touch.\n\nGanun's people did not decide to become traders. They became traders because the river was there.\n\n---\n\n## Geography\n\nGanun occupies the descending ground east and southeast of the Lond plateau — the long slope where the highland gives way to lower river country before the Smunders Bay coast and the northeastern Orched Hills approaches. It is lower than Lond and wetter, receiving the drainage from the plateau's eastern face and adding its own rainfall to produce a landscape of broad river valleys, water meadows, and the occasional marshy stretch where a tributary has slowed and spread.\n\nThe land is fertile in a different register from Lond. The Lond plateau's fertility comes from accumulated highland peat and compressed organic matter — the dark, stable soil of elevated ground. Ganun's fertility comes from the Sond's floodplain, which the river has been depositing across for as long as it has run. The bottomland soil along the Sond is deep, dark, and annually refreshed by the spring flood that the Oremindi snowmelt and the plateau runoff together produce each year. It is productive for a longer growing season than Lond manages because the lower elevation moderates the frosts; the Sond valley floors stay workable for perhaps two to three weeks longer than the plateau, which in northern agriculture is the difference between one crop and one and a half.\n\nAway from the river valleys, Ganun's upland ground is rougher and less productive — heath and scrub, suitable for sheep and cattle but not grain, gradually thickening into the transitional woodland that marks the approach toward the northeastern Orched Hills. The communities that work this upland are fewer and quieter than the river communities and have a different character: less commercially oriented, more self-sufficient, more like Endevor's pastoral valleys than like the Sond's trade settlements. The Ganun that matters to the rest of the continent is the Sond corridor and its associated communities; the upland Ganun is there and is largely indifferent to the fact that nobody talks about it.\n\nThe northeastern coast — the Smunders Bay margin that forms Ganun's eastern edge — is low and irregular, subject to pack ice in hard winters and exposed to the grey, unsheltered fetch of the bay. It is not a pleasant coast for small craft. The bay ports that exist here are serviceable in fair conditions and dangerous in storms, and the Ganun coastal communities have organized their sailing accordingly: they move cargo in windows, they know the coast's behavior with the intimacy of people who have watched it go wrong, and they do not attempt the bay in conditions that the Bay of Lol's ocean sailors would consider merely challenging.\n\n---\n\n## The Sond\n\nThe Sond begins in the upland ground east of the Lond plateau and runs southeast through Ganun's river country before descending through the northeastern Orched Hills margin and, eventually, joining the Mittoli river network that reaches the continent's interior. It is not a short river. From its navigable head — approximately two days above Ondrath, where the channel widens and deepens enough for a loaded boat — to the point where it effectively becomes a southern river rather than a northern one is perhaps eight days of travel, longer in low water. The full course from Ganun to the point where a Mittoli merchant would recognize the surrounding country as familiar takes longer still.\n\nFor the northern portion of that course — the Ganun section and the upper Orched Hills section — the Sond is the most active trade artery in Northern Azhora. The qualification is specific and accurate: not the most traveled route (the coast road carries more individual travelers), not the most profitable in terms of value per unit (certain Bay of Lol ocean trade carries goods of greater per-weight value), but the most active by volume of goods in motion. Everything the north produces that is heavy, bulky, and priced for volume moves on the Sond if it can reach the river. Grain from Lond. Wool from Endevor, baled and cartable to the Sond's head. Iron and copper from the Orched Hills, moving upriver as often as down. Northern animal products — salt meat, dried fish, rendered fat, tallow — meeting the Mittoli luxury and manufactured goods moving in the other direction.\n\nThe river is not without difficulty. The Sond has three sections with distinct characters and distinct risks. The upper reach, through Ganun proper, is wide and reliable in spring and early summer, low and occasionally awkward by late summer, and blocked by ice for a variable portion of winter. Experienced Sond boat operators know the seasonal rhythms with a granularity that does not appear in any written guide — when the first ice forms in the upper bends, when the spring flood crests and when it settles, which shallows can be poled through in low water and which cannot. This knowledge is held in the communities and transmitted the way practical knowledge is always transmitted: in practice, slowly, over years. A boat operator who does not have it makes mistakes on the Sond. The mistakes on the upper reach are recoverable. Further south they are less so.\n\nThe middle reach, through the transition country, narrows and quickens. There are two significant rapids here — both passable with skill, both requiring unloading and portage at high water or during certain ice conditions. The portage points have become small settlements in their own right, sustaining themselves on portage labor and the brief commercial activity that accompanies any cargo transfer. They are rough communities with specific competencies and no interest in being anywhere other than where they are.\n\nThe lower reach, from the Orched Hills margin southward, is increasingly the Mittoli's river rather than the Sond — the character changes as the gradient eases and the surrounding country shifts from the northern transition zone into the Orched Hills proper. This is where northern merchants typically turn around and Mittoli merchants typically take over the cargo. The handoff point moves depending on who has the political leverage in any given period, but it has generally stabilized somewhere in the middle Orched Hills in the current era.\n\n**Ondrath** is the Sond's main settlement and the effective capital of Ganun's commercial life. It sits at the point where two significant tributaries join the Sond and the combined channel becomes reliably navigable for loaded boats in most conditions. This is not a dramatic location — it is a flat river bend with good landing ground on both banks and slightly elevated dry ground whe