occasional unexpected weather. The Ganun coastal sailors are not offshore navigators. They are intimately familiar with the Smunders Bay coastline, its currents, its tidal patterns, the specific seasonal behavior of the pack ice that forms in the bay's shallower western end, the headlands where weather can change faster than an inexperienced pilot anticipates. This knowledge makes them effective and reasonably safe on the bay. It does not make them ocean sailors.\n\nThe comparison to the Bay of Lol communities is the one Ganun sailors find least interesting to have explained to them. They know the Bay of Lol builds better ocean-going vessels and produces sailors who will take those vessels across the open northern sea in conditions that a Ganun boat operator assesses as poorly considered. They also know that Bay of Lol sailors who take a loaded boat through the Sond's middle rapids in high water generally do it once, learn something, and develop opinions about what Ganun sailors know that they did not previously hold. The skills are different. Both communities are aware of this.\n\nBy the standards of Southern Azhoran maritime cultures — the ocean-going traders of the continent's southern coasts and the long-distance navigators who move cargo between continents — Ganun's seafaring is competent and unremarkable. Southern merchants who come north via the Sond, which happens with modest regularity in the current era, regard the Ganun boatmen as reliable in their own water and limited outside it: the right tool for a specific job, correctly priced. The Ganun do not find this assessment offensive. They have been assessed by southerners for long enough that they have made peace with it.\n\n---\n\n## The People\n\nGanun communities are more outward-facing than any other interior northern culture, and the orientation is old enough to have shaped the character rather than just the economy. A Lond farmer's knowledge is hyper-local — the specific frost patterns of specific fie