    "West Witherst": {
      "name": "West Witherst",
      "color": "#d4ac0d",
      "lore": "# Witherst\n\nThe wind at Witherst is not like wind elsewhere. Wind elsewhere is weather — it arrives, it does something, it goes. The wind at Witherst is geography. It has come off open sea for more distance than can be counted without being slowed by anything, and it arrives at the White Hills coast with the full unspent weight of that passage. It does not stop. It varies in force and it varies in direction, shifting between the quadrants with the seasons, but the absence of it is not a condition of Witherst's coast. Shelter from the wind in Witherst means shelter relative to other positions on the same coast. It means something was blocking more of it than usual.\n\nThe people who live here know the difference between degrees of wind the way the Endevor fell-sheep shepherds know the difference between degrees of smoke. There is vocabulary for it that does not appear in any other Northern Mittoli variety — fourteen or more terms for wind quality, direction, and behavior that Witherst communities use with the precision of a farmer discussing soil. Outsiders who cross the Gorgi Mountains and reach the White Hills coast for the first time hear the wind and have no terms for what they are hearing. The Witherst have been listening to it since before they could hold a net.\n\n---\n\n## Geography\n\nThe Gorgi Mountains stand between Witherst and the rest of the continent as a wall stands between rooms: not an abstraction, not a gradual transition, but a definite surface that you cross or do not cross. They are not the tallest range on Azhora — the Oremindi exceeds them — but they are hard in the specific way of a compact range built from old resistant stone that weathers vertically rather than rounding with age. The southern approach from the Ganoss uplands is a long climb over increasingly bare and cold ground. The northern descent to the White Hills coast is shorter, steeper, and in winter conditions functionally impassable for most of the Gorgi's face.\n\nThe passes through the Gorgi are three in number and reliable for perhaps eight to ten weeks each year at the height of summer, when the snowpack retreats from the lower crests and the northern descent becomes a matter of difficulty rather than death. The rest of the year the passes are closed, not by decree but by snow and the specific wind behavior at the Gorgi crests where the northern and southern airflows meet and produce conditions that experienced Ganoss herders describe as \"the mountains arguing.\" Travelers who attempt the Gorgi outside the reliable window are recorded in the northern files at a rate that has made the Academy annotate its maps of the range with the specific dates of the summer window based on averaging multiple generations of reports.\n\nSouth of the Gorgi, the Ganoss uplands extend down to the interior of Northern Azhora — the pastoral plateau of the clans, cold and exposed but below the mountain threshold. North of the Gorgi, the land descends quickly to the coastal plain that is Witherst proper, and then to the coast, and then to the sea.\n\nThe White Hills that give the region its common name are the low coastal ridges that back the settlement zones — a series of rounded, irregular summits between fifty and two hundred feet, composed of pale quartzite and coastal limestone that weathers to white and cream and the specific mottled grey-white of rock that has been exposed to this particular combination of salt air and cold for a very long time. In winter they are snow-covered and invisible in the landscape by color. In summer, when the snowpack retreats to the highest points, the exposed rock face is what visitors consistently describe first: white hills, literally, the color of the stone distinguishing them from the darker uplands to the south. Orange and yellow lichen grows in dense patches on the south-facing faces where the summer sun reaches, and in the low summer light this produces a specific color combination — white rock, vivid lichen, grey sea — that Witherst communities use as a descriptive shorthand for the coast itself when they have reason to describe it to outsiders.\n\nThe coastline is fjord-cut and irregular, deeply indented in several places by inlets that reach inland far enough to provide shelter from the open-sea weather. The community sites cluster at these inlets — each one a natural harbor that reduces the wind fetch by enough to matter, each one the reason a community exists at that specific location rather than along the open stretches between them. The open coast between inlet communities is not uninhabited; there are seasonal stations, fishing camps, waypoints for the small-boat routes that run along the coast in summer. But permanent settlement requires the shelter that the inlets provide, and the number of adequate inlets has set a practical limit on the number of Witherst communities for as long as the region has been inhabited.\n\n---\n\n## The Northern Sea\n\nThe sea north of Witherst is the defining economic fact of the region. In its cold water column, fed by the deep currents that run from the further north and circulate through the colder latitudes before reaching this coast, lives the biological foundation that Witherst has sustained itself on since the communities here were first established.\n\nCold water is productive water. The nutrient upwelling that cold coastal seas produce supports fish populations of a density that warmer southern fisheries do not match, and the Witherst communities have been taking from those populations since before their records begin. The specific fish they take — the cold-water species that do not range significantly south — are the diet foundation of every Witherst household and the primary trade good that makes the annual Gorgi crossing worth someone's effort in summer.\n\nBeyond fish: the northern sea carries marine mammals in the same cold-water productive zones. The large sea-animals that haul out on the White Hills coast's more remote beaches and rocky platforms — heavy-bodied, blunt-featured, taken for their oil, their hides, and the ivory of their tusks — are the source of Witherst's most distinctive trade goods. Sea ivory reaches the southern markets as an exotic luxury; it is not available anywhere else on the Azhoran continent in comparable quantity, and its specific working properties — denser and more stable than land-animal ivory, taking fine carving with a quality that Mittoli craftsmen have found valuable — have made it a consistent demand item in the Academy's trade records going back several generations of documentation. The carving tradition that Witherst communities have developed around this material produces objects that serve functional purposes locally and arrive in the south as decorative objects whose origin their new owners frequently do not know.\n\nThe amber is the third sea product and the most traveled. It comes ashore at the White Hills beaches in quantities that vary by year and by storm — most abundant after the large northern storms of late autumn, which churn the sea floor deposits that the ancient submerged forests left behind. A good amber season produces pieces that range in size from fingertip to fist, in color from deep brown-red to the pale yellow that southern buyers prize for its clarity. Witherst communities collect it from the beachline systematically after storms, sort it, and hold it for the summer Gorgi trade. A single season's amber collected from the right beach can justify the entire expense of a southern trading expedition. It is the luxury element of the Witherst economy — the thing that the south wants from the very far north that the very far north can provide and nowhere else can.\n\nThe whale is not a trade good in the same sense; it is a community event. The large whales that pass the White Hills coast in their seasonal migrations are taken rarely, with effort that requires the whole active community, and when they are taken the resource they represent — oil for the winter lamps, bone for structural and craft purposes, meat in quantities that the community processes and preserves across several weeks — changes the economic condition of the winter significantly. A whale year is a different winter from a non-whale year. The vocabulary for it is distinct: the community plans around the difference, the resource calculation changes, the winter food security moves into a different category. Witherst children grow up understanding the difference before they understand much else about economics.\n\n---\n\n## The People\n\nThe traveler who crosses the Gorgi Mountains in the narrow summer window, descends to the White Hills coast, and finds a Witherst community has already accomplished something that establishes their seriousness. The Witherst know this. The response to a crossing-traveler is correspondingly immediate and unconditional: come inside, eat, warm up, sleep if you need to.\n\nThe hospitality of the White Hills communities is the thing that most surprises visitors from other northern regions, who arrive prepared for the northern pattern of reserve and find the opposite. The explanation is not sentiment. In a place this cold, with passes that close for most of the year and communities separated by stretches of wind-exposed coast, refusing shelter to a stranger in the wrong conditions is killing them. The Witherst communities developed their hospitality ethic out of the same environmental logic that shaped everything else about them: the conditions require it. What began as practical necessity has become the clearest identity marker they hold. A Witherst community that turned away a traveler would be doing something that the community itself would find incoherent. The wind is what it is. You come inside.\n\nThis does not extend infinitely. The hospitality is for arrivals, not for residents who have worn out their welcome, and the expectations of reciprocity once a visitor has recovered — contributing labor, sharing information from the south, returning in future seasons with the goods that the crossing justifies — are present and understood by both sides. The gift is the first night and the first meal, offered without condition. What follows is negotiated in the normal way of communities that have limited resources and specific needs.\n\nThe communities are small by the standards of the more southerly north — a typical White Hills inlet settlement numbers in the dozens of households, not hundreds. The scarcity of adequate inlet sites and the difficulty of feeding a large population from the coastal economy set natural limits. The social organization is correspondingly direct: governance is by council of household heads, disputes are resolved publicly, and the community's collective needs override individual property claims in the specific circumstances — communal whale processing, storm preparation, emergency food sharing — where the alternative is worse than the override. These are not theoretical circumstances. They occur with the frequency that a demanding environment produces.\n\nThe Witherst are not visibly hierarchical to outside observers. Families accrue respect from their record — good fishing, successful whale expeditions, reliable weather knowledge — and that respect is real and present in how the community makes decisions. But it does not accumulate into property or title in the way that commercial hierarchy does in Ganun or military-political hierarchy does in the inland communities. What you have is your boat, your household's winter stores, and your standing in the community's estimation. The standing is not inheritable; each generation rebuilds it.\n\n---\n\n## Trade and the Gorgi Crossing\n\nFor approximately eight weeks each summer, the Gorgi passes are reliable. In this window, trade moves in both directions: Ganoss herders who graze the southern Gorgi slopes in summer will bring the exchange goods from further south up to the pass mouths; Witherst traders who have been preparing goods through the winter and spring bring their own north to the same points. The transaction happens at the pass mouths on both sides, rarely at lower elevations — the effort of going further discourages it, and the pass-mouth locations have become established exchange sites that both sides return to without needing to renegotiate the location.\n\nWhat goes south: salt fish in large quantities; sea ivory and carved ivory objects; amber, sorted by quality; furs from the extreme-cold-adapted animals of the northern coast and the Gorgi uplands; rendered sea-mammal oil in sealed containers, used for lamps in communities where winter darkness is a practical problem rather than a metaphor; and occasional live animals — small, cold-adapted, occasionally kept as curiosities by southern households with money and insufficient judgment about what northern animals require.\n\nWhat comes north: iron in worked form (Witherst has no ore of its own and limited capacity to process raw metal in any case); grain and root vegetables in the preserved forms that travel the Gorgi crossing without spoiling; salt from the southern production communities; timber in modest quantities for the specific uses that the White Hills coast's own sparse treeline cannot supply; and, increasingly in the current era, manufactured goods from the southern trade network that have reached as far north as the Ganoss trading seasons.\n\nThe amber trade is the one that drives outside interest in Witherst most consistently. Mittoli merchants in the current era have begun making the full journey north themselves — crossing the Orched Hills, crossing the Ganoss territory with appropriate arrangement, crossing the Gorgi in the summer window — to establish direct relationships with the White Hills communities rather than buying amber through the several-step intermediate trade. The Witherst response to this is pragmatic: if a southerner crosses the Gorgi, they receive the same hospitality as anyone else and the same terms in the amber negotiation, which are the terms the community sets rather than the terms the southerner brings. The journey itself prices out the unreasonable buyer.\n\n---\n\n## History\n\nThe White Hills communities appear in no southern record until a period that the Academy dates, by context, to the early middle historical era — well after the first systematic contact between the north and Mittolo. The explanation is simple: the Gorgi Mountains made Witherst functionally inaccessible to anyone who did not already know it existed, and the northern communities on the southern side of the Gorgi had no particular incentive to publicize what lay beyond.\n\nThe first southern account describes the amber, which is how the south first understood Witherst's existence: as the origin of a luxury material that had been arriving in Mittoli markets for longer than anyone had thought to ask where it came from. The question of origin led back through the Ganoss trade network, over the Gorgi, to the White Hills beaches. The subsequent period saw the gradual establishment of the summer pass-mouth trade that now operates, replacing the multi-step intermediate trade that had functioned, invisibly to the south, for generations before anyone south of the Ganoss uplands knew to look for it.\n\nThe raiding period that shaped the more southerly northern regions did not affect Witherst directly. The White Hills communities were too remote to be useful targets — the Gorgi crossing as a raiding obstacle is prohibitive — and not organized as raiders themselves in the way that the Bay of Lol or the Riesov coastal communities were. Their isolation was their protection during a period when the rest of the north organized itself around violence and then around commerce. What the raiding period meant for Witherst was the occasional disruption of the intermediate amber trade, which affected them economically but not militarily.\n\nThe Gorgi Mountain crossings have never been governed by any external authority. The Ganoss clans approach the southern passes in summer as part of their seasonal grazing range and do not consider this territorial in the way that would require Witherst's permission. The Witherst approach the northern passes in summer as part of their trading calendar and do not consider this to require the Ganoss's permission. Both sides are correct: the mountains belong to whoever is currently in them, which in practice means the mountains belong to no one and the passes belong to the summer.\n\n---\n\n## Language\n\nThe Witherst speech is the most diverged variety of Northern Mittoli in the region — diverged enough that the Academy classification question (dialect versus separate language) is more contested for Witherst than for the Ganoss upland speech, and the Ganoss question is already contentious. The core grammar is recognizably Northern Mittoli in its structure to a specialist; the vocabulary is not recognizable to anyone who has not specifically studied it.\n\nThe substrate layer is very deep. A significant proportion of Witherst vocabulary — the wind terminology, the sea-and-weather lexicon, the terms for the specific animals and materials of the extreme north — does not trace to Proto-Western by any reconstruction that Academy linguists have attempted. The leading position is that Witherst was inhabited before the Northern Mittoli expansion reached this far, and that the pre-existing language has survived in the vocabulary of the domains where it was most precisely developed: the sea, the weather, the animals. The grammar shifted; the words for what mattered most did not.\n\nThe result is a spoken language that sounds like Northern Mittoli spoken by someone who has replaced most of the nouns with terms from a different lexicon. Visitors from Ganun or Riesov can parse the sentence structure and cannot follow what is being said. Visitors from the Ganoss upland communities, whose own speech has a different substrate layer of equivalent depth, have slightly more success — the two substrate layers do not appear to be the same language, but they appear to be from the same linguistic zone, which the Academy finds interesting and has not yet studied adequately.\n\nThe Witherst wind vocabulary — the fourteen or more terms, depending on the community — has been partially catalogued by one Academy scholar who spent a summer at the White Hills coast and spent most of it, by his account, being corrected. His catalogue notes that the terms are not interchangeable and that using the wrong one is considered not so much incorrect as simply useless: a community that has fourteen words for wind conditions does not have them because the speaker needed fourteen words. They have them because the conditions are different from each other and the difference matters.",
      "loreRef": "witherst",
      "climate": "Dfc"
    },
    "East Witherst": {
      "name": "East Witherst",
      "color": "#27ae60",
      "lore": "# Witherst\n\nThe wind at Witherst is not like wind elsewhere. Wind elsewhere is weather — it arrives, it does something, it goes. The wind at Witherst is geography. It has come off open sea for more distance than can be counted without being slowed by anything, and it arrives at the White Hills coast with the full unspent weight of that passage. It does not stop. It varies in force and it varies in direction, shifting between the quadrants with the seasons, but the absence of it is not a condition of Witherst's coast. Shelter from the wind in Witherst means shelter relative to other positions on the same coast. It means something was blocking more of it than usual.\n\nThe people who live here know the difference between degrees of wind the way the Endevor fell-sheep shepherds know the difference between degrees of smoke. There is vocabulary for it that does not appear in any other Northern Mittoli variety — fourteen or more terms for wind quality, direction, and behavior that Witherst communities use with the precision of a farmer discussing soil. Outsiders who cross the Gorgi Mountains and reach the White Hills coast for the first time hear the wind and have no terms for what they are hearing. The Witherst have been listening to it since before they could hold a net.\n\n---\n\n## Geography\n\nThe Gorgi Mountains stand between Witherst and the rest of the continent as a wall stands between rooms: not an abstraction, not a gradual transition, but a definite surface that you cross or do not cross. They are not the tallest range on Azhora — the Oremindi exceeds them — but they are hard in the specific way of a compact range built from old resistant stone that weathers vertically rather than rounding with age. The southern approach from the Ganoss uplands is a long climb over increasingly bare and cold ground. The northern descent to the White Hills coast is shorter, steeper, and in winter conditions functionally impassable for most of the Gorgi's face.\n\nThe passes through the Gorgi are three in number and reliable for perhaps eight to ten weeks each year at the height of summer, when the snowpack retreats from the lower crests and the northern descent becomes a matter of difficulty rather than death. The rest of the year the passes are closed, not by decree but by snow and the specific wind behavior at the Gorgi crests where the northern and southern airflows meet and produce conditions that experienced Ganoss herders describe as \"the mountains arguing.\" Travelers who attempt the Gorgi outside the reliable window are recorded in the northern files at a rate that has made the Academy annotate its maps of the range with the specific dates of the summer window based on averaging multiple generations of reports.\n\nSouth of the Gorgi, the Ganoss uplands extend down to the interior of Northern Azhora — the pastoral plateau of the clans, cold and exposed but below the mountain threshold. North of the Gorgi, the land descends quickly to the coastal plain that is Witherst proper, and then to the coast, and then to the sea.\n\nThe White Hills that give the region its common name are the low coastal ridges that back the settlement zones — a series of rounded, irregular summits between fifty and two hundred feet, composed of pale quartzite and coastal limestone that weathers to white and cream and the specific mottled grey-white of rock that has been exposed to this particular combination of salt air and cold for a very long time. In winter they are snow-covered and invisible in the landscape by color. In summer, when the snowpack retreats to the highest points, the exposed rock face is what visitors consistently describe first: white hills, literally, the color of the stone distinguishing them from the darker uplands to the south. Orange and yellow lichen grows in dense patches on the south-facing faces where the summer sun reaches, and in the low summer light this produces a specific color combination — white rock, vivid lichen, grey sea — that Witherst communities use as a descriptive shorthand for the coast itself when they have reason to describe it to outsiders.\n\nThe coastline is fjord-cut and irregular, deeply indented in several places by inlets that reach inland far enough to provide shelter from the open-sea weather. The community sites cluster at these inlets — each one a natural harbor that reduces the wind fetch by enough to matter, each one the reason a community exists at that specific location rather than along the open stretches between them. The open coast between inlet communities is not uninhabited; there are seasonal stations, fishing camps, waypoints for the small-boat routes that run along the coast in summer. But permanent settlement requires the shelter that the inlets provide, and the number of adequate inlets has set a practical limit on the number of Witherst communities for as long as the region has been inhabited.\n\n---\n\n## The Northern Sea\n\nThe sea north of Witherst is the defining economic fact of the region. In its cold water column, fed by the deep currents that run from the further north and circulate through the colder latitudes before reaching this coast, lives the biological foundation that Witherst has sustained itself on since the communities here were first established.\n\nCold water is productive water. The nutrient upwelling that cold coastal seas produce supports fish populations of a density that warmer southern fisheries do not match, and the Witherst communities have been taking from those populations since before their records begin. The specific fish they take — the cold-water species that do not range significantly south — are the diet foundation of every Witherst household and the primary trade good that makes the annual Gorgi crossing worth someone's effort in summer.\n\nBeyond fish: the northern sea carries marine mammals in the same cold-water productive zones. The large sea-animals that haul out on the White Hills coast's more remote beaches and rocky platforms — heavy-bodied, blunt-featured, taken for their oil, their hides, and the ivory of their tusks — are the source of Witherst's most distinctive trade goods. Sea ivory reaches the southern markets as an exotic luxury; it is not available anywhere else on the Azhoran continent in comparable quantity, and its specific working properties — denser and more stable than land-animal ivory, taking fine carving with a quality that Mittoli craftsmen have found valuable — have made it a consistent demand item in the Academy's trade records going back several generations of documentation. The carving tradition that Witherst communities have developed around this material produces objects that serve functional purposes locally and arrive in the south as decorative objects whose origin their new owners frequently do not know.\n\nThe amber is the third sea product and the most traveled. It comes ashore at the White Hills beaches in quantities that vary by year and by storm — most abundant after the large northern storms of late autumn, which churn the sea floor deposits that the ancient submerged forests left behind. A good amber season produces pieces that range in size from fingertip to fist, in color from deep brown-red to the pale yellow that southern buyers prize for its clarity. Witherst communities collect it from the beachline systematically after storms, sort it, and hold it for the summer Gorgi trade. A single season's amber collected from the right beach can justify the entire expense of a southern trading expedition. It is the luxury element of the Witherst economy — the thing that the south wants from the very far north that the very far north can provide and nowhere else can.\n\nThe whale is not a trade good in the same sense; it is a community event. The large whales that pass the White Hills coast in their seasonal migrations are taken rarely, with effort that requires the whole active community, and when they are taken the resource they represent — oil for the winter lamps, bone for structural and craft purposes, meat in quantities that the community processes and preserves across several weeks — changes the economic condition of the winter significantly. A whale year is a different winter from a non-whale year. The vocabulary for it is distinct: the community plans around the difference, the resource calculation changes, the winter food security moves into a different category. Witherst children grow up understanding the difference before they understand much else about economics.\n\n---\n\n## The People\n\nThe traveler who crosses the Gorgi Mountains in the narrow summer window, descends to the White Hills coast, and finds a Witherst community has already accomplished something that establishes their seriousness. The Witherst know this. The response to a crossing-traveler is correspondingly immediate and unconditional: come inside, eat, warm up, sleep if you need to.\n\nThe hospitality of the White Hills communities is the thing that most surprises visitors from other northern regions, who arrive prepared for the northern pattern of reserve and find the opposite. The explanation is not sentiment. In a place this cold, with passes that close for most of the year and communities separated by stretches of wind-exposed coast, refusing shelter to a stranger in the wrong conditions is killing them. The Witherst communities developed their hospitality ethic out of the same environmental logic that shaped everything else about them: the conditions require it. What began as practical necessity has become the clearest identity marker they hold. A Witherst community that turned away a traveler would be doing something that the community itself would find incoherent. The wind is what it is. You come inside.\n\nThis does not extend infinitely. The hospitality is for arrivals, not for residents who have worn out their welcome, and the expectations of reciprocity once a visitor has recovered — contributing labor, sharing information from the south, returning in future seasons with the goods that the crossing justifies — are present and understood by both sides. The gift is the first night and the first meal, offered without condition. What follows is negotiated in the normal way of communities that have limited resources and specific needs.\n\nThe communities are small by the standards of the more southerly north — a typical White Hills inlet settlement numbers in the dozens of households, not hundreds. The scarcity of adequate inlet sites and the difficulty of feeding a large population from the coastal economy set natural limits. The social organization is correspondingly direct: governance is by council of household heads, disputes are resolved publicly, and the community's collective needs override individual property claims in the specific circumstances — communal whale processing, storm preparation, emergency food sharing — where the alternative is worse than the override. These are not theoretical circumstances. They occur with the frequency that a demanding environment produces.\n\nThe Witherst are not visibly hierarchical to outside observers. Families accrue respect from their record — good fishing, successful whale expeditions, reliable weather knowledge — and that respect is real and present in how the community makes decisions. But it does not accumulate into property or title in the way that commercial hierarchy does in Ganun or military-political hierarchy does in the inland communities. What you have is your boat, your household's winter stores, and your standing in the community's estimation. The standing is not inheritable; each generation rebuilds it.\n\n---\n\n## Trade and the Gorgi Crossing\n\nFor approximately eight weeks each summer, the Gorgi passes are reliable. In this window, trade moves in both directions: Ganoss herders who graze the southern Gorgi slopes in summer will bring the exchange goods from further south up to the pass mouths; Witherst traders who have been preparing goods through the winter and spring bring their own north to the same points. The transaction happens at the pass mouths on both sides, rarely at lower elevations — the effort of going further discourages it, and the pass-mouth locations have become established exchange sites that both sides return to without needing to renegotiate the location.\n\nWhat goes south: salt fish in large quantities; sea ivory and carved ivory objects; amber, sorted by quality; furs from the extreme-cold-adapted animals of the northern coast and the Gorgi uplands; rendered sea-mammal oil in sealed containers, used for lamps in communities where winter darkness is a practical problem rather than a metaphor; and occasional live animals — small, cold-adapted, occasionally kept as curiosities by southern households with money and insufficient judgment about what northern animals require.\n\nWhat comes north: iron in worked form (Witherst has no ore of its own and limited capacity to process raw metal in any case); grain and root vegetables in the preserved forms that travel the Gorgi crossing without spoiling; salt from the southern production communities; timber in modest quantities for the specific uses that the White Hills coast's own sparse treeline cannot supply; and, increasingly in the current era, manufactured goods from the southern trade network that have reached as far north as the Ganoss trading seasons.\n\nThe amber trade is the one that drives outside interest in Witherst most consistently. Mittoli merchants in the current era have begun making the full journey north themselves — crossing the Orched Hills, crossing the Ganoss territory with appropriate arrangement, crossing the Gorgi in the summer window — to establish direct relationships with the White Hills communities rather than buying amber through the several-step intermediate trade. The Witherst response to this is pragmatic: if a southerner crosses the Gorgi, they receive the same hospitality as anyone else and the same terms in the amber negotiation, which are the terms the community sets rather than the terms the southerner brings. The journey itself prices out the unreasonable buyer.\n\n---\n\n## History\n\nThe White Hills communities appear in no southern record until a period that the Academy dates, by context, to the early middle historical era — well after the first systematic contact between the north and Mittolo. The explanation is simple: the Gorgi Mountains made Witherst functionally inaccessible to anyone who did not already know it existed, and the northern communities on the southern side of the Gorgi had no particular incentive to publicize what lay beyond.\n\nThe first southern account describes the amber, which is how the south first understood Witherst's existence: as the origin of a luxury material that had been arriving in Mittoli markets for longer than anyone had thought to ask where it came from. The question of origin led back through the Ganoss trade network, over the Gorgi, to the White Hills beaches. The subsequent period saw the gradual establishment of the summer pass-mouth trade that now operates, replacing the multi-step intermediate trade that had functioned, invisibly to the south, for generations before anyone south of the Ganoss uplands knew to look for it.\n\nThe raiding period that shaped the more southerly northern regions did not affect Witherst directly. The White Hills communities were too remote to be useful targets — the Gorgi crossing as a raiding obstacle is prohibitive — and not organized as raiders themselves in the way that the Bay of Lol or the Riesov coastal communities were. Their isolation was their protection during a period when the rest of the north organized itself around violence and then around commerce. What the raiding period meant for Witherst was the occasional disruption of the intermediate amber trade, which affected them economically but not militarily.\n\nThe Gorgi Mountain crossings have never been governed by any external authority. The Ganoss clans approach the southern passes in summer as part of their seasonal grazing range and do not consider this territorial in the way that would require Witherst's permission. The Witherst approach the northern passes in summer as part of their trading calendar and do not consider this to require the Ganoss's permission. Both sides are correct: the mountains belong to whoever is currently in them, which in practice means the mountains belong to no one and the passes belong to the summer.\n\n---\n\n## Language\n\nThe Witherst speech is the most diverged variety of Northern Mittoli in the region — diverged enough that the Academy classification question (dialect versus separate language) is more contested for Witherst than for the Ganoss upland speech, and the Ganoss question is already contentious. The core grammar is recognizably Northern Mittoli in its structure to a specialist; the vocabulary is not recognizable to anyone who has not specifically studied it.\n\nThe substrate layer is very deep. A significant proportion of Witherst vocabulary — the wind terminology, the sea-and-weather lexicon, the terms for the specific animals and materials of the extreme north — does not trace to Proto-Western by any reconstruction that Academy linguists have attempted. The leading position is that Witherst was inhabited before the Northern Mittoli expansion reached this far, and that the pre-existing language has survived in the vocabulary of the domains where it was most precisely developed: the sea, the weather, the animals. The grammar shifted; the words for what mattered most did not.\n\nThe result is a spoken language that sounds like Northern Mittoli spoken by someone who has replaced most of the nouns with terms from a different lexicon. Visitors from Ganun or Riesov can parse the sentence structure and cannot follow what is being said. Visitors from the Ganoss upland communities, whose own speech has a different substrate layer of equivalent depth, have slightly more success — the two substrate layers do not appear to be the same language, but they appear to be from the same linguistic zone, which the Academy finds interesting and has not yet studied adequately.\n\nThe Witherst wind vocabulary — the fourteen or more terms, depending on the community — has been partially catalogued by one Academy scholar who spent a summer at the White Hills coast and spent most of it, by his account, being corrected. His catalogue notes that the terms are not interchangeable and that using the wrong one is considered not so much incorrect as simply useless: a community that has fourteen words for wind conditions does not have them because the speaker needed fourteen words. They have them because the conditions are different from each other and the difference matters.",
