    "Cape Thalmagar": {
      "name": "Cape Thalmagar",
      "color": "#546e7a",
      "lore": "# The Cape of Thalmagar\n\nThe Cape of Thalmagar extends from the northwestern terminus of the Oremindi Mountains into the sea at the point where the western coast of Azhora bends and then stops making sense as a connected landmass. On three sides: water. On the fourth, southeast: the Oremindi, which at their northern end do not decline graciously into the coastal plain but simply arrive at the sea, leaving the cape cut off behind them as a consequence of geology rather than any political arrangement.\n\nThe result is a piece of Azhora that is, for all practical purposes, an island. Ships can reach it. Overland travelers cannot — the northern Oremindi passes are among the most severe on the range, seasonal at best, impassable in the worst years and most years are the worst years. What lies on the other side of the mountains from the rest of the continent sits in its own world.\n\nThe cape is on the southern shore of the bay that separates the main body of Azhora from the northern extension — the same bay whose northern coast holds the Bay of Lol and the ports of northern Azhora. From the north coast of this bay, Thalmagar is visible on clear days as a dark line of cliff and forest: close enough to know is there, far enough that few bother to cross. Northern Azhoran sailors call the bay itself *the Outer Deep*. The water between is cold, rough in most seasons, and offers nothing to repay the crossing except whatever Thalmagar offers, which until recently has not been much that outside traders need.\n\nThe name \"Thalmagar\" is the outside name, carried by the sailors who mapped the cape from the water. What it means has been argued without resolution. The leading proposal is that *thal* is a northern coastal-dialect word for a headland that juts into cold water, and that *magar* is a corruption of a term carried back by the first sailors to make contact with the cape's inhabitants. The people of the cape do not use it. What they call their land in their own language has not been recorded in a form that outside scholars have agreed on how to spell.\n\n---\n\n## Geography\n\nThe cape is not large — several days' walk from its junction with the mountains to its outermost point, perhaps two days across at the widest. But it is varied in the compressed way of landscapes that have been shaped by exposure on every side and connection to nothing.\n\nThe mountain barrier at its back is the defining feature. The northwestern terminus of the Oremindi does not diminish into hills before meeting the sea; the range ends in sea cliffs, a sudden vertical junction that leaves no passable coast between mountain foot and water on the southern side. This means there is no route from the cape to the rest of Azhora that does not cross either the mountains themselves or the bay. Both crossings are serious undertakings. The mountains are not offered at their gentlest at this end of the range; the passes here are the ones the Academy's Oremindi records describe as technically possible and practically avoided. The bay crossing requires vessels and weather that cooperate, neither of which is reliably available.\n\nNorth of the mountain junction, the cape opens into its own geography. The land here is lower than the adjacent mountains but not flat — a series of rounded headlands and sheltered embayments along the northern coast, facing the Outer Deep and the distant northern shore; a wilder, more exposed western coastline that takes the open ocean without interruption; and an interior of moorland and ancient forest that has not been significantly cleared. The rivers are short and fast, draining from the elevated interior into either the bay or the open sea, and they run cold in every season.\n\nThe forests of the interior are not the Ibenwood in character — they are northern forests, dominated by species adapted to cold and exposure — but they have the quality of forests that have been undisturbed long enough that the disturbance-adapted species have ceded back to slower, longer-lived forms. The oldest trees here are large. The understory is dense. The light on the forest floor is the reduced, filtered quality of canopy that closes over most of the available sky.\n\nThe northern coast, more sheltered than the open west, is where the cape's handful of human settlements concentrate. A protected inlet, a freshwater source, a beach that can be safely worked in most winds — these are the requirements, and they are met in perhaps eight or ten places along the bay-facing shore. These are where the people are. The rest belongs to other things.\n\n---\n\n## Climate\n\nCold maritime. The Outer Deep moderates the worst extremes — the cape does not experience the continental cold of the Ganoss uplands, which have no ocean to buffer them — but the moderation is to cold-and-wet rather than to mild. The prevailing weather comes from the west and northwest, off the open sea, carrying persistent moisture and the kind of grey overcast that is not the temporary grey of incoming storms but the structural grey of a climate that has organized itself around the condition.\n\nSnow falls in winter at all but the lowest coastal margins and persists at elevation through spring. The summers are short, cool, and — when the weather clears, which it does in brief windows — unexpectedly luminous. Visitors who have crossed the bay in summer describe the light on Thalmagar on a clear day in terms that recur across accounts without coordination: a quality of illumination that makes the landscape seem more present than usual, details more precise, distances more legible. The Academy has proposed this is a function of the clean maritime air and the low sun angle. The Northern Azhoran sailors who cross seasonally say it is the cape showing itself when it chooses to.\n\nFog is frequent at the cape's extremities in the warmer months, where cold sea surface and warming air produce it reliably. The lighthouse tradition of the northern Azhoran coast does not extend to Thalmagar. Ships that approach the outer cape in fog do so with the navigation methods of people who understand the risks.\n\n---\n\n## The People\n\nThere are, by the most recent external estimates, between three and five thousand people on the cape. The range reflects the difficulty of counting rather than any particular ambiguity about whether people exist. They live in small settlements on the northern bay coast — fishing villages, essentially, supplemented by hunting and gathering on the moor and in the forest — and they do not aggregate. The largest settlement known to outside sailors is perhaps two hundred people. There may be larger concentrations in the interior that bay-oriented visitors have not encountered, but if so they have not made themselves known.\n\nHow long people have lived on the cape is unknown. The people themselves make no claim, in any form that outside visitors have understood, to having arrived from anywhere. The oral traditions recorded in second-hand accounts describe the cape in terms that suggest a very long presence — events set against geological features, an integrated knowledge of the megafauna's long-cycle behaviors, names for individual landscape features that include the slow and catastrophic alongside the immediate — but no founding narrative, no arrival story, no record of a first generation. They have, in the phrasing of one Northern Azhoran merchant who spent a winter on the bay coast, \"always been here, and they know it.\"\n\nWhether they came by sea from the north before the Outer Deep became the dangerous crossing it currently is, or whether they crossed the Oremindi during a period when the northern passes were navigable, or whether they are a remnant of a population that once occupied the whole northwestern coast before the mountains achieved their current severity — none of this is established. Their own account, as far as it has been transmitted to outside observers, is that the land remembers them and they remember the land, and that is sufficient description of how they came to be where they are.\n\nThey are a quiet people, by outside report. Not unfriendly — sailors making the crossing for trade in seal oil, walrus ivory, and preserved fish have been received and traded with for several centuries. But not forthcoming. The outside world's interest in them is commercial and occasional; their interest in the outside world appears, from their conduct, to be roughly equivalent. The question of whether their reticence is cultural, linguistic, or simply the demeanor of people who have found that outside visitors are usually passing through has not been determined.\n\n---\n\n## Language\n\nThe language of the cape is not a dialect of Northern Mittoli. Every outside linguist who has obtained vocabulary samples has established this and then found themselves unable to say what it is instead. It is not Moreshi, not Grassic, not any of the known Iberos coastal languages, and the comparison with Sevronese — which was proposed by a scholar who obtained samples of both — produced no correspondences that could not be attributed to chance.\n\nThe one consistent observation is that the language appears to be old. Not simply conservative in the way Taler is old — that is, preserving ancient forms of a documented ancestor — but old in the way Grassic is old: no ancestor in any surviving record, no relatives among any documented family, the product of a history that is not traceable from the outside. If the Prior Presence Hypothesis developed for Grassic has merit — that the language family's isolation reflects pre-dating the arrival of the Mittoli, Pyrosi, Moreshi, and Bouéni families rather than extreme internal change — then the language of Thalmagar may represent a third such case. Or it may represent the same population in a different location. Whether the cape people and the Plains peoples share a common ancestor is a question that the Academy has noted twice as worth investigating and has not investigated.\n\nWhat outside scholars have recorded of the language reflects the limits of brief trading contacts. The cape people learn enough Northern Mittoli or trade-dialect for commercial purposes. They do not teach their own language to visitors, which is not unusual, but they also do not translate it — when asked what something means, they tend to describe its use rather than render it into Mittoli equivalents, which is either a teaching philosophy or a statement about the impossibility of the task.\n\n---\n\n## The Megafauna\n\nThe northern Azhoran sailors who began trading with the cape several centuries ago returned with two reliable observations: the seal oil and walrus ivory were good quality, and the animals on the cape were like nothing they had seen elsewhere on the coast. Specifically: they were large. Specifically: not all of them were recognizable.\n\nThis has since been documented more carefully. The cape's isolation from the rest of Azhora by both the Oremindi and the Outer Deep has produced a fauna community that shares its origins with broader Azhoran megafauna but has been developing independently for a very long time. Hunting pressure has been minimal — the sparse human population does not, in general, hunt the large animals at all; the question of why is discussed below. The result is animals that have reached sizes no longer seen elsewhere, in a landscape where the ecological relationships between them have not been disrupted by the large-scale habitat modification that has reshaped the rest of the continent.\n\nWhat the cape has, in a phrase that has circulated among naturalists at the Caeras Valley Academy since the first formal account reached them a generation ago, is what Azhora used to be.\n\n### The Shore Elder\n\nThe Shore Elder is the first thing arriving sailors see that convinces them they are somewhere different. It occupies the flat rocky beaches of the northern and western coast in groups of three to eight animals. It is semi-aquatic — capable in the water, slow and deliberate on land — and roughly four meters from the broad face to the end of the tapering tail, with a build that is neither seal nor bear but suggests both without being either. The forelimbs are long enough to allow it to raise the front portion of the body upright; it does this frequently, apparently for observation. The head is large, broad-muzzled, and the eyes are well-forward in the face, with the binocular orientation more typical of predators than of grazers. The teeth, when visible, are the teeth of something that processes large prey — but its primary food, from what has been observed, is fish, large mollusks worked from the rocks at low tide, and the occasional seal.\n\nIt is not aggressive toward people under normal circumstances. It is simply large enough that \"not aggressive\" and \"not dangerous\" are not the same thing. A Shore Elder that feels blocked will defend the space available to it. The people of the cape do not approach them closely. They fish from the same beaches and have specific knowledge of which locations are occupied at which seasons. The knowledge is detailed, generational, and taken seriously.\n\nOutside sailors who did not know this have occasionally found out the consequence. The incidents are recorded in trading-house logs as warnings rather than complaints; the shore elder's response to encroachment on its resting beach is not attack in the sense of pursuit, but the consequences of standing ground at that scale are equivalent.\n\nThe Shore Elder does not appear in Azhoran fauna records from any other location. The nearest candidate species — the **bay walrus** of the Bay of Lol and Smunders Bay — shares some coastal habits but is otherwise not closely similar in scale or form. Whether the two share common ancestry or are convergent adaptations is a question for which sufficient material has not been collected.\n\n### The Cape Palmant\n\nThe interior of the cape, and the moor and forest margins where they open into wider ground, is the territory of the Cape Palmant — the largest ungulate documented in the northwest. It is elk-like in general structure and in the confirmed relationship of male to female to juvenile, but its scale exceeds mainland elk by a factor that requires recalibration. Mature males stand above two meters at the shoulder and carry flattened, palmated antlers that span three meters from tip to tip — not the branched form of mainland elk, but broad, shovel-shaped, and ridged in a pattern that Caeras Valley Academy naturalists who have examined shed examples describe as more archaic than any living cervid form they know. The sheds are collected by the cape people and traded; the antler material reaches northern Azhoran markets as a curiosity.\n\nThe Palmant moves in small mixed groups in the warmer months and breaks into larger herds for the winter lowland concentrations. The cape people know their wintering grounds precisely and route around them. Whether this routing is primarily practical — the Palmant, encountering a human settlement in its winter concentration territory, would be destructive without any intent — or whether it reflects a protocol older than the practical calculation is something the people have not explained to outside visitors in terms that translate cleanly.\n\nThe Palmant is not hunted. This is stated by the cape people as a fact rather than a prohibition, in the same register they use to say that the sea is cold or that the passes are closed in winter. Why it is not hunted — whether by active decision, by some ancient agreement, by pragmatic assessment of the difficulty, or by something else — has not been established from outside.\n\n### The Rack-Wing\n\nThe sea cliffs of Thalmagar's western and northwestern face are the nesting ground of the largest bird documented in Azhoran natural history. Northern Azhoran sailors named it the Rack-Wing for the profile it makes when seen against the cliff face — the wings stretched thin and angled, the body hanging between them in a way that looks, from the water, like a frame hung against the rock. The wingspan is between three and four meters in adults. The weight is substantial enough that it cannot hunt from a perched launch; it requires the cliff updrafts for takeoff and landing, and it spends most of its active hours in extended soaring over the bay and the open sea, hunting at the surface.\n\nIt is not closely related to any other Azhoran raptor. The Academy's most recent assessment is that it represents a distinct lineage with a morphology suggesting very long isolation — the bone structure of the wing, as examined from a found carcase, shows proportional differences from all known birds of comparable size that are too systematic to attribute to individual variation. It is not merely a large version of something known. It is something else, scaled to the conditions that the cape and the cliffs and the cold upwelling of the Outer Deep have selected for over a very long period.\n\nThe Rack-Wing has taken children. This is recorded in the trading-house accounts and confirmed by the cape people's conduct: approaching the cliff nesting areas is prohibited in specific seasons, and the prohibition is treated with the same seriousness as the Palmant routing protocols. The people do not take Rack-Wing eggs or young. Whether the reverse prohibition holds by design or coincidence — Rack-Wings have not, in recorded trading contact, attacked adults in the open — is not something the cape people have offered an analysis of.\n\n### The Standing Hul\n\nThe forest interior of the cape has a large land predator. It is called the Hul in the nearest phonetic approximation of the local name that has been rendered in outside records; Northern Azhoran sailors who have seen it call it the Standing One, which describes the most immediately striking feature of its behavior. The Hul is built like a very large bear — it is bear-like in the general plan of the body, heavy in the forequarters, capable of sustained bipedal stance — and in mature males it reaches weights that field estimates put between six and eight hundred kilograms. This is substantially heavier than any bear species documented elsewhere on Azhora.\n\nIt stands upright frequently and for extended periods. Not in the alarm posture of mainland bears but in the observation posture of something that habitually surveys from height and has built a physical structure that makes this effective. When standing, a large adult male is the tallest land animal on the cape, visible above the moorland brush, turning its head to read the landscape with the patience of an animal that does not have predators rather than the urgency of one that has.\n\nIt is the apex land predator. The cape people give it the specific attention one gives a permanent and serious fact of the landscape. There are protocols for what to do when one is encountered at close range, and the protocols are in regular use rather than theoretical. The settlements are sited and structured partly in reference to Hul territory — which areas of the coast they use seasonally, which valleys their paths cross, which forest margins they occupy through the breeding season. This knowledge is old enough and detailed enough to function as cartography.\n\nThe Hul is not hunted. Like the Palmant, this is stated as fact. Unlike the Palmant — whose size and herd character make hunting a purely theoretical exercise in courage — hunting the Hul would be physically feasible for a coordinated group. That it does not happen, and that this does not appear to require ongoing communal enforcement as a prohibition, has not been explained to outside observers in terms they have found fully satisfying.\n\n### The Long-Back\n\nA browser of archaic form, found in the interior forest and the forest margins. The Long-Back is smaller than the Palmant — the size of a large horse at the shoulder — but its neck is proportionally long in a way that no living Azhoran ungulate's is, and the effect at first encounter, even for observers who know to expect it, is disorienting. It feeds from the upper forest canopy on a level that other browsers cannot reach, and its range and movement are organized around the distribution of specific tree species whose upper-crown growth it prefers. The lower vegetation is largely untouched by it.\n\nThe Academy's one formal account of a Long-Back specimen — a skeleton obtained through trade, provenance uncertain — produced significant correspondence among natural historians. The proportions do not match any living species they know. They match, in partial and imprecise ways, forms described in the fossil record of several Azhoran regions: illustrations and descriptions of ancient remains found in the deep sedimentary layers of the Lotharn Mountains' fossil-bearing valleys, in the canyon country of the Moroshé margin, and in one northern Azhoran coastal deposit. The animals those fossils represent are understood to have been gone from the continent for a very long time.\n\nWhether the Long-Back is a direct descendant of those ancient populations, or an independent development of a similar body plan in a similar ecological role, is not established. The Lotharn Mountains' oral tradition of deep-time geological reading includes accounts of animals that no longer exist, rendered in enough physical detail that several Academy naturalists have been prompted to ask whether these accounts might be describing the Long-Back from the other end of its range. The Lotharn valley communities who hold these traditions have been asked this question and have given answers that the Academy has recorded as significant and difficult to interpret.\n\n---\n\n## Living with the Animals\n\nThe cape people's relationship to the megafauna is the thing that outside visitors find hardest to characterize. It is not worship — the people do not appear to address the animals ceremonially, and the protocols governing behavior near them are practical in character rather than ritual. It is not domestication — there is no management, no feeding, no breeding selection. It is not simple avoidance — the settlements and the animals' territories overlap, and the people navigate the overlap with constant and detailed attention rather than by leaving a safe margin around the unknown.\n\nIt is something closer to cohabitation at a scale of mutual awareness that requires everything both parties know about each other. The cape people know the megafauna the way farmers know weather — not controlling it, not ignoring it, but reading it with the continuous attention of someone whose life's conditions are shaped by a force that operates on its own terms. The seasonal routes of the Palmant herds, the Hul's territorial ranges by year, the Rack-Wing's nesting calendar, the Shore Elder's beach occupancy patterns — all of this is in active use at any given time in any settlement's decision-making.\n\nThe resulting culture has organized itself around the capability required for this attention. The knowledge of the megafauna is the central body of practical and social knowledge on the cape. What other cultures organize around agriculture, trade, or military capacity, the cape organizes around knowing where the large animals are and moving through the landscape that belongs to all of them. The elders who hold the most detailed and accurate knowledge of long-cycle animal behavior are the people whose counsel is most sought in every other domain.\n\nWhy the megafauna are not hunted is a question that outside traders and naturalists have asked and received incomplete answers to. The most consistent response, in the Northern Mittoli required for trade, is a construction that translates approximately as: \"They were here first.\" This may be a statement about history. It may be a statement about priority. It may be something that requires the cape's own language to say precisely, and the Mittoli approximation carries the shape of the meaning without its full weight. Outside visitors have tended to accept it as the most complete answer available.\n\n---\n\n## Contact with the Outside\n\nThe cape's trading relationship with northern Azhora is old but thin. Seal oil and preserved sea mammals, walrus ivory from the Shore Elder's beach neighbors, rare materials from the cliff-nesting Rack-Wing — feathers, occasionally a found specimen — and the shed antler of the Palmant, traded as curiosity and worked material. In exchange: iron, which the cape has no source of; cordage and fabric materials that can be made elsewhere but whose production requires time the cape's sparse population cannot spare from the ongoing business of knowing the landscape; and occasionally, requests for information that can only be filled by making the crossing.\n\nThe crossing is made in summer, when the Outer Deep is as calm as it gets. A few ships per season. The contacts are brief and practical. The cape people are not interested in expansion, in trade debt, in the political arrangements of northern Azhora, or in any offer that requires sustained outside presence on the cape. Several attempts have been made over the centuries to establish a more permanent outpost — a trading station, a seasonal camp, a Caeras Valley Academy observation post — and all have ended in the same way: the outside party found the cape's conditions difficult, the locals' assistance present but minimal, and the megafauna's presence more immediately relevant than anticipated. The trading contacts that have persisted are conducted on the cape's terms, which are: come in summer, trade fairly, leave before the season closes the crossing.\n\nThe Academy's formal file on Thalmagar contains accumulated trading-contact accounts, the sketches made by naturalists who crossed the bay, the skeleton and shed-antler samples obtained through trade, and several vocabulary lists of uncertain quality. The file is classified as high interest and low documentation. Several expeditions have been proposed. The most recent proposal noted that the crossing, the climate, the season window, and the likely cooperation of the inhabitants would make a full survey possible in perhaps three to five seasons if sufficient resources were committed.\n\nThe proposal has not been funded. The Academy has other priorities, and the cape has shown no sign of changing in the interim, which is the kind of patience that belongs to places that have been waiting longer than any institution whose priorities they are not.\n\n---\n\n## On the Age of the Place\n\nThe megafauna of Thalmagar are, in aggregate, the evidence that the cape has been isolated for a very long time. Animals do not become what the Shore Elder is, or the Palmant, or the Rack-Wing, in a few centuries. The body plans require either very long local development or preservation of forms no longer found elsewhere — and both options imply an isolation that measures in thousands of years rather than hundreds.\n\nThe Long-Back, specifically, raises the possibility that the cape has been cut off for long enough that animals present on its terrain before the final closure of the passes have had sufficient time to diverge substantially from their mainland cousins — or to survive, in a landscape where human pressure has been absent, while those cousins were hunted to local extinction across the rest of the continent.\n\nThe natural historians who have engaged most seriously with the Thalmagar materials have tended to reach for the same framework applied to the Ibenwood: not that the cape is unusual, but that the continent around it has changed. The megafauna that the bay-crossing sailors find strange were not always strange. They were, or something like them was, the fauna of a larger landscape that is no longer what it was. The Rack-Wing soared over more coastline. The Palmant's antler form appears in fossil records from regions where no living cervid of comparable scale exists. The Shore Elder is an archaic form that the Academy's records from the deep Lotharn deposits suggest may have been widespread.\n\nWhat Thalmagar preserves is what the rest of Azhora lost, and the vehicle for that preservation is the bay, and the mountains, and the sparse people on the sheltered northern coast who have lived alongside the large animals long enough that neither finds the other's presence remarkable.\n\nThis is either the most complete picture of what ancient Azhoran coastal ecology looked like, or a remnant so far from its context that it cannot be used to reconstruct that context. Both interpretations are currently in play.",
      "loreRef": "thalmagar",
      "climate": "Cfb"
    },
    "Narcosh": {
      "name": "Narcosh",
      "color": "#6d4c41",
      "lore": "# Narcosh\n\nThe first thing most travelers encounter from Narcosh is not a person. It is a goat standing on a ledge that should not bear weight, on a cliff face that should not be climbable, at an elevation that the ledge in question does not appear wide enough to permit. The goat is near-black, with pale facial markings in a pattern that, from a distance, the brain insists must be deliberate. Its horns sweep back and outward from the brow in a long, heavily ridged arc. Its eyes — pale yellow against the dark face, the iris large enough to be visible at distance — are oriented toward you with an attention that does not read like the attention of an animal that has decided you are not a problem. By the time you have completed this assessment, there are three more goats on ledges above and beside the first, and they are all looking at you with the same quality of attention, and then a Narcoshi herder appears from somewhere the cliff face offered no indication of concealing.\n\nThis is the normal introduction to Narcosh. The goat comes first. The person second. The understanding of either comes much later, if it comes at all.\n\n---\n\n## Geography\n\nThe Narcosh valleys occupy a section of the northern Oremindi where the mountain system does something unexpected: rather than running as a single spine, the range fractures into a cluster of parallel ridges that create, between them, a network of enclosed valleys oriented differently from the typical glacial carve. Most of these valleys run roughly east-facing, catching the early sun that the range's geometry would otherwise deny them. A few have geothermal springs — the northern Oremindi are young mountains, and youth in mountains means unfinished geological business, and the unfinished business here includes vents and upwellings that keep specific valley floors ice-free in the winters that close everything around them.\n\nThese two accidents of geology — the unusual orientation and the thermal activity — make the Narcosh valleys livable at altitudes where livable is not a word that would otherwise apply. The terrain is still extreme: the ridges between the valleys are not the gentle saddles of an old range but the sharp, fractured rock of young mountains still in the process of deciding their final form. What makes the Narcosh valleys a coherent inhabited system rather than a series of isolated pockets is the goat routes.\n\nThe Narcoshi call the routes between valleys *vel-narcoth* — a term that translates clumsily as \"the ways the goats go\" but carries the implication that the goats found them and the people learned them rather than the reverse. The routes are not paths. From outside, they are not visible as anything. They traverse cliff faces at specific sequences of handholds, cross ridges at specific points where the rock has fissures that accept human and goat feet, descend through chimney-cracks in the granite that outsiders standing at the base of the same rock face cannot identify from below. A Narcoshi moving between two valleys crosses terrain that a lowland observer, watching from a distance, would describe as a person climbing a wall, moving sideways across a wall, and descending another wall on the other side. This is accurate. The key difference is that the Narcoshi know which sections of the wall hold and which sections do not, and that knowledge — accumulated over generations of following the goats — is the effective geography of Narcosh in a way that maps cannot represent.\n\nThe valleys are six in number, ranging from the largest — **Narcoth-main**, the central valley that serves as the gathering place of the whole system — down to the two smallest eastern pockets that outsiders are not sure are inhabited but whose cheese and fiber appear in Narcoshi trade. They are connected by the vel-narcoth and by a shared awareness that the valley system is one thing and that being one thing has been the difference, at several moments in Narcosh's history, between surviving the winters and not.\n\n---\n\n## The Oremindi Above\n\nNarcosh is the last inhabited country before the northern passes. Beyond the Narcosh valleys, the Oremindi do what the Oremindi do in their northern section: they close. The passes above Narcosh are among the most severe on the range — open in perhaps one year in three, and in the years they are open, open briefly and unpredictably. What lies beyond them is the Cape of Thalmagar, cut off from the mainland as thoroughly as geography can manage.\n\nThe Narcoshi are aware of what the terrain above their valleys contains. They have terms for the large browsing animal that the Academy's naturalists have attempted to catalog from secondhand accounts — the Long-Back, in the Academy's provisional name — and their awareness is not academic. The creature's range extends into the upper reaches of the northern Oremindi in specific seasons, and the Narcoshi have encountered it often enough that their relationship to it is neither mystified nor casual but is the specific orientation that people develop toward large animals that share their terrain and require respect without requiring fear. The Long-Back does not come into the inhabited valleys. The goats' behavior changes when one is in the upper reaches, and that change in behavior is how the Narcoshi know.\n\nWhat else the northern passes conceal, on the Cape side, is not a question the Narcoshi find more interesting than the question of whether the passes will be navigable this year. They have their own terrain to manage.\n\n---\n\n## The Goats\n\nThere are three distinct Narcoshi breeds, and the distinction matters to anyone who works with them and is invisible to anyone who does not. To an outside observer, all three share the features that produce the slur: the dark coloring that ranges from charcoal grey to near-black, the pale facial markings in the high-contrast patterns that map onto the brain's pattern-recognition in ways the brain finds hard to dismiss, the swept-back horns that carry the length and curvature of something larger than a goat seems entitled to, the pale-yellow eyes that in a dark-faced animal appear to glow in low light, and the specific quality of the attention the animals direct at whatever interests them — a quality that lowland visitors consistently describe as wrong without being able to say exactly what is wrong about it.\n\nWhat is actually producing these reactions is the result of very long adaptation to specific conditions. The dark coloring absorbs the limited solar radiation available at altitude. The pale eyes are adapted to the lower light levels of north-facing rock and the long dawn and dusk that the valley orientations create. The horn conformation that reads as aggressive from below is the specific geometry that allows a heavy-horned animal to move through rock chimneys and narrow passages without the horns catching; they sweep back because that is what fits through the vel-narcoth. The markings, on close examination, are not uniform — they vary individually and are used by Narcoshi herders as identification markers in the same way that a brand or an ear-notch is used elsewhere, except that they are natural and present from birth and have apparently been consistent enough in the breeding population to be diagnostic.\n\nThe posture of attention — the quality that visitors describe as the goat seeming to evaluate rather than merely observe — is the hardest feature to explain without watching a Narcoshi goat in its actual environment for long enough to understand what it is doing. The goat is assessing the terrain. The goats of Narcosh have been selecting, across generations, for the specific cognitive quality that makes a cliff-face navigable: the ability to assess a complex sequence of footholds several moves ahead and commit to the sequence without hesitation. This requires a quality of attention applied to the environment at all times that, from outside, reads as the goat looking at you with calculation. The goat is not particularly interested in you. It is doing what it always does: reading the terrain. You happen to be in the terrain.\n\nThe three breeds serve different functions. The **Narcoth-main** breed is the largest, producing the most fiber and the most milk but restricted to the central valley's easier slopes. The **vel-ridge breed** — no outside name has been standardized — is the specific mountain-face animal, smaller and lighter with the most extreme version of the terrain-navigation ability; it rarely comes to valley floor and some of its range overlaps with the upper reaches where the Long-Back occasionally descends. The third, the **eastern-valley breed**, is the oldest in the oral records and produces the cheese that is most prized in lowland markets, a sharper and more complex flavor than the standard Narcoth product, from a different diet and a slightly warmer valley environment.\n\n---\n\n## The Herding Culture\n\nThe relationship between the Narcoshi and their goats is not one thing. It is a relationship between people and animals who have been cohabitants of the same extreme environment long enough that the practical and the cultural have become impossible to separate cleanly.\n\nThe practical: goat herding in the northern Oremindi requires knowing what the goat knows. A herder who cannot follow the vel-narcoth cannot manage a herd that uses them. The training of Narcoshi herders is the training of Narcoshi people generally — every person in the valleys learns the routes as children learn to walk, which is to say by following adults who know them across surfaces that look, from below, like they should not work. By early adulthood, a Narcoshi person can move between any two of the six valleys by three or four different routes, depending on season and conditions, carrying a load or driving animals. This mobility is what makes Narcosh a single country rather than six separate valleys. The goats taught the routes. The people learned them. The country is the sum of what both know.\n\nThe cultural: the Narcoshi breed-keeping tradition is among the most detailed on the continent. The oral records of individual bloodlines in the major breeding families extend to a depth that the Academy's animal husbandry division, when it received a partial translation, described as \"genealogical records more carefully maintained than those of most noble houses, and more consequential.\" The markings of each animal are memorized and recorded. The seasonal ceremonies that mark the transitions of the herding year — the spring climb to the high pasture, the breeding season pairings, the autumn descent — involve rituals that the Narcoshi describe, if asked by outsiders who manage not to be offensive about it, as \"keeping the record\" rather than worship. The record being kept is the relationship between the breeding lines and the terrain, which specific families know which sections of which routes, which animals have shown the route-reading quality in the most reliable form, which pairings produced the vel-ridge animals with the strongest high-altitude range. Keeping the record correctly is understood as keeping Narcosh intact. Not keeping it is how valleys fail.\n\nThe goats are given individual names. They are attended at birth. Old animals past their productive years are kept until natural death. There are specific ceremonies when a breeding-line matriarch dies that the Narcoshi decline to describe in detail to outsiders, citing not secrecy but the inadequacy of Mittoli vocabulary for what the ceremony involves. This is, in the Narcoshi understanding, simply true. Whether it reads as worship from outside is not a question they find interesting.\n\n---\n\n## The Slur\n\n*Devil Goat Worshippers* is not a phrase the Narcoshi use for themselves. It is what happens when a lowland traveler who has never seen Narcoshi goats encounters them at a livestock market, looks at the coloring and the markings and the eyes and the horns and has a specific reaction, and then notices that the Narcoshi herders who brought these animals have a relationship with them that does not look like the way livestock handlers elsewhere handle livestock, and draws a conclusion.\n\nThe Narcoshi are aware of the slur. Their awareness has had sufficient time to move through the stages that awareness of a persistent insult typically moves through — initial offense, then contempt, then a settled dryness that is not quite amusement but is in that direction. A Narcoshi trader at the Celder horse fair or the Vaundrel market knows, before the conversation begins, what the lowlander opposite them is trying not to say. The usual approach is to let them not say it as long as they want, conduct the transaction, and save the observation about the slur for the walk back to wherever the Narcoshi are sleeping that night.\n\nThe more patient Narcoshi, in contexts where explanation seems worth attempting, will note the following: the animals are dark because high-altitude solar absorption is a survival trait. The markings are natural identification patterns, not symbols. The eyes are adapted to low-light mountain conditions and appear luminous because they are reflecting whatever available light exists. The horns sweep back because that is the geometry that fits through narrow passages; an animal with forward-pointing horns cannot navigate the vel-narcoth and would not survive the terrain. The attention the animals give to their environment is constant because the terrain they navigate requires constant terrain assessment. None of this is demonic. All of it is competence.\n\nThe less patient Narcoshi do not offer this explanation. They have found that outsiders who arrive at the \"devil goat\" reading from one glance at the animals do not reliably revise it on the basis of information. They find this philosophically interesting in a way that does not make the conversation more enjoyable.\n\nWhether the Narcoshi have, at some level, a certain sardonic affection for the slur — whether there is a version of the \"devil goat\" framing that they use in private to describe their position in the world — is a question that the Narcoshi decline to answer for outsiders and that their behavior occasionally implies an answer to.\n\n---\n\n## Governance\n\nThe six valleys govern themselves and each other through the **Valley Assembly**, which meets in Narcoth-main at the spring gathering and the autumn descent, and which any adult Narcoshi who can be present may attend. The spring gathering is when the allocation of high pasture is determined — which breeding groups go to which sections of the upper ranges, which family takes responsibility for which vel-narcoth route this season — and the autumn descent is when the season's outcomes are assessed and the planning for the following year begins. The Assembly does not have a standing authority between meetings; the individual valleys manage themselves on the practical consensus of the people in them.\n\nThe closest thing to a standing office is the **route-keeper**, a role held by the elder with the most comprehensive knowledge of the vel-narcoth network. The route-keeper's authority is entirely technical — they arbitrate disputes about route usage and safety, their assessment of whether a specific crossing is dangerous this season is binding, and they train the next generation of herders in the full network — but this technical authority produces a kind of institutional standing that exceeds most formally political positions. The people who know the routes most completely are the people whose judgment about the routes most governs what is possible, and what is possible in Narcosh is defined by the vel-narcoth. The route-keeper knows the whole country in a way that the other valley-level elders do not.\n\nNarcosh has never been successfully administered by any outside power. This is partly terrain and partly a specific characteristic of the connected-valley system: there is no single point whose control constitutes control of Narcosh. Narcoth-main can be entered, with sufficient military effort, by a force willing to take the casualties of mountain terrain at altitude. Narcosh cannot. The other five valleys continue.\n\n---\n\n## Economy\n\nWhat Narcosh sells is specific and not easily replaced by other sources. The cheeses — particularly the eastern-valley variety — are traded south through Celder and Yunethre and eventually to the Iberos coast markets, where they command prices that outsiders consistently find hard to reconcile with the quantity they represent. The premium is not irrational: the flavor profile of the eastern-valley cheese cannot be reproduced outside the conditions that produced it, and the buyers who know it know this. The cheese trade is managed by a specific family in the eastern valley whose route knowledge and lowland commercial connections have been maintained across generations; they are the people who show up at Vaundrel's market with the product that causes the toll official to note the weight carefully and the merchant opposite them to try not to appear too interested.\n\nThe fiber from the vel-ridge and main breeds is a secondary export — heavy, dense, with an insulating quality that northern buyers value for specific applications, and a texture that requires processing methods the Narcoshi maintain that outside craftspeople have not successfully reproduced. The goat fiber trade is smaller than the cheese trade and more local, mostly going to the Celder and Yunethre wool markets.\n\nNarcosh imports grain. The valley floors produce some crops in the warmer eastern pockets, but not enough to sustain the population through the winters, and the cheese and fiber trade exists specifically to sustain the grain imports that the valleys cannot replace from their own resources. The balance has been maintained for as long as the records exist. It is exact in the way that mountain economies that cannot afford inexactness become exact over sufficient generations.\n\n---\n\n## Character\n\nNarcoshi people are, by most outside accounts, difficult to offend and difficult to impress. These two qualities have the same root: they have been navigating extreme terrain since childhood and managing their goats' reputation since adolescence, and both experiences produce a specific kind of equanimity about what the world offers. The terrain is what it is. The slur is what it is. The cheese price is what it is, and they know what it is better than the buyer does, and they are prepared to wait.\n\nThe one thing that reliably produces Narcoshi interest is technical competence. An outsider who demonstrably knows what they are doing — at anything, the subject is not important — receives from a Narcoshi a quality of attention that is noticeably different from the patient neutrality extended to everyone else. The vel-ridge animals show route-reading competence in the terrain; the Narcoshi value route-reading competence in people. Someone who is good at their particular equivalent of the vel-narcoth is someone a Narcoshi finds worth talking to.\n\nThe dry quality of Narcoshi humor is consistent enough to be described as a cultural feature. The standard joke format is the literal answer to a question whose actual import was different from its stated form. Asked whether they found the accusation of devil worship offensive, one famous Narcoshi elder said that they found it imprecise, and declined to elaborate. Whether the imprecision was the offense or the description was worth keeping is a question that the answer left open, which was the point.\n\n---\n\n## Language\n\nNarcoshi is a Mittoli-derived mountain dialect — recognizable grammar, shared core vocabulary — with a goat vocabulary that specialists have found more extensive than the vocabularies that other herding cultures maintain for their primary animals. The reason is not that the Narcoshi are more attached to their goats than other herders are to their animals; it is that the vel-narcoth requires precise communication about terrain and behavior that Standard Mittoli must describe in phrases. There are specific words for the moment when a goat pauses before a section of route to assess it (distinct from pausing for other reasons), for the specific behavior that indicates a vel-ridge animal has found a new route section (distinguished from simply wandering), for the sound the herd makes when one of the large animals from above has come into range, and for the quality of attention a breeding-line matriarch has versus a young female in her first season on the high routes. Outside scholars who have obtained vocabulary lists describe these as extremely specific. Narcoshi herders describe them as barely adequate.\n\nThe substrate layer that appears in Narcoshi valley names and terrain terms is from the same pre-Mittoli layer as the Mithala place names and the Canerd/Yunethre names — the same older phonological profile, the same resistance to Mittoli root analysis. The Academy has noted this connection without being able to use it. The name Narcosh itself is from this substrate; proposed derivations include \"northern pocket,\" \"the held place,\" and \"the country of the dark ones,\" the last referring to the goats rather than the people, or possibly to both.",
      "loreRef": "narcosh",
      "climate": "Cfb"
    },
    "South Oremindi Mountains": {
      "name": "South Oremindi Mountains",
      "color": "#00897b",
      "lore": "# The Oremindi Mountains\n\nThe western spine of Azhora does not ascend gradually. The land rises through the forest hills of the interior and through the higher ground behind them with reasonable predictability, and then the Oremindi Mountains begin, and the reasonable stops. The scale here is different. The peaks visible from the highest passes of the transitional hills — the ones that travelers from Ibenale or the Mittolo interior reach before the mountains proper begin — are not the Oremindi. They are the approach. The Oremindi are what you see when the approach is finished and you look up.\n\nScholars who have visited the base camps and the lower passes and returned to write about it use similar language despite coming from different traditions. The words they reach for are: absolute. Permanent. Indifferent. One geographer from the Caeras Valley Academy wrote that the Oremindi are not hostile to human presence so much as simply not oriented toward it. The distinction mattered to her enough to include in her formal account. The mountains, in her view, did not need to oppose you. They merely continued being themselves.\n\n---\n\n## Geography\n\nThe Oremindi run along the western edge of Azhora's interior, separating the coastal regions from the continent's central plateau and the Mittolo heartland beyond. The range is long, the spine continuous except for a small number of passes, and the passes are high enough that most of them are usable only in the narrow window between the melting of the previous winter's snowfall and the arrival of the next. The seasonal window varies by year. Experienced guides speak of \"open passes\" and \"closed passes\" as weather designations rather than permanent features; a pass that is open this year may be closed for three years thereafter.\n\nThe peaks reach altitudes that produce permanent snowfield. The upper range is glaciated — sheets of ice that move at the pace of continental time, calving meltwater streams that run west to the coastal plains or east into the Mittolo drainage systems. This meltwater is the source of the forest rivers of Alezhor and the upper reaches of Ibenale's corridor streams; the mountains are the origin of the water that makes the coastal regions work, even when the coastal people have never seen them.\n\nThe lower ranges and transitional valleys contain the only significant habitation. A number of small communities exist in the protected valleys below the permanent snowline — herders, mostly, and the merchants who supply them and the specialists who serve them. These people are not numerous and they do not aggregate. The mountains do not favor large settlements at most altitudes. What the mountains favor is the specific and the self-sufficient.\n\nThe northeastern terminus of the Ibenale corridor arrives at the foot of the Oremindi where the coastal plain runs out of room: the mountains, the forest, and the sea converge at the same point. The mountain-end community at this convergence sits precisely at this junction and serves as the last lowland supply point for anyone going into the mountains and the first civilized stop for anyone coming down.\n\n---\n\n## The Passes\n\nEntry into the Oremindi is controlled by geography more reliably than it could ever be controlled by deliberate fortification. The main passes number four. Of these, one is high enough to be impassable to most travelers without specialized preparation; one is navigable only in summer and only by experienced parties; one is seasonal and technically adequate but involves a three-day exposed traverse with no shelter; and one is Sevron's pass.\n\nSevron's pass — the Verath-nath in local speech, meaning approximately \"the pass that receives\" — is the most navigable of the four. It is still a mountain pass at significant elevation, with all the associated demands on preparation, supply, and weather judgment. It is not a comfortable route. But it is the route that has been used for as long as there is any record of the Oremindi being crossed, and it has acquired over those centuries the infrastructure that repeated use produces: way stations at appropriate intervals, shelter points cut into the rock, marked cairn routes on the exposed sections. The infrastructure is old and maintained by people who do not leave their names attached to their maintenance work. It functions.\n\nThat the other passes are not maintained in this way is a fact that observers have noted. Whether this is deliberate or simply a consequence of concentrated use is a question that different parties answer differently. The mountain communities use the navigable pass because it is the navigable pass. Sevron does not comment on the other passes.\n\n---\n\n## Sevron\n\nThe city of Sevron sits in a high valley on the interior side of the Oremindi's central section — far enough from the coastal approach to require serious commitment to reach, close enough to the main spine to be genuinely inside the mountains rather than merely near them. The valley is large enough for the city and its supporting agricultural terraces; the terraces produce food at altitude in ways that have required centuries to establish and that would take centuries to replace if they were abandoned. Sevron is not going anywhere. It has not gone anywhere for as long as its records extend, and its records extend considerably.\n\nThe city is built for altitude. The architecture is compressed and low-profile — not because the Sevronese lack ambition but because the wind at this elevation is architectural. The oldest buildings are partially below ground level, using the thermal mass of the earth rather than fighting the cold with insulation. The streets are covered where they need to be. The public gathering spaces are interior. The aesthetic of the place is, from outside reports, one of extreme functional precision: everything is where it is because that is where it needs to be, and nothing is where it is because it looked well.\n\nThe city is hard to reach. Having reached it, travelers report that the difficulty of the approach was appropriate preparation. Sevron is not inhospitable — visitors who arrive by the recognized routes with evident serious purpose are received with the particular courtesy of people who understand effort because they practice it themselves. But the city is not oriented toward ease in any direction. It is oriented toward something else, and the visitor's comfort is contingent on whether they can adjust.\n\nOutside Azhora, Sevron is mostly unknown. Within the continent, it is known primarily through the traditions that have descended from it — which is to say that most people who have heard of Sevron have heard of it backwards, encountering one of the lowland practices that traces its lineage to the mountains and only later, if ever, learning where that lineage begins.\n\n---\n\n## The Verath\n\nWhat Sevron has preserved, and what the mountains have been the occasion for, is called the Verath — a Sevronese word that resists clean translation into Mittoli or any other Azhoran language because the concept it names does not have an exact equivalent outside the practice itself. The scholarly approximations used by outside researchers include \"the integrated discipline,\" \"the mountain way,\" and \"the practice,\" none of which are wrong and none of which are adequate.\n\nThe Verath is four things simultaneously, and the Sevronese position — to the extent that they state it, which is not often — is that separating the four is the fundamental error that the lowland traditions have all made. The four aspects are: the physical (breath, cold endurance, the exact control of what the body does and does not do), the mental (precision of attention, the specific quality of sustained concentration that they distinguish sharply from general intelligence), the spiritual (the relationship between the practitioner and the mountains as sacred geography — not metaphorically, but as a literal ongoing encounter), and the sorcerous (real effects in the world, including survival of conditions that should not be survivable, perception at a range and depth that training does not otherwise produce, and healing of the specific kind that requires understanding what a body is doing rather than what it should be doing). These four are not four things. They are four facets of one thing, and the one thing is what the Verath teaches. The facets are how it gets described.\n\nThe cold is the teacher. This is not poetic language. The specific cold of the Oremindi — altitude cold, dry cold, the cold that is present even in midsummer above the snowline — does something to the body and attention that cannot be replicated in warmer conditions. The physical practice is calibrated to what cold demands and what cold reveals. The sorcerous effects are achieved at temperatures that practitioners in lowland sects have spent centuries attempting to substitute for with other methods. The results are not identical. Sevronese practitioners who are aware of lowland sect work — which is not all of them, and not their primary concern — describe the results as \"adjacent.\" Something in the same direction, achieved by a different route, arriving somewhere near but not at the same place.\n\nThe lineage in Sevron is unbroken. This matters to the Sevronese and the reasons it matters are not entirely communicable to outside scholars. The unbroken nature is not a claim about institutional continuity — teachers and students, records and authority — though it is also that. It is a claim about something within the practice that must be transmitted directly and cannot be reconstructed from written description, that gets thinner with each generation of removed transmission, and that is still present in Sevron and diminishing in all the traditions that have descended from it. The Sevronese describe this without distress. They describe their own position with equanimity. They also do not seek to correct the lowland sects, which they could do, if they wanted to.\n\nThey do not want to.\n\n---\n\n## The Sects Below the Mountains\n\nThe traditions that have descended from the Verath are numerous enough that outside scholars have given them a collective name — \"the Verath-derived traditions,\" though individual practitioners of each tradition generally do not use this term and some would object to it. The traditions have spread across several regions of Azhora, are present in a few contexts outside it, and are various enough in their current form that a scholar encountering two of them in succession might not immediately identify them as related.\n\nThey are related. The shared elements — certain physical postures, certain breath practices, specific attention instructions that appear in recognizable form across traditions that have otherwise diverged completely — trace back to practitioners who came down from the Oremindi and carried fragments of what they had learned into contexts where it could be taught and practiced without the mountains. The fragments were genuine. What got lost was the integration: each tradition captured one of the four aspects, or at most one and a half, and built its practice around that capture.\n\nThe physical traditions emphasize breath and cold endurance — cold being simulated by various methods in lowland contexts, none of which quite work, though practitioners develop genuine capacities that are useful and real. The mental traditions are the most widely distributed; the attention practices of the Verath travel well, perhaps because attention does not require altitude, and several of the larger mental-tradition sects are practiced in cities far from the mountains. The spiritual traditions have the most varied interpretations of what the Sevronese originally intended; the relationship to sacred geography has been reconstructed around other geographies, producing traditions that preserve the form of the relationship while substituting different content. The sorcerous traditions are the most secretive, possess the most dramatic claims, and achieve the most inconsistent results. Practitioners of the sorcerous traditions know that something is missing. They theorize about what it is. Most of their theories are interesting and wrong.\n\nThe lowland sects are mostly unaware that they are incomplete. They experience themselves as practicing the Verath or something derivative of it, and their practices are functional and produce real effects, and the people who practice them are serious and in most cases genuinely changed by the practice. The question of completeness is not available to them from inside the traditions they are in. A few practitioners have, over the centuries, experienced something inside their practice — a direction the practice seems to point without arriving — and have traced the pointing backward and found their way to the Oremindi and made the ascent to Sevron. These people are received in Sevron. What happens when they arrive is not reported in any outside source. They do not, in the main, return to the traditions they came from.\n\n---\n\n## Sevron and the World\n\nSevron does not trade in the ordinary sense. The city's self-sufficiency is not a political statement but a practical consequence of where it is: provisioning a high mountain city through difficult passes is inherently different from provisioning a coastal port, and Sevron has organized itself over centuries to not require what it cannot reliably receive. The agricultural terraces, the preserved food stores, the particular Sevronese relationship with scarcity — all of these reflect the arithmetic of high-altitude subsistence.\n\nWhat Sevron does occasionally send out is people. Not emissaries, not ambassadors, not missionaries. The Verath has never sought expansion; the lowland traditions that call themselves Verath-derived are all products of practitioners who left Sevron of their own will and carried what they knew to where they were going. Occasionally a Sevronese practitioner leaves. The city does not prevent this. The city also does not maintain ongoing relationships with the traditions that descend from departed practitioners; the connection, if any, is in the practice and not in any institution.\n\nFrom outside, Sevron appears to have neither ambitions nor vulnerabilities. Powers that have looked at the mountain approach and calculated whether conquest was feasible have concluded that it was not, and that the strategic value of what was at the top of those passes did not repay the attempt. This calculation has been made and remade enough times that it now functions as tradition rather than strategy. Sevron has not been attacked in living memory and may not have been attacked in generational memory. The city knows this history in the way that old cities know things: not as a record that needs to be consulted but as a ground condition that shapes everything without being frequently named.\n\nThe Caeras Valley Academy has a file on Sevron. The file contains travelers' accounts, several second-hand reports, one detailed account by a geographer who reached the lower approach before altitude sickness required her to turn back, and a copy of a text that claims to be a Sevronese document of uncertain provenance. The Academy's position on the text is that it may be authentic, may be a sophisticated forgery, and that distinguishing the two from the outside is not currently possible. The file is cross-referenced with the entries on the Verath-derived traditions. The cross-references are extensive. The conclusions drawn from them are hedged.\n\n---\n\n## Language\n\nThe language of Sevron is not related to any of the five Azhoran language families. Scholars who have obtained samples — which is rare, since Sevronese practitioners who descend to the lowlands tend to learn the local language rather than teach their own — have established this negatively, by exhausting the comparison possibilities, rather than positively, by finding what it is related to. The negative establishment is thorough. Sevronese is not Forest Mittoli, not Standard Mittoli, not any known dialect of Moreshi, not Grassic, not any of the coastal trade languages. It is structurally opaque to Azhoran linguists in ways that suggest either extreme age or entirely separate development or both.\n\nThe Verath texts, to the extent that any exist in transmissible form, are in Sevronese. Translations have been attempted by practitioners who learned the language; the translations are agreed by Sevronese speakers — the few who have seen them — to be approximately correct in content and unable to transmit what the original does. Whether this is a property of translation generally or a specific property of the relationship between the language and the practice is a question that the Sevronese regard as already answered and lowland scholars regard as still open.\n\nThe word *verath* itself, borrowed into scholarly Mittoli as a technical term, appears to contain an internal structure that resists decomposition. Attempts to identify a root and a suffix have produced multiple competing analyses. The Sevronese, when this analysis project has been described to them, have been politely interested and have not helped.",
      "loreRef": "oremindi",
      "climate": "Dwc"
    },
    "East Oremindi Mountains": {
      "name": "East Oremindi Mountains",
      "color": "#1e88e5",
      "lore": "# The Oremindi Mountains\n\nThe western spine of Azhora does not ascend gradually. The land rises through the forest hills of the interior and through the higher ground behind them with reasonable predictability, and then the Oremindi Mountains begin, and the reasonable stops. The scale here is different. The peaks visible from the highest passes of the transitional hills — the ones that travelers from Ibenale or the Mittolo interior reach before the mountains proper begin — are not the Oremindi. They are the approach. The Oremindi are what you see when the approach is finished and you look up.\n\nScholars who have visited the base camps and the lower passes and returned to write about it use similar language despite coming from different traditions. The words they reach for are: absolute. Permanent. Indifferent. One geographer from the Caeras Valley Academy wrote that the Oremindi are not hostile to human presence so much as simply not oriented toward it. The distinction mattered to her enough to include in her formal account. The mountains, in her view, did not need to oppose you. They merely continued being themselves.\n\n---\n\n## Geography\n\nThe Oremindi run along the western edge of Azhora's interior, separating the coastal regions from the continent's central plateau and the Mittolo heartland beyond. The range is long, the spine continuous except for a small number of passes, and the passes are high enough that most of them are usable only in the narrow window between the melting of the previous winter's snowfall and the arrival of the next. The seasonal window varies by year. Experienced guides speak of \"open passes\" and \"closed passes\" as weather designations rather than permanent features; a pass that is open this year may be closed for three years thereafter.\n\nThe peaks reach altitudes that produce permanent snowfield. The upper range is glaciated — sheets of ice that move at the pace of continental time, calving meltwater streams that run west to the coastal plains or east into the Mittolo drainage systems. This meltwater is the source of the forest rivers of Alezhor and the upper reaches of Ibenale's corridor streams; the mountains are the origin of the water that makes the coastal regions work, even when the coastal people have never seen them.\n\nThe lower ranges and transitional valleys contain the only significant habitation. A number of small communities exist in the protected valleys below the permanent snowline — herders, mostly, and the merchants who supply them and the specialists who serve them. These people are not numerous and they do not aggregate. The mountains do not favor large settlements at most altitudes. What the mountains favor is the specific and the self-sufficient.\n\nThe northeastern terminus of the Ibenale corridor arrives at the foot of the Oremindi where the coastal plain runs out of room: the mountains, the forest, and the sea converge at the same point. The mountain-end community at this convergence sits precisely at this junction and serves as the last lowland supply point for anyone going into the mountains and the first civilized stop for anyone coming down.\n\n---\n\n## The Passes\n\nEntry into the Oremindi is controlled by geography more reliably than it could ever be controlled by deliberate fortification. The main passes number four. Of these, one is high enough to be impassable to most travelers without specialized preparation; one is navigable only in summer and only by experienced parties; one is seasonal and technically adequate but involves a three-day exposed traverse with no shelter; and one is Sevron's pass.\n\nSevron's pass — the Verath-nath in local speech, meaning approximately \"the pass that receives\" — is the most navigable of the four. It is still a mountain pass at significant elevation, with all the associated demands on preparation, supply, and weather judgment. It is not a comfortable route. But it is the route that has been used for as long as there is any record of the Oremindi being crossed, and it has acquired over those centuries the infrastructure that repeated use produces: way stations at appropriate intervals, shelter points cut into the rock, marked cairn routes on the exposed sections. The infrastructure is old and maintained by people who do not leave their names attached to their maintenance work. It functions.\n\nThat the other passes are not maintained in this way is a fact that observers have noted. Whether this is deliberate or simply a consequence of concentrated use is a question that different parties answer differently. The mountain communities use the navigable pass because it is the navigable pass. Sevron does not comment on the other passes.\n\n---\n\n## Sevron\n\nThe city of Sevron sits in a high valley on the interior side of the Oremindi's central section — far enough from the coastal approach to require serious commitment to reach, close enough to the main spine to be genuinely inside the mountains rather than merely near them. The valley is large enough for the city and its supporting agricultural terraces; the terraces produce food at altitude in ways that have required centuries to establish and that would take centuries to replace if they were abandoned. Sevron is not going anywhere. It has not gone anywhere for as long as its records extend, and its records extend considerably.\n\nThe city is built for altitude. The architecture is compressed and low-profile — not because the Sevronese lack ambition but because the wind at this elevation is architectural. The oldest buildings are partially below ground level, using the thermal mass of the earth rather than fighting the cold with insulation. The streets are covered where they need to be. The public gathering spaces are interior. The aesthetic of the place is, from outside reports, one of extreme functional precision: everything is where it is because that is where it needs to be, and nothing is where it is because it looked well.\n\nThe city is hard to reach. Having reached it, travelers report that the difficulty of the approach was appropriate preparation. Sevron is not inhospitable — visitors who arrive by the recognized routes with evident serious purpose are received with the particular courtesy of people who understand effort because they practice it themselves. But the city is not oriented toward ease in any direction. It is oriented toward something else, and the visitor's comfort is contingent on whether they can adjust.\n\nOutside Azhora, Sevron is mostly unknown. Within the continent, it is known primarily through the traditions that have descended from it — which is to say that most people who have heard of Sevron have heard of it backwards, encountering one of the lowland practices that traces its lineage to the mountains and only later, if ever, learning where that lineage begins.\n\n---\n\n## The Verath\n\nWhat Sevron has preserved, and what the mountains have been the occasion for, is called the Verath — a Sevronese word that resists clean translation into Mittoli or any other Azhoran language because the concept it names does not have an exact equivalent outside the practice itself. The scholarly approximations used by outside researchers include \"the integrated discipline,\" \"the mountain way,\" and \"the practice,\" none of which are wrong and none of which are adequate.\n\nThe Verath is four things simultaneously, and the Sevronese position — to the extent that they state it, which is not often — is that separating the four is the fundamental error that the lowland traditions have all made. The four aspects are: the physical (breath, cold endurance, the exact control of what the body does and does not do), the mental (precision of attention, the specific quality of sustained concentration that they distinguish sharply from general intelligence), the spiritual (the relationship between the practitioner and the mountains as sacred geography — not metaphorically, but as a literal ongoing encounter), and the sorcerous (real effects in the world, including survival of conditions that should not be survivable, perception at a range and depth that training does not otherwise produce, and healing of the specific kind that requires understanding what a body is doing rather than what it should be doing). These four are not four things. They are four facets of one thing, and the one thing is what the Verath teaches. The facets are how it gets described.\n\nThe cold is the teacher. This is not poetic language. The specific cold of the Oremindi — altitude cold, dry cold, the cold that is present even in midsummer above the snowline — does something to the body and attention that cannot be replicated in warmer conditions. The physical practice is calibrated to what cold demands and what cold reveals. The sorcerous effects are achieved at temperatures that practitioners in lowland sects have spent centuries attempting to substitute for with other methods. The results are not identical. Sevronese practitioners who are aware of lowland sect work — which is not all of them, and not their primary concern — describe the results as \"adjacent.\" Something in the same direction, achieved by a different route, arriving somewhere near but not at the same place.\n\nThe lineage in Sevron is unbroken. This matters to the Sevronese and the reasons it matters are not entirely communicable to outside scholars. The unbroken nature is not a claim about institutional continuity — teachers and students, records and authority — though it is also that. It is a claim about something within the practice that must be transmitted directly and cannot be reconstructed from written description, that gets thinner with each generation of removed transmission, and that is still present in Sevron and diminishing in all the traditions that have descended from it. The Sevronese describe this without distress. They describe their own position with equanimity. They also do not seek to correct the lowland sects, which they could do, if they wanted to.\n\nThey do not want to.\n\n---\n\n## The Sects Below the Mountains\n\nThe traditions that have descended from the Verath are numerous enough that outside scholars have given them a collective name — \"the Verath-derived traditions,\" though individual practitioners of each tradition generally do not use this term and some would object to it. The traditions have spread across several regions of Azhora, are present in a few contexts outside it, and are various enough in their current form that a scholar encountering two of them in succession might not immediately identify them as related.\n\nThey are related. The shared elements — certain physical postures, certain breath practices, specific attention instructions that appear in recognizable form across traditions that have otherwise diverged completely — trace back to practitioners who came down from the Oremindi and carried fragments of what they had learned into contexts where it could be taught and practiced without the mountains. The fragments were genuine. What got lost was the integration: each tradition captured one of the four aspects, or at most one and a half, and built its practice around that capture.\n\nThe physical traditions emphasize breath and cold endurance — cold being simulated by various methods in lowland contexts, none of which quite work, though practitioners develop genuine capacities that are useful and real. The mental traditions are the most widely distributed; the attention practices of the Verath travel well, perhaps because attention does not require altitude, and several of the larger mental-tradition sects are practiced in cities far from the mountains. The spiritual traditions have the most varied interpretations of what the Sevronese originally intended; the relationship to sacred geography has been reconstructed around other geographies, producing traditions that preserve the form of the relationship while substituting different content. The sorcerous traditions are the most secretive, possess the most dramatic claims, and achieve the most inconsistent results. Practitioners of the sorcerous traditions know that something is missing. They theorize about what it is. Most of their theories are interesting and wrong.\n\nThe lowland sects are mostly unaware that they are incomplete. They experience themselves as practicing the Verath or something derivative of it, and their practices are functional and produce real effects, and the people who practice them are serious and in most cases genuinely changed by the practice. The question of completeness is not available to them from inside the traditions they are in. A few practitioners have, over the centuries, experienced something inside their practice — a direction the practice seems to point without arriving — and have traced the pointing backward and found their way to the Oremindi and made the ascent to Sevron. These people are received in Sevron. What happens when they arrive is not reported in any outside source. They do not, in the main, return to the traditions they came from.\n\n---\n\n## Sevron and the World\n\nSevron does not trade in the ordinary sense. The city's self-sufficiency is not a political statement but a practical consequence of where it is: provisioning a high mountain city through difficult passes is inherently different from provisioning a coastal port, and Sevron has organized itself over centuries to not require what it cannot reliably receive. The agricultural terraces, the preserved food stores, the particular Sevronese relationship with scarcity — all of these reflect the arithmetic of high-altitude subsistence.\n\nWhat Sevron does occasionally send out is people. Not emissaries, not ambassadors, not missionaries. The Verath has never sought expansion; the lowland traditions that call themselves Verath-derived are all products of practitioners who left Sevron of their own will and carried what they knew to where they were going. Occasionally a Sevronese practitioner leaves. The city does not prevent this. The city also does not maintain ongoing relationships with the traditions that descend from departed practitioners; the connection, if any, is in the practice and not in any institution.\n\nFrom outside, Sevron appears to have neither ambitions nor vulnerabilities. Powers that have looked at the mountain approach and calculated whether conquest was feasible have concluded that it was not, and that the strategic value of what was at the top of those passes did not repay the attempt. This calculation has been made and remade enough times that it now functions as tradition rather than strategy. Sevron has not been attacked in living memory and may not have been attacked in generational memory. The city knows this history in the way that old cities know things: not as a record that needs to be consulted but as a ground condition that shapes everything without being frequently named.\n\nThe Caeras Valley Academy has a file on Sevron. The file contains travelers' accounts, several second-hand reports, one detailed account by a geographer who reached the lower approach before altitude sickness required her to turn back, and a copy of a text that claims to be a Sevronese document of uncertain provenance. The Academy's position on the text is that it may be authentic, may be a sophisticated forgery, and that distinguishing the two from the outside is not currently possible. The file is cross-referenced with the entries on the Verath-derived traditions. The cross-references are extensive. The conclusions drawn from them are hedged.\n\n---\n\n## Language\n\nThe language of Sevron is not related to any of the five Azhoran language families. Scholars who have obtained samples — which is rare, since Sevronese practitioners who descend to the lowlands tend to learn the local language rather than teach their own — have established this negatively, by exhausting the comparison possibilities, rather than positively, by finding what it is related to. The negative establishment is thorough. Sevronese is not Forest Mittoli, not Standard Mittoli, not any known dialect of Moreshi, not Grassic, not any of the coastal trade languages. It is structurally opaque to Azhoran linguists in ways that suggest either extreme age or entirely separate development or both.\n\nThe Verath texts, to the extent that any exist in transmissible form, are in Sevronese. Translations have been attempted by practitioners who learned the language; the translations are agreed by Sevronese speakers — the few who have seen them — to be approximately correct in content and unable to transmit what the original does. Whether this is a property of translation generally or a specific property of the relationship between the language and the practice is a question that the Sevronese regard as already answered and lowland scholars regard as still open.\n\nThe word *verath* itself, borrowed into scholarly Mittoli as a technical term, appears to contain an internal structure that resists decomposition. Attempts to identify a root and a suffix have produced multiple competing analyses. The Sevronese, when this analysis project has been described to them, have been politely interested and have not helped.",
      "loreRef": "oremindi",
      "climate": "Dfc"
    },
    "West Oremindi Mountains": {
      "name": "West Oremindi Mountains",
      "color": "#5e35b1",
      "lore": "# The Oremindi Mountains\n\nThe western spine of Azhora does not ascend gradually. The land rises through the forest hills of the interior and through the higher ground behind them with reasonable predictability, and then the Oremindi Mountains begin, and the reasonable stops. The scale here is different. The peaks visible from the highest passes of the transitional hills — the ones that travelers from Ibenale or the Mittolo interior reach before the mountains proper begin — are not the Oremindi. They are the approach. The Oremindi are what you see when the approach is finished and you look up.\n\nScholars who have visited the base camps and the lower passes and returned to write about it use similar language despite coming from different traditions. The words they reach for are: absolute. Permanent. Indifferent. One geographer from the Caeras Valley Academy wrote that the Oremindi are not hostile to human presence so much as simply not oriented toward it. The distinction mattered to her enough to include in her formal account. The mountains, in her view, did not need to oppose you. They merely continued being themselves.\n\n---\n\n## Geography\n\nThe Oremindi run along the western edge of Azhora's interior, separating the coastal regions from the continent's central plateau and the Mittolo heartland beyond. The range is long, the spine continuous except for a small number of passes, and the passes are high enough that most of them are usable only in the narrow window between the melting of the previous winter's snowfall and the arrival of the next. The seasonal window varies by year. Experienced guides speak of \"open passes\" and \"closed passes\" as weather designations rather than permanent features; a pass that is open this year may be closed for three years thereafter.\n\nThe peaks reach altitudes that produce permanent snowfield. The upper range is glaciated — sheets of ice that move at the pace of continental time, calving meltwater streams th