rect immediately, because willingness implies the horse is choosing to cooperate and *vel-caund* describes something prior to and deeper than choice. The horse is formed that way. It is not deciding. The quality is what the breeding produces, and it is why a Celder horse purchased by a buyer in Nyross or Ambron and kept three generations from the Celder grasslands will have horses by the fourth generation that have something adjacent but not identical to what the original animal had. The buyers come back. The prices are what they are.\n\nThe cavalry horse — the specific animal bred for military use — is the product of a crossing tradition that combines two of the five major bloodlines in proportions that vary by house and are not shared outside the breeding community. The result is an animal that can carry an armored rider at combat speeds across the kind of terrain Celder's own cavalry fights on, and that does not break its quality under battlefield conditions in the way that physically comparable horses from other countries do. This last property — the behavior under conditions that frighten horses — is the thing that military commanders from outside Celder remark on first and find hardest to account for. A Celder cavalry horse and a horse from another country, side by side in any field inspection, look roughly comparable. In the fourth charge of a long battle, they are not comparable at all.\n\n---\n\n## The Cavalry\n\nCelder cavalry is the best on the continent. This is not a claim made primarily by Celder, because Celder does not make claims of this kind; it is a conclusion reached repeatedly by commanders from other powers who have had occasion to fight beside Celder cavalry or against it. The records in the Caeras Academy's military archive contain a pattern that experienced strategists recognize: early assessments of a Celder cavalry force emphasizing the quality of the horses, middle-battle assessments emphasizing the quality of the riders, and post-engagem