tue that the breeders invoke in other contexts is *vel-caund* — the same quality they identify in the horses. Applied to people, it means something like: not waiting to be told, not deciding either, but being the kind of person or horse who responds fully when the moment is genuinely there. The opposite of this quality is the horse that has been drilled into obedience and stops being able to respond with its own fullness; also the person who has been trained into a role so completely that they cannot act outside it. Celder people apply this to their neighbors with a consistency that those neighbors find tiresome and occasionally accurate.\n\nThe relationship with the Oremindi is one that Celder does not discuss much and maintains carefully. The mountain rivers feed the grass that produces the horses; this is understood and respected without ceremony. A small number of Celder people in each generation make the approach to Sevron's pass and some of them complete the crossing. What brings them is not uniform. What they return with, if they return, they do not describe in terms that their neighbors find fully intelligible. The Celder breeders who have made this journey tend afterward to produce horses that other breeders describe as unusual without being able to say exactly how. This is noted. It is not discussed.\n\n---\n\n## Language\n\nCelder speaks a dialect of Mittoli that the Caeras valley cities classify as \"upper plain Mittoli\" — recognizably the same language, diverged enough to have a distinct character. The divergence is most evident in the horse vocabulary, which is immense by standard Mittoli measures. Celder has specific terms for gaits, temperaments, conformation features, age-related behavioral changes, bloodline tendencies, and the various states of the horse-rider relationship that Standard Mittoli must describe in phrases. Outside scholars attempting to learn these terms find that many of them are not simply labels for observable features but descriptions of relationships and dynamics that require the horse to be present and in motion to have a referent.\n\nThe word *vel-caund* resists clean translation for the same reason. It is not a description of a quality one can observe in a stable. It is a description of something that appears in the relationship between an animal and a situation, and between a person and a situation, and it requires knowing what to look for. Celder people use it in ordinary conversation and adjust their expectations when outsiders do not track it.\n\nThe name Canerd is not Mittoli in any analyzed form. The Academy's linguistic files note it alongside the other pre-Mittoli place names of the upper plain and suggest a connection to the same substrate layer that appears in the Mithala place names further east — the older language, or languages, that the plain held before the Mittoli spread across it. What the word meant in that older system has not been established. What it means now is the castle on the mound and everything the castle stands for, which is enough.",
      "loreRef": "celder",
      "climate": "Dfa"
    },
    "South Mithala": {
      "name": "South Mithala",
      "color": "#16a085",
      "lore": "# Mithala\n\nThe Mithala is the name the land gives itself — the flat country north of the Lotharn Mountains where the Lizeem River leaves the wooded hills behind and spreads across a plain that has no interest in rising. The horizon here is real. You can see it: a line, uninterrupted except by weather, circling the full compass. The first time a hill-country traveler descends onto the Mithala, the sky takes up too much of the visual field and they feel briefly exposed, the way animals feel when they leave the treeline. After a day or two the feeling resolves into something else — a sense of scale that the Mithala plains do not manufacture but simply make impossible to ignore.\n\nThe name is older than the other name. Scholars at the Caeras valley academies call the broader region Mittolo, after the family of peoples and languages that settled it; people who live between the rivers east of the interior hills tend to say Mithala, and they say it in the way that names which predate their current users are said: as given fact, not as choice. Whether Mithala is a Mittoli word in archaic form, or a word from something spoken here before Mittoli came, is a question the Academy's linguistic division has been discussing without resolution for several generations. The word does not decompose cleanly in any Mittoli root system. The river-valley communities, when asked, do not find the question interesting. Mithala is what this is called because that is what it was called.\n\n---\n\n## Geography\n\nThe Mithala is the eastern section of the Mittolo Plains, the great flat country that makes up the agricultural heart of the continent. The broader Plains extend west to the Oremindi Mountains, south to the Ibenwood and the Lizeem's lower reaches. Mithala is the portion specifically north of the Lotharn Mountains — the flat, sky-dominated, flood-managed river plains that the Lizeem crosses on its way to the Iberos coast.\n\nThe boundaries of Mithala are, in most directions, gradual. To the north, the plain eventually gives way to the Acorwood — the great dark forest that closes off northern Azhora from the continental interior. The approach to the Acorwood from the south is the slow thickening of the treeline along the plain's northern horizon, the edge communities sitting against the open country without wall or cliff to announce the boundary. To the west, the character changes before the land does: the Mittolo hill country begins some distance before the first real rise, in the thinning of the Lizeem's channels and the gradual reappearance of continuous tree cover and gradient. Minora sits at the approximate western entrance — the city at the first fork of the Lizeem, where the river commits to the plain and the plain begins in earnest.\n\nTo the south, the Mithala ends at the Lotharn Mountains. This is the one direction where the boundary is clear. The Lotharn does not announce itself dramatically — these are old mountains, rounded and forested and approachable — but the flat ends, and flat country of this extent does not end without notice. The Mithala farmers describe the Lotharn as \"the places where the land went wrong,\" which is not hostility but precision: the regular, predictable, flood-managed flat is the right land for what they do, and the Lotharn's ridges are a departure from it, the land having refused to cooperate with the river's plan.\n\nTo the east, the plain continues toward the Iberos coast without a sharp internal boundary. The Lizeem's channels eventually gather again as they approach the sea, and the flat country shades into the coastal country that ends at Nylon's harbor.\n\n---\n\n## The Lizeem and the Channels\n\nThe Lizeem makes the Mithala. This is not a metaphor: the soil of the plain is river deposit, laid down over long ages by the Lizeem and its predecessors, a deep dark accumulation of mountain sediment that makes the Mithala the most productive grain land on the continent. The river descends from the Oremindi, gathers the water of the interior hill country, and arrives at the Mithala already carrying the fine sediment that will become next year's soil. Every spring, when the snowmelt off the Oremindi arrives with the rains of the interior, the Lizeem rises. The channels spread. The plain between them is inundated — not catastrophically, not in the way that a flood is a disaster, but systematically, in a pattern that the farming communities have been reading and working with for longer than records extend.\n\nThe flood deposits what it carries and retreats, leaving behind a thin layer of new soil over the old, refreshing the land the way the sky refreshes the pastures. A Mithala field that has been farmed for ten generations is not exhausted; it may be more fertile than when it was first cleared, because ten generations of floods have added to it. This is not an accident the farmers stumbled into. It is a system they maintain, with a precision about what to plant where, when to plant it, which fields need the flood and which need to be protected from it, that is as old and as specific as any knowledge in the region.\n\nThe channels themselves are many and braided. Below Minora, where the river first forks, the branches multiply: first two main arms, then distributaries from each, then smaller branches, then the summer-only channels that fill in high-water years and run dry otherwise. A Mithala farmer navigating their stretch of river knows each channel by name and behavior. An outsider looking at the same stretch sees a complicated wetland. The distinction is expertise, and the expertise is entirely local.\n\nThe Lizeem's braided plain is not uniformly navigable. The main channels are deep and carry substantial vessels in all but the lowest-water periods. The branches vary; some are reliable enough for the flat-bottomed barges that carry grain east to the coastal cities, some are seasonal and usable only during the flood, some navigable only by the shallow-draft boats the river-channel communities have developed specifically for this landscape. The knowledge of which channel does what in which season gets passed from parents to children rather than written down, because writing it down would require a different book every few years as the channels shift.\n\n---\n\n## The Sky\n\nThe Mithala is known for its sky. This is not something people in the Mithala say as a boast; they say it matter-of-factly when pressed, the way people describe something obvious. The sky is large here because there is nothing to interrupt it, and a sky that is large behaves differently from a sky in hilly or forested country: weather comes from further away, the approach of storm is visible long in advance, and the light at morning and evening moves across the full dome without obstruction.\n\nMithala farmers read weather from the sky the way Lotharn valley farmers read weather from the ridges above them. The difference is one of scale: a Lotharn farmer reads the clouds forming on the ridge above their valley, which tells them about tomorrow; a Mithala farmer reads the western horizon, which tells them about the next three days. The Mithala sky-reading tradition is extensive, specific, and largely incomprehensible to outsiders, because it uses the Mithali vocabulary for cloud formation, wind direction, and atmospheric quality that precedes Standard Mittoli and resists translation. The words are not imprecise; they are precise about things that Standard Mittoli has not found it necessary to name.\n\nThe astronomical traditions of the Mithala are among the oldest on the continent. When the sky is real and present in every direction, and when the community's food supply depends on getting the timing of planting exactly right with respect to the flood calendar, the stars are not decorative. Several of the standard Azhoran astronomical frameworks were derived from or influenced by Mithala observational tradition, which had been tracking the sky systematically for longer than the Caeras valley cities had existed. The Academy's astronomical division has a complicated relationship with this fact.\n\n---\n\n## Agriculture\n\nThe Mithala feeds the continent. This is understood from the outside as a geographic fact and from the inside as a social obligation of extraordinary weight. The Mithala does not feed only the Mithala; it feeds the coastal cities that cannot grow grain at the required scale, the hill communities of the interior, the trading populations of the Iberos coast, and, in years when the harvest is large enough, reserves that the interior grain stores hold against the years when it is not.\n\nThis creates a specific relationship between the Mithala farmers and the political structures that attempt to govern them. Whoever controls the Mithala controls the food supply of a significant portion of Azhora, which makes the Mithala a constant object of political attention, military planning, and commercial maneuvering from outside. The Mithala farmers have a word for these periods of outside interest: *moravel*, roughly \"the grain-attention,\" said with a tone that indicates resigned familiarity rather than alarm. Powers have been in the Mithala before. They eat the grain while they are here and move on.\n\nThe primary crops are the floodplain grains — the wheats and barleys and rye varieties that the river communities have developed in precise relationship with the flood pattern. Each grain has its place in the cycle: floodwheat on freshly deposited silt closest to the channels, barley on the drained terraces further back, rye on the levees and causeways that organize the field layout. The full taxonomy of Mithala grain varieties is treated in **Grains of Mittolo**.\n\nThe animal partner of the Mithala grain system is the river-horn — the wetland cattle described in **Livestock of Azhora** — which pulls harrows through flooded fields too heavy for horses, provides the manure that supplements the flood deposit, and serves as the primary rural wealth indicator in the valley communities. A household with grain but no river-horn is not considered fully established by Mithala standards; the combination is what makes a farm a farm.\n\n---\n\n## Settlements\n\nMithala settlements are built for the flood. Villages and towns sit on the highest available ground — the natural levees along the main channel banks, the slightly elevated patches between braids, the occasional low rise that breaks the plain's absolute flatness. The buildings are stone-footed and timber-framed, the storage buildings raised off the ground floor, the lower levels of working buildings understood to be wet for part of the year and planned for accordingly. A Mithala village looks, to a hill-country visitor, as if it is braced for something; to a Mithala resident, it simply looks like a village that has been built correctly.\n\n**Minora** is the city of the Mithala in the same way that a lock on a river is a city: it controls the point through which everything must pass. At the first fork of the Lizeem, where the river commits to the plain, Minora collects its portion of everything that moves east. It is the largest inland city on the continent by most measures. Its position has made it a city of negotiators and traders and toll-takers, cosmopolitan in the specific way of places where every interest eventually passes through and some of those interests find it convenient to stay. See the **Minora** entry for detail.\n\nThe river communities below Minora are organized by channel affiliation rather than by political unit — a village describes itself as being on the North Braid or the Third Olveth Arm or whatever the relevant channel is called, and this affiliation is the primary spatial identity. Two villages on different channels in the same stretch of plain can have meaningfully different agricultural traditions because the channels are different: different flood timing, different sediment composition, different fish runs, different water court obligations, often different grain varieties developed in response to different water behavior.\n\nThe largest settlements outside Minora tend to be at channel confluences, where braids meet and the combined traffic justifies a market and a storage facility and a set of docks. Several of these channel-confluence towns are substantial, known throughout the Iberos trade network for the grain they consolidate from the surrounding river communities and package for eastern shipment. They do not have the political weight of Minora, but they have the economic reality that comes from being where the grain goes before it goes anywhere else.\n\n---\n\n## The People\n\nMithala people do not generally call themselves Mithala people; they call themselves people of the Olveth Arm or the Minoran plain or whichever river-section describes their actual location. The category \"Mithala\" is a geographic abstraction that outsiders use more confidently than insiders. This is not unusual in the Azhoran world — Lotharn people say the same thing about the Lotharn, and Amodians do it at least as thoroughly — but the Mithala version of it is specific to the river structure: your identity is your channel, because your channel is your flood timing, your water rights, your grain calendar, your neighbors.\n\nThe river communities share certain broad cultural traits. They are patient in the specific way of people whose primary activity is determined by forces they can manage but not control — the flood comes or it does not, in the proportions it arrives, and the community's expertise lies entirely in what it does with what shows up. This produces a practical orientation toward planning that accepts variance as the baseline condition, and a particular suspicion of people who promise outcomes rather than preparations.\n\nThe Mithala flood calendar — organized around the river's arrival, peak, and recession rather than the astronomical year — is maintained in parallel with the Standard Mittoli calendar and causes regular friction with administrative systems that use the astronomical one. The Mithala communities do not find this complicated; they find the insistence on an astronomical calendar in a flood-farming context to be an indicator that the insisting party does not farm the flood.\n\n---\n\n## Language\n\nThe Mithali dialects are Mittoli in family — the grammar, the core vocabulary, the fundamental structure are recognizable to a speaker of Standard Mittoli — but old enough in their divergence to cause significant comprehension problems in rapid speech. A Caeras valley merchant and a Mithala channel farmer can communicate slowly and carefully; in casual speech at ordinary speed they often cannot. The Caeras cities' Standard Mittoli is the prestige dialect precisely because it is the form most distant from any specific regional river pattern; the Mithala dialects are the form most deeply shaped by what the land requires.\n\nThe astronomical and meteorological vocabulary in the Mithala dialects is extensive and specific. There are terms for sky conditions that Standard Mittoli must describe in phrases — kinds of cloud at specific heights, kinds of evening color that indicate specific weather patterns, the appearance of certain stars in specific positions at specific seasons. These terms are used in daily speech by people who grew up needing them, and outside scholars who attempt to study the Mithala astronomical tradition regularly find that understanding the vocabulary is prerequisite to understanding the tradition, and that understanding the vocabulary requires living with the sky it describes.\n\nThe name *Mithala* itself — the name the region gives itself, the name that precedes Standard Mittoli's *Mittolo* — is not fully analyzable in any Mittoli root system. The Academy's linguistic file on it has been growing for two centuries and has not produced a consensus etymology. The name is there before the records are.\n\n---\n\n## Position\n\nThe Mithala plains sit at the center of Azhora's agricultural geography in a way that is not subtle. Everything that depends on grain knows where Mithala is. The trade routes from the Iberos coast cities run up the Lizeem channels to the channel-confluence markets. The road networks from the western hill communities thread down to the plain edges and onto the flat, where they can run straight. The Lotharn passes to the south bring mountain goods to Mithala markets and take grain back in exchange; Amodian chestnuts and Lotharn iron travel north while Mithala grain travels south, a two-directional trade that has persisted through every political arrangement the Lotharn has witnessed.\n\nThe mountains to the south — the Lotharn — are a permeable boundary, crossable but not trivial. The Acorwood to the north is a different kind of boundary: the forest that closes the gap between the northern landmass and the continent proper, dark in summer, strange in its light, and not routinely crossed by the river-plain communities who have no reason that would outweigh its navigation requirements. Between these two boundaries, north and south, the Mithala plain is a bounded world — large, but bounded — and the people who farm it have the self-contained quality of people who live in the middle of something rather than at the edge of it.\n\nThe proverb the Caeras Academy most often cites from the Mithala channel communities — found in a dozen variant forms from the Minoran plain to the eastern channel braids — is: *Vet mithalan, vel noreth*. In approximate translation: \"The flood returns. The grain does not ask.\" A description of the relationship between the land and the people who depend on it, and a description of the disposition required to farm it: you prepare, and the flood does what it does, and the grain grows or does not, and you prepare again.",
      "loreRef": "mithala",
      "climate": "Dfa"
    },
